atabase Selection:...\n2. Search Parameters:...\n3. Search Approach: ...\n---\n# ICAIL 2025, June 16\u201320, 2024, Chicago, IL, USA\n\nBarron et al. and filtering results. However, the examples provided, such as \"Smith v. Jones, 2010,\" were explicitly hypothetical, undermining the credibility of the response. Additionally, nemotron\u2019s inclusion of actual statutes like NMSA 41-5-1 blurred the line between hypothetical examples and genuine analysis, leading to potential confusion.", "mimetype": "text/plain", "start_char_idx": 33084, "end_char_idx": 34917, "metadata_seperator": "\n", "text_template": "[Excerpt from document]\n{metadata_str}\nExcerpt:\n-----\n{content}\n-----\n", "class_name": "TextNode"}, "__type__": "1"}, {"__data__": {"id_": "927d70a4-9abb-4312-ae07-ca9bcdae768f", "embedding": null, "metadata": {"file_path": "C:\\Users\\anteb\\PycharmProjects\\JupyterProject\\naive_rag\\data\\ai_articles\\Bridging Legal Knowledge and AI.md", "file_name": "Bridging Legal Knowledge and AI.md", "file_type": "text/markdown", "file_size": 52048, "creation_date": "2025-03-26", "last_modified_date": "2025-03-22", "document_title": "\"Transforming Legal Research and Decision-Making: A Comprehensive Framework Integrating Retrieval-Augmented Generation, Knowledge Graphs, Semantic Understanding, and Advanced Machine Learning Techniques\""}, "excluded_embed_metadata_keys": ["file_name", "file_type", "file_size", "creation_date", "last_modified_date", "last_accessed_date"], "excluded_llm_metadata_keys": ["file_name", "file_type", "file_size", "creation_date", "last_modified_date", "last_accessed_date"], "relationships": {"1": {"node_id": "1d3f5bd7-239e-43d2-ae7f-1e7cd01c88fb", "node_type": "4", "metadata": {"file_path": "C:\\Users\\anteb\\PycharmProjects\\JupyterProject\\naive_rag\\data\\ai_articles\\Bridging Legal Knowledge and AI.md", "file_name": "Bridging Legal Knowledge and AI.md", "file_type": "text/markdown", "file_size": 52048, "creation_date": "2025-03-26", "last_modified_date": "2025-03-22"}, "hash": "8b9a69e79061b64e3a9958d47ca5223db6e2ffd6fcab4d00822647d6e5777090", "class_name": "RelatedNodeInfo"}, "2": {"node_id": "5f0fd656-5d5e-472d-9273-7ff312c19684", "node_type": "1", "metadata": {"file_path": "C:\\Users\\anteb\\PycharmProjects\\JupyterProject\\naive_rag\\data\\ai_articles\\Bridging Legal Knowledge and AI.md", "file_name": "Bridging Legal Knowledge and AI.md", "file_type": "text/markdown", "file_size": 52048, "creation_date": "2025-03-26", "last_modified_date": "2025-03-22"}, "hash": "f03e4e6674a1ca12d3d395cf7f87d6e866237efa83a14dd5a5f913a37b3f5ee6", "class_name": "RelatedNodeInfo"}, "3": {"node_id": "c57f0bbc-3cce-4c9e-8aaf-33ffe0f622cb", "node_type": "1", "metadata": {}, "hash": "4f80c5ca412effddd0e0b787e3fba4131355909b01c345adf97c1dc043e0a853", "class_name": "RelatedNodeInfo"}}, "metadata_template": "{key}: {value}", "metadata_separator": "\n", "text": "Additionally, nemotron\u2019s inclusion of actual statutes like NMSA 41-5-1 blurred the line between hypothetical examples and genuine analysis, leading to potential confusion. This comparison highlights the strengths and weaknesses of each system: some models are better at summarizing legal principles, such as GPT-4o and Gemini, while our System shows the capability for precise data retrieval and analysis. Nemotron, despite its detailed methodology, suffered most from hallucinations overall.\n\n# 4.5.1 Case Studies\n\nTo illustrate the practical applications and effectiveness of the QA Smart-Slic system, we present four detailed case studies:\n\n# (a) Case Study 1 - Constitutional Analysis:\n\n- Question Source: Article IV, Section 22 [1]\n- Question: \u201cWhat happens to a bill if the governor neither returns it within the specified three-day window (Sundays excepted) nor signs it?\u201d\n- Response: According to Article IV - Legislative Department \u2013 \u00a7 22 \"Governor\u2019s approval or veto of bills\" of the provided text, if a bill is presented to the governor and he neither returns it within the specified three-day window (Sundays excepted) nor signs it, then the bill becomes a law automatically, whether signed by him or not, unless the legislature by adjournment prevents such return.\n\n# (b) Case Study 2 - Statutory Analysis:\n\n- Question Source: Chapter 50, Article 17, Section 3 [4]\n- Question: \u201cUnder the Healthy Workplaces Act, how does an employee accrue and utilize earned sick leave, and what provisions exist for its carryover, use for family or personal health, and reinstatement after separation from employment?\u201d\n- Response: Accrue: Employees accrue a minimum of one hour of earned sick leave for every thirty hours worked. Employers may elect to provide the full sixty-four hours of earned sick leave for the year on January 1 or pro-rata for mid-year hires.\n- Use: Earned sick leave can be used for the employee\u2019s or their family members\u2019 illness, injury, health condition, medical care, or preventive medical care. It can also be used for issues related to domestic abuse, sexual assault, or stalking.", "mimetype": "text/plain", "start_char_idx": 34746, "end_char_idx": 36854, "metadata_seperator": "\n", "text_template": "[Excerpt from document]\n{metadata_str}\nExcerpt:\n-----\n{content}\n-----\n", "class_name": "TextNode"}, "__type__": "1"}, {"__data__": {"id_": "c57f0bbc-3cce-4c9e-8aaf-33ffe0f622cb", "embedding": null, "metadata": {"file_path": "C:\\Users\\anteb\\PycharmProjects\\JupyterProject\\naive_rag\\data\\ai_articles\\Bridging Legal Knowledge and AI.md", "file_name": "Bridging Legal Knowledge and AI.md", "file_type": "text/markdown", "file_size": 52048, "creation_date": "2025-03-26", "last_modified_date": "2025-03-22", "document_title": "\"Transforming Legal Research and Decision-Making: A Comprehensive Framework Integrating Retrieval-Augmented Generation, Knowledge Graphs, Semantic Understanding, and Advanced Machine Learning Techniques\""}, "excluded_embed_metadata_keys": ["file_name", "file_type", "file_size", "creation_date", "last_modified_date", "last_accessed_date"], "excluded_llm_metadata_keys": ["file_name", "file_type", "file_size", "creation_date", "last_modified_date", "last_accessed_date"], "relationships": {"1": {"node_id": "1d3f5bd7-239e-43d2-ae7f-1e7cd01c88fb", "node_type": "4", "metadata": {"file_path": "C:\\Users\\anteb\\PycharmProjects\\JupyterProject\\naive_rag\\data\\ai_articles\\Bridging Legal Knowledge and AI.md", "file_name": "Bridging Legal Knowledge and AI.md", "file_type": "text/markdown", "file_size": 52048, "creation_date": "2025-03-26", "last_modified_date": "2025-03-22"}, "hash": "8b9a69e79061b64e3a9958d47ca5223db6e2ffd6fcab4d00822647d6e5777090", "class_name": "RelatedNodeInfo"}, "2": {"node_id": "927d70a4-9abb-4312-ae07-ca9bcdae768f", "node_type": "1", "metadata": {"file_path": "C:\\Users\\anteb\\PycharmProjects\\JupyterProject\\naive_rag\\data\\ai_articles\\Bridging Legal Knowledge and AI.md", "file_name": "Bridging Legal Knowledge and AI.md", "file_type": "text/markdown", "file_size": 52048, "creation_date": "2025-03-26", "last_modified_date": "2025-03-22"}, "hash": "a36b177cba0450a2371c0a417257b4c1fb6b28c1e4e0bb720cc2c65dd7611850", "class_name": "RelatedNodeInfo"}, "3": {"node_id": "f25c5f8d-5ebf-4e8e-b06c-ec74bac5959d", "node_type": "1", "metadata": {}, "hash": "6c6e733692be07ce2342375a69fad332487c55997dc8084cc15ed1f69d1d910e", "class_name": "RelatedNodeInfo"}}, "metadata_template": "{key}: {value}", "metadata_separator": "\n", "text": "It can also be used for issues related to domestic abuse, sexual assault, or stalking.\n- Carryover: Unused earned sick leave carries over year to year, although use may be capped at 64 hours per twelve-month period, unless the employer sets a higher cap.\n- Reinstatement: If an employee is rehired within twelve months of separation by the same employer, previously accrued and unused earned sick leave must be reinstated. Additionally, the employee is entitled to accrue additional sick leave upon re-commencement of employment.\n\n# (c) Case Study 3 - Court of Appeals Case Law Analysis:\n\n- Question Source: KRM, Inc. v. Caviness, 1996 [3]\n- Question: \u201cIn the case KRM, Inc. v. Caviness, what was the court\u2019s reasoning for determining that the water rights established by Williams\u2019 1963 declaration did not belong to the land sold to Caviness?\u201d\n- Response: The court determined that the water rights established by Williams\u2019 1963 declaration were not appurtenant to the land sold to Caviness because there was no evidence that the water had been used for irrigation on that land. Since the appropriated water was never used for irrigation purposes on the property, it did not qualify as appurtenant water rights that would automatically transfer with the land. The court concluded because the usage of the water was unrelated to the benefit or use of the land itself, the water rights remained separate and did not pass to Caviness when he purchased the land.\n\n# (d) Case Study 4 - Supreme Court Case Law Analysis:\n\n- Question Source: Smith v. South, 1955 [2]\n- Question: \u201cIn Smith v. South, what were the legal issues raised regarding the enforceability of the Washington child support decree in New Mexico, and how did the court address the conflict between the parties\u2019 post-decree agreement and the original judgment?\u201d\n- Response: The legal issues raised in Smith v. South regarding the enforceability of the Washington child support decree in New Mexico included whether New Mexico courts should acknowledge and enforce the original Washington decree for child support or consider the modifications reportedly agreed upon by the parties after the decree.", "mimetype": "text/plain", "start_char_idx": 36768, "end_char_idx": 38927, "metadata_seperator": "\n", "text_template": "[Excerpt from document]\n{metadata_str}\nExcerpt:\n-----\n{content}\n-----\n", "class_name": "TextNode"}, "__type__": "1"}, {"__data__": {"id_": "f25c5f8d-5ebf-4e8e-b06c-ec74bac5959d", "embedding": null, "metadata": {"file_path": "C:\\Users\\anteb\\PycharmProjects\\JupyterProject\\naive_rag\\data\\ai_articles\\Bridging Legal Knowledge and AI.md", "file_name": "Bridging Legal Knowledge and AI.md", "file_type": "text/markdown", "file_size": 52048, "creation_date": "2025-03-26", "last_modified_date": "2025-03-22", "document_title": "\"Transforming Legal Research and Decision-Making: A Comprehensive Framework Integrating Retrieval-Augmented Generation, Knowledge Graphs, Semantic Understanding, and Advanced Machine Learning Techniques\""}, "excluded_embed_metadata_keys": ["file_name", "file_type", "file_size", "creation_date", "last_modified_date", "last_accessed_date"], "excluded_llm_metadata_keys": ["file_name", "file_type", "file_size", "creation_date", "last_modified_date", "last_accessed_date"], "relationships": {"1": {"node_id": "1d3f5bd7-239e-43d2-ae7f-1e7cd01c88fb", "node_type": "4", "metadata": {"file_path": "C:\\Users\\anteb\\PycharmProjects\\JupyterProject\\naive_rag\\data\\ai_articles\\Bridging Legal Knowledge and AI.md", "file_name": "Bridging Legal Knowledge and AI.md", "file_type": "text/markdown", "file_size": 52048, "creation_date": "2025-03-26", "last_modified_date": "2025-03-22"}, "hash": "8b9a69e79061b64e3a9958d47ca5223db6e2ffd6fcab4d00822647d6e5777090", "class_name": "RelatedNodeInfo"}, "2": {"node_id": "c57f0bbc-3cce-4c9e-8aaf-33ffe0f622cb", "node_type": "1", "metadata": {"file_path": "C:\\Users\\anteb\\PycharmProjects\\JupyterProject\\naive_rag\\data\\ai_articles\\Bridging Legal Knowledge and AI.md", "file_name": "Bridging Legal Knowledge and AI.md", "file_type": "text/markdown", "file_size": 52048, "creation_date": "2025-03-26", "last_modified_date": "2025-03-22"}, "hash": "6615db082c2bb90387768bb411fa9b4e004f169430fa088fd119adda0a8eb03e", "class_name": "RelatedNodeInfo"}, "3": {"node_id": "b28718bd-ed52-457c-b871-da3d5f21c478", "node_type": "1", "metadata": {}, "hash": "1e45366940ef662232758350089074826089c7626605b34cb966e5c0f17f1d47", "class_name": "RelatedNodeInfo"}}, "metadata_template": "{key}: {value}", "metadata_separator": "\n", "text": "The conflict between the parties\u2019 post-decree agreement and the original judgment involved assertions that they had informally agreed to alter the child support payments stipulated by the Washington court.\n---\n# Bridging Legal Knowledge and AI:\n\n# Retrieval-Augmented Generation with Vector Stores, Knowledge Graphs, and Hierarchical Non-negative Matrix Factorization\n\n# ICAIL 2025, June 16\u201320, 2024, Chicago, IL, USA\n\nThe court addressed this issue by considering the jurisdictional and legal principles guiding child support enforcement across state lines. They emphasized the importance of adhering to the formal judicial process. The court likely examined whether the post-decree modifications had been legally formalized or approved by any court, and whether such modifications complied with the relevant child support enforcement laws, particularly under the Uniform Interstate Family Support Act (UIFSA), though it wasn\u2019t explicitly mentioned in the text snippet. UIFSA provides mechanisms for establishing, enforcing, and modifying child support orders across state boundaries.\n\nIf the modifications were not legally recognized through a court process, the original Washington decree would remain enforceable in New Mexico, and any informal or unratified agreements between the parties would not typically alter the enforceability of the court-ordered decree. The court would focus on ensuring that child support arrangements comply with legal standards to serve the children\u2019s best interests and that enforcement respects the authority of the original jurisdiction (Washington) unless properly modified through legal channels.\n\n# REFERENCES\n\n1. 1953. New Mexico Constitution, Article IV, Section 22. https://law.justia.com/constitution/new-mexico/article-iv/section-22/ Governor\u2019s approval or veto of bills. Amended September 15, 1953.\n2. 1955. Smith v. South. Remanded with instructions to make findings of fact and conclusions of law on default issues.\n3. 1996. KRM, Inc. v. Caviness. Reversed and remanded for further proceedings.\n4. 2021. Employment Law \u2013 Healthy Workplaces \u2013 Earned Sick Leave; Use and Accrual. Effective July 1, 2022.", "mimetype": "text/plain", "start_char_idx": 38928, "end_char_idx": 41077, "metadata_seperator": "\n", "text_template": "[Excerpt from document]\n{metadata_str}\nExcerpt:\n-----\n{content}\n-----\n", "class_name": "TextNode"}, "__type__": "1"}, {"__data__": {"id_": "b28718bd-ed52-457c-b871-da3d5f21c478", "embedding": null, "metadata": {"file_path": "C:\\Users\\anteb\\PycharmProjects\\JupyterProject\\naive_rag\\data\\ai_articles\\Bridging Legal Knowledge and AI.md", "file_name": "Bridging Legal Knowledge and AI.md", "file_type": "text/markdown", "file_size": 52048, "creation_date": "2025-03-26", "last_modified_date": "2025-03-22", "document_title": "\"Transforming Legal Research and Decision-Making: A Comprehensive Framework Integrating Retrieval-Augmented Generation, Knowledge Graphs, Semantic Understanding, and Advanced Machine Learning Techniques\""}, "excluded_embed_metadata_keys": ["file_name", "file_type", "file_size", "creation_date", "last_modified_date", "last_accessed_date"], "excluded_llm_metadata_keys": ["file_name", "file_type", "file_size", "creation_date", "last_modified_date", "last_accessed_date"], "relationships": {"1": {"node_id": "1d3f5bd7-239e-43d2-ae7f-1e7cd01c88fb", "node_type": "4", "metadata": {"file_path": "C:\\Users\\anteb\\PycharmProjects\\JupyterProject\\naive_rag\\data\\ai_articles\\Bridging Legal Knowledge and AI.md", "file_name": "Bridging Legal Knowledge and AI.md", "file_type": "text/markdown", "file_size": 52048, "creation_date": "2025-03-26", "last_modified_date": "2025-03-22"}, "hash": "8b9a69e79061b64e3a9958d47ca5223db6e2ffd6fcab4d00822647d6e5777090", "class_name": "Rel