 \u2013 Code of Federal Regulations Title 21, Volume 4 \u2013 Part 211 Current Good Manufacturing Practice for Finished Pharmaceuticals, available at: http://www.accessdata.fda.gov/scripts/cdrh/cfdocs/cfcfr/cfrsearch.cfm?cfrpart=211 (accessed 26 December 2013).|\n|32.|U.S. Food and Drug Administration \u2013 FDA (2013b). CFR \u2013 Code of Federal Regulations Title 21, Volume 4 \u2013 Part 11 Electronic Records; Electronic Signatures, available at: http://www.accessdata.fda.gov/scripts/cdrh/cfdocs/cfcfr/cfrsearch.cfm?cfrpart=11 (accessed 26 December 2013).|\n|33.|Velkovrh Remec, B. (2007). Validation in pharmaceutical industry, available at: http://www.scribd.com/doc/23022819/VALIDACIJE (accessed 25 February 2017).|\n|34.|Yin, R.K. (2009), Case study research: Design and methods, 4\u1d57\u02b0 ed., Sage, Thousand Oaks.|\n\n# VALIDACIJA RA\u010cUNALNOG SUSTAVA: PRIMJER UPRAVLJANJA DIZAJNA SUSTAVA UPRAVLJANJA KVALITETOM I PROCESNE IMPLEMENTACIJE\n\n# Sa\u017eetak\n\nCilj ovog rada je prezentacija sustava upravljanja kvalitetom (Quality Management System - QMS) za validaciju ra\u010dunalnih sustava te prikaz validacijskog procesa na prakti\u010dnom slu\u010daju farmaceutske tvrtke.", "mimetype": "text/plain", "start_char_idx": 50445, "end_char_idx": 51877, "metadata_seperator": "\n", "text_template": "[Excerpt from document]\n{metadata_str}\nExcerpt:\n-----\n{content}\n-----\n", "class_name": "TextNode"}, "__type__": "1"}, {"__data__": {"id_": "873c18bd-a885-47d6-a3e0-b722f3ef672e", "embedding": null, "metadata": {"file_path": "C:\\Users\\anteb\\PycharmProjects\\JupyterProject\\naive_rag\\data\\ai_articles\\COMPUTER SYSTEM VALIDATION EXAMPLE OF QUALITY MANAGEMENT SYSTEM.md", "file_name": "COMPUTER SYSTEM VALIDATION EXAMPLE OF QUALITY MANAGEMENT SYSTEM.md", "file_type": "text/markdown", "file_size": 53509, "creation_date": "2025-03-26", "last_modified_date": "2025-03-21", "document_title": "\"Comprehensive Approaches to Quality Management and Computer System Validation in the Pharmaceutical Industry: Regulatory Frameworks, Best Practices, and Case Studies\""}, "excluded_embed_metadata_keys": ["file_name", "file_type", "file_size", "creation_date", "last_modified_date", "last_accessed_date"], "excluded_llm_metadata_keys": ["file_name", "file_type", "file_size", "creation_date", "last_modified_date", "last_accessed_date"], "relationships": {"1": {"node_id": "2f2b69d9-5dae-4221-9dcb-f2fbedd34145", "node_type": "4", "metadata": {"file_path": "C:\\Users\\anteb\\PycharmProjects\\JupyterProject\\naive_rag\\data\\ai_articles\\COMPUTER SYSTEM VALIDATION EXAMPLE OF QUALITY MANAGEMENT SYSTEM.md", "file_name": "COMPUTER SYSTEM VALIDATION EXAMPLE OF QUALITY MANAGEMENT SYSTEM.md", "file_type": "text/markdown", "file_size": 53509, "creation_date": "2025-03-26", "last_modified_date": "2025-03-21"}, "hash": "1137d67616953033e3ad92507bb961ae6095138365fd269914446ae7383f7d2e", "class_name": "RelatedNodeInfo"}, "2": {"node_id": "c0473b25-9bde-4761-9b15-eae5a4841a8e", "node_type": "1", "metadata": {"file_path": "C:\\Users\\anteb\\PycharmProjects\\JupyterProject\\naive_rag\\data\\ai_articles\\COMPUTER SYSTEM VALIDATION EXAMPLE OF QUALITY MANAGEMENT SYSTEM.md", "file_name": "COMPUTER SYSTEM VALIDATION EXAMPLE OF QUALITY MANAGEMENT SYSTEM.md", "file_type": "text/markdown", "file_size": 53509, "creation_date": "2025-03-26", "last_modified_date": "2025-03-21"}, "hash": "b870ed061dc9cf2b9f1466af8d2174e54c398c4a83b5a2be9b1ec968d5def509", "class_name": "RelatedNodeInfo"}, "3": {"node_id": "8bee8365-db77-453a-a440-d4666eb4ee08", "node_type": "1", "metadata": {}, "hash": "5d09627f5e499ad4180ab72ab2833c2bb2bff81057987e3510f073e418b97697", "class_name": "RelatedNodeInfo"}}, "metadata_template": "{key}: {value}", "metadata_separator": "\n", "text": "Na temelju europskih i ameri\u010dkih pravnih zahtjeva, definiramo QMS za validaciju elemenata ra\u010dunalnog sustava. Primjer procesa validacije, zasnovan na kori\u0161tenju op\u0107eg V-modela, pru\u017ea detaljno razumijevanje prakti\u010dne implementacije validacije u praksi. Validacija ra\u010dunalnog sustava u konkretnoj organizaciji mo\u017ee se temeljiti na op\u0107im i specifi\u010dnim standardnim operativnim procedurama, koje formiraju QMS. Validacijske aktivnosti planiranja, specificiranja, razvoja/izgradnje, verificiranja i izvje\u0161tavanja se prezentiraju kori\u0161tenjem procesnih dijagrama, zasnovanih na prakti\u010dnom primjeru validacije ra\u010dunalnog sustava za upravljanje proizvodnjom Supervisory Control and Data Acquisition (SCADA). Empirijski dio rada koristi dvije istra\u017eiva\u010dke strategije: studiju slu\u010daja i akcijsko istra\u017eivanje. Predstavljeni procesa validacije, kao i primjer ra\u010dunalnog sustava za upravljanje kvalitetom mogu pru\u017eiti smjernice za sva poduze\u0107a, kojima su ra\u010dunalni sustavi zna\u010dajni. Iako se prezentirani QMS i proces validacije ra\u010dunalnog sustava zasnivaju na primjeru konkretnog farmaceutskog poduze\u0107a i njegovih pravnih zahtjeva, iskustva iz visoko regulirane industrije se mogu na odgovaraju\u0107i na\u010din koristiti i u manje reguliranim industrijama.", "mimetype": "text/plain", "start_char_idx": 51878, "end_char_idx": 53112, "metadata_seperator": "\n", "text_template": "[Excerpt from document]\n{metadata_str}\nExcerpt:\n-----\n{content}\n-----\n", "class_name": "TextNode"}, "__type__": "1"}, {"__data__": {"id_": "8bee8365-db77-453a-a440-d4666eb4ee08", "embedding": null, "metadata": {"file_path": "C:\\Users\\anteb\\PycharmProjects\\JupyterProject\\naive_rag\\data\\ai_articles\\COMPUTER SYSTEM VALIDATION EXAMPLE OF QUALITY MANAGEMENT SYSTEM.md", "file_name": "COMPUTER SYSTEM VALIDATION EXAMPLE OF QUALITY MANAGEMENT SYSTEM.md", "file_type": "text/markdown", "file_size": 53509, "creation_date": "2025-03-26", "last_modified_date": "2025-03-21", "document_title": "\"Comprehensive Approaches to Quality Management and Computer System Validation in the Pharmaceutical Industry: Regulatory Frameworks, Best Practices, and Case Studies\""}, "excluded_embed_metadata_keys": ["file_name", "file_type", "file_size", "creation_date", "last_modified_date", "last_accessed_date"], "excluded_llm_metadata_keys": ["file_name", "file_type", "file_size", "creation_date", "last_modified_date", "last_accessed_date"], "relationships": {"1": {"node_id": "2f2b69d9-5dae-4221-9dcb-f2fbedd34145", "node_type": "4", "metadata": {"file_path": "C:\\Users\\anteb\\PycharmProjects\\JupyterProject\\naive_rag\\data\\ai_articles\\COMPUTER SYSTEM VALIDATION EXAMPLE OF QUALITY MANAGEMENT SYSTEM.md", "file_name": "COMPUTER SYSTEM VALIDATION EXAMPLE OF QUALITY MANAGEMENT SYSTEM.md", "file_type": "text/markdown", "file_size": 53509, "creation_date": "2025-03-26", "last_modified_date": "2025-03-21"}, "hash": "1137d67616953033e3ad92507bb961ae6095138365fd269914446ae7383f7d2e", "class_name": "RelatedNodeInfo"}, "2": {"node_id": "873c18bd-a885-47d6-a3e0-b722f3ef672e", "node_type": "1", "metadata": {"file_path": "C:\\Users\\anteb\\PycharmProjects\\JupyterProject\\naive_rag\\data\\ai_articles\\COMPUTER SYSTEM VALIDATION EXAMPLE OF QUALITY MANAGEMENT SYSTEM.md", "file_name": "COMPUTER SYSTEM VALIDATION EXAMPLE OF QUALITY MANAGEMENT SYSTEM.md", "file_type": "text/markdown", "file_size": 53509, "creation_date": "2025-03-26", "last_modified_date": "2025-03-21"}, "hash": "8e29de02d6866dd85ada5f859cab2a7b4b75a94fec00c0142efc310721ceb707", "class_name": "RelatedNodeInfo"}}, "metadata_template": "{key}: {value}", "metadata_separator": "\n", "text": "Za verifikaciju predlo\u017eenog modela, potrebno ih je dalje testirati, kako u farmaceutskim, tako i u drugim, manje reguliranim industrijama.\n\n# Klju\u010dne rije\u010di\n\nsustav za upravljanje kvalitetom, ra\u010dunalni sustavi, validacija, V-model, farmaceutska industrija", "mimetype": "text/plain", "start_char_idx": 53113, "end_char_idx": 53368, "metadata_seperator": "\n", "text_template": "[Excerpt from document]\n{metadata_str}\nExcerpt:\n-----\n{content}\n-----\n", "class_name": "TextNode"}, "__type__": "1"}, {"__data__": {"id_": "db5907bd-82bb-4c46-9940-3e64a3d55692", "embedding": null, "metadata": {"file_path": "C:\\Users\\anteb\\PycharmProjects\\JupyterProject\\naive_rag\\data\\ai_articles\\CORRIGENDUM COMPUTER SYSTEM VALIDATION EXAMPLE OF QUALITY MANAGEMENT.md", "file_name": "CORRIGENDUM COMPUTER SYSTEM VALIDATION EXAMPLE OF QUALITY MANAGEMENT.md", "file_type": "text/markdown", "file_size": 1537, "creation_date": "2025-03-26", "last_modified_date": "2025-03-21", "document_title": "\"Correction Notice: Acknowledgment of Co-Author Toma\u017e Sallubier in Research on Computer System Validation and Quality Management Systems\""}, "excluded_embed_metadata_keys": ["file_name", "file_type", "file_size", "creation_date", "last_modified_date", "last_accessed_date"], "excluded_llm_metadata_keys": ["file_name", "file_type", "file_size", "creation_date", "last_modified_date", "last_accessed_date"], "relationships": {"1": {"node_id": "05387561-eb05-450d-869d-96db8edea133", "node_type": "4", "metadata": {"file_path": "C:\\Users\\anteb\\PycharmProjects\\JupyterProject\\naive_rag\\data\\ai_articles\\CORRIGENDUM COMPUTER SYSTEM VALIDATION EXAMPLE OF QUALITY MANAGEMENT.md", "file_name": "CORRIGENDUM COMPUTER SYSTEM VALIDATION EXAMPLE OF QUALITY MANAGEMENT.md", "file_type": "text/markdown", "file_size": 1537, "creation_date": "2025-03-26", "last_modified_date": "2025-03-21"}, "hash": "7b45d2b548352019eae3872de3845222400fdb057fc5dc8b7d67af7181785ca9", "class_name": "RelatedNodeInfo"}}, "metadata_template": "{key}: {value}", "metadata_separator": "\n", "text": "# CORRIGENDUM: COMPUTER SYSTEM VALIDATION: EXAMPLE OF QUALITY MANAGEMENT SYSTEM DESIGN AND PROCESS IMPLEMENTATION\n\n# Toma\u017e Sallubier*\n\n# Borut Rusjan**\n\nReceived: 5. 3. 2021\n\nAccepted: 12. 3. 2021\n\nOriginal scientific paper\n\nUDC 005.6:004\n\nDOI https://doi.org/10.30924/mjcmi.26.1.1\n\n# Abstract\n\nThe authors regret that in \u201eComputer system validation: Example of quality management system design and process implementation\u201c the following co-author was mistakenly missed from the authorship: Toma\u017e Sallubier, TS4U d.o.o, Kamnik, Slovenia.\n\n# Keywords\n\nQuality Management System, computer systems, validation, V-model, pharmaceutical industry\n\n# RELATED ARTICLE:\n\nRusjan, B. (2020). Computer system validation: Example of quality management system design and process implementation. Management, 25 (2), 1-23. https://doi.org/10.30924/mjcmi.25.2.1\n\n# Authors\u2019 request for post-publication changes of authorship has been processed and approved, according to the COPE (Committee on Publication Ethics) guideline, available at:\n\nhttps://publicationethics.org/resources/flowcharts/changes-authorship-addition-extra-author-after-publication\n\n# Contact Information\n\n* Toma\u017e Sallubier, TS4U d.o.o., Ljubljanska cesta 4e, 1241 Kamnik, Slovenia, Phone: +386 31 428 878, Email: tsallu@gmail.com\n\n** Borut Rusjan, PhD, Professor, University of Ljubljana, School of Economics and Business, Kardeljeva plo\u0161\u010dad 17, 1000 Ljubljana, Slovenia, Phone: +386 1 5892 553, E-mail: borut.rusjan@ef.uni-lj.si, ORCID: https://orcid.org/0000-0002-7061-9242", "mimetype": "text/plain", "start_char_idx": 0, "end_char_idx": 1526, "metadata_seperator": "\n", "text_template": "[Excerpt from document]\n{metadata_str}\nExcerpt:\n-----\n{content}\n-----\n", "class_name": "TextNode"}, "__type__": "1"}]}, "6b02c8e8bb86aa770130593fa2627e0aa1899f6eac6886d346f98f8b816b3dfb": {"nodes": [{"__data__": {"id_": "f9a4aab7-db90-49e2-9911-587e885a835e", "embedding": null, "metadata": {"file_path": "C:\\Users\\anteb\\PycharmProjects\\JupyterProject\\naive_rag\\data\\ai_articles\\ADDRESSING THE SUSTAINABLE AI TRILEMMA A CASE STUDY ON LLM AGENTS AND RAG.md", "file_name": "ADDRESSING THE SUSTAINABLE AI TRILEMMA A CASE STUDY ON LLM AGENTS AND RAG.md", "file_type": "text/markdown", "file_size": 72913, "creation_date": "2025-03-26", "last_modified_date": "2025-03-22", "document_title": "\"Navigating the Sustainable AI Trilemma: Balancing Energy Efficiency, Digital Equity, and Capability in Large Language Models and AI Agent Design\"", "section_summary": "The section discusses the challenges and considerations surrounding the sustainability of large language models (LLMs) in artificial intelligence. It introduces the concept of the \"Sustainable AI Trilemma,\" which emphasizes the conflicting demands of AI capability, digital equity, and environmental sustainability. The authors, Hui Wu, Xiaoyang Wang, and Zhong Fan from the University of Exeter, present a case study on LLM agents and retrieval-augmented generation (RAG), focusing on the energy consumption associated with memory module designs. They propose new metrics to evaluate the trade-offs between energy use and system performance, revealing significant inefficiencies in current frameworks, particularly for resource-constrained environments. The findings challenge the dominant LLM-centric approach in AI agent design and aim to provide insights for creating more sustainable AI systems. The document is dated January 15, 2025, and includes contact information for the authors."}, "excluded_embed_metadata_keys": ["file_name", "file_type", "file_size", "creation_date", "last_modified_date", "last_accessed_date"], "excluded_llm_metadata_keys": ["file_name", "file_type", "file_size", "creation_date", "last_modified_date", "last_accessed_date"], "relationships": {"1": {"node_id": "5bbe2f3b-a5cf-473e-9e50-1d182dcad9fb", "node_type": "4", "metadata": {"file_path": "C:\\Users\\anteb\\PycharmProjects\\JupyterProject\\naive_rag\\data\\ai_articles\\ADDRESSING THE SUSTAINABLE AI TRILEMMA A CASE STUDY ON LLM AGENTS AND RAG.md", "file_name": "ADDRESSING THE SUSTAINABLE AI TRILEMMA A CASE STUDY ON LLM AGENTS AND RAG.md", "file_type": "text/markdown", "file_size": 72913, "creation_date": "2025-03-26", "last_modified_date": "2025-03-22"}, "hash": "47c48bbdebdc34c45547d1ddc9efb86758d4c6bea59cc0a6ae9786ef666e699e", "class_name": "RelatedNodeInfo"}, "3": {"node_id": "f1123945-a91f-481b-bd3a-1b2e7288e928", "node_type": "1", "metadata": {}, "hash": "63edde37a908dc6be19aa989e51e558793d39ebc6ee9850b7c415fd87f6b2d7b", "class_name": "RelatedNodeInfo"}}, "metadata_template": "{key}: {value}", "metadata_separator": "\n", "text": "# ADDRESSING THE SUSTAINABLE AI TRILEMMA: A CASE STUDY ON LLM AGENTS AND RAG\n\nHui Wu\n\nDept. of Engineering\n\nUniversity of Exeter\n\nExeter, UK\n\nEX4 4QF\n\nhw865@exeter.ac.uk\n\nXiaoyang Wang\n\nDept. of Computer Science\n\nUniversity of Exeter\n\nExeter, UK\n\nEX4 4QF\n\nx.wang7@exeter.ac.uk\n\nZhong Fan\n\nDept. of Engineering\n\nUniversity of Exeter\n\nExeter, UK\n\nEX4 4QF\n\nz.fan@exeter.ac.uk\n\nJanuary 15, 2025\n\n# ABSTRACT\n\nLarge language models (LLMs) have demonstrated significant capabilities, but their widespread deployment and more advanced applications raise critical sustainability challenges, particularly in inference energy consumption. We propose the concept of the Sustainable AI Trilemma, highlighting the tensions between AI capability, digital equity, and environmental sustainability. Through a systematic case study of LLM agents and retrieval-augmented generation (RAG), we analyze the energy costs embedded in memory module designs and introduce novel metrics to quantify the trade-offs between energy consumption and system performance. Our experimental results reveal significant energy inefficiencies in current memory-augmented frameworks and demonstrate that resource-constrained environments face disproportionate efficiency penalties. Our findings challenge the prevailing LLM-centric paradigm in agent design and provide practical insights for developing more sustainable AI systems. Our code is available online1.\n\n# 1 Introduction\n\nLarge language models (LLMs) have played an important role in artificial intelligence, significantly advancing natural language understanding and generative AI. These models are now powering applications across healthcare, education, and business, demonstrating their growing practical impact in everyday services. However, this rapid development comes with significant challenges, particularly in terms of energy use and its impact on the environment and society.", "mimetype": "text/plain", "start_char_idx": 0, "end_char_idx": 1906, "metadata_seperator": "\n", "text_template": "[Excerpt from document]\n{metadata_str}\nExcerpt:\n-----\n{content}\n-----\n", "class_name": "TextNode"}, "__type__": "1"}, {"__data__": {"id_": "f1123945-a91f-481b-bd3a-1b2e7288e928", "embedding": null, "metadata": {"file_path": "C:\\Users\\anteb\\PycharmProjects\\JupyterProject\\naive_rag\\data\\ai_articles\\ADDRESSING THE SUSTAINABLE AI TRILEMMA A CASE STUDY ON LLM AGENTS AND RAG.md", "file_name": "ADDRESSING THE SUSTAINABLE AI TRILEMMA A CASE STUDY ON LLM AGENTS AND RAG.md", "file_type": "text/markdown", "file_size": 72913, "creation_date": "2025-03-26", "last_modified_date": "2025-03-22", "document_title": "\"Navigating the Sustainable AI Trilemma: Balancing Energy Efficiency, Digital Equity, and Capability in Large Language Models and AI Agent Design\"", "section_summary": "The section discusses the challenges associated with the rapid development of large language models (LLMs), particularly focusing on their energy consumption and environmental impact. It introduces the concept of the \"Sustainable AI Trilemma,\" which emphasizes the trade-offs between capability, digital equity, and environmental sustainability in AI design. The text highlights that while LLM deployments prioritize performance, this often comes at the cost of equity and sustainability, especially with the increasing use of LLM-based agents in various domains such as enterprise automation and personalized assistance. The section raises concerns about the energy demands of LLM inference and the potential for exacerbating resource inequities and undermining global sustainability goals. It notes that current research primarily focuses on performance improvements, neglecting sustainability and equity metrics, and proposes using LLM agents as a case study to explore these challenges further. \n\nKey topics include:\n- Energy consumption of LLMs\n- Sustainable AI Trilemma (capability, digital equity, environmental sustainability)\n- LLM-based agents and their applications\n- Research focus on performance vs. sustainability and equity\n\nEntities mentioned:\n- Large Language Models (LLMs)\n- AI agents\n- Sustainability goals\n- Research metrics on performance, sustainability, and equity"}, "excluded_embed_metadata_keys": ["file_name", "file_type", "file_size", "creation_date", "last_modified_date", "last_accessed_date"], "excluded_llm_metadata_keys": ["file_name", "file_type", "file_size", "creation_date", "last_modified_date", "last_accessed_date"], "relationships": {"1": {"node_id": "5bbe2f3b-a5cf-473e-9e50-1d182dcad9fb", "node_type": "4", "metadata": {"file_path": "C:\\Users\\anteb\\PycharmProjects\\JupyterProject\\naive_rag\\data\\ai_articles\\ADDRESSING THE SUSTAINABLE AI TRILEMMA A CASE STUDY ON LLM AGENTS AND RAG.md", "file_name": "ADDRESSING THE SUSTAINABLE AI TRILEMMA A CASE STUDY ON LLM AGENTS AND RAG.md", "file_type": "text/markdown", "file_size": 72913, "creation_date": "2025-03-26", "last_modified_date": "2025-03-22"}, "hash": "47c48bbdebdc34c45547d1ddc9efb86758d4c6bea59cc0a6ae9786ef666e699e", "class_name": "RelatedNodeInfo"}, "2": {"node_id": "f9a4aab7-db90-49e2-9911-587e885a835e", "node_type": "1", "metadata": {"file_path": "C:\\Users\\anteb\\PycharmProjects\\JupyterProject\\naive_rag\\data\\ai_articles\\ADDRESSING THE SUSTAINABLE AI TRILEMMA A CASE STUDY ON LLM AGENTS AND RAG.md", "file_name": "ADDRESSING THE SUSTAINABLE AI TRILEMMA A CASE STUDY ON LLM AGENTS AND RAG.md", "file_type": "text/markdown", "file_size": 72913, "creation_date": "2025-03-26", "last_modified_date": "2025-03-22"}, "hash": "66f959adaa79ebdfd8a6f6e0c627a6f079e3a8cf67202508567c13f6ed667dbb", "class_name": "RelatedNodeInfo"}, "3": {"node_id": "30054381-b1c2-488b-ae41-72a0ab77e7ce", "node_type": "1", "metadata": {}, "hash": "1868feb6dd9a5366bbf460f7cdd0f946efafe6d7f4cfb18036c7661cbdd4914a", "class_name": "RelatedNodeInfo"}}, "metadata_template": "{key}: {value}", "metadata_separator": "\n", "text": "However, this rapid development comes with significant challenges, particularly in terms of energy use and its impact on the environment and society. While the energy consumption of LLM training has received much attention[1], recent evidence[2][3][4] suggests that the inference phase, particularly in large-scale deployments, has emerged as the dominant contributor to the overall energy footprint, highlighting a critical and growing concern in the sustainability of LLM-powered services.\n\nIn response to these challenges, we propose the concept of the Sustainable AI Trilemma, which draws parallels to the sustainability trilemma balancing Economy, Society, and Environment[5]. In the AI context, this trilemma highlights the trade-offs among capability, digital equity, and environmental sustainability. Current LLM deployments heavily prioritize capability\u2014pushing for greater model performance and broader applications\u2014often at the expense of equity and sustainability. This imbalance is made worse by the increasing use of LLM-based agents, and the scaling of multi-agents, which applies LLMs in a frequent manner for long-term operations.\n\nLLM-based agents, capable of executing complex, multi-step tasks, are increasingly integrated across domains such as enterprise automation, scientific research, and personalized assistance[6]. This paradigm amplifies the energy demands of LLM inference, raising urgent questions about the sustainable scalability of such AI services. Without deliberate attention to energy efficiency, the widespread use of these agents risks accelerating resource inequities[7] and undermining global sustainability goals[8].\n\nhttps://github.com/huiwxing/llmagent_trilemma\n---\n# A PREPRINT - JANUARY 15, 2025\n\nDespite these challenges, most research on LLM agents focuses on improving performance, with little consideration of sustainability or equity metrics. As a first step toward addressing the trilemma, we use LLM agents as a case study to better understand these challenges.", "mimetype": "text/plain", "start_char_idx": 1757, "end_char_idx": 3771, "metadata_seperator": "\n", "text_template": "[Excerpt from document]\n{metadata_str}\nExcerpt:\n-----\n{content}\n-----\n", "class_name": "TextNode"}, "__type__": "1"}, {"__data__": {"id_": "30054381-b1c2-488b-ae41-72a0ab77e7ce", "embedding": null, "metadata": {"file_path": "C:\\Users\\anteb\\PycharmProjects\\JupyterProject\\naive_rag\\data\\ai_articles\\ADDRESSING THE SUSTAINABLE AI TRILEMMA A CASE STUDY ON LLM AGENTS AND RAG.md", "file_name": "ADDRESSING THE SUSTAINABLE AI TRILEMMA A CASE STUDY ON LLM AGENTS AND RAG.md", "file_type": "text/markdown", "file_size": 72913, "creation_date": "2025-03-26", "last_modified_date": "2025-03-22", "document_title": "\"Navigating the Sustainable AI Trilemma: Balancing Energy Efficiency, Digital Equity, and Capability in Large Language Models and AI Agent Design\"", "section_summary": "The section discusses the Sustainable AI Trilemma, focusing on the challenges associated with balancing energy efficiency, digital equity, and capability in the design of Large Language Models (LLMs) and AI agents. Key topics include:\n\n1. **Sustainable AI Trilemma**: The paper proposes this concept, emphasizing the risks of prioritizing productivity in LLM deployments at the expense of equity and environmental sustainability.\n\n2. **Energy Cost Analysis**: A systematic analysis of energy costs in LLM agents and Retrieval-Augmented Generation (RAG) designs is presented, highlighting trade-offs between functionality and sustainability.\n\n3. **New Metrics**: The introduction of novel metrics to assess energy and performance trade-offs in LLM agents, aimed at optimizing design for energy efficiency without sacrificing effectiveness.\n\n4. **Critique of LLM-Centric Autonomy**: The paper challenges the trend of relying heavily on LLMs for all tasks, arguing that this can lead to unnecessary computational overhead and does not necessarily enhance agent autonomy or performance.\n\nThe document is structured into sections that cover the Sustainable AI Trilemma, a case study on memory modules, experimental results, and implications for future sustainable AI development."}, "excluded_embed_metadata_keys": ["file_name", "file_type", "file_size", "creation_date", "last_modified_date", "last_accessed_date"], "excluded_llm_metadata_keys": ["file_name", "file_type", "file_size", "creation_date", "last_modified_date", "last_accessed_date"], "relationships": {"1": {"node_id": "5bbe2f3b-a5cf-473e-9e50-1d182dcad9fb", "node_type": "4", "metadata": {"file_path": "C:\\Users\\anteb\\PycharmProjects\\JupyterProject\\naive_rag\\data\\ai_articles\\ADDRESSING THE SUSTAINABLE AI TRILEMMA A CASE STUDY ON LLM AGENTS AND RAG.md", "file_name": "ADDRESSING THE SUSTAINABLE AI TRILEMMA A CASE STUDY ON LLM AGENTS AND RAG.md", "file_type": "text/markdown", "file_size": 72913, "creation_date": "2025-03-26", "last_modified_date": "2025-03-22"}, "hash": "47c48bbdebdc34c45547d1ddc9efb86758d4c6bea59cc0a6ae9786ef666e699e", "class_name": "RelatedNodeInfo"}, "2": {"node_id": "f1123945-a91f-481b-bd3a-1b2e7288e928", "node_type": "1", "metadata": {"file_path": "C:\\Users\\anteb\\PycharmProjects\\JupyterProject\\naive_rag\\data\\ai_articles\\ADDRESSING THE SUSTAINABLE AI TRILEMMA A CASE STUDY ON LLM AGENTS AND RAG.md", "file_name": "ADDRESSING THE SUSTAINABLE AI TRILEMMA A CASE STUDY ON LLM AGENTS AND RAG.md", "file_type": "text/markdown", "file_size": 72913, "creation_date": "2025-03-26", "last_modified_date": "2025-03-22"}, "hash": "fd444bbf0a49b4e17d332d7b4005be3063fcb24356a1eccc5a9af03024818c46", "class_name": "RelatedNodeInfo"}, "3": {"node_id": "d080ed82-792a-4d33-83a6-d813c2e6182f", "node_type": "1", "metadata": {}, "hash": "340f40cc4518e6e586e2fc6dbe1f24e4cdcdbccde84bf912a9526791731e736d", "class_name": "RelatedNodeInfo"}}, "metadata_template": "{key}: {value}", "metadata_separator": "\n", "text": "As a first step toward addressing the trilemma, we use LLM agents as a case study to better understand these challenges. This paper aims to address this gap with the following contributions:\n\n# Proposing the Sustainable AI Trilemma and Highlighting its Urgency:\n\nWe articulate the risks posed by an overemphasis on productivity in LLM agent deployments, which exacerbates the trilemma by neglecting equity and environmental sustainability.\n\n# Unified Analysis of Energy Cost in LLM Agents:\n\nThrough systematic analysis and a case study of the memory module, we reveal the energy costs embedded in LLM agent and RAG designs. Our findings emphasize the trade-offs between enhanced functionality and sustainability, demonstrating that resource-intensive designs often yield diminishing performance returns and identifying opportunities for more sustainable implementations.\n\n# New Metrics for Energy and Performance Trade-offs:\n\nWe introduce novel metrics to evaluate the trade-offs between energy cost and performance in LLM agents. These metrics provide a foundation for optimizing the design and operation of LLM agents to achieve more energy efficiency without compromising effectiveness.\n\n# Challenging the Paradigm of LLM-Centric Autonomy:\n\nA growing trend in agent design involves offloading all subtasks to the LLMs, purportedly to enhance autonomy. From the perspectives of environmental impact and cost-efficiency, we challenge this prevailing trend, demonstrating through empirical evidence that this approach often results in unnecessary computational overhead without proportional benefits in agent autonomy or performance, relying excessively on LLMs undermines the broader objectives of sustainable AI.\n\nThe remainder of this paper is organized as follows: Section 2 introduces the Sustainable AI Trilemma and its four constituent dilemmas, along with background on LLM agents and LLM energy consumption. Section 3 presents a case study on memory modules in LLM-based agents, examining their impact on energy efficiency and system complexity, and proposes metrics for evaluating energy-performance trade-offs. Section 4 presents our experimental results and analysis. Finally, Section 5 discusses implications and concludes with insights for future sustainable AI development.", "mimetype": "text/plain", "start_char_idx": 3651, "end_char_idx": 5939, "metadata_seperator": "\n", "text_template": "[Excerpt from document]\n{metadata_str}\nExcerpt:\n-----\n{content}\n-----\n", "class_name": "TextNode"}, "__type__": "1"}, {"__data__": {"id_": "d080ed82-792a-4d33-83a6-d813c2e6182f", "embedding": null, "metadata": {"file_path": "C:\\Users\\anteb\\PycharmProjects\\JupyterProject\\naive_rag\\data\\ai_articles\\ADDRESSING THE SUSTAINABLE AI TRILEMMA A CASE STUDY ON LLM AGENTS AND RAG.md", "file_name": "ADDRESSING THE SUSTAINABLE AI TRILEMMA A CASE STUDY ON LLM AGENTS AND RAG.md", "file_type": "text/markdown", "file_size": 72913, "creation_date": "2025-03-26", "last_modified_date": "2025-03-22", "document_title": "\"Navigating the Sustainable AI Trilemma: Balancing Energy Efficiency, Digital Equity, and Capability in Large Language Models and AI Agent Design\"", "section_summary": "The section discusses the concept of the \"Sustainable AI Trilemma,\" which emphasizes the challenge of balancing three key goals in AI development: capability or performance, sustainability, and equity. It highlights that optimizing for any two of these goals often compromises the third. The authors argue that this trilemma needs to be redefined in light of the rapid development of large language models (LLMs) and their significant impact.\n\nThe section identifies four core dilemmas related to the Sustainable AI Trilemma, focusing on technical challenges in AI design and deployment. Two of these dilemmas are elaborated upon:\n\n1. **Evaluation Dilemma: Awareness vs. Measurement** - This dilemma addresses the lack of accurate assessment tools for measuring the environmental impacts of LLMs. Existing tools are inadequate for the unique challenges posed by LLMs, leading to uncertainty about their true environmental footprint.\n\n2. **Design Dilemma: Autonomy vs. Efficiency** - This dilemma discusses the tension between developing highly autonomous AI systems and the need for efficiency in their design and operation.\n\nOverall, the section sets the stage for further exploration of these dilemmas and their implications for sustainable AI development."}, "excluded_embed_metadata_keys": ["file_name", "file_type", "file_size", "creation_date", "last_modified_date", "last_accessed_date"], "excluded_llm_metadata_keys": ["file_name", "file_type", "file_size", "creation_date", "last_modified_date", "last_accessed_date"], "relationships": {"1": {"node_id": "5bbe2f3b-a5cf-473e-9e50-1d182dcad9fb", "node_type": "4", "metadata": {"file_path": "C:\\Users\\anteb\\PycharmProjects\\JupyterProject\\naive_rag\\data\\ai_articles\\ADDRESSING THE SUSTAINABLE AI TRILEMMA A CASE STUDY ON LLM AGENTS AND RAG.md", "file_name": "ADDRESSING THE SUSTAINABLE AI TRILEMMA A CASE STUDY ON LLM AGENTS AND RAG.md", "file_type": "text/markdown", "file_size": 72913, "creation_date": "2025-03-26", "last_modified_date": "2025-03-22"}, "hash": "47c48bbdebdc34c45547d1ddc9efb86758d4c6bea59cc0a6ae9786ef666e699e", "class_name": "RelatedNodeInfo"}, "2": {"node_id": "30054381-b1c2-488b-ae41-72a0ab77e7ce", "node_type": "1", "metadata": {"file_path": "C:\\Users\\anteb\\PycharmProjects\\JupyterProject\\naive_rag\\data\\ai_articles\\ADDRESSING THE SUSTAINABLE AI TRILEMMA A CASE STUDY ON LLM AGENTS AND RAG.md", "file_name": "ADDRESSING THE SUSTAINABLE AI TRILEMMA A CASE STUDY ON LLM AGENTS AND RAG.md", "file_type": "text/markdown", "file_size": 72913, "creation_date": "2025-03-26", "last_modified_date": "2025-03-22"}, "hash": "42305c9f554ea61810ead89900194e9f14ffc23541a1afca7b838d95fb577c33", "class_name": "RelatedNodeInfo"}, "3": {"node_id": "14474a85-328e-4daa-84d2-f73e99806fcf", "node_type": "1", "metadata": {}, "hash": "83b9eee4f1fd9054966ee3cfc07492c0a8226d5a027669ddc9d50270677a0d12", "class_name": "RelatedNodeInfo"}}, "metadata_template": "{key}: {value}", "metadata_separator": "\n", "text": "Section 4 presents our experimental results and analysis. Finally, Section 5 discusses implications and concludes with insights for future sustainable AI development.\n\n# 2 Background\n\n# 2.1 Sustainable AI Trilemma\n\nThe concept of a trilemma, which highlights the difficulty of balancing competing priorities, has been explored in various contexts, including sociology and ethics. Recent sociological studies have applied this trilemma framework to AI [9], and others have emphasized the sustainability of AI [10][11]. The AI trilemma posits that AI development must balance three fundamental goals: AI capability or performance, sustainability, and equity, where optimizing for any two often comes at the expense of the third. However, with the rapid rise of LLMs and their pervasive influence, we argue that this trilemma must be redefined and addressed from a technical perspective. The Sustainable AI Trilemma emerges from a series of intertwined technical dilemmas that reflect conflicting priorities in the design, deployment, and long-term operation of AI systems. Below, we identify and analyze four core dilemmas, each highlighting a critical technical challenge.\n\n# Evaluation Dilemma: Awareness vs. Measurement\n\nWhile the environmental impacts of LLMs and other AI systems have become a recognized concern, the lack of accurate and comprehensive assessment tools hinders progress toward sustainability. Existing tools, such as MLCO2 [12] and Green Algorithms [13], are designed for traditional machine learning and earlier AI algorithms. These tools fail to account for the unique challenges posed by LLMs and their applications, such as memory-intensive inference processes, highly variable usage patterns, and the lifecycle emissions from large-scale deployment. This technical gap leaves practitioners and policymakers uncertain about the true scale of AI\u2019s environmental footprint, creating a paradox where actionable insights are hindered despite widespread recognition of the problem.\n\n# Design Dilemma: Autonomy vs. Efficiency\n\nThe pursuit of highly autonomous AI systems [6], exemplified by the rise of single- and multi-agent frameworks, drives the demand for increasingly sophisticated architectures.", "mimetype": "text/plain", "start_char_idx": 5773, "end_char_idx": 7993, "metadata_seperator": "\n", "text_template": "[Excerpt from document]\n{metadata_str}\nExcerpt:\n-----\n{content}\n-----\n", "class_name": "TextNode"}, "__type__": "1"}, {"__data__": {"id_": "14474a85-328e-4daa-84d2-f73e99806fcf", "embedding": null, "metadata": {"file_path": "C:\\Users\\anteb\\PycharmProjects\\JupyterProject\\naive_rag\\data\\ai_articles\\ADDRESSING THE SUSTAINABLE AI TRILEMMA A CASE STUDY ON LLM AGENTS AND RAG.md", "file_name": "ADDRESSING THE SUSTAINABLE AI TRILEMMA A CASE STUDY ON LLM AGENTS AND RAG.md", "file_type": "text/markdown", "file_size": 72913, "creation_date": "2025-03-26", "last_modified_date": "2025-03-22", "document_title": "\"Navigating the Sustainable AI Trilemma: Balancing Energy Efficiency, Digital Equity, and Capability in Large Language Models and AI Agent Design\"", "section_summary": "The section discusses the challenges and trade-offs associated with the design and operation of autonomous AI systems, particularly focusing on large language models (LLMs) and multi-agent frameworks. Key topics include:\n\n1. **Efficiency**: The need for sophisticated architectures in AI systems can lead to inefficiencies, such as redundant workflows and over-reliance on LLMs for tasks that could be handled by specialized modules.\n\n2. **Autonomy vs. Efficiency**: There is a tension between expanding the autonomy of AI systems and maintaining energy-efficient operations, which complicates resource management and can result in diminishing returns in capability.\n\n3. **Scalability vs. Sustainability**: As AI services scale to meet demand, maintaining Quality of Service (QoS) becomes challenging. The infrastructure needed for low-latency and real-time responsiveness increases energy consumption and operational costs, raising concerns about the long-term sustainability of these systems.\n\n4. **Operational Dilemma**: The complexity of LLM applications poses significant challenges for traditional operational tools like MLOps and AIOps, particularly in resource-constrained environments.\n\n5. **Access Dilemma**: The section hints at a discussion on the balance between the benefits and barriers of AI access, although this part is not fully elaborated.\n\nOverall, the excerpt highlights the intricate balance between efficiency, autonomy, scalability, and sustainability in the development and deployment of AI systems."}, "excluded_embed_metadata_keys": ["file_name", "file_type", "file_size", "creation_date", "last_modified_date", "last_accessed_date"], "excluded_llm_metadata_keys": ["file_name", "file_type", "file_size", "creation_date", "last_modified_date", "last_accessed_date"], "relationships": {"1": {"node_id": "5bbe2f3b-a5cf-473e-9e50-1d182dcad9fb", "node_type": "4", "metadata": {"file_path": "C:\\Users\\anteb\\PycharmProjects\\JupyterProject\\naive_rag\\data\\ai_articles\\ADDRESSING THE SUSTAINABLE AI TRILEMMA A CASE STUDY ON LLM AGENTS AND RAG.md", "file_name": "ADDRESSING THE SUSTAINABLE AI TRILEMMA A CASE STUDY ON LLM AGENTS AND RAG.md", "file_type": "text/markdown", "file_size": 72913, "creation_date": "2025-03-26", "last_modified_date": "2025-03-22"}, "hash": "47c48bbdebdc34c45547d1ddc9efb86758d4c6bea59cc0a6ae9786ef666e699e", "class_name": "RelatedNodeInfo"}, "2": {"node_id": "d080ed82-792a-4d33-83a6-d813c2e6182f", "node_type": "1", "metadata": {"file_path": "C:\\Users\\anteb\\PycharmProjects\\JupyterProject\\naive_rag\\data\\ai_articles\\ADDRESSING THE SUSTAINABLE AI TRILEMMA A CASE STUDY ON LLM AGENTS AND RAG.md", "file_name": "ADDRESSING THE SUSTAINABLE AI TRILEMMA A CASE STUDY ON LLM AGENTS AND RAG.md", "file_type": "text/markdown", "file_size": 72913, "creation_date": "2025-03-26", "last_modified_date": "2025-03-22"}, "hash": "7234495d8536ecc44a9b8b71fc138caf237055004ca12a63fcd2d6ed42db779c", "class_name": "RelatedNodeInfo"}, "3": {"node_id": "83d2eb9c-9fb8-4b6d-9e75-2509be0b4bed", "node_type": "1", "metadata": {}, "hash": "2e043fb98bc98ad49af50f89515c99ebfd39bead27a5746398e46064bfaffeef", "class_name": "RelatedNodeInfo"}}, "metadata_template": "{key}: {value}", "metadata_separator": "\n", "text": "Efficiency\n\nThe pursuit of highly autonomous AI systems [6], exemplified by the rise of single- and multi-agent frameworks, drives the demand for increasingly sophisticated architectures. While these systems promise greater flexibility and functionality, their designs often introduce redundancy and inefficiencies [14], such as poorly optimized workflows leading to excessive and unnecessary model calls, frequent over-reliance on LLMs for subtasks that could be addressed more efficiently with specialized modules, or maintaining multiple concurrent LLM instances for similar tasks across different services. This tradeoff between expanding autonomy and maintaining energy-efficient operations adds strain to system-level resource management, resulting in diminishing returns in capability.\n---\n# A PREPRINT - JANUARY 15, 2025\n\n# Capability\n\n# Autonomy-VS. Efficiency\n\n# LLM-Centric Autonomy\n\n# LLM v Agents\n\n# Multi-Agents\n\n# Computing Power\n\n# Long-Term Operation\n\n# Scalability vs. Sustainability\n\n# Equity\n\n# Benefits vs. Barriers\n\n# Energy consumption\n\n# Awareness vs. Measurement Environment\n\nFigure 1: The Sustainable AI Trilemma.\n\n# Operational Dilemma: Scalability vs. Sustainability\n\nScaling AI services to meet increasing demand exacerbates the tension between scalability and sustainability. Expanding the number of deployed LLM-based agents requires maintaining consistent Quality of Service (QoS) under growing workloads. However, the infrastructure required to ensure QoS\u2014such as low-latency inference, real-time responsiveness, aggressive caching and pre-warming strategies [15]\u2014inevitably leads to heightened energy consumption and operational costs. At scale, these architectural inefficiencies can be further amplified. While traditional AI systems benefit from MLOps and AIOps [16] to streamline operations, these tools face significant challenges when applied to the complexity of LLM applications. The memory-intensive processes, cross-agent interactions, and continuous learning demands of LLM-based systems strain the existing operational workflows, raising questions about their scalability and long-term feasibility in resource-constrained environments.\n\n# Access Dilemma: Benefits vs.", "mimetype": "text/plain", "start_char_idx": 7806, "end_char_idx": 10019, "metadata_seperator": "\n", "text_template": "[Excerpt from document]\n{metadata_str}\nExcerpt:\n-----\n{content}\n-----\n", "class_name": "TextNode"}, "__type__": "1"}, {"__data__": {"id_": "83d2eb9c-9fb8-4b6d-9e75-2509be0b4bed", "embedding": null, "metadata": {"file_path": "C:\\Users\\anteb\\PycharmProjects\\JupyterProject\\naive_rag\\data\\ai_articles\\ADDRESSING THE SUSTAINABLE AI TRILEMMA A CASE STUDY ON LLM AGENTS AND RAG.md", "file_name": "ADDRESSING THE SUSTAINABLE AI TRILEMMA A CASE STUDY ON LLM AGENTS AND RAG.md", "file_type": "text/markdown", "file_size": 72913, "creation_date": "2025-03-26", "last_modified_date": "2025-03-22", "document_title": "\"Navigating the Sustainable AI Trilemma: Balancing Energy Efficiency, Digital Equity, and Capability in Large Language Models and AI Agent Design\"", "section_summary": "The section discusses the challenges and implications of resource-intensive AI applications, particularly focusing on large language models (LLMs) and their impact on digital equity and environmental sustainability. Key topics include:\n\n1. **Access Dilemma**: The disparity in access to AI technologies, where well-resourced organizations benefit disproportionately, while underprivileged communities face barriers due to high costs associated with proprietary LLMs and the hardware requirements for open-source models.\n\n2. **Digital Divide**: The economic and environmental pressures of AI technologies exacerbate the digital divide, limiting access for communities with inadequate computing infrastructure and hindering potential societal and economic advancements.\n\n3. **Sustainable AI Trilemma**: The need to balance AI capability, digital equity, and environmental concerns is emphasized, suggesting a paradigm shift in the design, deployment, and evaluation of AI systems.\n\n4. **LLM-based Agents**: The emergence of LLM-based agents is highlighted as a transformative development, enabling multi-turn dialogues and complex task execution across various sectors, which could revolutionize workflows.\n\n5. **Energy Costs and Environmental Impacts**: The section raises concerns about the growing energy consumption of AI systems, particularly during the inference phase, and its implications for carbon footprints, stressing the importance of addressing these issues in the context of widespread AI deployment.\n\nOverall, the excerpt underscores the need for a balanced approach to AI development that considers economic, social, and environmental factors."}, "excluded_embed_metadata_keys": ["file_name", "file_type", "file_size", "creation_date", "last_modified_date", "last_accessed_date"], "excluded_llm_metadata_keys": ["file_name", "file_type", "file_size", "creation_date", "last_modified_date", "last_accessed_date"], "relationships": {"1": {"node_id": "5bbe2f3b-a5cf-473e-9e50-1d182dcad9fb", "node_type": "4", "metadata": {"file_path": "C:\\Users\\anteb\\PycharmProjects\\JupyterProject\\naive_rag\\data\\ai_articles\\ADDRESSING THE SUSTAINABLE AI TRILEMMA A CASE STUDY ON LLM AGENTS AND RAG.md", "file_name": "ADDRESSING THE SUSTAINABLE AI TRILEMMA A CASE STUDY ON LLM AGENTS AND RAG.md", "file_type": "text/markdown", "file_size": 72913, "creation_date": "2025-03-26", "last_modified_date": "2025-03-22"}, "hash": "47c48bbdebdc34c45547d1ddc9efb86758d4c6bea59cc0a6ae9786ef666e699e", "class_name": "RelatedNodeInfo"}, "2": {"node_id": "14474a85-328e-4daa-84d2-f73e99806fcf", "node_type": "1", "metadata": {"file_path": "C:\\Users\\anteb\\PycharmProjects\\JupyterProject\\naive_rag\\data\\ai_articles\\ADDRESSING THE SUSTAINABLE AI TRILEMMA A CASE STUDY ON LLM AGENTS AND RAG.md", "file_name": "ADDRESSING THE SUSTAINABLE AI TRILEMMA A CASE STUDY ON LLM AGENTS AND RAG.md", "file_type": "text/markdown", "file_size": 72913, "creation_date": "2025-03-26", "last_modified_date": "2025-03-22"}, "hash": "2d2efe99da8ea99e98f7c7db64ace139a635a3f378eace4509d02d763d8ffe43", "class_name": "RelatedNodeInfo"}, "3": {"node_id": "e9448068-1bb8-4a81-befb-4393cfd27d21", "node_type": "1", "metadata": {}, "hash": "357fbf1b359bc9a9fa387313b7f4a075de6bc2dc660320c07d2b913552cbff0c", "class_name": "RelatedNodeInfo"}}, "metadata_template": "{key}: {value}", "metadata_separator": "\n", "text": "# Access Dilemma: Benefits vs. Barriers\n\nResource-intensive AI applications disproportionately benefit well-resourced organizations and regions while marginalizing underprivileged communities. Proprietary LLMs often rely on expensive API-based access models, imposing high financial costs on users. Open-source LLMs, while enabling local deployment, demand highly efficient hardware to mitigate their energy-intensive operations, which are often unavailable in resource-limited regions. These twin pressures\u2014economic costs and environmental impacts\u2014place an outsized burden on communities with limited access to affordable and reliable computing infrastructure. The resulting disparities exacerbate the digital divide [7], effectively excluding these communities from accessing and benefiting from advanced AI services that could otherwise drive meaningful societal and economic change [17][18].\n\nThese four dilemmas collectively form the foundation of the Sustainable AI Trilemma: the need to balance AI capability, digital equity, and environment. To address this trilemma, we believe a paradigm shift in designing, deploying, and evaluating AI systems is required.\n\n# 2.2 LLM-based Agents\n\nThe emergence of LLM-based agents represents a transformative shift in the application of large language models. Unlike traditional applications that rely on single-turn interactions, LLM-based agents are designed to perform multi-turn dialogues and execute complex, multi-step tasks autonomously [19]. These agents are increasingly deployed across diverse sectors, from intelligent customer support systems to advanced research assistants, showcasing their potential to revolutionize workflows and decision-making processes. At their core, LLM-based agents integrate multiple functional components to achieve greater autonomy and adaptability. Key components typically include:\n---\n# A PREPRINT - JANUARY 15, 2025\n\n# 2.3 Energy Cost and Environmental Impacts of LLM Inference\n\nThe growing energy consumption of AI systems, particularly LLMs, has become a significant concern in the field and inference-phase energy consumption deserves particular attention, especially in terms of carbon footprints [25]. [3] suggests that the multiplicative effect due to widespread AI deployment remains a crucial consideration.", "mimetype": "text/plain", "start_char_idx": 9989, "end_char_idx": 12296, "metadata_seperator": "\n", "text_template": "[Excerpt from document]\n{metadata_str}\nExcerpt:\n-----\n{content}\n-----\n", "class_name": "TextNode"}, "__type__": "1"}, {"__data__": {"id_": "e9448068-1bb8-4a81-befb-4393cfd27d21", "embedding": null, "metadata": {"file_path": "C:\\Users\\anteb\\PycharmProjects\\JupyterProject\\naive_rag\\data\\ai_articles\\ADDRESSING THE SUSTAINABLE AI TRILEMMA A CASE STUDY ON LLM AGENTS AND RAG.md", "file_name": "ADDRESSING THE SUSTAINABLE AI TRILEMMA A CASE STUDY ON LLM AGENTS AND RAG.md", "file_type": "text/markdown", "file_size": 72913, "creation_date": "2025-03-26", "last_modified_date": "2025-03-22", "document_title": "\"Navigating the Sustainable AI Trilemma: Balancing Energy Efficiency, Digital Equity, and Capability in Large Language Models and AI Agent Design\"", "section_summary": "The section discusses the energy efficiency and environmental impact of large language models (LLMs) and AI agents, particularly focusing on the challenges posed by their deployment and operation. Key topics include:\n\n1. **Carbon Emissions**: The significant carbon emissions associated with AI services, highlighting that inference-related emissions for models like ChatGPT can be 25 times greater than those from training GPT-3.\n\n2. **Measurement Tools**: The development of specialized tools for estimating computational carbon footprints, such as Green Algorithms and LLMCarbon, which provide predictions for carbon emissions in various AI architectures.\n\n3. **Empirical Studies**: Research on LLM inference energy consumption, including large-scale testing of LLaMA models across different GPU platforms and the creation of predictive models for energy consumption and runtime.\n\n4. **Factors Affecting Energy Consumption**: Exploration of how model size, layer count, and optimization techniques (like batch processing and quantization) influence energy usage.\n\n5. **Sustainability Awareness**: Evaluation of the sustainability of commercial AI models, including GitHub Copilot and ChatGPT-3, and the need to consider trade-offs in optimizing AI energy consumption.\n\n6. **Memory Modules in LLM Agents**: The section introduces the memory module as a transformative feature in LLM agents, discussing the challenges it presents in terms of energy efficiency and design complexity.\n\nOverall, the excerpt emphasizes the importance of addressing the energy and environmental costs associated with the deployment of AI technologies while exploring potential inefficiencies in their design and operations."}, "excluded_embed_metadata_keys": ["file_name", "file_type", "file_size", "creation_date", "last_modified_date", "last_accessed_date"], "excluded_llm_metadata_keys": ["file_name", "file_type", "file_size", "creation_date", "last_modified_date", "last_accessed_date"], "relationships": {"1": {"node_id": "5bbe2f3b-a5cf-473e-9e50-1d182dcad9fb", "node_type": "4", "metadata": {"file_path": "C:\\Users\\anteb\\PycharmProjects\\JupyterProject\\naive_rag\\data\\ai_articles\\ADDRESSING THE SUSTAINABLE AI TRILEMMA A CASE STUDY ON LLM AGENTS AND RAG.md", "file_name": "ADDRESSING THE SUSTAINABLE AI TRILEMMA A CASE STUDY ON LLM AGENTS AND RAG.md", "file_type": "text/markdown", "file_size": 72913, "creation_date": "2025-03-26", "last_modified_date": "2025-03-22"}, "hash": "47c48bbdebdc34c45547d1ddc9efb86758d4c6bea59cc0a6ae9786ef666e699e", "class_name": "RelatedNodeInfo"}, "2": {"node_id": "83d2eb9c-9fb8-4b6d-9e75-2509be0b4bed", "node_type": "1", "metadata": {"file_path": "C:\\Users\\anteb\\PycharmProjects\\JupyterProject\\naive_rag\\data\\ai_articles\\ADDRESSING THE SUSTAINABLE AI TRILEMMA A CASE STUDY ON LLM AGENTS AND RAG.md", "file_name": "ADDRESSING THE SUSTAINABLE AI TRILEMMA A CASE STUDY ON LLM AGENTS AND RAG.md", "file_type": "text/markdown", "file_size": 72913, "creation_date": "2025-03-26", "last_modified_date": "2025-03-22"}, "hash": "9f19bd26b34068f958ce8c0d1245caafdada7d0fcdd44b88b4513e334d10a31d", "class_name": "RelatedNodeInfo"}, "3": {"node_id": "8dc10db9-10b4-4290-a5cf-18d625c4d261", "node_type": "1", "metadata": {}, "hash": "7dddb43fdf99eb7f5bf9ed9320d2269034578daf326aeb7f1df307eb14e7db06", "class_name": "RelatedNodeInfo"}}, "metadata_template": "{key}: {value}", "metadata_separator": "\n", "text": "[3] suggests that the multiplicative effect due to widespread AI deployment remains a crucial consideration. Studies in [2] have shown that for services like ChatGPT, inference-related carbon emissions in one year amount to 25 times that of GPT-3\u2019s training. This trend has driven the development of specialized measurement tools and analysis. Green Algorithms [13] provides an online platform for estimating computational carbon footprints, while LLMCarbon [8] specifically addresses LLM scenarios, offering end-to-end carbon emission predictions for both dense and mixture-of-experts (MoE) architectures.\n\nEmpirical studies have extensively investigated LLM inference energy consumption. [26] have conducted large-scale testing of LLaMA models across different GPU platforms (V100 and A100), including scenarios with up to 32-GPU model sharding. Through comprehensive characterization studies of mainstream LLMs, Wilkins et al. [27] have developed high-accuracy (R2 > 0.96) models for energy consumption and runtime prediction. Further research [28] has explored how factors such as model size, layer count, parallel attention mechanisms affect energy consumption, along with optimization techniques like batch processing and quantization. In code generation, Vartziotis et al. [29] have evaluated the sustainability awareness of commercial AI models like GitHub Copilot, ChatGPT-3, and Amazon CodeWhisperer. What\u2019s more, the optimization of AI energy consumption requires careful consideration of trade-offs - while some AI models aim to reduce carbon emissions, their training and deployment also generate environmental impacts, yielding positive net benefits only under appropriate scenarios [30].\n\n# 3 Computational and Energy Costs in LLM-based Agents: A Case Study on Memory Modules\n\nThe memory module is a transformative feature in LLM agents. However, the incorporation of memory introduces notable challenges in energy efficiency and design complexity. In this section, we take the memory module of LLM-based agents as an example to explore potential inefficiencies in resource utilization within their design and operations.", "mimetype": "text/plain", "start_char_idx": 12188, "end_char_idx": 14324, "metadata_seperator": "\n", "text_template": "[Excerpt from document]\n{metadata_str}\nExcerpt:\n-----\n{content}\n-----\n", "class_name": "TextNode"}, "__type__": "1"}, {"__data__": {"id_": "8dc10db9-10b4-4290-a5cf-18d625c4d261", "embedding": null, "metadata": {"file_path": "C:\\Users\\anteb\\PycharmProjects\\JupyterProject\\naive_rag\\data\\ai_articles\\ADDRESSING THE SUSTAINABLE AI TRILEMMA A CASE STUDY ON LLM AGENTS AND RAG.md", "file_name": "ADDRESSING THE SUSTAINABLE AI TRILEMMA A CASE STUDY ON LLM AGENTS AND RAG.md", "file_type": "text/markdown", "file_size": 72913, "creation_date": "2025-03-26", "last_modified_date": "2025-03-22", "document_title": "\"Navigating the Sustainable AI Trilemma: Balancing Energy Efficiency, Digital Equity, and Capability in Large Language Models and AI Agent Design\"", "section_summary": "The section discusses the design and operational inefficiencies of LLM-based agents, specifically focusing on their memory modules. It begins by defining the simplest form of an LLM agent that operates without memory, illustrating its limitations in generating responses based solely on pretrained knowledge and immediate user queries. This configuration is efficient but prone to inaccuracies when faced with information outside its training scope.\n\nThe section then introduces the concept of memory-augmented agents, distinguishing between internal and external memory. Internal memory consists of historical interactions, while external memory includes data from various domain-specific sources. This enhancement aims to improve the agent's capabilities in knowledge-intensive and context-dependent applications.\n\nKey Topics:\n- LLM agents\n- Memory modules (internal and external)\n- Resource utilization inefficiencies\n- Limitations of LLMs without memory\n- Knowledge-intensive applications\n\nEntities:\n- LLM (Large Language Model)\n- User queries (Q)\n- Responses (A)\n- Internal memory (Minternal)\n- External memory (Mexternal)\n- Domain-specific repositories and databases (D)"}, "excluded_embed_metadata_keys": ["file_name", "file_type", "file_size", "creation_date", "last_modified_date", "last_accessed_date"], "excluded_llm_metadata_keys": ["file_name", "file_type", "file_size", "creation_date", "last_modified_date", "last_accessed_date"], "relationships": {"1": {"node_id": "5bbe2f3b-a5cf-473e-9e50-1d182dcad9fb", "node_type": "4", "metadata": {"file_path": "C:\\Users\\anteb\\PycharmProjects\\JupyterProject\\naive_rag\\data\\ai_articles\\ADDRESSING THE SUSTAINABLE AI TRILEMMA A CASE STUDY ON LLM AGENTS AND RAG.md", "file_name": "ADDRESSING THE SUSTAINABLE AI TRILEMMA A CASE STUDY ON LLM AGENTS AND RAG.md", "file_type": "text/markdown", "file_size": 72913, "creation_date": "2025-03-26", "last_modified_date": "2025-03-22"}, "hash": "47c48bbdebdc34c45547d1ddc9efb86758d4c6bea59cc0a6ae9786ef666e699e", "class_name": "RelatedNodeInfo"}, "2": {"node_id": "e9448068-1bb8-4a81-befb-4393cfd27d21", "node_type": "1", "metadata": {"file_path": "C:\\Users\\anteb\\PycharmProjects\\JupyterProject\\naive_rag\\data\\ai_articles\\ADDRESSING THE SUSTAINABLE AI TRILEMMA A CASE STUDY ON LLM AGENTS AND RAG.md", "file_name": "ADDRESSING THE SUSTAINABLE AI TRILEMMA A CASE STUDY ON LLM AGENTS AND RAG.md", "file_type": "text/markdown", "file_size": 72913, "creation_date": "2025-03-26", "last_modified_date": "2025-03-22"}, "hash": "bd6940fb17f03a1355b888e45df17b7283b681c29f5591ef9b732181eba3da92", "class_name": "RelatedNodeInfo"}, "3": {"node_id": "7102bb57-2c7e-462e-a8a8-78678ac3394b", "node_type": "1", "metadata": {}, "hash": "13793702682fe592ce8bd29bcfbcc9ef94fff15939474abedda03eeba32b468d", "class_name": "RelatedNodeInfo"}}, "metadata_template": "{key}: {value}", "metadata_separator": "\n", "text": "In this section, we take the memory module of LLM-based agents as an example to explore potential inefficiencies in resource utilization within their design and operations.\n\n# 3.1 Problem Definition\n\nTo understand its impact, we first define the simplest case of an LLM agent without memory before formalizing the enhanced capabilities of a memory-augmented agent.\n\nWithout Memory In its simplest configuration, an LLM agent operates as a standalone inference system. Given a user query Q, the LLM generates a response A:\n\nLLM(Q) = A (1)\n\nThis formulation assumes no access to external data or stored context. The model relies entirely on its pretrained knowledge and immediate input Q. While computationally efficient, this approach is limited by the LLM\u2019s inherent knowledge boundaries, which cannot be updated without retraining. When Q references information outside the model\u2019s scope, the output A is prone to inaccuracies or hallucination, failing to meet the requirements of knowledge-intensive or context-dependent applications.\n---\n# A PREPRINT - JANUARY 15, 2025\n\n# With Memory\n\nWhen a memory module is incorporated, the memory M is typically categorized into internal memory and external memory, i.e., M = {Minternal, Mexternal}. Internal memory comprises historical information collected during the agent\u2019s operation. For example, an agent solving iterative tasks may store user inputs i and its own responses o from prior interactions at time t: Minternal = {(i1, o1),(i2, o2), . . . , (it\u22121, ot\u22121)}. External memory refers to data retrieved from domain-specific repositories, databases, documents, or other external sources: Mexternal = {D1, D2, . . . , Dn}, where these external resources D are indexed and queried as needed.", "mimetype": "text/plain", "start_char_idx": 14152, "end_char_idx": 15892, "metadata_seperator": "\n", "text_template": "[Excerpt from document]\n{metadata_str}\nExcerpt:\n-----\n{content}\n-----\n", "class_name": "TextNode"}, "__type__": "1"}, {"__data__": {"id_": "7102bb57-2c7e-462e-a8a8-78678ac3394b", "embedding": null, "metadata": {"file_path": "C:\\Users\\anteb\\PycharmProjects\\JupyterProject\\naive_rag\\data\\ai_articles\\ADDRESSING THE SUSTAINABLE AI TRILEMMA A CASE STUDY ON LLM AGENTS AND RAG.md", "file_name": "ADDRESSING THE SUSTAINABLE AI TRILEMMA A CASE STUDY ON LLM AGENTS AND RAG.md", "file_type": "text/markdown", "file_size": 72913, "creation_date": "2025-03-26", "last_modified_date": "2025-03-22", "document_title": "\"Navigating the Sustainable AI Trilemma: Balancing Energy Efficiency, Digital Equity, and Capability in Large Language Models and AI Agent Design\"", "section_summary": "The section discusses the integration of memory modules in large language models (LLMs) and their implications for energy consumption and performance. Key topics include:\n\n1. **Memory-Enabled Agents**: The behavior of memory-enabled agents is described using a formula that relates queries (Q) to relevant memory subsets (Mq).\n\n2. **Energy Model**: An analytical model for LLM inference energy consumption is presented, which factors in input and output text lengths. The energy consumption (E) is expressed as a function of input tokens (Tin) and output tokens (Tout), with model-specific coefficients (\u03b1K,0, \u03b1K,1, \u03b1K,2) indicating that output generation is typically more energy-intensive.\n\n3. **Challenges of Memory Module Design**: The section highlights the challenges associated with existing memory module designs, which often prioritize performance over computational efficiency. It identifies three critical phases in memory architecture: \n   - Memory Formation: Organizing input data into storage.\n   - Memory Reading: Optimizing retrieval processes.\n   - Memory Utilization: Enhancing answer generation through effective memory use.\n\nOverall, the excerpt emphasizes the need to balance energy efficiency, performance, and resource consumption in the design of memory-augmented LLMs."}, "excluded_embed_metadata_keys": ["file_name", "file_type", "file_size", "creation_date", "last_modified_date", "last_accessed_date"], "excluded_llm_metadata_keys": ["file_name", "file_type", "file_size", "creation_date", "last_modified_date", "last_accessed_date"], "relationships": {"1": {"node_id": "5bbe2f3b-a5cf-473e-9e50-1d182dcad9fb", "node_type": "4", "metadata": {"file_path": "C:\\Users\\anteb\\PycharmProjects\\JupyterProject\\naive_rag\\data\\ai_articles\\ADDRESSING THE SUSTAINABLE AI TRILEMMA A CASE STUDY ON LLM AGENTS AND RAG.md", "file_name": "ADDRESSING THE SUSTAINABLE AI TRILEMMA A CASE STUDY ON LLM AGENTS AND RAG.md", "file_type": "text/markdown", "file_size": 72913, "creation_date": "2025-03-26", "last_modified_date": "2025-03-22"}, "hash": "47c48bbdebdc34c45547d1ddc9efb86758d4c6bea59cc0a6ae9786ef666e699e", "class_name": "RelatedNodeInfo"}, "2": {"node_id": "8dc10db9-10b4-4290-a5cf-18d625c4d261", "node_type": "1", "metadata": {"file_path": "C:\\Users\\anteb\\PycharmProjects\\JupyterProject\\naive_rag\\data\\ai_articles\\ADDRESSING THE SUSTAINABLE AI TRILEMMA A CASE STUDY ON LLM AGENTS AND RAG.md", "file_name": "ADDRESSING THE SUSTAINABLE AI TRILEMMA A CASE STUDY ON LLM AGENTS AND RAG.md", "file_type": "text/markdown", "file_size": 72913, "creation_date": "2025-03-26", "last_modified_date": "2025-03-22"}, "hash": "e95b88aa116855a192ef6b6fe0b4234828019e4c64cc57c7c91d8168904fdf3d", "class_name": "RelatedNodeInfo"}, "3": {"node_id": "ddf54b42-5681-48be-b13e-3ce031e9e2f8", "node_type": "1", "metadata": {}, "hash": "ef77a951fe34ab5a2c493ef5ad24949d43f917f6f52105fe9389d8ac5b8e5df4", "class_name": "RelatedNodeInfo"}}, "metadata_template": "{key}: {value}", "metadata_separator": "\n", "text": ". . , Dn}, where these external resources D are indexed and queried as needed. The behavior of the memory-enabled agent can be described as:\n\nLLM(Q, Mq) = A (2)\n\nHere, Mq \u2208 M represents the relevant subset of the memory M, retrieved based on the query Q. We assume that for every query there is a ground truth answer AG, which can be generated based on their ground truth memories MG.\n\n# Energy Model\n\nPrior work [27] has established an analytical model for LLM inference energy consumption that accounts for both input and output text lengths, as well as their interaction. Specifically, the energy consumption E of a primary input-output of a certain LLM K can be expressed as:\n\nE = eK(Tin, Tout) = \u03b1K,0 \u2217 Tin + \u03b1K,1 \u2217 Tout + \u03b1K,2 \u2217 Tin \u2217 Tout (3)\n\nwhere Tin and Tout represent the number of input and output tokens respectively, and \u03b1K,0, \u03b1K,1, and \u03b1K,2 are model-specific (K) coefficients. Since output token generation is more energy-intensive than input processing [27], \u03b1K,1 normally exceeds both \u03b1K,0 and \u03b1K,2. We will use this model to perform some theoretical simulation analyses in the next section and some metrics calculations in Experiment 4.3.\n\n# 3.2 Cost of Existing Memory Module Design Patterns\n\nThe integration of memory modules introduces more challenges. Many existing memory designs completely rely on LLM operations (as shown in Figure 2), prioritizing performance and autonomy improvements without considering computational workloads and resource consumption. Through analyzing different components of memory-augmented frameworks, we identify three critical phases in modern LLM memory architecture: (a) Memory Formation, which processes and organizes internal/external input data into storage structures; (b-c) Memory Reading, encompassing retrieval necessity detection and query optimization for better retrieval; and (d-f) Memory utilization, to generate better answers by optimizing memory usage.", "mimetype": "text/plain", "start_char_idx": 15814, "end_char_idx": 17738, "metadata_seperator": "\n", "text_template": "[Excerpt from document]\n{metadata_str}\nExcerpt:\n-----\n{content}\n-----\n", "class_name": "TextNode"}, "__type__": "1"}, {"__data__": {"id_": "ddf54b42-5681-48be-b13e-3ce031e9e2f8", "embedding": null, "metadata": {"file_path": "C:\\Users\\anteb\\PycharmProjects\\JupyterProject\\naive_rag\\data\\ai_articles\\ADDRESSING THE SUSTAINABLE AI TRILEMMA A CASE STUDY ON LLM AGENTS AND RAG.md", "file_name": "ADDRESSING THE SUSTAINABLE AI TRILEMMA A CASE STUDY ON LLM AGENTS AND RAG.md", "file_type": "text/markdown", "file_size": 72913, "creation_date": "2025-03-26", "last_modified_date": "2025-03-22", "document_title": "\"Navigating the Sustainable AI Trilemma: Balancing Energy Efficiency, Digital Equity, and Capability in Large Language Models and AI Agent Design\"", "section_summary": "The section discusses two key aspects of memory management in large language models (LLMs): Memory Formation and Memory Reading.\n\n1. **Memory Formation**:\n   - Focuses on transforming raw data into structured, retrievable representations.\n   - Highlights challenges with vector-based storage, particularly in scalability and complex queries.\n   - Introduces various indexing strategies:\n     - **Keyword/Topic-based Indexing**: Utilizes LLMs to extract key terms (e.g., MemoChat, MemoryBank).\n     - **Summary-based Indexing**: Generates concise memory representations (e.g., SCM, MemGPT, MPC).\n     - **Structured Representations**: Involves creating triplets and knowledge graphs through entity and relationship extraction (e.g., Think-in-Memory, RET-LLM).\n   - Discusses the trade-offs between computational overhead and performance benefits, emphasizing the need for aligning indexing strategies with application requirements to optimize resource utilization.\n\n2. **Memory Reading**:\n   - Describes the process of querying the memory/knowledge base established during Memory Formation.\n   - Involves retrieving relevant information that matches the user's query.\n\nOverall, the section emphasizes the importance of balancing energy efficiency, scalability, and performance in the design of memory systems for LLMs."}, "excluded_embed_metadata_keys": ["file_name", "file_type", "file_size", "creation_date", "last_modified_date", "last_accessed_date"], "excluded_llm_metadata_keys": ["file_name", "file_type", "file_size", "creation_date", "last_modified_date", "last_accessed_date"], "relationships": {"1": {"node_id": "5bbe2f3b-a5cf-473e-9e50-1d182dcad9fb", "node_type": "4", "metadata": {"file_path": "C:\\Users\\anteb\\PycharmProjects\\JupyterProject\\naive_rag\\data\\ai_articles\\ADDRESSING THE SUSTAINABLE AI TRILEMMA A CASE STUDY ON LLM AGENTS AND RAG.md", "file_name": "ADDRESSING THE SUSTAINABLE AI TRILEMMA A CASE STUDY ON LLM AGENTS AND RAG.md", "file_type": "text/markdown", "file_size": 72913, "creation_date": "2025-03-26", "last_modified_date": "2025-03-22"}, "hash": "47c48bbdebdc34c45547d1ddc9efb86758d4c6bea59cc0a6ae9786ef666e699e", "class_name": "RelatedNodeInfo"}, "2": {"node_id": "7102bb57-2c7e-462e-a8a8-78678ac3394b", "node_type": "1", "metadata": {"file_path": "C:\\Users\\anteb\\PycharmProjects\\JupyterProject\\naive_rag\\data\\ai_articles\\ADDRESSING THE SUSTAINABLE AI TRILEMMA A CASE STUDY ON LLM AGENTS AND RAG.md", "file_name": "ADDRESSING THE SUSTAINABLE AI TRILEMMA A CASE STUDY ON LLM AGENTS AND RAG.md", "file_type": "text/markdown", "file_size": 72913, "creation_date": "2025-03-26", "last_modified_date": "2025-03-22"}, "hash": "dc3b324b6cc3542fc83c6cd1396460853387587f7c6eaa4d9be5b8eb7dee1a25", "class_name": "RelatedNodeInfo"}, "3": {"node_id": "fffb7cdf-7e63-4265-84f9-2ca4a53a504f", "node_type": "1", "metadata": {}, "hash": "e1d17d79f8b8e96f63d1956bf57432b80efa1a4d18ae7d0f2f116bcb63455d2a", "class_name": "RelatedNodeInfo"}}, "metadata_template": "{key}: {value}", "metadata_separator": "\n", "text": "This paper aims to uncover the hidden costs of these components and quantify the trade-offs between enhanced task performance and increased energy consumption.\n\n# 3.2.1 Memory Formation\n\nMemory formation involves the transformation of raw data into structured and retrievable representations using various indexing and storage optimization techniques. Vector-based storage and retrieval, which transforms raw data into vector embeddings for efficient similarity-based searches, struggles with scalability and complex query requirements such as comprehensive full-text searches, relation-based graph, and keyword search [31]. Several alternative LLM indexing strategies have been proposed. Keyword or topic-based indexing, implemented in systems like MemoChat [32] and MemoryBank [33], leverages LLMs to extract salient terms from the input data. Summary-based indexing, adopted by systems such as SCM [34], MemGPT [35], and MPC [36], employs LLMs to generate concise representations of memory segments. A more sophisticated approach is to build structured representations, such as triplets and knowledge graphs, by extracting entities and relationships through LLM. Systems like Think-in-Memory [37] and RET-LLM [38] exemplify this approach.\n\nWhile advanced memory representations excel in supporting complex reasoning tasks, they impose significant computational overhead due to multiple LLM processing iterations, raising concerns about scalability and efficiency as memory data volumes grow. This raises important questions about the trade-off between costs and performance benefits. For instance, when most of indexed memories see limited or no retrieval, the substantial energy investment in memory formation processes becomes difficult to justify against the minimal practical benefits. Such scenarios highlight the critical challenge of aligning indexing strategies with specific application requirements to ensure optimal resource utilization.\n\n# 3.2.2 Memory Reading\n\nThrough the preprocessing process of Memory Formation, the LLM agent is now equipped with a retrievable memory/knowledge base. Memory Reading, on the other hand, is the process of receiving a query and then getting a portion of information from the base that matches the query.", "mimetype": "text/plain", "start_char_idx": 17739, "end_char_idx": 19993, "metadata_seperator": "\n", "text_template": "[Excerpt from document]\n{metadata_str}\nExcerpt:\n-----\n{content}\n-----\n", "class_name": "TextNode"}, "__type__": "1"}, {"__data__": {"id_": "fffb7cdf-7e63-4265-84f9-2ca4a53a504f", "embedding": null, "metadata": {"file_path": "C:\\Users\\anteb\\PycharmProjects\\JupyterProject\\naive_rag\\data\\ai_articles\\ADDRESSING THE SUSTAINABLE AI TRILEMMA A CASE STUDY ON LLM AGENTS AND RAG.md", "file_name": "ADDRESSING THE SUSTAINABLE AI TRILEMMA A CASE STUDY ON LLM AGENTS AND RAG.md", "file_type": "text/markdown", "file_size": 72913, "creation_date": "2025-03-26", "last_modified_date": "2025-03-22", "document_title": "\"Navigating the Sustainable AI Trilemma: Balancing Energy Efficiency, Digital Equity, and Capability in Large Language Models and AI Agent Design\"", "section_summary": "The section discusses the concept of Memory Reading in the context of Large Language Models (LLMs) and Retrieval-Augmented Generation (RAG). It introduces a Memory Module that distinguishes between internal and external memory operations, highlighting the importance of memory formation, retrieval necessity detection, query optimization, reranking, compression, and generation in LLM agents.\n\nKey topics include:\n\n1. **Memory Reading**: The process of retrieving relevant information based on a query.\n2. **Memory Module Design**: A table outlining internal and external memory operations, including retrieval and memory types (e.g., gold memory, compressed memory).\n3. **Retrieval Necessity Detection**: A mechanism to optimize efficiency by determining when to access memory, reducing unnecessary overhead from indiscriminate retrieval.\n4. **Techniques and Approaches**: Various methods for enhancing retrieval necessity detection, including:\n   - Self-Asking and Judgment prompts for assessing memory activation.\n   - MemGPT, which treats LLMs as operating systems for autonomous memory management.\n   - A multilingual semantic-aware detection module for cross-language consistency.\n   - SKR's approach using prompting and classifiers to identify self-knowledge.\n\nThe section emphasizes the need for efficient memory access in LLMs to improve response accuracy and reduce hallucinations while outlining the foundational operations involved in the retrieval necessity detection process."}, "excluded_embed_metadata_keys": ["file_name", "file_type", "file_size", "creation_date", "last_modified_date", "last_accessed_date"], "excluded_llm_metadata_keys": ["file_name", "file_type", "file_size", "creation_date", "last_modified_date", "last_accessed_date"], "relationships": {"1": {"node_id": "5bbe2f3b-a5cf-473e-9e50-1d182dcad9fb", "node_type": "4", "metadata": {"file_path": "C:\\Users\\anteb\\PycharmProjects\\JupyterProject\\naive_rag\\data\\ai_articles\\ADDRESSING THE SUSTAINABLE AI TRILEMMA A CASE STUDY ON LLM AGENTS AND RAG.md", "file_name": "ADDRESSING THE SUSTAINABLE AI TRILEMMA A CASE STUDY ON LLM AGENTS AND RAG.md", "file_type": "text/markdown", "file_size": 72913, "creation_date": "2025-03-26", "last_modified_date": "2025-03-22"}, "hash": "47c48bbdebdc34c45547d1ddc9efb86758d4c6bea59cc0a6ae9786ef666e699e", "class_name": "RelatedNodeInfo"}, "2": {"node_id": "ddf54b42-5681-48be-b13e-3ce031e9e2f8", "node_type": "1", "metadata": {"file_path": "C:\\Users\\anteb\\PycharmProjects\\JupyterProject\\naive_rag\\data\\ai_articles\\ADDRESSING THE SUSTAINABLE AI TRILEMMA A CASE STUDY ON LLM AGENTS AND RAG.md", "file_name": "ADDRESSING THE SUSTAINABLE AI TRILEMMA A CASE STUDY ON LLM AGENTS AND RAG.md", "file_type": "text/markdown", "file_size": 72913, "creation_date": "2025-03-26", "last_modified_date": "2025-03-22"}, "hash": "54acfeff3360174d4f07ba234af73afd688d6e175717b1de2ccd774ba9161057", "class_name": "RelatedNodeInfo"}, "3": {"node_id": "f06db60f-469c-43d1-acb7-7fd61f89df0b", "node_type": "1", "metadata": {}, "hash": "3ec607988f823ead1680817bd4cd02c2079482dd3494ac1939f0daba90174641", "class_name": "RelatedNodeInfo"}}, "metadata_template": "{key}: {value}", "metadata_separator": "\n", "text": "Memory Reading, on the other hand, is the process of receiving a query and then getting a portion of information from the base that matches the query.\n---\n# A PREPRINT - JANUARY 15, 2025\n\n# Memory Module\n\n|Internal|External|\n|---|---|\n|Yes or No|Query|\n|Retrieval|New Query|\n|LLM|Useless memory|\n|Gold memory|Compressed memory|\n|Answer| |\n\nFigure 2: Design of Memory Modules in LLM Agents or RAG. The figure contains the operations that require LLM inference: (a) Memory Formation, (b) Retrieval Necessity Detection, (c) Query Optimization, (d) Reranking, (e) Compression, (f) Generation. The red markers represent data that is necessary for a QA process to generate a correct response, while black indicates non-necessary data.\n\n# Retrieval Necessity Detection\n\nWhile RAG improves response accuracy and reduces hallucinations, persistent indiscriminate retrieval can cause unnecessary overhead. Retrieval necessity detection optimizes efficiency by using the LLM\u2019s inherent knowledge for simple tasks and enabling selective memory access when necessary. SCM [34] and Ren et al. [39] design prompt like Self-Asking prompt and Judgment prompt to assess memory activation needs. MemGPT [35] treats LLM as an operating system and empowers the LLM processor to autonomously decide, update, and search its memory based on the current context. Rowen [40] introduces a multilingual semantic-aware detection module that evaluates cross-language response consistency and triggers retrieval augmentation when factual inconsistencies are detected. SKR [41] uses both direct prompting and training a smaller classifier, to detect whether the model has \u201dself-knowledge\u201d.\n\nBuilding on these studies, we distill the Retrieval Necessity Detection process to its fundamental form, as outlined below in terms of the LLM inference operations (To simplify the analysis, system prompt templates in the input are temporarily excluded. The following analysis remains consistent.):\n\n- Input: Query (token number = Tq).", "mimetype": "text/plain", "start_char_idx": 19843, "end_char_idx": 21837, "metadata_seperator": "\n", "text_template": "[Excerpt from document]\n{metadata_str}\nExcerpt:\n-----\n{content}\n-----\n", "class_name": "TextNode"}, "__type__": "1"}, {"__data__": {"id_": "f06db60f-469c-43d1-acb7-7fd61f89df0b", "embedding": null, "metadata": {"file_path": "C:\\Users\\anteb\\PycharmProjects\\JupyterProject\\naive_rag\\data\\ai_articles\\ADDRESSING THE SUSTAINABLE AI TRILEMMA A CASE STUDY ON LLM AGENTS AND RAG.md", "file_name": "ADDRESSING THE SUSTAINABLE AI TRILEMMA A CASE STUDY ON LLM AGENTS AND RAG.md", "file_type": "text/markdown", "file_size": 72913, "creation_date": "2025-03-26", "last_modified_date": "2025-03-22", "document_title": "\"Navigating the Sustainable AI Trilemma: Balancing Energy Efficiency, Digital Equity, and Capability in Large Language Models and AI Agent Design\"", "section_summary": "The section discusses several key topics related to optimizing query retrieval in the context of large language models (LLMs) and AI agent design, particularly focusing on energy efficiency, digital equity, and capability. \n\n1. **Query Optimization**: This involves enhancing the accuracy of information retrieval by enriching queries with semantic information. Two main approaches are highlighted:\n   - **Query Expansion**: This generates additional queries from the original input, such as breaking down complex questions or using prompt engineering techniques (e.g., Least-to-Most prompting, Chain-of-Thought, Self-Ask, Multi Query Retrieval).\n   - **Query Transformation**: This focuses on rewriting or reinterpreting the original query to better align with retrieval goals, utilizing methods like Hypothetical Document Embedding (HyDE), Step-back prompting, and Rewrite-Retrieve-Read.\n\n2. **Energy Consumption**: The section presents a formula for calculating energy consumption related to query processing, indicating that both the input query and the output score contribute to energy usage.\n\n3. **Memory Utilization**: This involves processing retrieved memory through re-ranking and compression to effectively integrate it with the query for LLM input.\n\n4. **Reranking**: This is a refinement step in the retrieval-augmented generation (RAG) process, where documents are reordered based on relevance to the query. The section notes advancements in LLM-based reranking, citing research that shows LLMs like LLaMA and Falcon have strong zero-shot ranking capabilities.\n\nOverall, the section emphasizes the importance of optimizing queries and memory utilization to improve retrieval systems while considering the associated computational costs and energy efficiency."}, "excluded_embed_metadata_keys": ["file_name", "file_type", "file_size", "creation_date", "last_modified_date", "last_accessed_date"], "excluded_llm_metadata_keys": ["file_name", "file_type", "file_size", "creation_date", "last_modified_date", "last_accessed_date"], "relationships": {"1": {"node_id": "5bbe2f3b-a5cf-473e-9e50-1d182dcad9fb", "node_type": "4", "metadata": {"file_path": "C:\\Users\\anteb\\PycharmProjects\\JupyterProject\\naive_rag\\data\\ai_articles\\ADDRESSING THE SUSTAINABLE AI TRILEMMA A CASE STUDY ON LLM AGENTS AND RAG.md", "file_name": "ADDRESSING THE SUSTAINABLE AI TRILEMMA A CASE STUDY ON LLM AGENTS AND RAG.md", "file_type": "text/markdown", "file_size": 72913, "creation_date": "2025-03-26", "last_modified_date": "2025-03-22"}, "hash": "47c48bbdebdc34c45547d1ddc9efb86758d4c6bea59cc0a6ae9786ef666e699e", "class_name": "RelatedNodeInfo"}, "2": {"node_id": "fffb7cdf-7e63-4265-84f9-2ca4a53a504f", "node_type": "1", "metadata": {"file_path": "C:\\Users\\anteb\\PycharmProjects\\JupyterProject\\naive_rag\\data\\ai_articles\\ADDRESSING THE SUSTAINABLE AI TRILEMMA A CASE STUDY ON LLM AGENTS AND RAG.md", "file_name": "ADDRESSING THE SUSTAINABLE AI TRILEMMA A CASE STUDY ON LLM AGENTS AND RAG.md", "file_type": "text/markdown", "file_size": 72913, "creation_date": "2025-03-26", "last_modified_date": "2025-03-22"}, "hash": "fc63e9f5131305011f33b20ed2d5e20ade1ffdd3cc35bb60473a79f6e72f8bbc", "class_name": "RelatedNodeInfo"}, "3": {"node_id": "64973664-4deb-47d9-b5e6-bd42eb46ea45", "node_type": "1", "metadata": {}, "hash": "91f6037cf4639502888bc25f387fff8b4c5dfc4ea712c4ab07833a07b3c5dfe7", "class_name": "RelatedNodeInfo"}}, "metadata_template": "{key}: {value}", "metadata_separator": "\n", "text": "The following analysis remains consistent.):\n\n- Input: Query (token number = Tq).\n- Output: A boolean or numeric score (indicating whether retrieval is needed, token number = C, where C is a small constant).\n- Energy: E1 = eK(Tq, C) = \u03b1K,0 \u2217 Tq + \u03b1K,1 \u2217 C + \u03b1K,2 \u2217 Tq \u2217 C\n\n# Query Optimization\n\nQuery optimization enhances retrieval accuracy by enriching raw or under-contextualized queries with semantic information through query expansion or transformation. Query expansion generates additional queries derived from the original input like decomposing complex questions into sub-questions, or employing prompt engineering techniques to create multiple contextually enriched variations, such as the Least-to-most prompting [42], Chain-of-Thought [43], Self-Ask [44], and Multi Query Retrieval [45]. Query transformation focuses on rewriting or reinterpreting the original query to better align with retrieval objectives, such as Hypothetical Document Embedding (HyDE) [46], Step-back prompting [47], Rewrite-Retrieve-Read [48]. Both approaches increase the semantic richness and correctness of the query, enabling retrieval systems to access a broader and more relevant information space. However, it is important to note that these enhancement methods typically not only increase the token count of the original query but also represent an additional computational cost in themselves.\n---\n# A PREPRINT - JANUARY 15, 2025\n\n# 3.2.3 Memory Utilization\n\nMemory utilization involves additional processing of retrieved memory, such as re-ranking and compression, to integrate the memory with the query in an appropriate format as input to LLM for generating answers.\n\n# Reranking\n\nReranking serves as a refinement step in the RAG process, where retrieved documents are reordered based on their relevance to the query. Recent advancements have increasingly focused on LLM-based reranking. Zhuang et al. [51] demonstrated that LLMs pre-trained on unstructured text, like LLaMA and Falcon, exhibit strong zero-shot ranking capabilities.", "mimetype": "text/plain", "start_char_idx": 21756, "end_char_idx": 23785, "metadata_seperator": "\n", "text_template": "[Excerpt from document]\n{metadata_str}\nExcerpt:\n-----\n{content}\n-----\n", "class_name": "TextNode"}, "__type__": "1"}, {"__data__": {"id_": "64973664-4deb-47d9-b5e6-bd42eb46ea45", "embedding": null, "metadata": {"file_path": "C:\\Users\\anteb\\PycharmProjects\\JupyterProject\\naive_rag\\data\\ai_articles\\ADDRESSING THE SUSTAINABLE AI TRILEMMA A CASE STUDY ON LLM AGENTS AND RAG.md", "file_name": "ADDRESSING THE SUSTAINABLE AI TRILEMMA A CASE STUDY ON LLM AGENTS AND RAG.md", "file_type": "text/markdown", "file_size": 72913, "creation_date": "2025-03-26", "last_modified_date": "2025-03-22", "document_title": "\"Navigating the Sustainable AI Trilemma: Balancing Energy Efficiency, Digital Equity, and Capability in Large Language Models and AI Agent Design\"", "section_summary": "The section discusses the capabilities and challenges associated with large language models (LLMs) in the context of zero-shot ranking and retrieval-augmented generation (RAG). Key topics include:\n\n1. **LLM Performance**: Zhuang et al. highlight that LLMs like LLaMA and Falcon, when pre-trained on unstructured text, demonstrate strong zero-shot ranking abilities.\n\n2. **Prompt Engineering**: Various methods (LRL, UPR, RankGPT, PRP) utilize prompt engineering with models such as GPT-3/4, UL2, and T5 for zero-shot passage reranking. Different prompt types are employed, including query generation and relevance generation.\n\n3. **Computational Costs**: While prompt-based approaches enhance agent autonomy and reduce fine-tuning needs, they introduce additional computational costs during inference due to the simultaneous processing of multiple documents.\n\n4. **LLM Reranker Structure**: The basic structure of an LLM reranker is outlined, detailing inputs (new queries and retrieved memories), outputs (ranked indices), and energy consumption associated with the reranking process.\n\n5. **Compression Strategies**: After retrieval and reranking, documents may still exceed LLM input capacity, necessitating compression strategies. Current LLM-based summarization and specialized compression models (e.g., PRCA, RECOMP) are noted for their performance but also for generating more output tokens and consuming more energy compared to simpler methods.\n\nOverall, the section emphasizes the balance between enhancing LLM capabilities and managing computational efficiency, particularly in the context of energy consumption and output generation."}, "excluded_embed_metadata_keys": ["file_name", "file_type", "file_size", "creation_date", "last_modified_date", "last_accessed_date"], "excluded_llm_metadata_keys": ["file_name", "file_type", "file_size", "creation_date", "last_modified_date", "last_accessed_date"], "relationships": {"1": {"node_id": "5bbe2f3b-a5cf-473e-9e50-1d182dcad9fb", "node_type": "4", "metadata": {"file_path": "C:\\Users\\anteb\\PycharmProjects\\JupyterProject\\naive_rag\\data\\ai_articles\\ADDRESSING THE SUSTAINABLE AI TRILEMMA A CASE STUDY ON LLM AGENTS AND RAG.md", "file_name": "ADDRESSING THE SUSTAINABLE AI TRILEMMA A CASE STUDY ON LLM AGENTS AND RAG.md", "file_type": "text/markdown", "file_size": 72913, "creation_date": "2025-03-26", "last_modified_date": "2025-03-22"}, "hash": "47c48bbdebdc34c45547d1ddc9efb86758d4c6bea59cc0a6ae9786ef666e699e", "class_name": "RelatedNodeInfo"}, "2": {"node_id": "f06db60f-469c-43d1-acb7-7fd61f89df0b", "node_type": "1", "metadata": {"file_path": "C:\\Users\\anteb\\PycharmProjects\\JupyterProject\\naive_rag\\data\\ai_articles\\ADDRESSING THE SUSTAINABLE AI TRILEMMA A CASE STUDY ON LLM AGENTS AND RAG.md", "file_name": "ADDRESSING THE SUSTAINABLE AI TRILEMMA A CASE STUDY ON LLM AGENTS AND RAG.md", "file_type": "text/markdown", "file_size": 72913, "creation_date": "2025-03-26", "last_modified_date": "2025-03-22"}, "hash": "48fb9b40cd7ec7dcf6925376072cee6d2af48182adfd2532d350b5cda3006a9d", "class_name": "RelatedNodeInfo"}, "3": {"node_id": "0bb3548f-8125-42ec-bcdc-d6bff9226013", "node_type": "1", "metadata": {}, "hash": "269a5efb6844572de339c3a73345fa214f7d4112f1ec8c2a837dddd7c4d7f42f", "class_name": "RelatedNodeInfo"}}, "metadata_template": "{key}: {value}", "metadata_separator": "\n", "text": "Zhuang et al. [51] demonstrated that LLMs pre-trained on unstructured text, like LLaMA and Falcon, exhibit strong zero-shot ranking capabilities. These methods such as LRL [52], UPR [53], RankGPT [54], PRP [55] leverage prompt engineering with models like GPT-3/4, UL2, and T5 for zero-shot passage reranking, employing various prompt types, including query generation, relevance generation [56], pairwise ranking, and permutation for ranked lists. Although prompt-based approaches reduce fine-tuning overhead and enhance agent autonomy, they themselves introduce extra computational costs during inference by processing multiple retrieved documents simultaneously. However, similar to Retrieval Necessity Detection, considering that reranker outputs are typically concise (ranked indices or scores, a small constant of output token numbers), these approaches have the potential to be cost-effective with moderate number of documents retrieved.\n\nThe basic form of LLM reranker is as follows (if using permutation generation prompting):\n\n- Input: New query or queries, Retrieved related memories (token number = Tqn + Tm).\n- Output: Ranked index (token number = K, where K represents the number of top-K retrieved entries).\n- Energy: E3 = eK(Tqn + Tm, K) = \u03b1K,0 \u2217 (Tqn + Tm) + \u03b1K,1 \u2217 K + \u03b1K,2 \u2217 (Tqn + Tm) \u2217 K\n\n# Compression\n\nAfter retrieval and reranking, relevant documents may still exceed LLM input capacity and introduce noise. While compression strategies can help by extracting essential information, current approaches face significant trade-offs. LLM-based summarization and specialized compression models (e.g. PRCA [57], RECOMP [58]) achieve good performance, but generate significantly more output tokens and consume more energy (owing to the large output coefficient \u03b1K,1 and the number of output tokens \u03b2 \u2217 Tm, and the square interaction term \u03b2 \u2217 T2) than simpler steps like retrieval detection or reranking.", "mimetype": "text/plain", "start_char_idx": 23640, "end_char_idx": 25561, "metadata_seperator": "\n", "text_template": "[Excerpt from document]\n{metadata_str}\nExcerpt:\n-----\n{content}\n-----\n", "class_name": "TextNode"}, "__type__": "1"}, {"__data__": {"id_": "0bb3548f-8125-42ec-bcdc-d6bff9226013", "embedding": null, "metadata": {"file_path": "C:\\Users\\anteb\\PycharmProjects\\JupyterProject\\naive_rag\\data\\ai_articles\\ADDRESSING THE SUSTAINABLE AI TRILEMMA A CASE STUDY ON LLM AGENTS AND RAG.md", "file_name": "ADDRESSING THE SUSTAINABLE AI TRILEMMA A CASE STUDY ON LLM AGENTS AND RAG.md", "file_type": "text/markdown", "file_size": 72913, "creation_date": "2025-03-26", "last_modified_date": "2025-03-22", "document_title": "\"Navigating the Sustainable AI Trilemma: Balancing Energy Efficiency, Digital Equity, and Capability in Large Language Models and AI Agent Design\"", "section_summary": "The section discusses the efficiency and management of large language models (LLMs) in the context of sustainable AI design, specifically addressing the trade-offs between energy efficiency, digital equity, and capability. Key topics include:\n\n1. **Entropy-Based Methods**: Alternative methods like Selective Context and LLMLingua are mentioned for reducing costs by avoiding full LLM inference, although they may compromise compression quality.\n\n2. **LLM Compression**: The basic structure of an LLM compressor is outlined, detailing the input (ranked memories), output (compressed memories), and energy consumption associated with different compression ratios (\u03b2).\n\n3. **Generation Process**: The final step involves synthesizing the input query and compressed memory to produce an answer, highlighting the computational costs involved and the importance of balancing compression ratio with answer quality.\n\n4. **Memory Management**: The section touches on advanced memory management strategies for LLM agents, emphasizing the importance of agent autonomy in managing memory over the long term.\n\nOverall, the excerpt emphasizes the need to balance computational efficiency with the quality of outputs in LLM applications, while also considering the implications for energy consumption and memory management."}, "excluded_embed_metadata_keys": ["file_name", "file_type", "file_size", "creation_date", "last_modified_date", "last_accessed_date"], "excluded_llm_metadata_keys": ["file_name", "file_type", "file_size", "creation_date", "last_modified_date", "last_accessed_date"], "relationships": {"1": {"node_id": "5bbe2f3b-a5cf-473e-9e50-1d182dcad9fb", "node_type": "4", "metadata": {"file_path": "C:\\Users\\anteb\\PycharmProjects\\JupyterProject\\naive_rag\\data\\ai_articles\\ADDRESSING THE SUSTAINABLE AI TRILEMMA A CASE STUDY ON LLM AGENTS AND RAG.md", "file_name": "ADDRESSING THE SUSTAINABLE AI TRILEMMA A CASE STUDY ON LLM AGENTS AND RAG.md", "file_type": "text/markdown", "file_size": 72913, "creation_date": "2025-03-26", "last_modified_date": "2025-03-22"}, "hash": "47c48bbdebdc34c45547d1ddc9efb86758d4c6bea59cc0a6ae9786ef666e699e", "class_name": "RelatedNodeInfo"}, "2": {"node_id": "64973664-4deb-47d9-b5e6-bd42eb46ea45", "node_type": "1", "metadata": {"file_path": "C:\\Users\\anteb\\PycharmProjects\\JupyterProject\\naive_rag\\data\\ai_articles\\ADDRESSING THE SUSTAINABLE AI TRILEMMA A CASE STUDY ON LLM AGENTS AND RAG.md", "file_name": "ADDRESSING THE SUSTAINABLE AI TRILEMMA A CASE STUDY ON LLM AGENTS AND RAG.md", "file_type": "text/markdown", "file_size": 72913, "creation_date": "2025-03-26", "last_modified_date": "2025-03-22"}, "hash": "2c4dc990a73f6fb4b87a2d234698368629637e7e76c599a222a877b318cee51c", "class_name": "RelatedNodeInfo"}, "3": {"node_id": "c479f389-0256-4caf-b406-7d394b1123d3", "node_type": "1", "metadata": {}, "hash": "3a1e2472e0e7566c0952d09e98c350bf10f4ed7c791038415cb187618ae3bf52", "class_name": "RelatedNodeInfo"}}, "metadata_template": "{key}: {value}", "metadata_separator": "\n", "text": "Alternative entropy-based methods like Selective Context [59] and LLMLingua [60] reduce costs by avoiding full LLM inference, but may compromise compression quality [61]. The overall efficiency gains versus computational overhead remains a critical consideration, especially at scale.\n\nThe basic form of LLM compressor is as follows:\n\n- Input: Ranked memories, which has the same token as unranked just in a different order (token number = Tm).\n- Output: Compressed memories (token number = \u03b2 \u2217 Tm, 0 < \u03b2 < 1, where \u03b2 is the compression ratio).\n- Energy: E4 = eK(Tm, \u03b2 \u2217 Tm) = \u03b1K,0 \u2217 Tm + \u03b1K,1 \u2217 \u03b2 \u2217 Tm + \u03b1K,2 \u2217 \u03b2 \u2217 T2\n\n# Generation\n\nThe final step synthesizes the query and compressed memory to produce a coherent answer. The input query and output answer are unavoidable computational costs, making compressed memories the key variable factor. While a larger compression ratio (\u03b2) increases energy costs, it also preserves more information, potentially leading to more accurate and complete answers. The optimal compression ratio thus needs to balance the trade-off between computational efficiency and answer quality based on specific application requirements.\n\n- Input: Original query (token number = Tq), Compressed memories (token number = \u03b2 \u2217 Tm, 0 < \u03b2 < 1).\n---\n# A PREPRINT - JANUARY 15, 2025\n\n\u2022 Output: Answer (token number = Ta).\n\n\u2022 Energy: E5 = eK(Tq + \u03b2 \u2217 Tm, Ta) = \u03b1K,0 \u2217 (Tq + \u03b2 \u2217 Tm) + \u03b1K,1 \u2217 Ta + \u03b1K,2 \u2217 (Tq + \u03b2 \u2217 Tm) \u2217 Ta\n\n# 3.2.4 Memory Management\n\nBeyond basic memory read and write operations, some research focuses on the long-term maintenance and management of memory in LLM agents. These approaches often emphasize agent autonomy, delegating much of the memory management process to the LLM itself.", "mimetype": "text/plain", "start_char_idx": 25562, "end_char_idx": 27284, "metadata_seperator": "\n", "text_template": "[Excerpt from document]\n{metadata_str}\nExcerpt:\n-----\n{content}\n-----\n", "class_name": "TextNode"}, "__type__": "1"}, {"__data__": {"id_": "c479f389-0256-4caf-b406-7d394b1123d3", "embedding": null, "metadata": {"file_path": "C:\\Users\\anteb\\PycharmProjects\\JupyterProject\\naive_rag\\data\\ai_articles\\ADDRESSING THE SUSTAINABLE AI TRILEMMA A CASE STUDY ON LLM AGENTS AND RAG.md", "file_name": "ADDRESSING THE SUSTAINABLE AI TRILEMMA A CASE STUDY ON LLM AGENTS AND RAG.md", "file_type": "text/markdown", "file_size": 72913, "creation_date": "2025-03-26", "last_modified_date": "2025-03-22", "document_title": "\"Navigating the Sustainable AI Trilemma: Balancing Energy Efficiency, Digital Equity, and Capability in Large Language Models and AI Agent Design\"", "section_summary": "The section discusses the challenges and considerations in managing memory within large language models (LLMs) and AI agents, particularly in the context of sustainability. Key topics include:\n\n1. **Agent Autonomy and Memory Management**: The section highlights approaches that promote agent autonomy, allowing LLMs to handle memory management, with specific mention of the Think-in-Memory method that focuses on memory merging and forgetting. It notes the uncertainties in energy consumption associated with these methods.\n\n2. **Metrics for Energy and Performance Trade-offs**: The evaluation of Retrieval-Augmented Generation (RAG) systems is divided into two phases: Retrieval and Generation. Various metrics are introduced to assess performance, including relevance, accuracy, faithfulness, and correctness, with a focus on the relationships between queries, answers, and memories.\n\n3. **Energy and Performance Metrics**: A table outlines the trade-offs between energy consumption and performance, detailing how different elements (e.g., retrieved memory, LLM operations) relate to energy costs and performance outcomes.\n\n4. **Trade-offs in Memory Formation and Retrieval**: The choice of indexing methods during memory formation is emphasized as a critical factor that influences both energy consumption and retrieval efficiency.\n\nOverall, the section underscores the need for further research to balance autonomy and sustainability in memory management within AI systems."}, "excluded_embed_metadata_keys": ["file_name", "file_type", "file_size", "creation_date", "last_modified_date", "last_accessed_date"], "excluded_llm_metadata_keys": ["file_name", "file_type", "file_size", "creation_date", "last_modified_date", "last_accessed_date"], "relationships": {"1": {"node_id": "5bbe2f3b-a5cf-473e-9e50-1d182dcad9fb", "node_type": "4", "metadata": {"file_path": "C:\\Users\\anteb\\PycharmProjects\\JupyterProject\\naive_rag\\data\\ai_articles\\ADDRESSING THE SUSTAINABLE AI TRILEMMA A CASE STUDY ON LLM AGENTS AND RAG.md", "file_name": "ADDRESSING THE SUSTAINABLE AI TRILEMMA A CASE STUDY ON LLM AGENTS AND RAG.md", "file_type": "text/markdown", "file_size": 72913, "creation_date": "2025-03-26", "last_modified_date": "2025-03-22"}, "hash": "47c48bbdebdc34c45547d1ddc9efb86758d4c6bea59cc0a6ae9786ef666e699e", "class_name": "RelatedNodeInfo"}, "2": {"node_id": "0bb3548f-8125-42ec-bcdc-d6bff9226013", "node_type": "1", "metadata": {"file_path": "C:\\Users\\anteb\\PycharmProjects\\JupyterProject\\naive_rag\\data\\ai_articles\\ADDRESSING THE SUSTAINABLE AI TRILEMMA A CASE STUDY ON LLM AGENTS AND RAG.md", "file_name": "ADDRESSING THE SUSTAINABLE AI TRILEMMA A CASE STUDY ON LLM AGENTS AND RAG.md", "file_type": "text/markdown", "file_size": 72913, "creation_date": "2025-03-26", "last_modified_date": "2025-03-22"}, "hash": "66f81dd9ac7529a043d92b1f11ec5188c2b7a5ec0a412f63cd2070bcbd437824", "class_name": "RelatedNodeInfo"}, "3": {"node_id": "b6d05c51-3453-4b6a-894b-13831a084ceb", "node_type": "1", "metadata": {}, "hash": "d8b868f07a42c6734a5c100bfedbfa0bdb18570049230065cb0c61965886d425", "class_name": "RelatedNodeInfo"}}, "metadata_template": "{key}: {value}", "metadata_separator": "\n", "text": "These approaches often emphasize agent autonomy, delegating much of the memory management process to the LLM itself. For instance, Think-in-Memory [37] introduces prompts designed specifically for memory merging and forgetting. These methods also introduce more uncertainties in energy consumption over long-term deployment. Further exploration in our future research to balance autonomy and sustainability in memory management.\n\n# 3.3 Metrics for Energy and Performance Trade-offs\n\nIn the evaluation of RAG systems, assessment tasks are typically divided into two phases: Retrieval and Generation. Corresponding to the evaluation subjects defined in Section 3.1, we consider the following elements: query (Q), answer (A), ground truth answer (AG), retrieved relevant memories (Mq), and ground truth memories (MG). Yu et al. [62] synthesized current research on RAG evaluation and categorized various metrics into five fundamental types: Relevance (Mq \u2194 Q) and Accuracy (Mq \u2194 MG) in the Retrieval phase, Relevance (A \u2194 Q), Faithfulness (A \u2194 Mq), and Correctness (A \u2194 AG) in the Generation phase, where \u2194 denotes the alignment or relationship between two elements (e.g., Mq \u2194 Q represents how relevant the retrieved documents are to the query).\n\n|Energy|Performance| | |\n|---|---|---|---|\n|Memory| |Real Case|Optimal Case|\n|Retrieved Memory|Ground Truth Memory| | |\n|LLM Operations|Memory Reading| | |\n|Memory Utilization|Answer|Ground Truth Answer| |\n| | |Efficiency: the trade-off between Energy and Performance| |\n| |Energy for Memory|Mq < A|A < AG|\n| |Energy for Answer|Mq < Q|A < Q|\n| |Time Cost|Mq\u2076MG|Real Case < Optimal Case|\n\nFigure 3: New efficiency metrics to balance energy cost and task performance.\n\n# 3.3.1 Trade-offs Between Memory Formation and Retrieval\n\nThe choice of indexing methods during memory formation not only affects system energy consumption but also directly impacts subsequent retrieval efficiency and effectiveness.", "mimetype": "text/plain", "start_char_idx": 27168, "end_char_idx": 29113, "metadata_seperator": "\n", "text_template": "[Excerpt from document]\n{metadata_str}\nExcerpt:\n-----\n{content}\n-----\n", "class_name": "TextNode"}, "__type__": "1"}, {"__data__": {"id_": "b6d05c51-3453-4b6a-894b-13831a084ceb", "embedding": null, "metadata": {"file_path": "C:\\Users\\anteb\\PycharmProjects\\JupyterProject\\naive_rag\\data\\ai_articles\\ADDRESSING THE SUSTAINABLE AI TRILEMMA A CASE STUDY ON LLM AGENTS AND RAG.md", "file_name": "ADDRESSING THE SUSTAINABLE AI TRILEMMA A CASE STUDY ON LLM AGENTS AND RAG.md", "file_type": "text/markdown", "file_size": 72913, "creation_date": "2025-03-26", "last_modified_date": "2025-03-22", "document_title": "\"Navigating the Sustainable AI Trilemma: Balancing Energy Efficiency, Digital Equity, and Capability in Large Language Models and AI Agent Design\"", "section_summary": "The section discusses the evaluation of indexing strategies for large language models (LLMs) and AI agents, focusing on retrieval effectiveness and efficiency. Key metrics for effectiveness include Relevance, which measures how well retrieved memories match a query, and Accuracy, assessed through comparisons with a ground truth set using metrics like recall and F1 scores. For efficiency, the average retrieval time per memory token (Tretr) is considered, along with energy consumption (Etoken_m) and processing time (Tcost) per memory token during the memory formation process.\n\nTwo evaluation indicators are introduced: the Retrieval Energy-to-Relevance Ratio (RERR) and the Energy-to-Accuracy Ratio (EAR), both quantifying the energy cost for improvements in retrieval relevance and accuracy, respectively.\n\nAdditionally, the section outlines an ideal scenario for generating correct answers to queries, emphasizing minimal data usage and operations. In this optimal case, the system retrieves only relevant memories and generates answers without additional enhancements, focusing on the essential energy consumption from retrieval and response generation costs. \n\nKey topics include:\n- Indexing strategies for LLMs\n- Metrics for retrieval effectiveness (Relevance, Accuracy)\n- Metrics for retrieval efficiency (Tretr, Etoken_m, Tcost)\n- Evaluation indicators (RERR, EAR)\n- Optimal scenarios for query generation and energy consumption."}, "excluded_embed_metadata_keys": ["file_name", "file_type", "file_size", "creation_date", "last_modified_date", "last_accessed_date"], "excluded_llm_metadata_keys": ["file_name", "file_type", "file_size", "creation_date", "last_modified_date", "last_accessed_date"], "relationships": {"1": {"node_id": "5bbe2f3b-a5cf-473e-9e50-1d182dcad9fb", "node_type": "4", "metadata": {"file_path": "C:\\Users\\anteb\\PycharmProjects\\JupyterProject\\naive_rag\\data\\ai_articles\\ADDRESSING THE SUSTAINABLE AI TRILEMMA A CASE STUDY ON LLM AGENTS AND RAG.md", "file_name": "ADDRESSING THE SUSTAINABLE AI TRILEMMA A CASE STUDY ON LLM AGENTS AND RAG.md", "file_type": "text/markdown", "file_size": 72913, "creation_date": "2025-03-26", "last_modified_date": "2025-03-22"}, "hash": "47c48bbdebdc34c45547d1ddc9efb86758d4c6bea59cc0a6ae9786ef666e699e", "class_name": "RelatedNodeInfo"}, "2": {"node_id": "c479f389-0256-4caf-b406-7d394b1123d3", "node_type": "1", "metadata": {"file_path": "C:\\Users\\anteb\\PycharmProjects\\JupyterProject\\naive_rag\\data\\ai_articles\\ADDRESSING THE SUSTAINABLE AI TRILEMMA A CASE STUDY ON LLM AGENTS AND RAG.md", "file_name": "ADDRESSING THE SUSTAINABLE AI TRILEMMA A CASE STUDY ON LLM AGENTS AND RAG.md", "file_type": "text/markdown", "file_size": 72913, "creation_date": "2025-03-26", "last_modified_date": "2025-03-22"}, "hash": "95f4c1e0cb25c0933ded5ba4ee1e78950c9eac156265b8d61ab2f00093d54ca8", "class_name": "RelatedNodeInfo"}, "3": {"node_id": "9e15e5de-c507-48a0-bc3f-e241cf83c94f", "node_type": "1", "metadata": {}, "hash": "beeceba56dbc7a37630451648f97c11b156df22e11d003ac97df6715e76c881f", "class_name": "RelatedNodeInfo"}}, "metadata_template": "{key}: {value}", "metadata_separator": "\n", "text": "To comprehensively evaluate different indexing strategies, we propose a multi-dimensional evaluation. For retrieval effectiveness, we employ two key metrics: Relevance measures how well a retrieved memory matches a query and is typically calculated by having the LLM judge each entry\u2019s relevance, then dividing the number of relevant entries by the total retrieved. Accuracy is evaluated by comparing retrieved documents to a ground truth set and metrics such as recall and F1 scores can often be used. For retrieval efficiency, we use the average retrieval time per memory token Tretr. For the memory formation process, we focus on energy consumption Etoken_m and processing time Tcost per memory token.\n\nBased on these metrics, we design two comprehensive evaluation indicators that quantify the energy cost required to achieve a unit improvement in retrieval relevance and accuracy:\n---\n# A PREPRINT - JANUARY 15, 2025\n\n# Retrieval Energy-to-Relevance Ratio (RERR)\n\nRERR = Etoken_m\nRelevance\n\n# Energy-to-Accuracy Ratio (EAR)\n\nEAR = Etoken_m\nAccuracy\n\n# 3.3.2 Trade-offs between Memory Reading, Utilization and Generation\n\n# a. For Correct Generations\n\nFor correct generations of the query Q (token number = Tq), we propose an ideal case scenario that achieves accurate answers through minimal necessary data (as shown in Figure 2) and operations: in this optimal case, the system would use only the original query with vector embeddings, employing sparse or dense retrieval to extract precisely all relevant ground truth memories MG (token number = Tmg) and no irrelevant memories, which are then combined with the original query to directly generate the ground truth answer AG (token number = Tag) through the LLM, without requiring any additional enhancement operations (such as query optimization, reranking or compression).\n\nIn the optimal case, the essential energy consumption comprises two components: retrieval cost (Eretr) and response generation cost Eg = eK(Tq + Tmg, Tag).", "mimetype": "text/plain", "start_char_idx": 29114, "end_char_idx": 31102, "metadata_seperator": "\n", "text_template": "[Excerpt from document]\n{metadata_str}\nExcerpt:\n-----\n{content}\n-----\n", "class_name": "TextNode"}, "__type__": "1"}, {"__data__": {"id_": "9e15e5de-c507-48a0-bc3f-e241cf83c94f", "embedding": null, "metadata": {"file_path": "C:\\Users\\anteb\\PycharmProjects\\JupyterProject\\naive_rag\\data\\ai_articles\\ADDRESSING THE SUSTAINABLE AI TRILEMMA A CASE STUDY ON LLM AGENTS AND RAG.md", "file_name": "ADDRESSING THE SUSTAINABLE AI TRILEMMA A CASE STUDY ON LLM AGENTS AND RAG.md", "file_type": "text/markdown", "file_size": 72913, "creation_date": "2025-03-26", "last_modified_date": "2025-03-22", "document_title": "\"Navigating the Sustainable AI Trilemma: Balancing Energy Efficiency, Digital Equity, and Capability in Large Language Models and AI Agent Design\"", "section_summary": "The section discusses the energy consumption associated with the generation process of large language models (LLMs) and AI agents, focusing on two main components: retrieval cost (Eretr) and response generation cost (Eg). The optimal total energy consumption (Eoptimal) is defined as the sum of these two components. In practice, however, additional memory operations increase the actual energy consumption (Ereal), which is expressed as a cumulative total across various stages of the generation process.\n\nTo evaluate energy efficiency, the section introduces the Generation Energy Optimality Ratio (GEOR), which compares Eoptimal to Ereal. A GEOR value closer to 100% indicates higher energy efficiency, while lower values suggest areas for optimization.\n\nAdditionally, the effectiveness and latency of response generation are influenced by memory reading and utilization methods. Three key metrics are used to assess generation effectiveness: relevance (alignment with the initial query), faithfulness (consistency with retrieved memories), and correctness (accuracy against ground truth). For generation efficiency, average latency per response token and energy consumption per token (Etoken_g) are measured.\n\nKey topics include:\n- Energy consumption in AI generation processes\n- Optimal vs. real energy consumption\n- Generation Energy Optimality Ratio (GEOR)\n- Metrics for evaluating generation effectiveness and efficiency (relevance, faithfulness, correctness, latency, and energy consumption per token)."}, "excluded_embed_metadata_keys": ["file_name", "file_type", "file_size", "creation_date", "last_modified_date", "last_accessed_date"], "excluded_llm_metadata_keys": ["file_name", "file_type", "file_size", "creation_date", "last_modified_date", "last_accessed_date"], "relationships": {"1": {"node_id": "5bbe2f3b-a5cf-473e-9e50-1d182dcad9fb", "node_type": "4", "metadata": {"file_path": "C:\\Users\\anteb\\PycharmProjects\\JupyterProject\\naive_rag\\data\\ai_articles\\ADDRESSING THE SUSTAINABLE AI TRILEMMA A CASE STUDY ON LLM AGENTS AND RAG.md", "file_name": "ADDRESSING THE SUSTAINABLE AI TRILEMMA A CASE STUDY ON LLM AGENTS AND RAG.md", "file_type": "text/markdown", "file_size": 72913, "creation_date": "2025-03-26", "last_modified_date": "2025-03-22"}, "hash": "47c48bbdebdc34c45547d1ddc9efb86758d4c6bea59cc0a6ae9786ef666e699e", "class_name": "RelatedNodeInfo"}, "2": {"node_id": "b6d05c51-3453-4b6a-894b-13831a084ceb", "node_type": "1", "metadata": {"file_path": "C:\\Users\\anteb\\PycharmProjects\\JupyterProject\\naive_rag\\data\\ai_articles\\ADDRESSING THE SUSTAINABLE AI TRILEMMA A CASE STUDY ON LLM AGENTS AND RAG.md", "file_name": "ADDRESSING THE SUSTAINABLE AI TRILEMMA A CASE STUDY ON LLM AGENTS AND RAG.md", "file_type": "text/markdown", "file_size": 72913, "creation_date": "2025-03-26", "last_modified_date": "2025-03-22"}, "hash": "9f123b8f5fa64d1fb898729e5b07562ed3dc93b8a46a322be49e1a45eba3cb17", "class_name": "RelatedNodeInfo"}, "3": {"node_id": "0f99212f-b9a3-4578-b9da-be568463aeb1", "node_type": "1", "metadata": {}, "hash": "05f964f37ae97fa8beb3ac98bbb809d5f75057ec25785b3fb21f86631d8df449", "class_name": "RelatedNodeInfo"}}, "metadata_template": "{key}: {value}", "metadata_separator": "\n", "text": "In the optimal case, the essential energy consumption comprises two components: retrieval cost (Eretr) and response generation cost Eg = eK(Tq + Tmg, Tag). Thus, the optimal total energy consumption can be expressed as:\n\nEoptimal = Eretr + Eg\n\nHowever, in real cases, to ensure generation quality, various additional memory reading and utilization operations in 3.2.2 and 3.2.3 are often necessary. We define Ereal as the cumulative energy consumption across all stages of the generation process, for example, when using all operations, Ereal = Eretr + E1 + E2 + E3 + E4 + E5.\n\nTo quantify the energy efficiency of the generation process, we propose:\n\n# Generation Energy Optimality Ratio (GEOR)\n\nGEOR = Eoptimal\nEreal\n\nThis ratio serves as an indicator of how close the system\u2019s actual energy consumption is to the theoretical minimum. A GEOR value closer to 100% indicates higher energy efficiency, suggesting that the system\u2019s energy consumption approaches the ideal case, while lower values indicate potential areas for energy optimization in the generation pipeline.\n\n# b. For All Generations\n\nThe effectiveness and latency of response generation are significantly influenced by memory reading and utilization methods. For generation effectiveness, we employ three key metrics: Relevance measures how well the generated response aligns with the intent and content of the initial query Q, typically calculated as a normalized score assigned by an LLM judge based on predefined assessment criteria. Faithfulness evaluates the consistency between the generated response and the retrieved memories Mq, calculated as the ratio of main points in the answer attributable to the context to the total main points in the answer. Correctness assesses the accuracy of the generated response against ground truth AG, measured using standard NLP metrics such as ROUGE, BLEU, or BERTScore.\n\nFor generation efficiency, we measure the average latency per response token and energy consumption per token Etoken_g which is the division of Ereal by response token number.", "mimetype": "text/plain", "start_char_idx": 30947, "end_char_idx": 33003, "metadata_seperator": "\n", "text_template": "[Excerpt from document]\n{metadata_str}\nExcerpt:\n-----\n{content}\n-----\n", "class_name": "TextNode"}, "__type__": "1"}, {"__data__": {"id_": "0f99212f-b9a3-4578-b9da-be568463aeb1", "embedding": null, "metadata": {"file_path": "C:\\Users\\anteb\\PycharmProjects\\JupyterProject\\naive_rag\\data\\ai_articles\\ADDRESSING THE SUSTAINABLE AI TRILEMMA A CASE STUDY ON LLM AGENTS AND RAG.md", "file_name": "ADDRESSING THE SUSTAINABLE AI TRILEMMA A CASE STUDY ON LLM AGENTS AND RAG.md", "file_type": "text/markdown", "file_size": 72913, "creation_date": "2025-03-26", "last_modified_date": "2025-03-22", "document_title": "\"Navigating the Sustainable AI Trilemma: Balancing Energy Efficiency, Digital Equity, and Capability in Large Language Models and AI Agent Design\"", "section_summary": "The section discusses the evaluation of energy efficiency in large language models (LLMs) and AI agents through specific metrics. It introduces three key energy efficiency ratios: \n\n1. **Generation Energy-to-Relevance Ratio (GERR)** - Measures energy cost per token in relation to relevance.\n2. **Energy-to-Faithfulness Ratio (EFR)** - Assesses energy cost per token concerning faithfulness.\n3. **Energy-to-Correctness Ratio (ECR)** - Evaluates energy cost per token in terms of correctness.\n\nLower values of these ratios indicate better energy efficiency in achieving effectiveness goals. \n\nAdditionally, the section outlines the experimental settings for evaluating these models, specifying the datasets used:\n- **LoCoMo** for internal memory tasks.\n- **HotpotQA** and **MuSiQue** for external memory tasks focusing on multi-hop reasoning.\n- **AI ArXiv** dataset for retrieval-augmented generation tasks.\n\nThe experiments aim to analyze the performance of LLMs in various contexts, with a focus on energy efficiency and effectiveness in generating responses."}, "excluded_embed_metadata_keys": ["file_name", "file_type", "file_size", "creation_date", "last_modified_date", "last_accessed_date"], "excluded_llm_metadata_keys": ["file_name", "file_type", "file_size", "creation_date", "last_modified_date", "last_accessed_date"], "relationships": {"1": {"node_id": "5bbe2f3b-a5cf-473e-9e50-1d182dcad9fb", "node_type": "4", "metadata": {"file_path": "C:\\Users\\anteb\\PycharmProjects\\JupyterProject\\naive_rag\\data\\ai_articles\\ADDRESSING THE SUSTAINABLE AI TRILEMMA A CASE STUDY ON LLM AGENTS AND RAG.md", "file_name": "ADDRESSING THE SUSTAINABLE AI TRILEMMA A CASE STUDY ON LLM AGENTS AND RAG.md", "file_type": "text/markdown", "file_size": 72913, "creation_date": "2025-03-26", "last_modified_date": "2025-03-22"}, "hash": "47c48bbdebdc34c45547d1ddc9efb86758d4c6bea59cc0a6ae9786ef666e699e", "class_name": "RelatedNodeInfo"}, "2": {"node_id": "9e15e5de-c507-48a0-bc3f-e241cf83c94f", "node_type": "1", "metadata": {"file_path": "C:\\Users\\anteb\\PycharmProjects\\JupyterProject\\naive_rag\\data\\ai_articles\\ADDRESSING THE SUSTAINABLE AI TRILEMMA A CASE STUDY ON LLM AGENTS AND RAG.md", "file_name": "ADDRESSING THE SUSTAINABLE AI TRILEMMA A CASE STUDY ON LLM AGENTS AND RAG.md", "file_type": "text/markdown", "file_size": 72913, "creation_date": "2025-03-26", "last_modified_date": "2025-03-22"}, "hash": "423ebd6017d03a2cab30e7eae46d8aced86771ad34b4a37735d9ac6203527c0b", "class_name": "RelatedNodeInfo"}, "3": {"node_id": "6e70e8eb-c370-4e9c-b167-70de6fb3d551", "node_type": "1", "metadata": {}, "hash": "ea17e5d27038087659d94a9f7ebdbc281f4a8b61f45796e522b98851e84efcde", "class_name": "RelatedNodeInfo"}}, "metadata_template": "{key}: {value}", "metadata_separator": "\n", "text": "For generation efficiency, we measure the average latency per response token and energy consumption per token Etoken_g which is the division of Ereal by response token number. Similarly, there are three energy efficiency ratios:\n\n# Generation Energy-to-Relevance Ratio (GERR)\n\nGERR = Etoken_g\nRelevance\n\n# Energy-to-Faithfulness Ratio (EFR)\n\nEFR = Etoken_g\nFaithfulness\n\n# Energy-to-Correctness Ratio (ECR)\n\nECR = Etoken_g\nCorrectness\n\nThese ratios quantify the energy cost required to achieve unit improvement in each generation effectiveness dimension. Lower values indicate better energy efficiency in achieving the respective effectiveness goals.\n---\n# A PREPRINT - JANUARY 15, 2025\n\n# 4 Experiments\n\n# 4.1 Experiment settings\n\nDatasets. For Internal Memory, we treat the task as that of a daily assistant agent and utilize the LoCoMo [63] dataset, which comprises very long-term dialogues. For External Memory, we use HotpotQA [64] and MuSiQue [65], which are well-known datasets for evaluating multi-hop and complex reasoning abilities in retrieval-augmented tasks. Additionally, we incorporate the AI ArXiv dataset [66], with 100 pairs of queries and answers on topics of RAG extracted by Eibich et al. [67]. We follow the same node splitting configuration as in Eibich\u2019s experiments. For all datasets we sampled some data and the characteristics of these sub-datasets are shown in Table 1.", "mimetype": "text/plain", "start_char_idx": 32828, "end_char_idx": 34225, "metadata_seperator": "\n", "text_template": "[Excerpt from document]\n{metadata_str}\nExcerpt:\n-----\n{content}\n-----\n", "class_name": "TextNode"}, "__type__": "1"}, {"__data__": {"id_": "6e70e8eb-c370-4e9c-b167-70de6fb3d551", "embedding": null, "metadata": {"file_path": "C:\\Users\\anteb\\PycharmProjects\\JupyterProject\\naive_rag\\data\\ai_articles\\ADDRESSING THE SUSTAINABLE AI TRILEMMA A CASE STUDY ON LLM AGENTS AND RAG.md", "file_name": "ADDRESSING THE SUSTAINABLE AI TRILEMMA A CASE STUDY ON LLM AGENTS AND RAG.md", "file_type": "text/markdown", "file_size": 72913, "creation_date": "2025-03-26", "last_modified_date": "2025-03-22", "document_title": "\"Navigating the Sustainable AI Trilemma: Balancing Energy Efficiency, Digital Equity, and Capability in Large Language Models and AI Agent Design\"", "section_summary": "The section discusses a study on the performance evaluation of large language models (LLMs) and AI agents, focusing on the sustainable AI trilemma, which involves balancing energy efficiency, digital equity, and capability. Key topics include:\n\n1. **Node Splitting Configuration**: The study follows a specific configuration based on previous experiments by Eibich, detailing the characteristics of various datasets used in the evaluation.\n\n2. **Dataset Characteristics**: A comparison table (Table 1) outlines the characteristics of four datasets: LoCoMo, HotpotQA, MuSiQue, and AI ArXiv, including metrics such as memory node number, memory node size, query types, difficulty, query length, answer length, and scenario.\n\n3. **Evaluation Metrics**: The evaluation of LLMs is conducted using various metrics, including Retrieval-Relevance, Generation-Relevance, and Faithfulness, utilizing UpTrain\u2019s LLM-based scorer with the GPT-4 API. Specific measures include Mean Reciprocal Rank (MRR), Recall, and ROUGE for assessing performance.\n\n4. **Hardware Configurations**: The study employs two hardware setups for performance evaluation: a resource-constrained environment with a single NVIDIA GeForce RTX 4080 GPU and an Intel i7-13700K processor, and a resource-abundant environment with a cluster of eight NVIDIA A100 GPUs and two AMD EPYC 7J13 processors, highlighting the differences in computing capabilities.\n\nOverall, the section emphasizes the methodology for evaluating LLM performance in relation to sustainability and resource management."}, "excluded_embed_metadata_keys": ["file_name", "file_type", "file_size", "creation_date", "last_modified_date", "last_accessed_date"], "excluded_llm_metadata_keys": ["file_name", "file_type", "file_size", "creation_date", "last_modified_date", "last_accessed_date"], "relationships": {"1": {"node_id": "5bbe2f3b-a5cf-473e-9e50-1d182dcad9fb", "node_type": "4", "metadata": {"file_path": "C:\\Users\\anteb\\PycharmProjects\\JupyterProject\\naive_rag\\data\\ai_articles\\ADDRESSING THE SUSTAINABLE AI TRILEMMA A CASE STUDY ON LLM AGENTS AND RAG.md", "file_name": "ADDRESSING THE SUSTAINABLE AI TRILEMMA A CASE STUDY ON LLM AGENTS AND RAG.md", "file_type": "text/markdown", "file_size": 72913, "creation_date": "2025-03-26", "last_modified_date": "2025-03-22"}, "hash": "47c48bbdebdc34c45547d1ddc9efb86758d4c6bea59cc0a6ae9786ef666e699e", "class_name": "RelatedNodeInfo"}, "2": {"node_id": "0f99212f-b9a3-4578-b9da-be568463aeb1", "node_type": "1", "metadata": {"file_path": "C:\\Users\\anteb\\PycharmProjects\\JupyterProject\\naive_rag\\data\\ai_articles\\ADDRESSING THE SUSTAINABLE AI TRILEMMA A CASE STUDY ON LLM AGENTS AND RAG.md", "file_name": "ADDRESSING THE SUSTAINABLE AI TRILEMMA A CASE STUDY ON LLM AGENTS AND RAG.md", "file_type": "text/markdown", "file_size": 72913, "creation_date": "2025-03-26", "last_modified_date": "2025-03-22"}, "hash": "2f17d8a359ec796b6ab93baa66531b880cae24003e8cc2c25aae177874d9360f", "class_name": "RelatedNodeInfo"}, "3": {"node_id": "76bd1c22-0d58-45f9-8425-2a64a9603700", "node_type": "1", "metadata": {}, "hash": "4e7a8112495f77096459bb25bbddc282d9e860c6ae9ea39803535e77c933929f", "class_name": "RelatedNodeInfo"}}, "metadata_template": "{key}: {value}", "metadata_separator": "\n", "text": "We follow the same node splitting configuration as in Eibich\u2019s experiments. For all datasets we sampled some data and the characteristics of these sub-datasets are shown in Table 1.\n\n**Table 1: Comparison of Dataset Characteristics**\n|Dataset|Memory Node Number|Memory Node Size*|Query Types|Difficulty|Query Length|Answer Length|Scenario|\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n|LoCoMo|419|54.6|5|Easy|Short|Short|Daily Life|\n|HotpotQA|992|128.5|1|Hard|Short|Short|Open-Domain|\n|MuSiQue|2000|111.7|1|Hard|Short|Short|Open-Domain|\n|AI ArXiv|51270|27.5|1|Medium|Long|Long|Academic|\n\n*The size of memory node is the average token number.\n\nMetrics tools. We evaluate Retrieval-Relevance, Generation-Relevance and Faithfulness using UpTrain\u2019s [68] LLM-based scorer with the GPT-4 API. For Retrieval-Accuracy we measure the Mean Reciprocal Rank (MRR) and Recall based on the ground truth provided by the datasets, while Generation-Correctness is measured using Recall-Oriented Understudy for Gisting Evaluation (ROUGE) [69]. All score ranges from 0 to 100.\n\nHardware. To evaluate LLM performance across different resource scenarios, we configured two hardware setups. The resource-constrained environment utilizes a single NVIDIA GeForce RTX 4080 GPU(16GB, 320W TDP) and an Intel i7-13700K processor (16-core 24 threads, 125W TDP). For the resource-abundant environment, we employed a cluster of eight NVIDIA A100 GPUs (80GB, 400W TDP) and two AMD EPYC 7J13 processors (64-Core 128 threads, 280W TDP), with NVIDIA NIM inference accelerating microservices 2, representing advanced high-performance computing capabilities compared with resource-constrained setup.", "mimetype": "text/plain", "start_char_idx": 34044, "end_char_idx": 35695, "metadata_seperator": "\n", "text_template": "[Excerpt from document]\n{metadata_str}\nExcerpt:\n-----\n{content}\n-----\n", "class_name": "TextNode"}, "__type__": "1"}, {"__data__": {"id_": "76bd1c22-0d58-45f9-8425-2a64a9603700", "embedding": null, "metadata": {"file_path": "C:\\Users\\anteb\\PycharmProjects\\JupyterProject\\naive_rag\\data\\ai_articles\\ADDRESSING THE SUSTAINABLE AI TRILEMMA A CASE STUDY ON LLM AGENTS AND RAG.md", "file_name": "ADDRESSING THE SUSTAINABLE AI TRILEMMA A CASE STUDY ON LLM AGENTS AND RAG.md", "file_type": "text/markdown", "file_size": 72913, "creation_date": "2025-03-26", "last_modified_date": "2025-03-22", "document_title": "\"Navigating the Sustainable AI Trilemma: Balancing Energy Efficiency, Digital Equity, and Capability in Large Language Models and AI Agent Design\"", "section_summary": "The section discusses the methodology and findings related to energy measurement and model selection in the context of resource-constrained and resource-abundant environments for large language models (LLMs) and AI agents. Key topics and entities include:\n\n1. **Energy Measurement Tools**: \n   - **Powercap**: Used for measuring CPU energy consumption via RAPL.\n   - **Pynvml**: A Python binding for NVIDIA Management Library (NVML) used for GPU energy monitoring.\n\n2. **LLM Selection**:\n   - In resource-constrained setups, the **Llama-3.1-8B-Instruct** model with 8-bit quantization is used.\n   - In resource-abundant setups, the same model is deployed on one A100 GPU, while the **Llama-3.1-70B-Instruct** is used on four A100 GPUs.\n\n3. **Task 1: Memory Formation and Retrieval**:\n   - The experiment involves selecting tokens from two datasets: **LoCoMo** (Internal Memory) and **MuSiQue** (External Memory) with varying sizes.\n   - **LlamaIndex** is utilized for different indexing implementations, with a retrieval top-K set to 5.\n   - The section highlights the energy and time cost comparison of different indexing methods for datasets with similar token counts.\n\n4. **Energy Cost Analysis**:\n   - The energy cost per token is influenced by the indexing method, with vector-based (non-LLM) indexing showing significantly lower energy costs.\n\nOverall, the section emphasizes the importance of energy efficiency in AI model deployment and the impact of indexing methods on energy consumption."}, "excluded_embed_metadata_keys": ["file_name", "file_type", "file_size", "creation_date", "last_modified_date", "last_accessed_date"], "excluded_llm_metadata_keys": ["file_name", "file_type", "file_size", "creation_date", "last_modified_date", "last_accessed_date"], "relationships": {"1": {"node_id": "5bbe2f3b-a5cf-473e-9e50-1d182dcad9fb", "node_type": "4", "metadata": {"file_path": "C:\\Users\\anteb\\PycharmProjects\\JupyterProject\\naive_rag\\data\\ai_articles\\ADDRESSING THE SUSTAINABLE AI TRILEMMA A CASE STUDY ON LLM AGENTS AND RAG.md", "file_name": "ADDRESSING THE SUSTAINABLE AI TRILEMMA A CASE STUDY ON LLM AGENTS AND RAG.md", "file_type": "text/markdown", "file_size": 72913, "creation_date": "2025-03-26", "last_modified_date": "2025-03-22"}, "hash": "47c48bbdebdc34c45547d1ddc9efb86758d4c6bea59cc0a6ae9786ef666e699e", "class_name": "RelatedNodeInfo"}, "2": {"node_id": "6e70e8eb-c370-4e9c-b167-70de6fb3d551", "node_type": "1", "metadata": {"file_path": "C:\\Users\\anteb\\PycharmProjects\\JupyterProject\\naive_rag\\data\\ai_articles\\ADDRESSING THE SUSTAINABLE AI TRILEMMA A CASE STUDY ON LLM AGENTS AND RAG.md", "file_name": "ADDRESSING THE SUSTAINABLE AI TRILEMMA A CASE STUDY ON LLM AGENTS AND RAG.md", "file_type": "text/markdown", "file_size": 72913, "creation_date": "2025-03-26", "last_modified_date": "2025-03-22"}, "hash": "295ff6a04610f6c627c62d4cfeb3f9ef040b710c3d48141bb4a0745c88672a8a", "class_name": "RelatedNodeInfo"}, "3": {"node_id": "5a4fca86-61b2-4468-8850-0bf9aa7aac69", "node_type": "1", "metadata": {}, "hash": "772716dcb83877820252f4151e73cc73e0feaa1f3f526cfcb86292ccf7f780d7", "class_name": "RelatedNodeInfo"}}, "metadata_template": "{key}: {value}", "metadata_separator": "\n", "text": "Tasks 1 and 2 are conducted on the resource-constrained setup, while Task 3 leverages the resource-abundant configuration.\n\nEnergy measurement tools. For hardware energy measurement, we employed two widely-used tools: powercap and pynvml. Powercap [70] interfaces with RAPL (Running Average Power Limit), which is supported by both our Intel and AMD processors, to collect CPU energy consumption data through records in system files. For GPU energy monitoring, we utilized pynvml [71], the Python binding for NVIDIA Management Library (NVML), which provides direct access to GPU power sensors. Both tools offer millisecond-level sampling precision with minimal overhead to the system being measured.\n\nLLM selection. In the resource-constrained environment, we select the Llama-3.1-8B-Instruct model with 8-bit quantization. In the resource-abundant environment, we deploy the Llama-3.1-8B-Instruct on one A100 and Llama-3.1-70B-Instruct on four A100.\n\n# 4.2 Task 1: Memory Formation and Retrieval\n\nIn this experiment, we selected 22,865 tokens of ground-truth documents from LoCoMo (Internal Mem), three different sized subsets (22,226, 99,946 and 202,676 tokens) from MuSiQue (External Mem). We use LlamaIndex [72] for different indexing implementations and the retrieval top-K is set to 5. Ten experiments were repeated to take the average value.\n\na. Different dataset, same number of tokens Table 2 shows the energy and time cost comparison of different indexing methods for different datasets with similar number of total memory token counts.\n\nEnergy Cost Per Token. The pattern of energy cost per token reveals two critical influencing factors. The indexing method shows substantial impact, with Vector-based (non-LLM) indexing demonstrating significantly lower energy 2.", "mimetype": "text/plain", "start_char_idx": 35696, "end_char_idx": 37472, "metadata_seperator": "\n", "text_template": "[Excerpt from document]\n{metadata_str}\nExcerpt:\n-----\n{content}\n-----\n", "class_name": "TextNode"}, "__type__": "1"}, {"__data__": {"id_": "5a4fca86-61b2-4468-8850-0bf9aa7aac69", "embedding": null, "metadata": {"file_path": "C:\\Users\\anteb\\PycharmProjects\\JupyterProject\\naive_rag\\data\\ai_articles\\ADDRESSING THE SUSTAINABLE AI TRILEMMA A CASE STUDY ON LLM AGENTS AND RAG.md", "file_name": "ADDRESSING THE SUSTAINABLE AI TRILEMMA A CASE STUDY ON LLM AGENTS AND RAG.md", "file_type": "text/markdown", "file_size": 72913, "creation_date": "2025-03-26", "last_modified_date": "2025-03-22", "document_title": "\"Navigating the Sustainable AI Trilemma: Balancing Energy Efficiency, Digital Equity, and Capability in Large Language Models and AI Agent Design\"", "section_summary": "The section discusses the energy efficiency and performance of different indexing methods used in large language models (LLMs) and AI agent design. Key topics include:\n\n1. **Energy Consumption**: \n   - Vector-based (non-LLM) indexing is highlighted for its significantly lower energy consumption (10\u22122 J/token) compared to LLM-dependent methods (10+1 J/token).\n   - The internal memory configuration (419 smaller nodes) consumes more energy than the external configuration (220 larger nodes).\n\n2. **Cost Metrics**: \n   - RERR (Relevance Energy Retrieval Rate) and EAR (Energy Accuracy Rate) metrics indicate that vector indexing achieves better retrieval effectiveness at lower energy costs (10\u22124 J) compared to LLM-dependent approaches (10\u22121 J).\n   - Internal data shows higher RERR and lower EAR values, while external memory exhibits the opposite trend.\n\n3. **Indexing Methods**: \n   - The Graph index, despite its sophistication, incurs higher energy costs in simpler queries compared to complex multi-hop queries, suggesting the importance of context-appropriate index selection.\n\n4. **Time Cost**: \n   - Node configuration affects processing times, with internal memory requiring longer times for indexing and querying than external data.\n   - The Vector-based method shows efficient processing times (10\u22125 s/token) for moderate token counts, while LLM-dependent methods require significantly longer indexing times.\n\nOverall, the section emphasizes the trade-offs between energy efficiency, processing time, and the choice of indexing methods in AI applications."}, "excluded_embed_metadata_keys": ["file_name", "file_type", "file_size", "creation_date", "last_modified_date", "last_accessed_date"], "excluded_llm_metadata_keys": ["file_name", "file_type", "file_size", "creation_date", "last_modified_date", "last_accessed_date"], "relationships": {"1": {"node_id": "5bbe2f3b-a5cf-473e-9e50-1d182dcad9fb", "node_type": "4", "metadata": {"file_path": "C:\\Users\\anteb\\PycharmProjects\\JupyterProject\\naive_rag\\data\\ai_articles\\ADDRESSING THE SUSTAINABLE AI TRILEMMA A CASE STUDY ON LLM AGENTS AND RAG.md", "file_name": "ADDRESSING THE SUSTAINABLE AI TRILEMMA A CASE STUDY ON LLM AGENTS AND RAG.md", "file_type": "text/markdown", "file_size": 72913, "creation_date": "2025-03-26", "last_modified_date": "2025-03-22"}, "hash": "47c48bbdebdc34c45547d1ddc9efb86758d4c6bea59cc0a6ae9786ef666e699e", "class_name": "RelatedNodeInfo"}, "2": {"node_id": "76bd1c22-0d58-45f9-8425-2a64a9603700", "node_type": "1", "metadata": {"file_path": "C:\\Users\\anteb\\PycharmProjects\\JupyterProject\\naive_rag\\data\\ai_articles\\ADDRESSING THE SUSTAINABLE AI TRILEMMA A CASE STUDY ON LLM AGENTS AND RAG.md", "file_name": "ADDRESSING THE SUSTAINABLE AI TRILEMMA A CASE STUDY ON LLM AGENTS AND RAG.md", "file_type": "text/markdown", "file_size": 72913, "creation_date": "2025-03-26", "last_modified_date": "2025-03-22"}, "hash": "820b4a50b2eecc7e9f37fd263faf9584f799fad6ad08883d996c61ba17a2b4c6", "class_name": "RelatedNodeInfo"}, "3": {"node_id": "aacdae21-3de9-4a12-a688-2b99ea2cbe03", "node_type": "1", "metadata": {}, "hash": "71fdf0732acf194e621e329594d0601a59296f3a406b8ff7efe361988229810c", "class_name": "RelatedNodeInfo"}}, "metadata_template": "{key}: {value}", "metadata_separator": "\n", "text": "The pattern of energy cost per token reveals two critical influencing factors. The indexing method shows substantial impact, with Vector-based (non-LLM) indexing demonstrating significantly lower energy 2.\n---\n# A PREPRINT - JANUARY 15, 2025\n\nconsumption (10\u22122 J/token) compared to LLM-dependent methods (Keyword, Summary, and Graph), which require approximately 10+1 J/token. Moreover, despite similar total token counts, the memory configuration significantly affects energy efficiency, with the internal memory setup (419 smaller nodes) consistently consuming more energy than the external configuration (220 larger nodes) across all indexing methods.\n\nRERR and EAR. The RERR and EAR cost metrics derive from the energy per token and essentially reflect the large differences between the different indexing approaches, with vector indexing achieving greater per-unit retrieval effectiveness at significantly lower energy cost (10\u22124J) compared to LLM-dependent approaches (10\u22121J). A distinctive pattern emerges in the cost distribution: internal data exhibits higher RERR and lower EAR values, while external memory shows the opposite trend, indicating varying energy investments required for achieving optimal Relevance and Accuracy across different scenarios. Notably, the Graph index, despite being the most sophisticated method, demands higher energy costs in simpler query scenarios (internal, 0.717 J and 1.035 J) compared to complex multi-hop queries (external, 0.565 J and 0.390 J), emphasizing the importance of context-appropriate index selection rather than defaulting to the most advanced option to avoid unnecessary energy expenditure.\n\nTime Cost. The time cost reveals that node configuration significantly impacts both writing and reading performance, with the internal memory\u2019s more nodes requiring longer processing times for both indexing and querying compared to external data. The Vector-based method, operating without LLM, demonstrates extremely efficient processing times (10\u22125 s/token) for the moderate token count (around 22k), while LLM-dependent methods demand substantially longer indexing times, highlighting the need to consider these significant preprocessing costs in specific use scenarios.", "mimetype": "text/plain", "start_char_idx": 37267, "end_char_idx": 39492, "metadata_seperator": "\n", "text_template": "[Excerpt from document]\n{metadata_str}\nExcerpt:\n-----\n{content}\n-----\n", "class_name": "TextNode"}, "__type__": "1"}, {"__data__": {"id_": "aacdae21-3de9-4a12-a688-2b99ea2cbe03", "embedding": null, "metadata": {"file_path": "C:\\Users\\anteb\\PycharmProjects\\JupyterProject\\naive_rag\\data\\ai_articles\\ADDRESSING THE SUSTAINABLE AI TRILEMMA A CASE STUDY ON LLM AGENTS AND RAG.md", "file_name": "ADDRESSING THE SUSTAINABLE AI TRILEMMA A CASE STUDY ON LLM AGENTS AND RAG.md", "file_type": "text/markdown", "file_size": 72913, "creation_date": "2025-03-26", "last_modified_date": "2025-03-22", "document_title": "\"Navigating the Sustainable AI Trilemma: Balancing Energy Efficiency, Digital Equity, and Capability in Large Language Models and AI Agent Design\"", "section_summary": "The section discusses the efficiency of different indexing methods in the context of large language models (LLMs) and AI agent design, particularly focusing on their reading times and energy consumption. It highlights that simpler indexing methods, like Keyword indexing, can outperform more complex methods, such as Graph indexing, in specific scenarios. The excerpt includes a table comparing the costs associated with memory formation across various indexing strategies, detailing metrics such as writing time, reading time, energy per token, and relative error rates (RERR) and energy accuracy rates (EAR) for both internal and external memory configurations. The data emphasizes the importance of aligning indexing strategies with application requirements to achieve optimal performance. \n\nKey topics include:\n- Indexing methods (Keyword vs. Graph)\n- Reading efficiency and time performance\n- Memory formation costs\n- Energy consumption in AI models\n- Relative error rates (RERR) and energy accuracy rates (EAR)\n\nEntities mentioned:\n- Large Language Models (LLMs)\n- AI agents\n- Internal and external memory configurations"}, "excluded_embed_metadata_keys": ["file_name", "file_type", "file_size", "creation_date", "last_modified_date", "last_accessed_date"], "excluded_llm_metadata_keys": ["file_name", "file_type", "file_size", "creation_date", "last_modified_date", "last_accessed_date"], "relationships": {"1": {"node_id": "5bbe2f3b-a5cf-473e-9e50-1d182dcad9fb", "node_type": "4", "metadata": {"file_path": "C:\\Users\\anteb\\PycharmProjects\\JupyterProject\\naive_rag\\data\\ai_articles\\ADDRESSING THE SUSTAINABLE AI TRILEMMA A CASE STUDY ON LLM AGENTS AND RAG.md", "file_name": "ADDRESSING THE SUSTAINABLE AI TRILEMMA A CASE STUDY ON LLM AGENTS AND RAG.md", "file_type": "text/markdown", "file_size": 72913, "creation_date": "2025-03-26", "last_modified_date": "2025-03-22"}, "hash": "47c48bbdebdc34c45547d1ddc9efb86758d4c6bea59cc0a6ae9786ef666e699e", "class_name": "RelatedNodeInfo"}, "2":