-01|4.76e-06|2.50e+01|9.23e-01|6.59e-01|1.71e-01|9.56e-06|2.48e+01|\n|Summary|7.87e-01|4.94e-01|1.76e-01|2.12e-04|2.52e+01|7.66e-01|4.81e-01|1.69e-01|3.73e-04|2.50e+01|\n|Graph|6.11e-01|3.21e-01|1.79e-01|4.29e-02|2.57e+01|6.17e-01|3.23e-01|1.", "mimetype": "text/plain", "start_char_idx": 42108, "end_char_idx": 43012, "metadata_seperator": "\n", "text_template": "[Excerpt from document]\n{metadata_str}\nExcerpt:\n-----\n{content}\n-----\n", "class_name": "TextNode"}, "__type__": "1"}, {"__data__": {"id_": "8dee8df6-47a0-484d-ba26-c34eb1234872", "embedding": null, "metadata": {"file_path": "C:\\Users\\anteb\\PycharmProjects\\JupyterProject\\naive_rag\\data\\ai_articles\\ADDRESSING THE SUSTAINABLE AI TRILEMMA A CASE STUDY ON LLM AGENTS AND RAG.md", "file_name": "ADDRESSING THE SUSTAINABLE AI TRILEMMA A CASE STUDY ON LLM AGENTS AND RAG.md", "file_type": "text/markdown", "file_size": 72913, "creation_date": "2025-03-26", "last_modified_date": "2025-03-22", "document_title": "\"Navigating the Sustainable AI Trilemma: Balancing Energy Efficiency, Digital Equity, and Capability in Large Language Models and AI Agent Design\"", "section_summary": "The section discusses an experiment related to memory reading, utilization, and generation in the context of large language models (LLMs) and AI agents. Key topics include:\n\n1. **Experiment Design**: The experiment involved selecting a variety of queries from different datasets, including LoCoMo, HotpotQA, MuSiQue, and ArXiv, focusing on simpler, non-multi-hop reasoning queries.\n\n2. **Methodology**: The indexing and retrieval method used was vector-based, with a top-K retrieval set to 5. The experiment was repeated ten times to obtain average results.\n\n3. **Results Presentation**: Results are summarized in two tables (Table 4 and Table 5), with Table 5 providing a multiple analysis based on metrics such as GERR (Generalized Error Rate Reduction), EFR (Efficiency Factor Ratio), and ECR (Efficiency Cost Ratio).\n\n4. **Query Optimization**: The HyDE method was chosen for query optimization due to its relatively better performance metrics, following previous research in retrieval-augmented generation (RAG) evaluation.\n\n5. **Prompt Engineering**: Ordinary LLM inference was implemented using prompt engineering techniques for other operations.\n\nEntities mentioned include:\n- Datasets: LoCoMo, HotpotQA, MuSiQue, ArXiv\n- Metrics: GERR, EFR, ECR\n- Method: HyDE (for query optimization)\n- Operations: Memory reading and utilization operations\n\nOverall, the section emphasizes the experimental approach to evaluating memory operations in AI systems, focusing on efficiency and effectiveness in query handling."}, "excluded_embed_metadata_keys": ["file_name", "file_type", "file_size", "creation_date", "last_modified_date", "last_accessed_date"], "excluded_llm_metadata_keys": ["file_name", "file_type", "file_size", "creation_date", "last_modified_date", "last_accessed_date"], "relationships": {"1": {"node_id": "5bbe2f3b-a5cf-473e-9e50-1d182dcad9fb", "node_type": "4", "metadata": {"file_path": "C:\\Users\\anteb\\PycharmProjects\\JupyterProject\\naive_rag\\data\\ai_articles\\ADDRESSING THE SUSTAINABLE AI TRILEMMA A CASE STUDY ON LLM AGENTS AND RAG.md", "file_name": "ADDRESSING THE SUSTAINABLE AI TRILEMMA A CASE STUDY ON LLM AGENTS AND RAG.md", "file_type": "text/markdown", "file_size": 72913, "creation_date": "2025-03-26", "last_modified_date": "2025-03-22"}, "hash": "47c48bbdebdc34c45547d1ddc9efb86758d4c6bea59cc0a6ae9786ef666e699e", "class_name": "RelatedNodeInfo"}, "2": {"node_id": "7cea014e-89cd-430a-9519-93e39a6346b5", "node_type": "1", "metadata": {"file_path": "C:\\Users\\anteb\\PycharmProjects\\JupyterProject\\naive_rag\\data\\ai_articles\\ADDRESSING THE SUSTAINABLE AI TRILEMMA A CASE STUDY ON LLM AGENTS AND RAG.md", "file_name": "ADDRESSING THE SUSTAINABLE AI TRILEMMA A CASE STUDY ON LLM AGENTS AND RAG.md", "file_type": "text/markdown", "file_size": 72913, "creation_date": "2025-03-26", "last_modified_date": "2025-03-22"}, "hash": "e035e4b8a662f860dafb84890bf6afc67b1f6ef505d9417b32b604529a519a22", "class_name": "RelatedNodeInfo"}, "3": {"node_id": "a23f2aca-b54f-41b6-b52b-a6c28c5ecec4", "node_type": "1", "metadata": {}, "hash": "e45e7aba34ed6608de1b500654994d4d2e8c03b056ba1fccccbb3b7616bb7526", "class_name": "RelatedNodeInfo"}}, "metadata_template": "{key}: {value}", "metadata_separator": "\n", "text": "79e-01|4.29e-02|2.57e+01|6.17e-01|3.23e-01|1.77e-01|3.58e-02|2.59e+01|\n---\n# A PREPRINT - JANUARY 15, 2025\n\n# 4.3 Task 2: Memory Reading, Utilization and Generation\n\na. For all generations In this experiment, we selected 100 simpler, non-multi-hop reasoning queries from the\nLoCoMo, 200 queries from HotpotQA & MuSiQue, and 100 academic queries from ArXiv dataset. The indexing and\nretrieval are fixed to the vector-based method and the retrieval top-K is set to 5. Ten experiments were repeated to take\nthe average value. The results are shown in Table 4 and Table 5, where Table 5 is a multiples analysis based on the\nGERR, EFR, and ECR values in Table 4. For the choice of query optimisation methods, we followed the experimental\nresults of other previous RAG evaluation work [73], choosing the HyDE method which has relatively better metrics.\nFor the other operations, we use prompt engineering for ordinary LLM inference to implement them.\n\n# Table 4: Cost Comparison of Memory Reading, Utilization Operations\n\n|Operation|Internal Mem.|External Mem. A|External Mem.", "mimetype": "text/plain", "start_char_idx": 42967, "end_char_idx": 44037, "metadata_seperator": "\n", "text_template": "[Excerpt from document]\n{metadata_str}\nExcerpt:\n-----\n{content}\n-----\n", "class_name": "TextNode"}, "__type__": "1"}, {"__data__": {"id_": "a23f2aca-b54f-41b6-b52b-a6c28c5ecec4", "embedding": null, "metadata": {"file_path": "C:\\Users\\anteb\\PycharmProjects\\JupyterProject\\naive_rag\\data\\ai_articles\\ADDRESSING THE SUSTAINABLE AI TRILEMMA A CASE STUDY ON LLM AGENTS AND RAG.md", "file_name": "ADDRESSING THE SUSTAINABLE AI TRILEMMA A CASE STUDY ON LLM AGENTS AND RAG.md", "file_type": "text/markdown", "file_size": 72913, "creation_date": "2025-03-26", "last_modified_date": "2025-03-22", "document_title": "\"Navigating the Sustainable AI Trilemma: Balancing Energy Efficiency, Digital Equity, and Capability in Large Language Models and AI Agent Design\"", "section_summary": "The section presents two tables that analyze the cost and energy efficiency of various memory operations in the context of large language models (LLMs) and AI agent design. \n\n**Key Topics:**\n1. **Cost Comparison of Memory Operations**: Table 4 outlines the costs associated with different operations (GERR, EFR, ECR, latency, retrieval detection, query optimization, reranking, compression) across three memory types: Internal Memory, External Memory A, and External Memory B.\n2. **Energy Cost Multiples Analysis**: Table 5 provides a comparative analysis of energy costs associated with the same operations, expressed as multiples relative to a baseline of retrieval and generation. It highlights the significant increases in energy costs for operations like query optimization and compression.\n3. **Impact of Dataset Characteristics**: The note emphasizes how the arXiv dataset, with its longer queries and larger node count, affects retrieval and detection costs, resulting in higher latency and influencing the overall efficiency of memory operations.\n\n**Entities:**\n- **Memory Types**: Internal Memory, External Memory A, External Memory B\n- **Operations**: GERR, EFR, ECR, retrieval detection, query optimization, reranking, compression\n- **Dataset**: arXiv dataset\n\nOverall, the section focuses on evaluating the trade-offs in cost and energy efficiency when utilizing different memory strategies in AI systems."}, "excluded_embed_metadata_keys": ["file_name", "file_type", "file_size", "creation_date", "last_modified_date", "last_accessed_date"], "excluded_llm_metadata_keys": ["file_name", "file_type", "file_size", "creation_date", "last_modified_date", "last_accessed_date"], "relationships": {"1": {"node_id": "5bbe2f3b-a5cf-473e-9e50-1d182dcad9fb", "node_type": "4", "metadata": {"file_path": "C:\\Users\\anteb\\PycharmProjects\\JupyterProject\\naive_rag\\data\\ai_articles\\ADDRESSING THE SUSTAINABLE AI TRILEMMA A CASE STUDY ON LLM AGENTS AND RAG.md", "file_name": "ADDRESSING THE SUSTAINABLE AI TRILEMMA A CASE STUDY ON LLM AGENTS AND RAG.md", "file_type": "text/markdown", "file_size": 72913, "creation_date": "2025-03-26", "last_modified_date": "2025-03-22"}, "hash": "47c48bbdebdc34c45547d1ddc9efb86758d4c6bea59cc0a6ae9786ef666e699e", "class_name": "RelatedNodeInfo"}, "2": {"node_id": "8dee8df6-47a0-484d-ba26-c34eb1234872", "node_type": "1", "metadata": {"file_path": "C:\\Users\\anteb\\PycharmProjects\\JupyterProject\\naive_rag\\data\\ai_articles\\ADDRESSING THE SUSTAINABLE AI TRILEMMA A CASE STUDY ON LLM AGENTS AND RAG.md", "file_name": "ADDRESSING THE SUSTAINABLE AI TRILEMMA A CASE STUDY ON LLM AGENTS AND RAG.md", "file_type": "text/markdown", "file_size": 72913, "creation_date": "2025-03-26", "last_modified_date": "2025-03-22"}, "hash": "0e4ee369df245cb51724eb10d0041635b7c538b4765a28b2dcc46d79944e069f", "class_name": "RelatedNodeInfo"}, "3": {"node_id": "b925935a-b57c-4a66-bd09-8c07673cd80e", "node_type": "1", "metadata": {}, "hash": "fc1fc929fe1a5b5c570da8ce1944855b7a4ef1addabadc0df66cddac58566d8b", "class_name": "RelatedNodeInfo"}}, "metadata_template": "{key}: {value}", "metadata_separator": "\n", "text": "# Table 4: Cost Comparison of Memory Reading, Utilization Operations\n\n|Operation|Internal Mem.|External Mem. A|External Mem. B|\n|---|---|---|---|\n|GERR|0.144|0.951|0.230|\n|EFR|0.096|0.102|0.176|\n|ECR|0.328|1.440|0.669|\n|latency(s)|0.059|0.059|0.102|\n|+ retrieval detection|0.188|1.114|0.385|\n|+ query optimization|5.005|15.957|2.806|\n|+ reranking|0.584|3.172|0.499|\n|+ compression|2.542|12.884|1.193|\n|+ all|6.561|28.568|3.433|\n\n# Table 5: Energy Cost Multiples Analysis (Retrieval + Generation as Baseline)\n\n|Operation|Internal Mem.|External Mem. A|External Mem. B|\n|---|---|---|---|\n|+ retrieval detection|1.30\u00d7|1.17\u00d7|1.67\u00d7|\n|+ query optimization|35.19\u00d7|15.95\u00d7|11.93\u00d7|\n|+ reranking|4.11\u00d7|3.31\u00d7|2.09\u00d7|\n|+ compression|17.07\u00d7|16.45\u00d7|5.21\u00d7|\n|+ all|45.70\u00d7|31.74\u00d7|15.08\u00d7|\n\nNote: Values represent geometric mean multiples of relative increases in GERR, EFR, and ECR External Mem B (ArXiv). The arXiv dataset, characterized by longer queries and a large number of nodes (51,270),\nimpacted retrieval and detection costs, leading to relatively high latency (0.102, 0.111). Longer responses contained\nmore information and divergence points, while smaller node sizes reduced the information in individual text fragments.", "mimetype": "text/plain", "start_char_idx": 43913, "end_char_idx": 45123, "metadata_seperator": "\n", "text_template": "[Excerpt from document]\n{metadata_str}\nExcerpt:\n-----\n{content}\n-----\n", "class_name": "TextNode"}, "__type__": "1"}, {"__data__": {"id_": "b925935a-b57c-4a66-bd09-8c07673cd80e", "embedding": null, "metadata": {"file_path": "C:\\Users\\anteb\\PycharmProjects\\JupyterProject\\naive_rag\\data\\ai_articles\\ADDRESSING THE SUSTAINABLE AI TRILEMMA A CASE STUDY ON LLM AGENTS AND RAG.md", "file_name": "ADDRESSING THE SUSTAINABLE AI TRILEMMA A CASE STUDY ON LLM AGENTS AND RAG.md", "file_type": "text/markdown", "file_size": 72913, "creation_date": "2025-03-26", "last_modified_date": "2025-03-22", "document_title": "\"Navigating the Sustainable AI Trilemma: Balancing Energy Efficiency, Digital Equity, and Capability in Large Language Models and AI Agent Design\"", "section_summary": "The section discusses the challenges and energy costs associated with optimizing large language models (LLMs) and AI agents in the context of sustainable AI. Key topics include:\n\n1. **Response Length and Information Density**: Longer responses provide more information but can lead to misalignment with retrieved text, resulting in higher energy costs for response generation.\n\n2. **External Memory (Mem A)**: The use of multi-hop queries increases query complexity, leading to higher energy costs (GERR and ECR metrics) for improving response relevance and accuracy. Compression techniques, while alleviating input length issues, significantly increase energy consumption.\n\n3. **Internal Memory (LoCoMo)**: Simple conversational queries exhibit low initial energy costs, but over-optimization during query processing can introduce redundant information, drastically increasing energy consumption and latency.\n\n4. **Cost Analysis of Operations**: Different operations have varying energy impacts. Retrieval detection is relatively low-cost and can be beneficial in simpler query scenarios, while sub-optimal operations, particularly in query optimization, can lead to extreme increases in energy costs.\n\nOverall, the section emphasizes the need for careful consideration of operational strategies to balance energy efficiency, response accuracy, and overall performance in AI systems."}, "excluded_embed_metadata_keys": ["file_name", "file_type", "file_size", "creation_date", "last_modified_date", "last_accessed_date"], "excluded_llm_metadata_keys": ["file_name", "file_type", "file_size", "creation_date", "last_modified_date", "last_accessed_date"], "relationships": {"1": {"node_id": "5bbe2f3b-a5cf-473e-9e50-1d182dcad9fb", "node_type": "4", "metadata": {"file_path": "C:\\Users\\anteb\\PycharmProjects\\JupyterProject\\naive_rag\\data\\ai_articles\\ADDRESSING THE SUSTAINABLE AI TRILEMMA A CASE STUDY ON LLM AGENTS AND RAG.md", "file_name": "ADDRESSING THE SUSTAINABLE AI TRILEMMA A CASE STUDY ON LLM AGENTS AND RAG.md", "file_type": "text/markdown", "file_size": 72913, "creation_date": "2025-03-26", "last_modified_date": "2025-03-22"}, "hash": "47c48bbdebdc34c45547d1ddc9efb86758d4c6bea59cc0a6ae9786ef666e699e", "class_name": "RelatedNodeInfo"}, "2": {"node_id": "a23f2aca-b54f-41b6-b52b-a6c28c5ecec4", "node_type": "1", "metadata": {"file_path": "C:\\Users\\anteb\\PycharmProjects\\JupyterProject\\naive_rag\\data\\ai_articles\\ADDRESSING THE SUSTAINABLE AI TRILEMMA A CASE STUDY ON LLM AGENTS AND RAG.md", "file_name": "ADDRESSING THE SUSTAINABLE AI TRILEMMA A CASE STUDY ON LLM AGENTS AND RAG.md", "file_type": "text/markdown", "file_size": 72913, "creation_date": "2025-03-26", "last_modified_date": "2025-03-22"}, "hash": "1c4b4ae8748effbbb0e5e87abcfde08c7e74076b21eba9e733f5bd2f9ae82884", "class_name": "RelatedNodeInfo"}, "3": {"node_id": "f5fe0bc8-a30d-4865-82c9-56e6ff3f31ef", "node_type": "1", "metadata": {}, "hash": "038bac63eb609c967b7bc0f12a7d9c6d5b604f57d022988e271b4482bf503bfd", "class_name": "RelatedNodeInfo"}}, "metadata_template": "{key}: {value}", "metadata_separator": "\n", "text": "Longer responses contained\nmore information and divergence points, while smaller node sizes reduced the information in individual text fragments.\nThis mismatch made it harder for the LLM to align responses with retrieved text, leading to higher EFR cost (0.176,\n0.294).\n\nExternal Mem A (HotpotQA & MuSiQue). The multi-hop query introduced higher query complexity, which notably\nled to high GERR and ECR metrics. These results highlighted the additional energy costs required to improve\nresponse-query relevance and ensure response accuracy. Although the compression effectively alleviated the pressure\nof larger nodes on LLM input length, it incurred a significant energy cost (a 16-fold increase), prompting a need to\ncarefully weigh the cost-effectiveness of such an expensive optimization step in various scenarios.\n\nInternal Mem (LoCoMo). LoCoMo consisted of simple, everyday conversational queries. Its low query complexity was\nreflected in the relatively low initial GERR (0.144) and ECR (0.328). However, during query optimization, the gener-\nated hypothetical documents for these simple tasks often introduced redundant or distracting information\u2014particularly\nin time-, location-, or person-based dialogues. This \"over-optimization\" led to a dramatic increase in energy consumption\nand latency (about 36-fold rise), underscoring the critical importance of carefully selecting operational strategies.\n\nCosts of different operations. With relatively low additional costs (1.17 to 1.67-fold increase), retrieval detection can be\nacceptable as a better trade-off operation, if in scenarios with simpler queries or where the LLM\u2019s own knowledge is\nsufficient, timely detection can prevent unnecessary downstream operations. In contrast, sub-optimal operations can\nhave extreme energy impacts, an example of which is query optimisation in internal memory. All operations (+all)\nresulted in energy costs increasing by an order of magnitude or more (45.7-fold, 31.74-fold, 15.08-fold).", "mimetype": "text/plain", "start_char_idx": 44978, "end_char_idx": 46963, "metadata_seperator": "\n", "text_template": "[Excerpt from document]\n{metadata_str}\nExcerpt:\n-----\n{content}\n-----\n", "class_name": "TextNode"}, "__type__": "1"}, {"__data__": {"id_": "f5fe0bc8-a30d-4865-82c9-56e6ff3f31ef", "embedding": null, "metadata": {"file_path": "C:\\Users\\anteb\\PycharmProjects\\JupyterProject\\naive_rag\\data\\ai_articles\\ADDRESSING THE SUSTAINABLE AI TRILEMMA A CASE STUDY ON LLM AGENTS AND RAG.md", "file_name": "ADDRESSING THE SUSTAINABLE AI TRILEMMA A CASE STUDY ON LLM AGENTS AND RAG.md", "file_type": "text/markdown", "file_size": 72913, "creation_date": "2025-03-26", "last_modified_date": "2025-03-22", "document_title": "\"Navigating the Sustainable AI Trilemma: Balancing Energy Efficiency, Digital Equity, and Capability in Large Language Models and AI Agent Design\"", "section_summary": "The section discusses the energy costs associated with operations in large language models (LLMs) and AI agents, particularly focusing on the Llama3.1 models. Key topics include:\n\n1. **Energy Costs**: The excerpt highlights a significant increase in energy costs during operations, with specific multipliers indicating the extent of this increase (e.g., 45.7-fold, 31.74-fold, 15.08-fold).\n\n2. **Memory Overhead**: It notes that while the increase in energy costs for external memory usage was smaller, it still scaled linearly with the workload of memory reading, particularly with top-K retrieved documents.\n\n3. **Model Comparisons**: The section compares different configurations of the Llama3.1 model, including resource-constrained (8-bit) and resource-abundant (standard and larger 70B versions).\n\n4. **Experimental Methodology**: The excerpt describes an experiment involving 100 correctly answered queries from various datasets (LoCoMo, HotpotQA, MuSiQue) and the measurement of energy consumption using monitoring tools and OLS regression.\n\n5. **Energy Model**: It presents a derived energy model with specific coefficients that effectively captures the relationship between energy consumption and token numbers, validated by a high R-squared value.\n\n6. **Generation Energy Optimality Ratio (GEOR)**: The section concludes with calculations of the GEOR for the datasets, revealing low values (1-2%), indicating inefficiencies in energy usage during generation.\n\nOverall, the excerpt emphasizes the challenges of balancing energy efficiency with performance in the design of AI models, particularly in the context of sustainable AI development."}, "excluded_embed_metadata_keys": ["file_name", "file_type", "file_size", "creation_date", "last_modified_date", "last_accessed_date"], "excluded_llm_metadata_keys": ["file_name", "file_type", "file_size", "creation_date", "last_modified_date", "last_accessed_date"], "relationships": {"1": {"node_id": "5bbe2f3b-a5cf-473e-9e50-1d182dcad9fb", "node_type": "4", "metadata": {"file_path": "C:\\Users\\anteb\\PycharmProjects\\JupyterProject\\naive_rag\\data\\ai_articles\\ADDRESSING THE SUSTAINABLE AI TRILEMMA A CASE STUDY ON LLM AGENTS AND RAG.md", "file_name": "ADDRESSING THE SUSTAINABLE AI TRILEMMA A CASE STUDY ON LLM AGENTS AND RAG.md", "file_type": "text/markdown", "file_size": 72913, "creation_date": "2025-03-26", "last_modified_date": "2025-03-22"}, "hash": "47c48bbdebdc34c45547d1ddc9efb86758d4c6bea59cc0a6ae9786ef666e699e", "class_name": "RelatedNodeInfo"}, "2": {"node_id": "b925935a-b57c-4a66-bd09-8c07673cd80e", "node_type": "1", "metadata": {"file_path": "C:\\Users\\anteb\\PycharmProjects\\JupyterProject\\naive_rag\\data\\ai_articles\\ADDRESSING THE SUSTAINABLE AI TRILEMMA A CASE STUDY ON LLM AGENTS AND RAG.md", "file_name": "ADDRESSING THE SUSTAINABLE AI TRILEMMA A CASE STUDY ON LLM AGENTS AND RAG.md", "file_type": "text/markdown", "file_size": 72913, "creation_date": "2025-03-26", "last_modified_date": "2025-03-22"}, "hash": "6d639ce91e4b3a39d63b55dded76ba21bf44d5015d2f4164cde46cd188f5ab03", "class_name": "RelatedNodeInfo"}, "3": {"node_id": "d4c9b878-dcc3-48a9-8beb-283b499810df", "node_type": "1", "metadata": {}, "hash": "f48a8721e7c8039fc19c016232df19707b9c98156a8aa4da291e3bae0fed6c56", "class_name": "RelatedNodeInfo"}}, "metadata_template": "{key}: {value}", "metadata_separator": "\n", "text": "All operations (+all)\nresulted in energy costs increasing by an order of magnitude or more (45.7-fold, 31.74-fold, 15.08-fold). Although\nthe relative increase of External Mem.B was smaller, the overhead linearly scaled as the memory reading workload\n(top-K retrieved documents) increased, as shown in Figure 4a.\n---\n# A PREPRINT - JANUARY 15, 2025\n\n# 32.5 GERR\n\n# Resource-constrained: Llama3.1-8B (8-bit)\n\n# Resource-abundant: Llama3.1-8B\n\n# Resource-abundant: Llama3.1-70B\n\n# Cost Multiple Comparison (External Mem. B)\n\n# Cost Multiple Comparison (Different Models)\n\n# b. For correct generations\n\nIn this experiment, we selected 100 correctly answered queries each from the LoCoMo, HotpotQA and MuSiQue respectively. Through 10 experimental iterations with 100 different (input length, output length) pairs in our resource-constrained environment, we measured the actual energy consumption using monitoring tools and then applying OLS regression to fit the energy model 3.1. We derived the coefficients \u03b1K,0 = 0.042933, \u03b1K,1 = 9.109322, and \u03b1K,2 = 0.000513 for the equation (3), where K represents Llama-3.1-8B-Instruct model with 8-bit quantization. The high R-squared value of 0.989 validates that this model effectively captures the energy-token numbers relationship.\n\nBased on our defined minimal essential operations and data in 3.3.2, we calculated Eg by substituting into equation (3) and measured the retrieval cost Eretr and real energy cost Ereal when all operations were performed. Following Equations (6) and (7), we calculated the Generation Energy Optimality Ratio (GEOR) for the three datasets. The results show that the GEOR values are extremely low as shown in Table 6, all only about 1-2%.", "mimetype": "text/plain", "start_char_idx": 46836, "end_char_idx": 48545, "metadata_seperator": "\n", "text_template": "[Excerpt from document]\n{metadata_str}\nExcerpt:\n-----\n{content}\n-----\n", "class_name": "TextNode"}, "__type__": "1"}, {"__data__": {"id_": "d4c9b878-dcc3-48a9-8beb-283b499810df", "embedding": null, "metadata": {"file_path": "C:\\Users\\anteb\\PycharmProjects\\JupyterProject\\naive_rag\\data\\ai_articles\\ADDRESSING THE SUSTAINABLE AI TRILEMMA A CASE STUDY ON LLM AGENTS AND RAG.md", "file_name": "ADDRESSING THE SUSTAINABLE AI TRILEMMA A CASE STUDY ON LLM AGENTS AND RAG.md", "file_type": "text/markdown", "file_size": 72913, "creation_date": "2025-03-26", "last_modified_date": "2025-03-22", "document_title": "\"Navigating the Sustainable AI Trilemma: Balancing Energy Efficiency, Digital Equity, and Capability in Large Language Models and AI Agent Design\"", "section_summary": "The section discusses the energy efficiency and performance of large language models (LLMs) in the context of a sustainable AI trilemma, focusing on a case study involving LLM agents and retrieval-augmented generation (RAG). Key topics include:\n\n1. **Generation Energy Optimality Ratio (GEOR)**: The results indicate that GEOR values for various datasets (LoCoMo, HotpotQA, MuSiQue) are extremely low, ranging from 1.012% to 1.456%. This highlights inefficiencies in energy utilization during the generation pipeline.\n\n2. **Factors Contributing to Energy Inefficiency**: Excessive energy consumption is attributed to the retrieval and processing of irrelevant memories, overhead from additional operations, and latency in retrieval and computation.\n\n3. **Resource-Constrained vs. Resource-Abundant Environments**: Experiments were conducted in both environments using Llama-3.1-8B-Instruct and Llama-3.1-70B-Instruct. The resource-abundant environment demonstrated better performance metrics, despite operating at full precision.\n\n4. **Cost-Effectiveness Paradox**: The section notes that the resource-constrained environment, despite using reduced precision (8-bit quantization), exhibited poor cost-efficiency ratios compared to the resource-abundant environment, which consumed less energy per memory token.\n\nOverall, the findings underscore the significant potential for improving energy efficiency in LLM design and the challenges posed by different operational environments."}, "excluded_embed_metadata_keys": ["file_name", "file_type", "file_size", "creation_date", "last_modified_date", "last_accessed_date"], "excluded_llm_metadata_keys": ["file_name", "file_type", "file_size", "creation_date", "last_modified_date", "last_accessed_date"], "relationships": {"1": {"node_id": "5bbe2f3b-a5cf-473e-9e50-1d182dcad9fb", "node_type": "4", "metadata": {"file_path": "C:\\Users\\anteb\\PycharmProjects\\JupyterProject\\naive_rag\\data\\ai_articles\\ADDRESSING THE SUSTAINABLE AI TRILEMMA A CASE STUDY ON LLM AGENTS AND RAG.md", "file_name": "ADDRESSING THE SUSTAINABLE AI TRILEMMA A CASE STUDY ON LLM AGENTS AND RAG.md", "file_type": "text/markdown", "file_size": 72913, "creation_date": "2025-03-26", "last_modified_date": "2025-03-22"}, "hash": "47c48bbdebdc34c45547d1ddc9efb86758d4c6bea59cc0a6ae9786ef666e699e", "class_name": "RelatedNodeInfo"}, "2": {"node_id": "f5fe0bc8-a30d-4865-82c9-56e6ff3f31ef", "node_type": "1", "metadata": {"file_path": "C:\\Users\\anteb\\PycharmProjects\\JupyterProject\\naive_rag\\data\\ai_articles\\ADDRESSING THE SUSTAINABLE AI TRILEMMA A CASE STUDY ON LLM AGENTS AND RAG.md", "file_name": "ADDRESSING THE SUSTAINABLE AI TRILEMMA A CASE STUDY ON LLM AGENTS AND RAG.md", "file_type": "text/markdown", "file_size": 72913, "creation_date": "2025-03-26", "last_modified_date": "2025-03-22"}, "hash": "27764f5494b6fc3eb119ac2872455ac7a30f2544a97b3e91ff428c57ab38e5ce", "class_name": "RelatedNodeInfo"}, "3": {"node_id": "5653fad3-fd1d-4a10-b1e3-786dec56366f", "node_type": "1", "metadata": {}, "hash": "1cab2ff185fa5aa4e4934ca4de5cd02783aa3d9492c96bc5f31e449c6bbe1828", "class_name": "RelatedNodeInfo"}}, "metadata_template": "{key}: {value}", "metadata_separator": "\n", "text": "The results show that the GEOR values are extremely low as shown in Table 6, all only about 1-2%. Several factors lead to excessive extra energy consumption, yielding these exceptionally low values: the retrieval and processing of many irrelevant memories, the accumulated overhead from additional operations, and the extended processing time from retrieval and computation latency. This indicates that while the LLM agent is capable of correctly answering memory-based tasks, there is a considerable gap between the system\u2019s current energy performance and the theoretical minimum, underscoring the inefficiency in energy utilization during the generation pipeline, as well as the significant room for improvement in optimizing the energy efficiency of the LLM agent\u2019s design pattern.\n\n**Generation Energy Optimality Ratio (when all operations are performed)**\n|Dataset|LoCoMo|HotpotQA|MuSiQue|\n|---|---|---|---|\n|GEOR|1.378%|1.012%|1.456%|\n\n# 4.4 Task 3: Resource-constrained vs. Resource-abundant Environment\n\nIn this part we repeated the same experiments for Task 1 and 2 in resource-abundant environment using Llama-3.1-8B-Instruct and Llama-3.1-70B-Instruct. The results shown in Figure 5 represent the average metrics of the three LLM-based index methods (Keyword, Summary, and Graph) for the Memory Formation and Retrieval phases, and Figure 4b, the energy of the Generation phases for performing all operations on the Arxiv dataset compared to the multiples of energy consumption of the baseline (Retrieval + Generation).\n\nCost-Effectiveness Paradox. Despite operating with reduced precision (8-bit quantization), the resource-constrained environment shows notably poor cost-efficiency ratios. The resource-abundant environment, running full-precision 8B model, achieves lower RERR (0.642 vs 0.769) and EAR (0.352 vs 0.488), and consumed less energy per memory token (18.7 vs 25.2 joules).", "mimetype": "text/plain", "start_char_idx": 48448, "end_char_idx": 50345, "metadata_seperator": "\n", "text_template": "[Excerpt from document]\n{metadata_str}\nExcerpt:\n-----\n{content}\n-----\n", "class_name": "TextNode"}, "__type__": "1"}, {"__data__": {"id_": "5653fad3-fd1d-4a10-b1e3-786dec56366f", "embedding": null, "metadata": {"file_path": "C:\\Users\\anteb\\PycharmProjects\\JupyterProject\\naive_rag\\data\\ai_articles\\ADDRESSING THE SUSTAINABLE AI TRILEMMA A CASE STUDY ON LLM AGENTS AND RAG.md", "file_name": "ADDRESSING THE SUSTAINABLE AI TRILEMMA A CASE STUDY ON LLM AGENTS AND RAG.md", "file_type": "text/markdown", "file_size": 72913, "creation_date": "2025-03-26", "last_modified_date": "2025-03-22", "document_title": "\"Navigating the Sustainable AI Trilemma: Balancing Energy Efficiency, Digital Equity, and Capability in Large Language Models and AI Agent Design\"", "section_summary": "The section discusses the energy efficiency and effectiveness of large language models (LLMs) in different resource environments, specifically contrasting resource-constrained and resource-abundant settings. Key topics include:\n\n1. **Energy Metrics**: The section introduces two metrics, Retrieval Energy Relevance Rate (RERR) and Energy Accuracy Rate (EAR), which measure the energy cost associated with achieving unit retrieval relevance and accuracy.\n\n2. **Operational Costs**: It highlights a counter-intuitive finding that resource-constrained environments incur higher operational costs (35% more energy per token) while achieving lower effectiveness compared to resource-abundant environments.\n\n3. **Model Comparisons**: The text compares different model sizes, specifically Llama3.1-8B (8-bit and full) and Llama3.1-70B, noting that the larger model consumes significantly more energy per memory token but offers only marginally better relevance and accuracy improvements.\n\n4. **Scaling Benefits**: The section emphasizes that the 70B model's performance improvements (in terms of RERR and EAR) do not scale linearly with its energy consumption, indicating that resource-abundant environments can leverage advantageous scaling properties that are not available to resource-constrained settings.\n\n5. **Visual Data**: A table and figure are referenced, illustrating the comparison of energy consumption and performance metrics between resource-constrained and resource-abundant environments.\n\nOverall, the excerpt underscores the challenges and trade-offs in designing AI agents and LLMs with respect to energy efficiency, digital equity, and capability."}, "excluded_embed_metadata_keys": ["file_name", "file_type", "file_size", "creation_date", "last_modified_date", "last_accessed_date"], "excluded_llm_metadata_keys": ["file_name", "file_type", "file_size", "creation_date", "last_modified_date", "last_accessed_date"], "relationships": {"1": {"node_id": "5bbe2f3b-a5cf-473e-9e50-1d182dcad9fb", "node_type": "4", "metadata": {"file_path": "C:\\Users\\anteb\\PycharmProjects\\JupyterProject\\naive_rag\\data\\ai_articles\\ADDRESSING THE SUSTAINABLE AI TRILEMMA A CASE STUDY ON LLM AGENTS AND RAG.md", "file_name": "ADDRESSING THE SUSTAINABLE AI TRILEMMA A CASE STUDY ON LLM AGENTS AND RAG.md", "file_type": "text/markdown", "file_size": 72913, "creation_date": "2025-03-26", "last_modified_date": "2025-03-22"}, "hash": "47c48bbdebdc34c45547d1ddc9efb86758d4c6bea59cc0a6ae9786ef666e699e", "class_name": "RelatedNodeInfo"}, "2": {"node_id": "d4c9b878-dcc3-48a9-8beb-283b499810df", "node_type": "1", "metadata": {"file_path": "C:\\Users\\anteb\\PycharmProjects\\JupyterProject\\naive_rag\\data\\ai_articles\\ADDRESSING THE SUSTAINABLE AI TRILEMMA A CASE STUDY ON LLM AGENTS AND RAG.md", "file_name": "ADDRESSING THE SUSTAINABLE AI TRILEMMA A CASE STUDY ON LLM AGENTS AND RAG.md", "file_type": "text/markdown", "file_size": 72913, "creation_date": "2025-03-26", "last_modified_date": "2025-03-22"}, "hash": "72ec21e591cf5535b7f06ce61446cc94770765ad6152ebf45951a72334c87ac7", "class_name": "RelatedNodeInfo"}, "3": {"node_id": "0867f85f-16c2-4aa1-bfcf-0b7147633128", "node_type": "1", "metadata": {}, "hash": "e72cf15fa1e8739db62d1c1ea89861efca4e5acba2b2843f0391800865a51586", "class_name": "RelatedNodeInfo"}}, "metadata_template": "{key}: {value}", "metadata_separator": "\n", "text": "Given that RERR and EAR measure the energy cost required to achieve unit retrieval relevance and accuracy respectively, this counter-intuitive result reveals that resource-constrained environments are forced to pay higher operational costs (35% more energy per token) while achieving lower effectiveness.\n---\n# A PREPRINT - JANUARY 15, 2025\n\n# Resource-constrained: Llama3.1-8B (8-bit)\n\n# Resource-abundant: Llama3.1-8B\n\n# Resource-abundant: Llama3.1-70B\n\n| | | |100|0.200| |0.175|0.172|\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n|2.5|1.687| |71.000|80|0.150| | |\n|1.5| | |60|8 @0.125| |3| |\n|2|1.0|0.769|0.979|40|0.075| | |\n|0.5|0.642|0.488|0.352|25.200|18.700| | |\n| | |20|0.050|0.041| |0.025|0.022|\n|0.0| |Energy per token|0.000|Writing Time|0.003|00| |\n\nRERR EAR Metrics per token Time Metrics per token\n\n# Figure 5: Comparison of Memory Formation in Resource-constrained vs. Resource-abundant Environments\n\nModel Size Scaling Benefits. The 70B model consumes approximately three times more energy per memory token than the quantized 8B model (67.5 vs 25.2 joules), while its RERR (1.687 vs 0.769) and EAR (0.979 vs 0.488) are only about twice as high. This disproportionate relationship indicates that the 70B model achieves higher relevance and accuracy improvements relative to its energy consumption increase. This advantageous scaling property - where performance improvements outpace energy cost increases - is exclusively available to resource-abundant environments.", "mimetype": "text/plain", "start_char_idx": 50346, "end_char_idx": 51815, "metadata_seperator": "\n", "text_template": "[Excerpt from document]\n{metadata_str}\nExcerpt:\n-----\n{content}\n-----\n", "class_name": "TextNode"}, "__type__": "1"}, {"__data__": {"id_": "0867f85f-16c2-4aa1-bfcf-0b7147633128", "embedding": null, "metadata": {"file_path": "C:\\Users\\anteb\\PycharmProjects\\JupyterProject\\naive_rag\\data\\ai_articles\\ADDRESSING THE SUSTAINABLE AI TRILEMMA A CASE STUDY ON LLM AGENTS AND RAG.md", "file_name": "ADDRESSING THE SUSTAINABLE AI TRILEMMA A CASE STUDY ON LLM AGENTS AND RAG.md", "file_type": "text/markdown", "file_size": 72913, "creation_date": "2025-03-26", "last_modified_date": "2025-03-22", "document_title": "\"Navigating the Sustainable AI Trilemma: Balancing Energy Efficiency, Digital Equity, and Capability in Large Language Models and AI Agent Design\"", "section_summary": "The section discusses the challenges and disparities in the performance and energy efficiency of Large Language Model (LLM) agent systems in resource-abundant versus resource-constrained environments. Key topics include:\n\n1. **Scaling Property**: The performance improvements in resource-abundant environments significantly outpace energy cost increases, which is not the case in resource-constrained settings.\n\n2. **Service Quality Disparity**: There is a notable difference in processing efficiency, with resource-constrained environments experiencing much slower indexing speeds and longer preprocessing times, leading to sub-optimal service quality.\n\n3. **Optimization Penalty Paradox**: The energy efficiency gap widens in resource-constrained environments during optimization operations, where energy costs increase dramatically with workload, contrasting with more moderate increases in resource-abundant settings.\n\n4. **Sustainable AI Trilemma**: The section highlights the tensions between capability, digital equity, and environmental sustainability in practical deployments of LLM agent systems.\n\n5. **Operational Dilemma**: The investigation reveals stark energy consumption patterns that illustrate the challenges faced in balancing these three aspects.\n\nOverall, the section emphasizes the empirical evidence of the Sustainable AI Trilemma and the implications for real-world applications of LLM agents."}, "excluded_embed_metadata_keys": ["file_name", "file_type", "file_size", "creation_date", "last_modified_date", "last_accessed_date"], "excluded_llm_metadata_keys": ["file_name", "file_type", "file_size", "creation_date", "last_modified_date", "last_accessed_date"], "relationships": {"1": {"node_id": "5bbe2f3b-a5cf-473e-9e50-1d182dcad9fb", "node_type": "4", "metadata": {"file_path": "C:\\Users\\anteb\\PycharmProjects\\JupyterProject\\naive_rag\\data\\ai_articles\\ADDRESSING THE SUSTAINABLE AI TRILEMMA A CASE STUDY ON LLM AGENTS AND RAG.md", "file_name": "ADDRESSING THE SUSTAINABLE AI TRILEMMA A CASE STUDY ON LLM AGENTS AND RAG.md", "file_type": "text/markdown", "file_size": 72913, "creation_date": "2025-03-26", "last_modified_date": "2025-03-22"}, "hash": "47c48bbdebdc34c45547d1ddc9efb86758d4c6bea59cc0a6ae9786ef666e699e", "class_name": "RelatedNodeInfo"}, "2": {"node_id": "5653fad3-fd1d-4a10-b1e3-786dec56366f", "node_type": "1", "metadata": {"file_path": "C:\\Users\\anteb\\PycharmProjects\\JupyterProject\\naive_rag\\data\\ai_articles\\ADDRESSING THE SUSTAINABLE AI TRILEMMA A CASE STUDY ON LLM AGENTS AND RAG.md", "file_name": "ADDRESSING THE SUSTAINABLE AI TRILEMMA A CASE STUDY ON LLM AGENTS AND RAG.md", "file_type": "text/markdown", "file_size": 72913, "creation_date": "2025-03-26", "last_modified_date": "2025-03-22"}, "hash": "e02a0198beb1b4c462035419eee5d131bd0b245b1d9800535df598c6933943b1", "class_name": "RelatedNodeInfo"}, "3": {"node_id": "7e0f8b69-1001-45b1-b63a-b54b69fd3f76", "node_type": "1", "metadata": {}, "hash": "5ce906696e52a802d7454bb4fb449a784ec05f6746b6357e9927cfa4333d1f72", "class_name": "RelatedNodeInfo"}}, "metadata_template": "{key}: {value}", "metadata_separator": "\n", "text": "This advantageous scaling property - where performance improvements outpace energy cost increases - is exclusively available to resource-abundant environments.\n\nService Quality Disparity. The most striking disparity appears in processing efficiency. The resource-constrained environment exhibits dramatically slower indexing speeds, with writing time per token (0.172s) being approximately 8 times slower than the resource-abundant environment (0.022s for 8B model), while the difference in read time is also four to six times greater. This difference in processing speed signals that resource-constrained environments are paying for longer preprocessing times but gaining a more sub-optimal quality of service, potentially rendering these LLM agent systems impractical for real-world applications in resource-limited settings.\n\nOptimization Penalty Paradox. Further analysis of the Generation phase (Figure 4b) reveals an even more severe energy efficiency gap with optimization operations (reranking, compression, etc.). While all configurations show increasing energy costs as the workload grows, the resource-constrained environment exhibits a dramatically steeper curve - from 15x to 32x baseline consumption as top-K increases from 5 to 50. In comparison, resource-abundant environments show more moderate increases (from 4x to 6x for 8B, and 7x to 12x for 70B). This divergent scaling behavior indicates that although the baseline energy overhead might be lower in resource-constrained environments, their efficiency deteriorates much more rapidly with increased context length and additional processing steps. This creates a paradoxical situation where attempts to improve system performance through optimization operations actually widen the energy efficiency gap between resource-abundant and resource-constrained deployments.\n\n# 5 Discussions and Conclusions\n\nOur experimental investigation into LLM agent systems provides empirical evidence for the Sustainable AI Trilemma and its constituent dilemmas, revealing how the tensions between capability, digital equity, and environmental sustainability manifest in practical deployments. Through a systematic examination of memory operations in LLM agents, we observe several critical patterns that validate and deepen our understanding of these challenges.\n\nThe Operational Dilemma manifests through stark energy consumption patterns in LLM agent systems.", "mimetype": "text/plain", "start_char_idx": 51656, "end_char_idx": 54070, "metadata_seperator": "\n", "text_template": "[Excerpt from document]\n{metadata_str}\nExcerpt:\n-----\n{content}\n-----\n", "class_name": "TextNode"}, "__type__": "1"}, {"__data__": {"id_": "7e0f8b69-1001-45b1-b63a-b54b69fd3f76", "embedding": null, "metadata": {"file_path": "C:\\Users\\anteb\\PycharmProjects\\JupyterProject\\naive_rag\\data\\ai_articles\\ADDRESSING THE SUSTAINABLE AI TRILEMMA A CASE STUDY ON LLM AGENTS AND RAG.md", "file_name": "ADDRESSING THE SUSTAINABLE AI TRILEMMA A CASE STUDY ON LLM AGENTS AND RAG.md", "file_type": "text/markdown", "file_size": 72913, "creation_date": "2025-03-26", "last_modified_date": "2025-03-22", "document_title": "\"Navigating the Sustainable AI Trilemma: Balancing Energy Efficiency, Digital Equity, and Capability in Large Language Models and AI Agent Design\"", "section_summary": "The section discusses the \"Sustainable AI Trilemma\" in the context of Large Language Models (LLMs) and AI agent design, highlighting four main dilemmas:\n\n1. **Operational Dilemma**: This dilemma focuses on the significant energy consumption of LLM systems, emphasizing the trade-off between scalability and sustainability. It notes that current architectures operate at a low Generation Energy Optimality Ratio (GEOR) of 1-2%, indicating a need for breakthroughs in energy efficiency to ensure sustainable deployment of LLM agents.\n\n2. **Evaluation Dilemma**: This addresses the transition from basic awareness to precise measurement of AI's environmental impact. The section critiques previous methods that relied on simple proxies and introduces a new evaluation framework with targeted metrics to better understand the trade-offs and patterns between energy costs and system performance.\n\n3. **Design Dilemma**: This dilemma critiques the \"LLM-centric\" approach that relies entirely on LLMs for operational control. It highlights inefficiencies in fully autonomous LLM operations and suggests a hybrid approach that combines LLMs with traditional non-LLM methods to optimize energy costs, especially as task complexity increases.\n\n4. **Access Dilemma**: This dilemma points to digital inequality, where limited computational resources create a negative feedback loop. It notes that those with fewer resources face higher operational costs and reduced capabilities, exacerbating technological disparities.\n\nOverall, the section emphasizes the need for a balanced approach to AI design that considers energy efficiency, equitable access, and effective evaluation methods."}, "excluded_embed_metadata_keys": ["file_name", "file_type", "file_size", "creation_date", "last_modified_date", "last_accessed_date"], "excluded_llm_metadata_keys": ["file_name", "file_type", "file_size", "creation_date", "last_modified_date", "last_accessed_date"], "relationships": {"1": {"node_id": "5bbe2f3b-a5cf-473e-9e50-1d182dcad9fb", "node_type": "4", "metadata": {"file_path": "C:\\Users\\anteb\\PycharmProjects\\JupyterProject\\naive_rag\\data\\ai_articles\\ADDRESSING THE SUSTAINABLE AI TRILEMMA A CASE STUDY ON LLM AGENTS AND RAG.md", "file_name": "ADDRESSING THE SUSTAINABLE AI TRILEMMA A CASE STUDY ON LLM AGENTS AND RAG.md", "file_type": "text/markdown", "file_size": 72913, "creation_date": "2025-03-26", "last_modified_date": "2025-03-22"}, "hash": "47c48bbdebdc34c45547d1ddc9efb86758d4c6bea59cc0a6ae9786ef666e699e", "class_name": "RelatedNodeInfo"}, "2": {"node_id": "0867f85f-16c2-4aa1-bfcf-0b7147633128", "node_type": "1", "metadata": {"file_path": "C:\\Users\\anteb\\PycharmProjects\\JupyterProject\\naive_rag\\data\\ai_articles\\ADDRESSING THE SUSTAINABLE AI TRILEMMA A CASE STUDY ON LLM AGENTS AND RAG.md", "file_name": "ADDRESSING THE SUSTAINABLE AI TRILEMMA A CASE STUDY ON LLM AGENTS AND RAG.md", "file_type": "text/markdown", "file_size": 72913, "creation_date": "2025-03-26", "last_modified_date": "2025-03-22"}, "hash": "47e3960df0ed7d2810429c8a15513e0ff058a56cbe99af2e2d57a04cb15c4719", "class_name": "RelatedNodeInfo"}, "3": {"node_id": "fef2fd32-473a-4b2a-acd2-87013a3fbede", "node_type": "1", "metadata": {}, "hash": "40fa73a2dc28d121bdefd9889c98943f8f6cc445b1069649737dba54deccf80c", "class_name": "RelatedNodeInfo"}}, "metadata_template": "{key}: {value}", "metadata_separator": "\n", "text": "The Operational Dilemma manifests through stark energy consumption patterns in LLM agent systems. The fundamental tension between scalability and sustainability is evident in the orders-of-magnitude difference between LLM-dependent and non-LLM methods, with optimization attempts often leading to significant energy penalties. The remarkably low Generation Energy Optimality Ratio (GEOR) of 1-2% highlights how current architectures operate far from optimal efficiency, suggesting that without fundamental breakthroughs in energy efficiency, the widespread deployment of LLM agents may face significant sustainability barriers.\n\nThe Evaluation Dilemma reflects the crucial transition from mere awareness to precise measurement of AI\u2019s environmental impact. While previous approaches relied on simple proxies like the product of GPU power and runtime, our evaluation framework introduces targeted metrics and more concrete evidences that reveal nuanced trade-offs.\n---\n# A PREPRINT - JANUARY 15, 2025\n\nand previously obscured patterns between energy costs and system performance. This progress in measurement capability, though significant, also highlights the continuing challenge of developing comprehensive assessment tools for increasingly complex AI applications.\n\n# The Design Dilemma\n\nchallenges the prevailing \"LLM-centric\" paradigm that delegates complete operational control to LLM. Our findings reveal the inefficiencies and overhead associated with fully LLM autonomous operation while only some LLM operations can be cost-effective like retrieval detection. The systematic increase in energy costs with task complexity suggests the need for a more balanced approach that strategically combines LLM and traditional low-cost non-LLM methods. In addition, whether LLMs can accurately assess the boundaries of their capabilities, adaptively avoid inappropriate operations and perform optimal operation allocation are all key issues that need to be further explored.\n\n# The Access Dilemma\n\nemerges as a profound manifestation of digital inequality, where the relationship between resources barriers and benefits creates a negative feedback loop of technological disparity: limited computational resources lead to both higher operational costs and reduced capabilities but also experience disproportionate penalties when attempting to optimize their systems.", "mimetype": "text/plain", "start_char_idx": 53973, "end_char_idx": 56337, "metadata_seperator": "\n", "text_template": "[Excerpt from document]\n{metadata_str}\nExcerpt:\n-----\n{content}\n-----\n", "class_name": "TextNode"}, "__type__": "1"}, {"__data__": {"id_": "fef2fd32-473a-4b2a-acd2-87013a3fbede", "embedding": null, "metadata": {"file_path": "C:\\Users\\anteb\\PycharmProjects\\JupyterProject\\naive_rag\\data\\ai_articles\\ADDRESSING THE SUSTAINABLE AI TRILEMMA A CASE STUDY ON LLM AGENTS AND RAG.md", "file_name": "ADDRESSING THE SUSTAINABLE AI TRILEMMA A CASE STUDY ON LLM AGENTS AND RAG.md", "file_type": "text/markdown", "file_size": 72913, "creation_date": "2025-03-26", "last_modified_date": "2025-03-22", "document_title": "\"Navigating the Sustainable AI Trilemma: Balancing Energy Efficiency, Digital Equity, and Capability in Large Language Models and AI Agent Design\"", "section_summary": "The section discusses the challenges and implications of developing sustainable AI systems, particularly focusing on large language models (LLMs) and AI agents. Key topics include:\n\n1. **Technological Poverty Trap**: The text highlights how efforts to improve AI accessibility, such as model quantization and retrieval-augmented generation (RAG) optimization, may inadvertently widen the capability gap between resource-rich and resource-poor communities, hindering AI democratization.\n\n2. **Sustainability Challenges**: It emphasizes the interconnected nature of sustainability challenges in AI development, indicating that addressing one aspect can have ripple effects across technical, social, and environmental dimensions.\n\n3. **Holistic Approach**: The authors advocate for a holistic approach to solution design that balances energy efficiency, digital equity, and capability in AI systems.\n\n4. **Future Research Directions**: The work aims to develop practical frameworks for sustainable AI practices that minimize environmental impact while maintaining the utility of LLM agents, particularly in resource-constrained environments.\n\n5. **Acknowledgments**: The authors acknowledge support from the NVIDIA Academic Grant Program and funding from the ARIA Safeguarded AI TA3 program.\n\n6. **References**: The section includes references to relevant literature on the carbon footprint of machine learning and strategies for reducing the carbon impact of generative AI.\n\nOverall, the section underscores the importance of responsible AI design and policy development to ensure equitable deployment and long-term sustainability of AI technologies."}, "excluded_embed_metadata_keys": ["file_name", "file_type", "file_size", "creation_date", "last_modified_date", "last_accessed_date"], "excluded_llm_metadata_keys": ["file_name", "file_type", "file_size", "creation_date", "last_modified_date", "last_accessed_date"], "relationships": {"1": {"node_id": "5bbe2f3b-a5cf-473e-9e50-1d182dcad9fb", "node_type": "4", "metadata": {"file_path": "C:\\Users\\anteb\\PycharmProjects\\JupyterProject\\naive_rag\\data\\ai_articles\\ADDRESSING THE SUSTAINABLE AI TRILEMMA A CASE STUDY ON LLM AGENTS AND RAG.md", "file_name": "ADDRESSING THE SUSTAINABLE AI TRILEMMA A CASE STUDY ON LLM AGENTS AND RAG.md", "file_type": "text/markdown", "file_size": 72913, "creation_date": "2025-03-26", "last_modified_date": "2025-03-22"}, "hash": "47c48bbdebdc34c45547d1ddc9efb86758d4c6bea59cc0a6ae9786ef666e699e", "class_name": "RelatedNodeInfo"}, "2": {"node_id": "7e0f8b69-1001-45b1-b63a-b54b69fd3f76", "node_type": "1", "metadata": {"file_path": "C:\\Users\\anteb\\PycharmProjects\\JupyterProject\\naive_rag\\data\\ai_articles\\ADDRESSING THE SUSTAINABLE AI TRILEMMA A CASE STUDY ON LLM AGENTS AND RAG.md", "file_name": "ADDRESSING THE SUSTAINABLE AI TRILEMMA A CASE STUDY ON LLM AGENTS AND RAG.md", "file_type": "text/markdown", "file_size": 72913, "creation_date": "2025-03-26", "last_modified_date": "2025-03-22"}, "hash": "55a88deac5a25a317dd0addf9caecb9015573593347817f536864844b2ec97b9", "class_name": "RelatedNodeInfo"}, "3": {"node_id": "a3adbf11-dc19-4dc5-a339-2344c4f20fe0", "node_type": "1", "metadata": {}, "hash": "4b37a1961d8819baaf15b2cb37428aa48f046a724dffd8196f23acaf87b4193a", "class_name": "RelatedNodeInfo"}}, "metadata_template": "{key}: {value}", "metadata_separator": "\n", "text": "This troubling \"technological poverty trap\" creates a fundamental barrier to AI democratization, where the attempts to improve accessibility through techniques like model quantization and RAG optimization may widen the capability gap between resource-abundant and resource-constrained deployments, effectively preventing disadvantaged communities from realizing the transformative potential of AI technologies. This pattern suggests that the current trajectory of AI development may inadvertently amplify existing digital divide rather than bridge them.\n\nThis work has highlighted the complex, interconnected challenges in developing sustainable AI systems, particularly for LLM agents. Our findings demonstrate that addressing any single aspect of sustainability often creates ripple effects across technical, social, and environmental dimensions, necessitating a holistic approach to solution design. Looking ahead, our research will focus on developing practical frameworks for sustainable AI practices that can effectively reduce environmental impact while preserving the utility of LLM agents. Through continued investigation of responsible AI design principles and their implementation, we aim to enable the equitable deployment of these systems across diverse contexts, including resource-constrained environments. This future direction emphasizes the critical balance between advancing AI capabilities and ensuring their long-term sustainability through coordinated technical innovation and thoughtful policy development.\n\n# Acknowledgments\n\nWe would like to thank the NVIDIA Academic Grant Program Award for providing computing resources. This work has also been partially funded by the ARIA Safeguarded AI TA3 programme.\n\n# References\n\n1. David Patterson et al. \u201cThe Carbon Footprint of Machine Learning Training Will Plateau, Then Shrink\u201d. In: Computer 55.7 (2022), pp. 18\u201328. DOI: 10.1109/MC.2022.3148714.\n2. Andrew A Chien et al. \u201cReducing the Carbon Impact of Generative AI Inference (today and in 2035)\u201d. In: Proceedings of the 2nd Workshop on Sustainable Computer Systems. HotCarbon \u201923. Boston, MA, USA: Association for Computing Machinery, 2023. DOI: 10.1145/3604930.3605705.\n3.", "mimetype": "text/plain", "start_char_idx": 56338, "end_char_idx": 58534, "metadata_seperator": "\n", "text_template": "[Excerpt from document]\n{metadata_str}\nExcerpt:\n-----\n{content}\n-----\n", "class_name": "TextNode"}, "__type__": "1"}, {"__data__": {"id_": "a3adbf11-dc19-4dc5-a339-2344c4f20fe0", "embedding": null, "metadata": {"file_path": "C:\\Users\\anteb\\PycharmProjects\\JupyterProject\\naive_rag\\data\\ai_articles\\ADDRESSING THE SUSTAINABLE AI TRILEMMA A CASE STUDY ON LLM AGENTS AND RAG.md", "file_name": "ADDRESSING THE SUSTAINABLE AI TRILEMMA A CASE STUDY ON LLM AGENTS AND RAG.md", "file_type": "text/markdown", "file_size": 72913, "creation_date": "2025-03-26", "last_modified_date": "2025-03-22", "document_title": "\"Navigating the Sustainable AI Trilemma: Balancing Energy Efficiency, Digital Equity, and Capability in Large Language Models and AI Agent Design\"", "section_summary": "The section discusses various scholarly articles and studies related to the sustainable development of artificial intelligence (AI), particularly focusing on large language models (LLMs) and AI agents. Key topics include:\n\n1. **Energy Efficiency in AI**: The energy consumption of AI inference and its implications for sustainability are explored, highlighting the need to balance performance with energy use.\n\n2. **Life-Cycle Impact**: Research on the life-cycle energy and carbon footprints of LLM-powered chatbots emphasizes the importance of mitigating environmental impacts.\n\n3. **Global Trade-offs**: The concept of the \"Triple Bottom Line\" is examined, addressing the trade-offs between economic prosperity, social inequality, and environmental sustainability.\n\n4. **Emergence of AI Agents**: The rise of LLM-based agents is surveyed, indicating their potential and the challenges they present.\n\n5. **Ethical Considerations**: The need for evaluations of sustainability, privacy, digital equity, and ethics in AI technologies, particularly in the context of tools like ChatGPT, is highlighted.\n\nEntities mentioned include:\n- Authors and researchers such as Radosvet Desislavov, Fernando Mart\u00ednez-Plumed, Jos\u00e9 Hern\u00e1ndez-Orallo, and others.\n- Journals and publications like \"Sustainable Computing: Informatics and Systems,\" \"World Development,\" and \"Cognitive Computation.\"\n- DOI links for accessing the studies and articles referenced. \n\nOverall, the section emphasizes the importance of addressing sustainability in AI development while considering ethical and social implications."}, "excluded_embed_metadata_keys": ["file_name", "file_type", "file_size", "creation_date", "last_modified_date", "last_accessed_date"], "excluded_llm_metadata_keys": ["file_name", "file_type", "file_size", "creation_date", "last_modified_date", "last_accessed_date"], "relationships": {"1": {"node_id": "5bbe2f3b-a5cf-473e-9e50-1d182dcad9fb", "node_type": "4", "metadata": {"file_path": "C:\\Users\\anteb\\PycharmProjects\\JupyterProject\\naive_rag\\data\\ai_articles\\ADDRESSING THE SUSTAINABLE AI TRILEMMA A CASE STUDY ON LLM AGENTS AND RAG.md", "file_name": "ADDRESSING THE SUSTAINABLE AI TRILEMMA A CASE STUDY ON LLM AGENTS AND RAG.md", "file_type": "text/markdown", "file_size": 72913, "creation_date": "2025-03-26", "last_modified_date": "2025-03-22"}, "hash": "47c48bbdebdc34c45547d1ddc9efb86758d4c6bea59cc0a6a