\u2019s Argument Against the Ruling of a Trial Court|\n|19|Document Terms and Conditions Regarding Business Transactions|\n|20|Mutual Insurance Policies for Vehicle and Individual Coverage|\n|21|Accident resulting from driver error on public roadways leads to legal consequences|\n|22|Court proceedings involving criminal trials and testimonial evidence|\n|23|Civil Litigation Proceedings and Trials|\n\n# 4.3 Knowledge Graph\n\nThe four data parts, 265 constitutional provisions, 28,251 Statute sections, 5,727 Supreme Court cases, and 10,072 Court of Appeals cases, were inserted into the neo4j knowledge graph.", "mimetype": "text/plain", "start_char_idx": 26764, "end_char_idx": 28738, "metadata_seperator": "\n", "text_template": "[Excerpt from document]\n{metadata_str}\nExcerpt:\n-----\n{content}\n-----\n", "class_name": "TextNode"}, "__type__": "1"}, {"__data__": {"id_": "a6d844fb-6559-452c-8226-a896caff5aa4", "embedding": null, "metadata": {"file_path": "C:\\Users\\anteb\\PycharmProjects\\JupyterProject\\naive_rag\\data\\ai_articles\\Bridging Legal Knowledge and AI.md", "file_name": "Bridging Legal Knowledge and AI.md", "file_type": "text/markdown", "file_size": 52048, "creation_date": "2025-03-26", "last_modified_date": "2025-03-22", "document_title": "\"Transforming Legal Research and Decision-Making: A Comprehensive Framework Integrating Retrieval-Augmented Generation, Knowledge Graphs, Semantic Understanding, and Advanced Machine Learning Techniques\"", "section_summary": "The section discusses the integration of advanced technologies in legal research and decision-making, focusing on a framework that utilizes Retrieval-Augmented Generation, Knowledge Graphs, Semantic Understanding, and Machine Learning techniques. Key topics include:\n\n1. **Graph Structure**: The section presents a table (Table 5) detailing the number of nodes and edges in a legal knowledge graph, highlighting various node types and their associated legal citations.\n\n2. **Node Types and Statistics**: The table outlines different node types such as NMFk Topics, NMFk Keywords, BOW Vocabulary, Constitution, Statutes, Supreme Court Cases, and Court of Appeals Cases, along with their respective counts of nodes, out edges, and legal citations.\n\n3. **Legal Document Decomposition**: It describes the hierarchical decomposition of legal documents, noting that the Constitution had limited documents for deeper analysis compared to other sources.\n\n4. **Named Entity Extraction**: The section explains how a language model (chat-gpt-3.5-turbo) was used to extract legal citations from text, functioning as a named entity extractor.\n\n5. **Query Cross Comparison**: It mentions the formulation of questions to query legal concepts from language model channels, indicating a structured approach to information retrieval.\n\nOverall, the section emphasizes the use of machine learning and natural language processing in enhancing legal research capabilities."}, "excluded_embed_metadata_keys": ["file_name", "file_type", "file_size", "creation_date", "last_modified_date", "last_accessed_date"], "excluded_llm_metadata_keys": ["file_name", "file_type", "file_size", "creation_date", "last_modified_date", "last_accessed_date"], "relationships": {"1": {"node_id": "1d3f5bd7-239e-43d2-ae7f-1e7cd01c88fb", "node_type": "4", "metadata": {"file_path": "C:\\Users\\anteb\\PycharmProjects\\JupyterProject\\naive_rag\\data\\ai_articles\\Bridging Legal Knowledge and AI.md", "file_name": "Bridging Legal Knowledge and AI.md", "file_type": "text/markdown", "file_size": 52048, "creation_date": "2025-03-26", "last_modified_date": "2025-03-22"}, "hash": "8b9a69e79061b64e3a9958d47ca5223db6e2ffd6fcab4d00822647d6e5777090", "class_name": "RelatedNodeInfo"}, "2": {"node_id": "62b965b7-27aa-49ce-b463-4573f7022841", "node_type": "1", "metadata": {"file_path": "C:\\Users\\anteb\\PycharmProjects\\JupyterProject\\naive_rag\\data\\ai_articles\\Bridging Legal Knowledge and AI.md", "file_name": "Bridging Legal Knowledge and AI.md", "file_type": "text/markdown", "file_size": 52048, "creation_date": "2025-03-26", "last_modified_date": "2025-03-22"}, "hash": "874ddedfa5d22a929b9591aab3a888671fa0b6f30d5e32648f0bbbc8c037605c", "class_name": "RelatedNodeInfo"}, "3": {"node_id": "56b83a38-6a6f-4133-8bf3-a44b55b7492f", "node_type": "1", "metadata": {}, "hash": "9b07c9314b2772018044734b2946abc411128310e6f87eca9a43883371602caa", "class_name": "RelatedNodeInfo"}}, "metadata_template": "{key}: {value}", "metadata_separator": "\n", "text": "The graph\u2019s number of nodes and edges can be seen in Table 5, where edge counts are where the triplet\u2019s tail originates with the row\u2019s node. The legal citations were collected by iterating the text of each case or section into chat-gpt-3.5-turbo with the following prompt: \"You\n---\n# ICAIL 2025, June 16\u201320, 2024, Chicago, IL, USA\n\n# Barron et al.\n\n|Node Type|Nodes|Out Edges|Legal Cites|\n|---|---|---|---|\n|NMFk Topics|2,469|92,634|\u2013|\n|NMFk Keyword|11,076|8,281,843|\u2013|\n|BOW Vocabulary|132,423|\u2013|\u2013|\n|Constitution|265|9,067|41|\n|Statute|28,251|1,930,707|81,353|\n|Supreme Court Case|5,727|2,437,161|76,478|\n|Court of Appeals Case|10,072|4,176,288|131,230|\n|Total Unique|190,283|16,927,700|289,102|\n\n# Figure 3: Legal Documents from New Mexico hierarchically decomposed.\n\nThe Constitution only had enough documents to decompose the first depths, whereas the other three sources continued to the terminal depth of 2 (a hyper-parameter of decomposition). Each H-cluster has a natural language label, where depth-0 from each can be seen in Tables 1, 2, 4, and 3.\n\n# Figure 4: Examination of \u2018Estoppel\u2019 relating to being a key-word in topics, vs bag of word vocabulary\n\nare an expert legal document analyzer. Your job is to find all references to the Constitution, Case Law, or Statutes in the text. The result was that the LLM acted like a named entity extractor, such that any citations in the text were pulled out in an enumerated list.\n\n# 4.4 Query Cross Comparison\n\nQuestions were formulated to query information about legal concepts from LLM channels. There were five questions from each.", "mimetype": "text/plain", "start_char_idx": 28739, "end_char_idx": 30326, "metadata_seperator": "\n", "text_template": "[Excerpt from document]\n{metadata_str}\nExcerpt:\n-----\n{content}\n-----\n", "class_name": "TextNode"}, "__type__": "1"}, {"__data__": {"id_": "56b83a38-6a6f-4133-8bf3-a44b55b7492f", "embedding": null, "metadata": {"file_path": "C:\\Users\\anteb\\PycharmProjects\\JupyterProject\\naive_rag\\data\\ai_articles\\Bridging Legal Knowledge and AI.md", "file_name": "Bridging Legal Knowledge and AI.md", "file_type": "text/markdown", "file_size": 52048, "creation_date": "2025-03-26", "last_modified_date": "2025-03-22", "document_title": "\"Transforming Legal Research and Decision-Making: A Comprehensive Framework Integrating Retrieval-Augmented Generation, Knowledge Graphs, Semantic Understanding, and Advanced Machine Learning Techniques\"", "section_summary": "The section discusses the comparison of responses from various language models (LLMs) regarding legal concepts, specifically focusing on the query cross-comparison of legal information. Key topics include:\n\n1. **Query Cross Comparison**: The section outlines the formulation of questions aimed at extracting information about legal concepts from different LLMs.\n\n2. **Legal Research Framework**: It emphasizes the integration of Retrieval-Augmented Generation, Knowledge Graphs, and advanced machine learning techniques in legal research.\n\n3. **Case Analysis**: The excerpt provides statistics on cases mentioning 'Habeas Corpus,' detailing counts from different time periods (Pre-1950, 1950-2000, 2001-Present) and highlighting the total of over 1,300 cases.\n\n4. **Model Performance**: It compares the capabilities of various models, including GPT-4 API, ChatGPT, Gemini, and nemotron:70b-instruct-q8_0, in retrieving and processing legal data. Notably, it points out the limitations of some models in accessing historical databases and the potential for hallucination in the outputs of offline models.\n\n5. **Legal Citations Inquiry**: A specific question regarding common citations in New Mexico Court of Appeals cases related to 'malpractice' is presented, showcasing the limitations of GPT-4 API in accessing specific databases.\n\nOverall, the section highlights the challenges and differences in the performance of LLMs in legal research and data retrieval."}, "excluded_embed_metadata_keys": ["file_name", "file_type", "file_size", "creation_date", "last_modified_date", "last_accessed_date"], "excluded_llm_metadata_keys": ["file_name", "file_type", "file_size", "creation_date", "last_modified_date", "last_accessed_date"], "relationships": {"1": {"node_id": "1d3f5bd7-239e-43d2-ae7f-1e7cd01c88fb", "node_type": "4", "metadata": {"file_path": "C:\\Users\\anteb\\PycharmProjects\\JupyterProject\\naive_rag\\data\\ai_articles\\Bridging Legal Knowledge and AI.md", "file_name": "Bridging Legal Knowledge and AI.md", "file_type": "text/markdown", "file_size": 52048, "creation_date": "2025-03-26", "last_modified_date": "2025-03-22"}, "hash": "8b9a69e79061b64e3a9958d47ca5223db6e2ffd6fcab4d00822647d6e5777090", "class_name": "RelatedNodeInfo"}, "2": {"node_id": "a6d844fb-6559-452c-8226-a896caff5aa4", "node_type": "1", "metadata": {"file_path": "C:\\Users\\anteb\\PycharmProjects\\JupyterProject\\naive_rag\\data\\ai_articles\\Bridging Legal Knowledge and AI.md", "file_name": "Bridging Legal Knowledge and AI.md", "file_type": "text/markdown", "file_size": 52048, "creation_date": "2025-03-26", "last_modified_date": "2025-03-22"}, "hash": "f7229b17dc3801155ff1a387dce9dabf427877e447a16eee9e008ba80611b209", "class_name": "RelatedNodeInfo"}, "3": {"node_id": "3fc79f36-7ae8-4a21-bbf9-6cf4f4ff4bcd", "node_type": "1", "metadata": {}, "hash": "ae82f7373844403f96f57459da6c7856882de44b0760c466b531325519502f93", "class_name": "RelatedNodeInfo"}}, "metadata_template": "{key}: {value}", "metadata_separator": "\n", "text": "# 4.4 Query Cross Comparison\n\nQuestions were formulated to query information about legal concepts from LLM channels. There were five questions from each.\n---\n# Bridging Legal Knowledge and AI:\n\n# Retrieval-Augmented Generation with Vector Stores, Knowledge Graphs, and Hierarchical Non-negative Matrix Factorization\n\n# ICAIL 2025, June 16\u201320, 2024, Chicago, IL, USA\n\n100% Attempted Accuracy across the selected databases: Approximate Number of Cases Mentioning 'Habeas Corpus': Over 1,300 ... Breakdown ...:\n\n- Pre-1950: 450\n- 1950-2000: 550\n- 2001-Present: 300\n\nThe responses highlight significant differences in model abilities. GPT-4 API and ChatGPT provided an understanding of their limits to offer real-time or exact counts due to limitations such as access to historical databases or the complexity of case records. Gemini followed a similar pattern, explaining why determining a precise number is challenging. In contrast, our system directly reported a specific count, stating 215 cases mentioning 'Habeas Corpus,' showing its ability to process and retrieve meaningful data. Meanwhile, nemotron:70b-instruct-q8_0 provided a detailed method to find the correct answer in an open context. It even went into breaking the count into approximate ranges across different periods, suggesting over 1,300 cases in total. However, these counts from Nemotron are part of an extreme hallucination since it was an offline model without the ability to make any lookups.\n\n# (b) Comparison 2 - Legal Citations:\n\n\u2022 Question: \u201cWhat are common citations among New Mexico Court of Appeals cases that mention \u2018malpractice\u2019?\u201d\n\nGPT-4,API: I don\u2019t have direct access to specific databases or the ability to browse the internet.", "mimetype": "text/plain", "start_char_idx": 30173, "end_char_idx": 31886, "metadata_seperator": "\n", "text_template": "[Excerpt from document]\n{metadata_str}\nExcerpt:\n-----\n{content}\n-----\n", "class_name": "TextNode"}, "__type__": "1"}, {"__data__": {"id_": "3fc79f36-7ae8-4a21-bbf9-6cf4f4ff4bcd", "embedding": null, "metadata": {"file_path": "C:\\Users\\anteb\\PycharmProjects\\JupyterProject\\naive_rag\\data\\ai_articles\\Bridging Legal Knowledge and AI.md", "file_name": "Bridging Legal Knowledge and AI.md", "file_type": "text/markdown", "file_size": 52048, "creation_date": "2025-03-26", "last_modified_date": "2025-03-22", "document_title": "\"Transforming Legal Research and Decision-Making: A Comprehensive Framework Integrating Retrieval-Augmented Generation, Knowledge Graphs, Semantic Understanding, and Advanced Machine Learning Techniques\"", "section_summary": "The section discusses legal principles and precedents related to malpractice cases in the New Mexico Court of Appeals. Key topics include:\n\n1. **Legal Principles and Precedents**: \n   - Elements of Legal Malpractice (Mireles v. Broderick, 1992)\n   - Statute of Limitations (Sharts v. Natelson, 1994)\n   - Expert Testimony Requirement (Rummell v. Edgemont Realty Partners, 1993)\n   - Causation and \"Case Within a Case\" Doctrine (Garcia v. Rodey, Dickason, Sloan, Akin & Robb, 1988)\n   - Damages (Sanders, Bruin, Coll & Worley, 2010)\n\n2. **Common Citations**: \n   - Various statutes and cases frequently cited in malpractice cases, including:\n     - NMSA 41-5-1 (36 cases)\n     - NMSA 41-5-13 (35 cases)\n     - NMSA 41-4-1 (23 cases)\n     - CERVANTES v FORBIS (1964) (18 cases)\n     - NMSA 37-1-8 (15 cases)\n     - Other statutes and cases with varying citation frequencies.\n\n3. **Additional Cases Mentioned**: \n   - Leyba v. Whitley (1995)\n   - Rancho del v. Weisfeld (1995)\n   - Bassett v. Sheehan, Sheehan & Stelzner (2008)\n   - Akutagawa v. Laflin, Pick & Heer (2005)\n\n4. **Steps to Find Common Citations**: \n   - A brief outline of steps to access legal databases and construct search queries to find common citations in malpractice cases.\n\nOverall, the section provides a comprehensive overview of legal malpractice in New Mexico, highlighting significant cases, statutes, and methodologies for legal research."}, "excluded_embed_metadata_keys": ["file_name", "file_type", "file_size", "creation_date", "last_modified_date", "last_accessed_date"], "excluded_llm_metadata_keys": ["file_name", "file_type", "file_size", "creation_date", "last_modified_date", "last_accessed_date"], "relationships": {"1": {"node_id": "1d3f5bd7-239e-43d2-ae7f-1e7cd01c88fb", "node_type": "4", "metadata": {"file_path": "C:\\Users\\anteb\\PycharmProjects\\JupyterProject\\naive_rag\\data\\ai_articles\\Bridging Legal Knowledge and AI.md", "file_name": "Bridging Legal Knowledge and AI.md", "file_type": "text/markdown", "file_size": 52048, "creation_date": "2025-03-26", "last_modified_date": "2025-03-22"}, "hash": "8b9a69e79061b64e3a9958d47ca5223db6e2ffd6fcab4d00822647d6e5777090", "class_name": "RelatedNodeInfo"}, "2": {"node_id": "56b83a38-6a6f-4133-8bf3-a44b55b7492f", "node_type": "1", "metadata": {"file_path": "C:\\Users\\anteb\\PycharmProjects\\JupyterProject\\naive_rag\\data\\ai_articles\\Bridging Legal Knowledge and AI.md", "file_name": "Bridging Legal Knowledge and AI.md", "file_type": "text/markdown", "file_size": 52048, "creation_date": "2025-03-26", "last_modified_date": "2025-03-22"}, "hash": "ae5eadc976b460a60ce0d810ad1d9e345c498b510dd8ef3b8a73697e76e61b9e", "class_name": "RelatedNodeInfo"}, "3": {"node_id": "5f0fd656-5d5e-472d-9273-7ff312c19684", "node_type": "1", "metadata": {}, "hash": "a9428f654095caa57ab8eec7b3eabeb31a29a7254acd418f9f650edbd8198cbd", "class_name": "RelatedNodeInfo"}}, "metadata_template": "{key}: {value}", "metadata_separator": "\n", "text": "GPT-4o,ChatGPT: In New Mexico Court of Appeals cases addressing malpractice, several key legal principles and precedents are frequently cited:\n\n- Elements of Legal Malpractice: Mireles v. Broderick, 1992\n- Statute of Limitations: Sharts v. Natelson, 1994\n- Expert Testimony Requirement: Rummell v. Edgemont Realty Partners, 1993\n- Causation and \"Case Within a Case\" Doctrine: Garcia v. Rodey, Dickason, Sloan, Akin & Robb, 1988\n- Damages: Sanders, Bruin, Coll & Worley, 2010\n\nSmart-Slic: The common citations among New Mexico Court of Appeals cases that mention 'malpractice' include:\n\n- NMSA 41-5-1 with 36 cases\n- NMSA 41-5-13 with 35 cases\n- NMSA 41-4-1 with 23 cases\n- CERVANTES v FORBIS 1964 with 18 cases\n- NMSA 37-1-8 with 15 cases\n- NMSA 41-5-3 and NMSA 41-5-15 each with 14 cases\n- NMSA 41-5-6 with 13 cases\n- GOODMAN v BROCK 1972 with 12 cases\n- NMSA 41-5-22 with 10 cases\n\nGemini: Common citations in New Mexico Court of Appeals cases mentioning \"malpractice\" often include:\n\n- Leyba v. Whitley, 1995\n- Rancho del v. Weisfeld, 1995\n- Bassett v. Sheehan, Sheehan & Stelzner, 2008\n- Akutagawa v. Laflin, Pick & Heer, 2005\n\nnemotron:70b-instruct-q8_0: Steps to Find Common Citations:\n\n1. Access Legal Databases:...\n2. Construct Your Search Query:...\n3. Filter Results (if possible):...\n4.", "mimetype": "text/plain", "start_char_idx": 31888, "end_char_idx": 33184, "metadata_seperator": "\n", "text_template": "[Excerpt from document]\n{metadata_str}\nExcerpt:\n-----\n{content}\n-----\n", "class_name": "TextNode"}, "__type__": "1"}, {"__data__": {"id_": "5f0fd656-5d5e-472d-9273-7ff312c19684", "embedding": null, "metadata": {"file_path": "C:\\Users\\anteb\\PycharmProjects\\JupyterProject\\naive_rag\\data\\ai_articles\\Bridging Legal Knowledge and AI.md", "file_name": "Bridging Legal Knowledge and AI.md", "file_type": "text/markdown", "file_size": 52048, "creation_date": "2025-03-26", "last_modified_date": "2025-03-22", "document_title": "\"Transforming Legal Research and Decision-Making: A Comprehensive Framework Integrating Retrieval-Augmented Generation, Knowledge Graphs, Semantic Understanding, and Advanced Machine Learning Techniques\"", "section_summary": "The section discusses the integration of advanced technologies in legal research and decision-making, specifically focusing on Retrieval-Augmented Generation, Knowledge Graphs, and Machine Learning techniques. Key topics include:\n\n1. **Accessing Legal Databases**: Emphasizes the importance of utilizing legal databases for research.\n2. **Constructing Search Queries**: Highlights the need for effective search strategies to retrieve relevant legal information.\n3. **Filtering Results**: Discusses the potential for refining search results to enhance accuracy.\n4. **Analyzing Legal Cases**: Encourages the examination of top cases for citations to support legal arguments.\n\nThe section also compares various AI models' capabilities in providing information about New Mexico Supreme Court cases mentioning \"Habeas Corpus.\" It notes the challenges in obtaining precise counts due to the vast number of cases and the lack of centralized databases. Specific AI models mentioned include GPT-4, Smart-Slic, Gemini, and nemotron, each providing different levels of accuracy and methodology in their responses.\n\nEntities referenced include:\n- New Mexico Supreme Court\n- \"Habeas Corpus\"\n- Various AI models (GPT-4, Smart-Slic, Gemini, nemotron)\n\nThe excerpt concludes with a mention of the ICAIL 2025 conference, indicating ongoing discussions in the field of AI and law."}, "excluded_embed_metadata_keys": ["file_name", "file_type", "file_size", "creation_date", "last_modified_date", "last_accessed_date"], "excluded_llm_metadata_keys": ["file_name", "file_type", "file_size", "creation_date", "last_modified_date", "last_accessed_date"], "relationships": {"1": {"node_id": "1d3f5bd7-239e-43d2-ae7f-1e7cd01c88fb", "node_type": "4", "metadata": {"file_path": "C:\\Users\\anteb\\PycharmProjects\\JupyterProject\\naive_rag\\data\\ai_articles\\Bridging Legal Knowledge and AI.md", "file_name": "Bridging Legal Knowledge and AI.md", "file_type": "text/markdown", "file_size": 52048, "creation_date": "2025-03-26", "last_modified_date": "2025-03-22"}, "hash": "8b9a69e79061b64e3a9958d47ca5223db6e2ffd6fcab4d00822647d6e5777090", "class_name": "RelatedNodeInfo"}, "2": {"node_id": "3fc79f36-7ae8-4a21-bbf9-6cf4f4ff4bcd", "node_type": "1", "metadata": {"file_path": "C:\\Users\\anteb\\PycharmProjects\\JupyterProject\\naive_rag\\data\\ai_articles\\Bridging Legal Knowledge and AI.md", "file_name": "Bridging Legal Knowledge and AI.md", "file_type": "text/markdown", "file_size": 52048, "creation_date": "2025-03-26", "last_modified_date": "2025-03-22"}, "hash": "554c0cf1e33eae2e52f79428d6e6570a49c4bdd73f36da7b0320f5bffb3bc20b", "class_name": "RelatedNodeInfo"}, "3": {"node_id": "927d70a4-9abb-4312-ae07-ca9bcdae768f", "node_type": "1", "metadata": {}, "hash": "928218ee49b693fa5ba5e2365bc4c733667b55dd06a662d3fb4353bdbe705640", "class_name": "RelatedNodeInfo"}}, "metadata_template": "{key}: {value}", "metadata_separator": "\n", "text": "Access Legal Databases:...\n2. Construct Your Search Query:...\n3. Filter Results (if possible):...\n4. Analyze Top Cases for Citations: ... Please note, the following responses are hypothetical examples...\n\nSome models were given points when they produced close numeric estimates, even if derived through flawed \u201cdatabase lookups.\u201d In contrast, our system provides traceable reasoning by referencing the KG and analyzing the decomposed hierarchical structure of the texts themselves.\n\n# Comparison 1 - Quantities:\n\n\u2022 Question: \u201cHow many New Mexico Supreme Court cases mention \u2018Habeas Corpus\u2019?\u201d\n\nGPT-4,API: I don\u2019t have the ability to provide real-time or exact counts of Supreme Court cases mentioning \"habeas corpus\" up to the current date...\n\nGPT-4o,ChatGPT: Determining the exact number of New Mexico Supreme Court cases that mention 'habeas corpus' is challenging due to the extensive history of the court\u2019s decisions and the limitations of available databases...\n\nSmart-Slic: There are 215 Supreme Court cases that mention 'Habeas Corpus.'\n\nGemini: The exact number of Supreme Court cases that mention \"Habeas Corpus\" is difficult to determine precisely. Here\u2019s why:\n\n- Vast Number of Cases:\n- Varied Mention:\n- No Centralized Database:\n\nnemotron:70b-instruct-q8_0: I\u2019ll outline my research methodology and then share the results. Research Methodology:\n\n1. Database Selection:...\n2. Search Parameters:...\n3. Search Approach: ...\n---\n# ICAIL 2025, June 16\u201320, 2024, Chicago, IL, USA\n\nBarron et al. and filtering results. However, the examples provided, such as \"Smith v. Jones, 2010,\" were explicitly hypothetical, undermining the credibility of the response. Additionally, nemotron\u2019s inclusion of actual statutes like NMSA 41-5-1 blurred the line between hypothetical examples and genuine analysis, leading to potential confusion.", "mimetype": "text/plain", "start_char_idx": 33084, "end_char_idx": 34917, "metadata_seperator": "\n", "text_template": "[Excerpt from document]\n{metadata_str}\nExcerpt:\n-----\n{content}\n-----\n", "class_name": "TextNode"}, "__type__": "1"}, {"__data__": {"id_": "927d70a4-9abb-4312-ae07-ca9bcdae768f", "embedding": null, "metadata": {"file_path": "C:\\Users\\anteb\\PycharmProjects\\JupyterProject\\naive_rag\\data\\ai_articles\\Bridging Legal Knowledge and AI.md", "file_name": "Bridging Legal Knowledge and AI.md", "file_type": "text/markdown", "file_size": 52048, "creation_date": "2025-03-26", "last_modified_date": "2025-03-22", "document_title": "\"Transforming Legal Research and Decision-Making: A Comprehensive Framework Integrating Retrieval-Augmented Generation, Knowledge Graphs, Semantic Understanding, and Advanced Machine Learning Techniques\"", "section_summary": "The section discusses the integration of advanced AI techniques in legal research and decision-making, specifically focusing on the QA Smart-Slic system. It highlights the strengths and weaknesses of various AI models, such as nemotron, GPT-4o, and Gemini, in summarizing legal principles and data retrieval. The excerpt includes two detailed case studies demonstrating the practical applications of the QA Smart-Slic system:\n\n1. **Case Study 1 - Constitutional Analysis**: Examines the process regarding what happens to a bill if the governor neither signs nor returns it within a specified timeframe, referencing Article IV, Section 22.\n\n2. **Case Study 2 - Statutory Analysis**: Discusses the accrual and utilization of earned sick leave under the Healthy Workplaces Act, detailing how employees earn sick leave, its usage, and provisions for carryover and reinstatement after employment separation.\n\nKey entities mentioned include:\n- Nemotron\n- GPT-4o\n- Gemini\n- NMSA 41-5-1 (statute)\n- Article IV, Section 22 (constitutional provision)\n- Healthy Workplaces Act (statutory provision)"}, "excluded_embed_metadata_keys": ["file_name", "file_type", "file_size", "creation_date", "last_modified_date", "last_accessed_date"], "excluded_llm_metadata_keys": ["file_name", "file_type", "file_size", "creation_date", "last_modified_date", "last_accessed_date"], "relationships": {"1": {"node_id": "1d3f5bd7-239e-43d2-ae7f-1e7cd01c88fb", "node_type": "4", "metadata": {"file_path": "C:\\Users\\anteb\\PycharmProjects\\JupyterProject\\naive_rag\\data\\ai_articles\\Bridging Legal Knowledge and AI.md", "file_name": "Bridging Legal Knowledge and AI.md", "file_type": "text/markdown", "file_size": 52048, "creation_date": "2025-03-26", "last_modified_date": "2025-03-22"}, "hash": "8b9a69e79061b64e3a9958d47ca5223db6e2ffd6fcab4d00822647d6e5777090", "class_name": "RelatedNodeInfo"}, "2": {"node_id": "5f0fd656-5d5e-472d-9273-7ff312c19684", "node_type": "1", "metadata": {"file_path": "C:\\Users\\anteb\\PycharmProjects\\JupyterProject\\naive_rag\\data\\ai_articles\\Bridging Legal Knowledge and AI.md", "file_name": "Bridging Legal Knowledge and AI.md", "file_type": "text/markdown", "file_size": 52048, "creation_date": "2025-03-26", "last_modified_date": "2025-03-22"}, "hash": "f03e4e6674a1ca12d3d395cf7f87d6e866237efa83a14dd5a5f913a37b3f5ee6", "class_name": "RelatedNodeInfo"}, "3": {"node_id": "c57f0bbc-3cce-4c9e-8aaf-33ffe0f622cb", "node_type": "1", "metadata": {}, "hash": "4f80c5ca412effddd0e0b787e3fba4131355909b01c345adf97c1dc043e0a853", "class_name": "RelatedNodeInfo"}}, "metadata_template": "{key}: {value}", "metadata_separator": "\n", "text": "Additionally, nemotron\u2019s inclusion of actual statutes like NMSA 41-5-1 blurred the line between hypothetical examples and genuine analysis, leading to potential confusion. This comparison highlights the strengths and weaknesses of each system: some models are better at summarizing legal principles, such as GPT-4o and Gemini, while our System shows the capability for precise data retrieval and analysis. Nemotron, despite its detailed methodology, suffered most from hallucinations overall.\n\n# 4.5.1 Case Studies\n\nTo illustrate the practical applications and effectiveness of the QA Smart-Slic system, we present four detailed case studies:\n\n# (a) Case Study 1 - Constitutional Analysis:\n\n- Question Source: Article IV, Section 22 [1]\n- Question: \u201cWhat happens to a bill if the governor neither returns it within the specified three-day window (Sundays excepted) nor signs it?\u201d\n- Response: According to Article IV - Legislative Department \u2013 \u00a7 22 \"Governor\u2019s approval or veto of bills\" of the provided text, if a bill is presented to the governor and he neither returns it within the specified three-day window (Sundays excepted) nor signs it, then the bill becomes a law automatically, whether signed by him or not, unless the legislature by adjournment prevents such return.\n\n# (b) Case Study 2 - Statutory Analysis:\n\n- Question Source: Chapter 50, Article 17, Section 3 [4]\n- Question: \u201cUnder the Healthy Workplaces Act, how does an employee accrue and utilize earned sick leave, and what provisions exist for its carryover, use for family or personal health, and reinstatement after separation from employment?\u201d\n- Response: Accrue: Employees accrue a minimum of one hour of earned sick leave for every thirty hours worked. Employers may elect to provide the full sixty-four hours of earned sick leave for the year on January 1 or pro-rata for mid-year hires.\n- Use: Earned sick leave can be used for the employee\u2019s or their family members\u2019 illness, injury, health condition, medical care, or preventive medical care. It can also be used for issues related to domestic abuse, sexual assault, or stalking.", "mimetype": "text/plain", "start_char_idx": 34746, "end_char_idx": 36854, "metadata_seperator": "\n", "text_template": "[Excerpt from document]\n{metadata_str}\nExcerpt:\n-----\n{content}\n-----\n", "class_name": "TextNode"}, "__type__": "1"}, {"__data__": {"id_": "c57f0bbc-3cce-4c9e-8aaf-33ffe0f622cb", "embedding": null, "metadata": {"file_path": "C:\\Users\\anteb\\PycharmProjects\\JupyterProject\\naive_rag\\data\\ai_articles\\Bridging Legal Knowledge and AI.md", "file_name": "Bridging Legal Knowledge and AI.md", "file_type": "text/markdown", "file_size": 52048, "creation_date": "2025-03-26", "last_modified_date": "2025-03-22", "document_title": "\"Transforming Legal Research and Decision-Making: A Comprehensive Framework Integrating Retrieval-Augmented Generation, Knowledge Graphs, Semantic Understanding, and Advanced Machine Learning Techniques\"", "section_summary": "The section discusses various legal topics and case studies related to domestic abuse, sick leave policies, and court rulings on water rights and child support decrees. \n\nKey Topics:\n1. **Domestic Abuse and Legal Protections**: The excerpt mentions the applicability of legal frameworks to issues such as domestic abuse, sexual assault, and stalking.\n2. **Sick Leave Policies**: It outlines the carryover of unused earned sick leave, the cap on usage, and the reinstatement of sick leave for re-hired employees.\n3. **Case Law Analysis**:\n   - **KRM, Inc. v. Caviness (1996)**: Focuses on the court's reasoning regarding water rights not being appurtenant to land sold due to lack of irrigation use.\n   - **Smith v. South (1955)**: Addresses the enforceability of a Washington child support decree in New Mexico and the conflict between the original judgment and post-decree agreements.\n\nEntities:\n- **KRM, Inc.**: A party involved in the water rights case.\n- **Caviness**: The individual who purchased land in the KRM case.\n- **Williams**: The individual associated with the 1963 water rights declaration.\n- **Smith**: A party in the child support case.\n- **South**: The opposing party in the Smith v. South case.\n\nOverall, the section highlights legal principles regarding sick leave, water rights, and child support enforcement, illustrated through specific case studies."}, "excluded_embed_metadata_keys": ["file_name", "file_type", "file_size", "creation_date", "last_modified_date", "last_accessed_date"], "excluded_llm_metadata_keys": ["file_name", "file_type", "file_size", "creation_date", "last_modified_date", "last_accessed_date"], "relationships": {"1": {"node_id": "1d3f5bd7-239e-43d2-ae7f-1e7cd01c88fb", "node_type": "4", "metadata": {"file_path": "C:\\Users\\anteb\\PycharmProjects\\JupyterProject\\naive_rag\\data\\ai_articles\\Bridging Legal Knowledge and AI.md", "file_name": "Bridging Legal Knowledge and AI.md", "file_type": "text/markdown", "file_size": 52048, "creation_date": "2025-03-26", "last_modified_date": "2025-03-22"}, "hash": "8b9a69e79061b64e3a9958d47ca5223db6e2ffd6fcab4d00822647d6e5777090", "class_name": "RelatedNodeInfo"}, "2": {"node_id": "927d70a4-9abb-4312-ae07-ca9bcdae768f", "node_type": "1", "metadata": {"file_path": "C:\\Users\\anteb\\PycharmProjects\\JupyterProject\\naive_rag\\data\\ai_articles\\Bridging Legal Knowledge and AI.md", "file_name": "Bridging Legal Knowledge and AI.md", "file_type": "text/markdown", "file_size": 52048, "creation_date": "2025-03-26", "last_modified_date": "2025-03-22"}, "hash": "a36b177cba0450a2371c0a417257b4c1fb6b28c1e4e0bb720cc2c65dd7611850", "class_name": "RelatedNodeInfo"}, "3": {"node_id": "f25c5f8d-5ebf-4e8e-b06c-ec74bac5959d", "node_type": "1", "metadata": {}, "hash": "6c6e733692be07ce2342375a69fad332487c55997dc8084cc15ed1f69d1d910e", "class_name": "RelatedNodeInfo"}}, "metadata_template": "{key}: {value}", "metadata_separator": "\n", "text": "It can also be used for issues related to domestic abuse, sexual assault, or stalking.\n- Carryover: Unused earned sick leave carries over year to year, although use may be capped at 64 hours per twelve-month period, unless the employer sets a higher cap.\n- Reinstatement: If an employee is rehired within twelve months of separation by the same employer, previously accrued and unused earned sick leave must be reinstated. Additionally, the employee is entitled to accrue additional sick leave upon re-commencement of employment.\n\n# (c) Case Study 3 - Court of Appeals Case Law Analysis:\n\n- Question Source: KRM, Inc. v. Caviness, 1996 [3]\n- Question: \u201cIn the case KRM, Inc. v. Caviness, what was the court\u2019s reasoning for determining that the water rights established by Williams\u2019 1963 declaration did not belong to the land sold to Caviness?\u201d\n- Response: The court determined that the water rights established by Williams\u2019 1963 declaration were not appurtenant to the land sold to Caviness because there was no evidence that the water had been used for irrigation on that land. Since the appropriated water was never used for irrigation purposes on the property, it did not qualify as appurtenant water rights that would automatically transfer with the land. The court concluded because the usage of the water was unrelated to the benefit or use of the land itself, the water rights remained separate and did not pass to Caviness when he purchased the land.\n\n# (d) Case Study 4 - Supreme Court Case Law Analysis:\n\n- Question Source: Smith v. South, 1955 [2]\n- Question: \u201cIn Smith v. South, what were the legal issues raised regarding the enforceability of the Washington child support decree in New Mexico, and how did the court address the conflict between the parties\u2019 post-decree agreement and the original judgment?\u201d\n- Response: The legal issues raised in Smith v. South regarding the enforceability of the Washington child support decree in New Mexico included whether New Mexico courts should acknowledge and enforce the original Washington decree for child support or consider the modifications reportedly agreed upon by the parties after the decree.", "mimetype": "text/plain", "start_char_idx": 36768, "end_char_idx": 38927, "metadata_seperator": "\n", "text_template": "[Excerpt from document]\n{metadata_str}\nExcerpt:\n-----\n{content}\n-----\n", "class_name": "TextNode"}, "__type__": "1"}, {"__data__": {"id_": "f25c5f8d-5ebf-4e8e-b06c-ec74bac5959d", "embedding": null, "metadata": {"file_path": "C:\\Users\\anteb\\PycharmProjects\\JupyterProject\\naive_rag\\data\\ai_articles\\Bridging Legal Knowledge and AI.md", "file_name": "Bridging Legal Knowledge and AI.md", "file_type": "text/markdown", "file_size": 52048, "creation_date": "2025-03-26", "last_modified_date": "2025-03-22", "document_title": "\"Transforming Legal Research and Decision-Making: A Comprehensive Framework Integrating Retrieval-Augmented Generation, Knowledge Graphs, Semantic Understanding, and Advanced Machine Learning Techniques\"", "section_summary": "The section discusses the legal complexities surrounding child support payments following a post-decree agreement between parties in a case involving a Washington court judgment. Key topics include:\n\n1. **Child Support Enforcement**: The court's consideration of jurisdictional and legal principles related to child support enforcement across state lines, particularly under the Uniform Interstate Family Support Act (UIFSA).\n\n2. **Legal Formalization**: The importance of formal judicial processes in modifying child support agreements, emphasizing that informal agreements may not alter the enforceability of the original court decree.\n\n3. **Best Interests of Children**: The court's focus on ensuring that child support arrangements comply with legal standards to serve the best interests of the children involved.\n\n4. **Authority of Original Jurisdiction**: The necessity for any modifications to be legally recognized to respect the authority of the original jurisdiction (Washington).\n\nEntities mentioned include:\n- **Jurisdiction**: Washington and New Mexico.\n- **Legal Framework**: Uniform Interstate Family Support Act (UIFSA).\n- **Court Cases**: References to specific legal cases and constitutional articles relevant to the discussion. \n\nOverall, the section highlights the legal intricacies of child support modifications and the necessity for adherence to formal legal processes."}, "excluded_embed_metadata_keys": ["file_name", "file_type", "file_size", "creation_date", "last_modified_date", "last_accessed_date"], "excluded_llm_metadata_keys": ["file_name", "file_type", "file_size", "creation_date", "last_modified_date", "last_accessed_date"], "relationships": {"1": {"node_id": "1d3f5bd7-239e-43d2-ae7f-1e7cd01c88fb", "node_type": "4", "metadata": {"file_path": "C:\\Users\\anteb\\PycharmProjects\\JupyterProject\\naive_rag\\data\\ai_articles\\Bridging Legal Knowledge and AI.md", "file_name": "Bridging Legal Knowledge and AI.md", "file_type": "text/markdown", "file_size": 52048, "creation_date": "2025-03-26", "last_modified_date": "2025-03-22"}, "hash": "8b9a69e79061b64e3a9958d47ca5223db6e2ffd6fcab4d00822647d6e5777090", "class_name": "RelatedNodeInfo"}, "2": {"node_id": "c57f0bbc-3cce-4c9e-8aaf-33ffe0f622cb", "node_type": "1", "metadata": {"file_path": "C:\\Users\\anteb\\PycharmProjects\\JupyterProject\\naive_rag\\data\\ai_articles\\Bridging Legal Knowledge and AI.md", "file_name": "Bridging Legal Knowledge and AI.md", "file_type": "text/markdown", "file_size": 52048, "creation_date": "2025-03-26", "last_modified_date": "2025-03-22"}, "hash": "6615db082c2bb90387768bb411fa9b4e004f169430fa088fd119adda0a8eb03e", "class_name": "RelatedNodeInfo"}, "3": {"node_id": "b28718bd-ed52-457c-b871-da3d5f21c478", "node_type": "1", "metadata": {}, "hash": "1e45366940ef662232758350089074826089c7626605b34cb966e5c0f17f1d47", "class_name": "RelatedNodeInfo"}}, "metadata_template": "{key}: {value}", "metadata_separator": "\n", "text": "The conflict between the parties\u2019 post-decree agreement and the original judgment involved assertions that they had informally agreed to alter the child support payments stipulated by the Washington court.\n---\n# Bridging Legal Knowledge and AI:\n\n# Retrieval-Augmented Generation with Vector Stores, Knowledge Graphs, and Hierarchical Non-negative Matrix Factorization\n\n# ICAIL 2025, June 16\u201320, 2024, Chicago, IL, USA\n\nThe court addressed this issue by considering the jurisdictional and legal principles guiding child support enforcement across state lines. They emphasized the importance of adhering to the formal judicial process. The court likely examined whether the post-decree modifications had been legally formalized or approved by any court, and whether such modifications complied with the relevant child support enforcement laws, particularly under the Uniform Interstate Family Support Act (UIFSA), though it wasn\u2019t explicitly mentioned in the text snippet. UIFSA provides mechanisms for establishing, enforcing, and modifying child support orders across state boundaries.\n\nIf the modifications were not legally recognized through a court process, the original Washington decree would remain enforceable in New Mexico, and any informal or unratified agreements between the parties would not typically alter the enforceability of the court-ordered decree. The court would focus on ensuring that child support arrangements comply with legal standards to serve the children\u2019s best interests and that enforcement respects the authority of the original jurisdiction (Washington) unless properly modified through legal channels.\n\n# REFERENCES\n\n1. 1953. New Mexico Constitution, Article IV, Section 22. https://law.justia.com/constitution/new-mexico/article-iv/section-22/ Governor\u2019s approval or veto of bills. Amended September 15, 1953.\n2. 1955. Smith v. South. Remanded with instructions to make findings of fact and conclusions of law on default issues.\n3. 1996. KRM, Inc. v. Caviness. Reversed and remanded for further proceedings.\n4. 2021. Employment Law \u2013 Healthy Workplaces \u2013 Earned Sick Leave; Use and Accrual. Effective July 1, 2022.", "mimetype": "text/plain", "start_char_idx": 38928, "end_char_idx": 41077, "metadata_seperator": "\n", "text_template": "[Excerpt from document]\n{metadata_str}\nExcerpt:\n-----\n{content}\n-----\n", "class_name": "TextNode"}, "__type__": "1"}, {"__data__": {"id_": "b28718bd-ed52-457c-b871-da3d5f21c478", "embedding": null, "metadata": {"file_path": "C:\\Users\\anteb\\PycharmProjects\\JupyterProject\\naive_rag\\data\\ai_articles\\Bridging Legal Knowledge and AI.md", "file_name": "Bridging Legal Knowledge and AI.md", "file_type": "text/markdown", "file_size": 52048, "creation_date": "2025-03-26", "last_modified_date": "2025-03-22", "document_title": "\"Transforming Legal Research and Decision-Making: A Comprehensive Framework Integrating Retrieval-Augmented Generation, Knowledge Graphs, Semantic Understanding, and Advanced Machine Learning Techniques\"", "section_summary": "The section primarily discusses various resources and research related to the integration of artificial intelligence (AI) in legal contexts, particularly focusing on legal research and decision-making. Key topics and entities include:\n\n1. **Legal Frameworks and Regulations**: Reference to employment law regarding earned sick leave effective from July 1, 2022.\n\n2. **Legal Information Resources**: Mention of Justia, a platform providing free legal information.\n\n3. **AI Models and Technologies**: Introduction of Google's generative AI model, Gemini, and its relevance to legal applications.\n\n4. **Research Contributions**: \n   - A patent related to source identification using non-negative matrix factorization and semi-supervised clustering.\n   - A book by Kevin D. Ashley discussing AI and legal analytics.\n   - A paper on domain-specific retrieval-augmented generation that utilizes vector stores, knowledge graphs, and tensor factorization.\n   - Research on the Longformer model, which is designed for processing long documents.\n   - A study on learning heterogeneous graph embeddings for legal document similarity.\n\n5. **Academic Publications**: Several references to academic papers and patents that contribute to the understanding and application of AI in the legal field.\n\nOverall, the section highlights the intersection of AI technologies and legal research, showcasing advancements in tools and methodologies that enhance legal practice."}, "excluded_embed_metadata_keys": ["file_name", "file_type", "file_size", "creation_date", "last_modified_date", "last_accessed_date"], "excluded_llm_metadata_keys": ["file_name", "file_type", "file_size", "creation_date", "last_modified_date", "last_accessed_date"], "relationships": {"1": {"node_id": "1d3f5bd7-239e-43d2-ae7f-1e7cd01c88fb", "node_type": "4", "metadata": {"file_path": "C:\\Users\\anteb\\PycharmProjects\\JupyterProject\\naive_rag\\data\\ai_articles\\Bridging Legal Knowledge and AI.md", "file_name": "Bridging Legal Knowledge and AI.md", "file_type": "text/markdown", "file_size": 52048, "creation_date": "2025-03-26", "last_modified_date": "2025-03-22"}, "hash": "8b9a69e79061b64e3a9958d47ca5223db6e2ffd6fcab4d00822647d6e5777090", "class_name": "RelatedNodeInfo"}, "2": {"node_id": "f25c5f8d-5ebf-4e8e-b06c-ec74bac5959d", "node_type": "1", "metadata": {"file_path": "C:\\Users\\anteb\\PycharmProjects\\Jupyt