ilarity. Knowledge-Based Systems 250 (2022), 109046. https://doi.org/10.1016/j.knosys.2022.109046\n12.", "mimetype": "text/plain", "start_char_idx": 40971, "end_char_idx": 42410, "metadata_seperator": "\n", "text_template": "[Excerpt from document]\n{metadata_str}\nExcerpt:\n-----\n{content}\n-----\n", "class_name": "TextNode"}, "__type__": "1"}, {"__data__": {"id_": "7d949dcc-09ab-4548-97bc-fe8c67426d82", "embedding": null, "metadata": {"file_path": "C:\\Users\\anteb\\PycharmProjects\\JupyterProject\\naive_rag\\data\\ai_articles\\Bridging Legal Knowledge and AI.md", "file_name": "Bridging Legal Knowledge and AI.md", "file_type": "text/markdown", "file_size": 52048, "creation_date": "2025-03-26", "last_modified_date": "2025-03-22", "document_title": "\"Transforming Legal Research and Decision-Making: A Comprehensive Framework Integrating Retrieval-Augmented Generation, Knowledge Graphs, Semantic Understanding, and Advanced Machine Learning Techniques\"", "section_summary": "The section primarily discusses advancements in legal research and decision-making through the integration of various technologies and methodologies. Key topics include:\n\n1. **Legal Research Innovations**: The use of advanced techniques such as Retrieval-Augmented Generation, Knowledge Graphs, and Semantic Understanding to enhance legal research processes.\n\n2. **Machine Learning Applications**: The application of advanced machine learning techniques in the legal domain, particularly focusing on how these technologies can improve decision-making.\n\n3. **Relevant Studies and Contributions**: The section references several significant studies and papers, including:\n   - Unsupervised identification of relevant prior cases.\n   - The development of language models that function as few-shot learners.\n   - Analysis of legal documents using non-negative matrix factorization methods.\n\n4. **Key Authors and Works**: Notable contributors mentioned include Shivangi Bithel, Sumitra S Malagi, Tom Brown, and Ryan Budahazy, among others, highlighting their contributions to the field through various conferences and publications.\n\nOverall, the excerpt emphasizes the intersection of legal knowledge and artificial intelligence, showcasing how emerging technologies can transform traditional legal practices."}, "excluded_embed_metadata_keys": ["file_name", "file_type", "file_size", "creation_date", "last_modified_date", "last_accessed_date"], "excluded_llm_metadata_keys": ["file_name", "file_type", "file_size", "creation_date", "last_modified_date", "last_accessed_date"], "relationships": {"1": {"node_id": "1d3f5bd7-239e-43d2-ae7f-1e7cd01c88fb", "node_type": "4", "metadata": {"file_path": "C:\\Users\\anteb\\PycharmProjects\\JupyterProject\\naive_rag\\data\\ai_articles\\Bridging Legal Knowledge and AI.md", "file_name": "Bridging Legal Knowledge and AI.md", "file_type": "text/markdown", "file_size": 52048, "creation_date": "2025-03-26", "last_modified_date": "2025-03-22"}, "hash": "8b9a69e79061b64e3a9958d47ca5223db6e2ffd6fcab4d00822647d6e5777090", "class_name": "RelatedNodeInfo"}, "2": {"node_id": "b28718bd-ed52-457c-b871-da3d5f21c478", "node_type": "1", "metadata": {"file_path": "C:\\Users\\anteb\\PycharmProjects\\JupyterProject\\naive_rag\\data\\ai_articles\\Bridging Legal Knowledge and AI.md", "file_name": "Bridging Legal Knowledge and AI.md", "file_type": "text/markdown", "file_size": 52048, "creation_date": "2025-03-26", "last_modified_date": "2025-03-22"}, "hash": "2d2c76e49fc4d566c5d33ca98163373f9075229b89241b85763e64fb4a57fceb", "class_name": "RelatedNodeInfo"}, "3": {"node_id": "49eb4e13-6d87-4eef-b959-ae1e630bb5df", "node_type": "1", "metadata": {}, "hash": "b436bc3ab67683bc7f031850dd0eebaa5c009a24a8fd7127e5eb64f8705166da", "class_name": "RelatedNodeInfo"}}, "metadata_template": "{key}: {value}", "metadata_separator": "\n", "text": "Knowledge-Based Systems 250 (2022), 109046. https://doi.org/10.1016/j.knosys.2022.109046\n12. Shivangi Bithel and Sumitra S Malagi. 2021. Unsupervised Identification of Relevant Prior Cases. In Proceedings of the 18th International Conference on Artificial Intelligence and Law. ACM, 10\u201319.\n13. Tom Brown, Benjamin Mann, Nick Ryder, Melanie Subbiah, Jared D Kaplan, Prafulla Dhariwal, Arvind Neelakantan, Pranav Shyam, Girish Sastry, Amanda Askell, Sandhini Agarwal, Ariel Herbert-Voss, Gretchen Krueger, Tom Henighan, Rewon Child, Aditya Ramesh, Daniel Ziegler, Jeffrey Wu, Clemens Winter, Chris Hesse, Mark Chen, Eric Sigler, Mateusz Litwin, Scott Gray, Benjamin Chess, Jack Clark, Christopher Berner, Sam McCandlish, Alec Radford, Ilya Sutskever, and Dario Amodei. 2020. Language Models are Few-Shot Learners. In Advances in Neural Information Processing Systems, H. Larochelle, M. Ranzato, R. Hadsell, M.F. Balcan, and H. Lin (Eds.), Vol. 33. Curran Associates, Inc., 1877\u20131901. https://proceedings.neurips.cc/paper_files/paper/2020/file/1457c0d6bfcb4967418bfb8ac142f64a-Paper.pdf\n14. Ryan Budahazy, Lu Cheng, Yihuan Huang, Andrew Johnson, Pengyu Li, Joshua Vendrow, Zhoutong Wu, Denali Molitor, Elizaveta Rebrova, and Deanna Needell. 2021. Analysis of Legal Documents via Non-negative Matrix Factorization Methods. ArXiv abs/2104.14028 (2021). https://api.semanticscholar.org/CorpusID:233444252\n15.", "mimetype": "text/plain", "start_char_idx": 42318, "end_char_idx": 43720, "metadata_seperator": "\n", "text_template": "[Excerpt from document]\n{metadata_str}\nExcerpt:\n-----\n{content}\n-----\n", "class_name": "TextNode"}, "__type__": "1"}, {"__data__": {"id_": "49eb4e13-6d87-4eef-b959-ae1e630bb5df", "embedding": null, "metadata": {"file_path": "C:\\Users\\anteb\\PycharmProjects\\JupyterProject\\naive_rag\\data\\ai_articles\\Bridging Legal Knowledge and AI.md", "file_name": "Bridging Legal Knowledge and AI.md", "file_type": "text/markdown", "file_size": 52048, "creation_date": "2025-03-26", "last_modified_date": "2025-03-22", "document_title": "\"Transforming Legal Research and Decision-Making: A Comprehensive Framework Integrating Retrieval-Augmented Generation, Knowledge Graphs, Semantic Understanding, and Advanced Machine Learning Techniques\"", "section_summary": "The section discusses various academic contributions related to the intersection of legal research and artificial intelligence, particularly focusing on the use of knowledge graphs, machine learning techniques, and natural language processing in legal contexts. Key topics include:\n\n1. **Knowledge Graphs**: The importance of trust, privacy, and transparency in legal governance, as highlighted by Pompeu Casanovas Romeu and colleagues in their 2021 paper.\n\n2. **Neural Legal Judgment Prediction**: Research by Ilias Chalkidis and others on predicting legal judgments using neural networks, presented at the 57th Annual Meeting of the Association for Computational Linguistics.\n\n3. **LEGAL-BERT**: A study introducing LEGAL-BERT, a model tailored for legal text processing, which was discussed by Ilias Chalkidis and his team in 2020.\n\n4. **LexGLUE Benchmark**: The introduction of LexGLUE, a benchmark dataset aimed at enhancing legal language understanding, as presented by Ilias Chalkidis and collaborators in 2022.\n\nEntities mentioned include:\n- Authors: Pompeu Casanovas Romeu, Daniel Schwabe, Carlos Laufer, Ilias Chalkidis, Ion Androutsopoulos, Nikolaos Aletras, Manos Fergadiotis, Prodromos Malakasiotis, Abhik Jana, Dirk Hartung, Michael Bommarito, Daniel Katz.\n- Organizations: Association for Computational Linguistics, EU H2020 Programme LYNX, CNPq.\n- Publications: Various conference proceedings and journals related to legal AI research. \n\nOverall, the section emphasizes the integration of advanced AI techniques in legal research and decision-making processes."}, "excluded_embed_metadata_keys": ["file_name", "file_type", "file_size", "creation_date", "last_modified_date", "last_accessed_date"], "excluded_llm_metadata_keys": ["file_name", "file_type", "file_size", "creation_date", "last_modified_date", "last_accessed_date"], "relationships": {"1": {"node_id": "1d3f5bd7-239e-43d2-ae7f-1e7cd01c88fb", "node_type": "4", "metadata": {"file_path": "C:\\Users\\anteb\\PycharmProjects\\JupyterProject\\naive_rag\\data\\ai_articles\\Bridging Legal Knowledge and AI.md", "file_name": "Bridging Legal Knowledge and AI.md", "file_type": "text/markdown", "file_size": 52048, "creation_date": "2025-03-26", "last_modified_date": "2025-03-22"}, "hash": "8b9a69e79061b64e3a9958d47ca5223db6e2ffd6fcab4d00822647d6e5777090", "class_name": "RelatedNodeInfo"}, "2": {"node_id": "7d949dcc-09ab-4548-97bc-fe8c67426d82", "node_type": "1", "metadata": {"file_path": "C:\\Users\\anteb\\PycharmProjects\\JupyterProject\\naive_rag\\data\\ai_articles\\Bridging Legal Knowledge and AI.md", "file_name": "Bridging Legal Knowledge and AI.md", "file_type": "text/markdown", "file_size": 52048, "creation_date": "2025-03-26", "last_modified_date": "2025-03-22"}, "hash": "073766d9f9d6ee77494e91abd72c44dfe818fcb1261f5cab56c00947a575f1f2", "class_name": "RelatedNodeInfo"}, "3": {"node_id": "38238bcb-e88c-4b99-9cb9-3ace55c1bf06", "node_type": "1", "metadata": {}, "hash": "a4196282387728674d6464bc854b770bcf2def979d59497555e9bcec50511aee", "class_name": "RelatedNodeInfo"}}, "metadata_template": "{key}: {value}", "metadata_separator": "\n", "text": "ArXiv abs/2104.14028 (2021). https://api.semanticscholar.org/CorpusID:233444252\n15. Pompeu Casanovas Romeu, Daniel Schwabe, and Carlos Laufer. 2021. Knowledge Graphs: Trust, Privacy, and Transparency from a Legal Governance Approach. 37, 1 (Aug. 2021), 24\u201341. https://doi.org/10.26826/law-in-context.v37i1.136. A. Daniel Schwabe was partially supported by a grant from CNPq. The present work by Pompeu Casanovas has been carried out for the EU H2020 Programme LYNX, Legal Knowledge Graph for Multilingual Compliance Services.\n16. Ilias Chalkidis, Ion Androutsopoulos, and Nikolaos Aletras. 2019. Neural Legal Judgment Prediction in English. In Proceedings of the 57th Annual Meeting of the Association for Computational Linguistics, Anna Korhonen, David Traum, and Llu\u00eds M\u00e0rquez (Eds.). Association for Computational Linguistics, Florence, Italy, 4317\u20134323. https://doi.org/10.18653/v1/P19-1424\n17. Ilias Chalkidis, Manos Fergadiotis, Prodromos Malakasiotis, Nikolaos Aletras, and Ion Androutsopoulos. 2020. LEGAL-BERT: The Muppets straight out of Law School. In Findings of the Association for Computational Linguistics: EMNLP 2020, Trevor Cohn, Yulan He, and Yang Liu (Eds.). Association for Computational Linguistics, Online, 2898\u20132904. https://doi.org/10.18653/v1/2020.findings-emnlp.261\n18. Ilias Chalkidis, Abhik Jana, Dirk Hartung, Michael Bommarito, Ion Androutsopoulos, Daniel Katz, and Nikolaos Aletras. 2022. LexGLUE: A Benchmark Dataset for Legal Language Understanding in English.", "mimetype": "text/plain", "start_char_idx": 43637, "end_char_idx": 45130, "metadata_seperator": "\n", "text_template": "[Excerpt from document]\n{metadata_str}\nExcerpt:\n-----\n{content}\n-----\n", "class_name": "TextNode"}, "__type__": "1"}, {"__data__": {"id_": "38238bcb-e88c-4b99-9cb9-3ace55c1bf06", "embedding": null, "metadata": {"file_path": "C:\\Users\\anteb\\PycharmProjects\\JupyterProject\\naive_rag\\data\\ai_articles\\Bridging Legal Knowledge and AI.md", "file_name": "Bridging Legal Knowledge and AI.md", "file_type": "text/markdown", "file_size": 52048, "creation_date": "2025-03-26", "last_modified_date": "2025-03-22", "document_title": "\"Transforming Legal Research and Decision-Making: A Comprehensive Framework Integrating Retrieval-Augmented Generation, Knowledge Graphs, Semantic Understanding, and Advanced Machine Learning Techniques\"", "section_summary": "The section discusses the development of Smart-Slic, a generative AI framework specifically designed for the legal domain. Key topics and entities include:\n\n1. **Smart-Slic Framework**: A generative AI system that integrates Retrieval-Augmented Generation (RAG), vector stores, and a Neo4j-based knowledge graph using Non-negative Matrix Factorization (NMFk).\n\n2. **Methodology**: The framework employs T-ELF (a technique for embedding legal texts) with metadata and chunking strategies to enhance accuracy and minimize hallucinations in legal text interpretation.\n\n3. **Data Types**: The system bridges structured and unstructured legal data, including constitutional provisions, statutes, and case law, facilitating advanced semantic reasoning.\n\n4. **Experimental Findings**: Results indicate that chunking combined with hierarchical NMFk improves accuracy, especially for large unstructured datasets, while short structured documents benefit from minimal chunking.\n\n5. **Challenges**: Issues such as incomplete author attribution in networks and the need for additional datasets (e.g., administrative codes, judicial rules) are highlighted as limitations. The reconciliation of informal agreements with formal judgments is also noted as necessary for a comprehensive legal model.\n\n6. **Impact**: The framework represents progress in computational law and legal AI, offering a scalable and interpretable approach for legal research, retrieval, and reasoning, leveraging semantic embeddings and knowledge graphs for improved insights."}, "excluded_embed_metadata_keys": ["file_name", "file_type", "file_size", "creation_date", "last_modified_date", "last_accessed_date"], "excluded_llm_metadata_keys": ["file_name", "file_type", "file_size", "creation_date", "last_modified_date", "last_accessed_date"], "relationships": {"1": {"node_id": "1d3f5bd7-239e-43d2-ae7f-1e7cd01c88fb", "node_type": "4", "metadata": {"file_path": "C:\\Users\\anteb\\PycharmProjects\\JupyterProject\\naive_rag\\data\\ai_articles\\Bridging Legal Knowledge and AI.md", "file_name": "Bridging Legal Knowledge and AI.md", "file_type": "text/markdown", "file_size": 52048, "creation_date": "2025-03-26", "last_modified_date": "2025-03-22"}, "hash": "8b9a69e79061b64e3a9958d47ca5223db6e2ffd6fcab4d00822647d6e5777090", "class_name": "RelatedNodeInfo"}, "2": {"node_id": "49eb4e13-6d87-4eef-b959-ae1e630bb5df", "node_type": "1", "metadata": {"file_path": "C:\\Users\\anteb\\PycharmProjects\\JupyterProject\\naive_rag\\data\\ai_articles\\Bridging Legal Knowledge and AI.md", "file_name": "Bridging Legal Knowledge and AI.md", "file_type": "text/markdown", "file_size": 52048, "creation_date": "2025-03-26", "last_modified_date": "2025-03-22"}, "hash": "5edd1ca56b491f1cc3a98afc8a2e5e85be7668922c723f22dc9fa2750f4786d0", "class_name": "RelatedNodeInfo"}, "3": {"node_id": "545fc94e-76b6-4074-ac0e-0bbd53265679", "node_type": "1", "metadata": {}, "hash": "70d672f1a858c900c406f6df3b5f8269d25a97aa42a9725bc91d0d79b284c17a", "class_name": "RelatedNodeInfo"}}, "metadata_template": "{key}: {value}", "metadata_separator": "\n", "text": "2022. LexGLUE: A Benchmark Dataset for Legal Language Understanding in English. In Proceedings of the 60th Annual Meeting of the Association for Computational Linguistics (Volume 1: Long Papers), Smaranda Muresan, Preslav Nakov, and Aline Villavicencio (Eds.). Association for Computational Linguistics, Dublin, Ireland, 4310\u20134330.\n\n# CONCLUSION\n\nThis work introduced Smart-Slic, a generative AI framework tailored to the legal domain, leveraging RAG, vector stores, and a Neo4j-based knowledge graph constructed through NMFk. Our approach uses T-ELF with metadata and chunking strategies to capture fine-grained H-clusters and minimize hallucinations to improve reliability. By bridging structured and unstructured data\u2013spanning constitutional provisions, statutes, and case law\u2013, our system supports advanced semantic reasoning and dimensionally reduced insight into the latent structure of legal texts.\n\nExperimental results across multiple retrieval strategies show that chunking, combined with hierarchical NMFk, improves accuracy, particularly for large, unstructured case law datasets. Short, highly structured documents, constitutional provisions, and statute sections benefit from minimal chunking, revealing the importance of aligning preprocessing approaches with data characteristics. We demonstrated the framework\u2019s capability to derive interpretive legal topics and precisely answer domain-specific queries by harnessing topically segmented embeddings and explicit links within the knowledge graph.\n\nDespite the positive results, challenges remain. Author attribution in networks is incomplete, limiting the knowledge graph\u2019s potential for thorough precedent tracing and interlinking. Including additional datasets\u2014such as administrative codes and judicial rules\u2014would provide richer context and increase the system\u2019s coverage of a functioning state government. Moreover, systematically reconciling informal post-decree agreements with formal judgments is needed to model a more acute legal flow.\n\nThis framework marks a step forward in computational law and legal AI, demonstrating a scalable, interpretable method for discovering, retrieving, and reasoning over complex legal corpora. Combining semantic embeddings, latent topic modeling, and knowledge graphs contributes to more robust, data-driven legal research pathways.", "mimetype": "text/plain", "start_char_idx": 45051, "end_char_idx": 47391, "metadata_seperator": "\n", "text_template": "[Excerpt from document]\n{metadata_str}\nExcerpt:\n-----\n{content}\n-----\n", "class_name": "TextNode"}, "__type__": "1"}, {"__data__": {"id_": "545fc94e-76b6-4074-ac0e-0bbd53265679", "embedding": null, "metadata": {"file_path": "C:\\Users\\anteb\\PycharmProjects\\JupyterProject\\naive_rag\\data\\ai_articles\\Bridging Legal Knowledge and AI.md", "file_name": "Bridging Legal Knowledge and AI.md", "file_type": "text/markdown", "file_size": 52048, "creation_date": "2025-03-26", "last_modified_date": "2025-03-22", "document_title": "\"Transforming Legal Research and Decision-Making: A Comprehensive Framework Integrating Retrieval-Augmented Generation, Knowledge Graphs, Semantic Understanding, and Advanced Machine Learning Techniques\"", "section_summary": "The section discusses the integration of advanced technologies in legal research and decision-making, focusing on the use of semantic embeddings, latent topic modeling, and knowledge graphs to enhance data-driven legal research pathways. Future directions highlighted include improving citation extraction processes, broadening the scope of legal instruments, and leveraging large language models (LLMs) for in-depth analysis of legal precedents and trend forecasting.\n\nKey topics include:\n- Semantic embeddings\n- Latent topic modeling\n- Knowledge graphs\n- Citation extraction\n- Legal instruments\n- LLM-driven reasoning\n- Precedent analysis\n- Trend prediction\n\nEntities mentioned:\n- U.S. Department of Energy National Nuclear Security Administration (DNN R&D)\n- LANL Institutional Computing Program\n- Conference: ICAIL 2025\n- Authors and works cited, including contributions from NVIDIA and various researchers in the field of computational linguistics and legal technology."}, "excluded_embed_metadata_keys": ["file_name", "file_type", "file_size", "creation_date", "last_modified_date", "last_accessed_date"], "excluded_llm_metadata_keys": ["file_name", "file_type", "file_size", "creation_date", "last_modified_date", "last_accessed_date"], "relationships": {"1": {"node_id": "1d3f5bd7-239e-43d2-ae7f-1e7cd01c88fb", "node_type": "4", "metadata": {"file_path": "C:\\Users\\anteb\\PycharmProjects\\JupyterProject\\naive_rag\\data\\ai_articles\\Bridging Legal Knowledge and AI.md", "file_name": "Bridging Legal Knowledge and AI.md", "file_type": "text/markdown", "file_size": 52048, "creation_date": "2025-03-26", "last_modified_date": "2025-03-22"}, "hash": "8b9a69e79061b64e3a9958d47ca5223db6e2ffd6fcab4d00822647d6e5777090", "class_name": "RelatedNodeInfo"}, "2": {"node_id": "38238bcb-e88c-4b99-9cb9-3ace55c1bf06", "node_type": "1", "metadata": {"file_path": "C:\\Users\\anteb\\PycharmProjects\\JupyterProject\\naive_rag\\data\\ai_articles\\Bridging Legal Knowledge and AI.md", "file_name": "Bridging Legal Knowledge and AI.md", "file_type": "text/markdown", "file_size": 52048, "creation_date": "2025-03-26", "last_modified_date": "2025-03-22"}, "hash": "d864b20903b193d0905d57b9bbc7fe68cbd5c168dc4f058ed6bd9eaa95d909e4", "class_name": "RelatedNodeInfo"}, "3": {"node_id": "3ed5f954-5841-4bc0-b0df-bd6b438b6b2a", "node_type": "1", "metadata": {}, "hash": "98c6d7fc6ae7370435844e07ec03cc189b642458073034f7f007fb3fb471b9b5", "class_name": "RelatedNodeInfo"}}, "metadata_template": "{key}: {value}", "metadata_separator": "\n", "text": "Combining semantic embeddings, latent topic modeling, and knowledge graphs contributes to more robust, data-driven legal research pathways. Future directions include refining the citation extraction pipeline, expanding the collection to encompass broader legal instruments, and applying advanced LLM-driven reasoning for deeper precedent analysis and trend prediction.\n\n# ACKNOWLEDGMENTS\n\nThis research was funded by the U.S. Department of Energy National Nuclear Security Administration\u2019s Office of Defense Nuclear Nonproliferation Research and Development (DNN R&D), supported by the U.S. DOE NNSA under Contract No. 89233218CNA000001, as well as by the LANL Institutional Computing Program.\n---\n# ICAIL 2025, June 16\u201320, 2024, Chicago, IL, USA\n\n# Barron et al.\n\n[38] NVIDIA. 2023. NeMoTron-70B Instruct: NVIDIA\u2019s Generative AI Model. https://build.nvidia.com/nvidia/llama-3_1-nemotron-70b-instruct. Accessed: 2025-01-26.\n\n[19] Ilias Chalkidis, Vasiliki Koukoulioti, and Nikolaos Aletras. 2021. Paragraph-level rationalization of legal judgments using reinforcement learning. In Proceedings of the 2021 Conference of the North American Chapter of the Association for Computational Linguistics: Human Language Technologies (NAACL-HLT). 1081\u20131090.\n\n[20] Arman Cohan, Sergey Feldman, Iz Beltagy, Doug Downey, and Daniel Weld. 2020. SPECTER: Document-level Representation Learning using Citation-informed Transformers. In Proceedings of the 58th Annual Meeting of the Association for Computational Linguistics, Dan Jurafsky, Joyce Chai, Natalie Schluter, and Joel Tetreault (Eds.). Association for Computational Linguistics, Online, 2270\u20132282.\n\n[21] Jacob Devlin, Ming-Wei Chang, Kenton Lee, and Kristina Toutanova. 2019. BERT: Pre-training of Deep Bidirectional Transformers for Language Understanding. In North American Chapter of the Association for Computational Linguistics.", "mimetype": "text/plain", "start_char_idx": 47252, "end_char_idx": 49129, "metadata_seperator": "\n", "text_template": "[Excerpt from document]\n{metadata_str}\nExcerpt:\n-----\n{content}\n-----\n", "class_name": "TextNode"}, "__type__": "1"}, {"__data__": {"id_": "3ed5f954-5841-4bc0-b0df-bd6b438b6b2a", "embedding": null, "metadata": {"file_path": "C:\\Users\\anteb\\PycharmProjects\\JupyterProject\\naive_rag\\data\\ai_articles\\Bridging Legal Knowledge and AI.md", "file_name": "Bridging Legal Knowledge and AI.md", "file_type": "text/markdown", "file_size": 52048, "creation_date": "2025-03-26", "last_modified_date": "2025-03-22", "document_title": "\"Transforming Legal Research and Decision-Making: A Comprehensive Framework Integrating Retrieval-Augmented Generation, Knowledge Graphs, Semantic Understanding, and Advanced Machine Learning Techniques\"", "section_summary": "The section primarily discusses advancements in legal research and decision-making through the integration of various AI techniques, particularly focusing on retrieval-augmented generation, knowledge graphs, and machine learning. Key topics include:\n\n1. **BERT**: A foundational model for language understanding, highlighting its significance in natural language processing.\n2. **Legal Knowledge Graphs**: The use of knowledge graphs to recommend similar legal cases, as explored in the work by Dhani et al. (2024).\n3. **Knowledge Graph Construction**: Dong et al. (2021) present methods for constructing knowledge graphs specifically for the legal domain.\n4. **Tensor Extraction of Latent Features (T-ELF)**: A technique introduced by Eren et al. (2023) for feature extraction.\n5. **Long Document Understanding**: The KALM framework by Feng et al. (2023) focuses on integrating various contexts for better comprehension of lengthy documents.\n6. **REALM**: A model for retrieval-augmented language model pre-training discussed by Guu et al. (2020).\n7. **Hierarchical Transformers**: Huang et al. (2023) explore query processing across multiple documents using hierarchical transformer models.\n8. **Generative Models for Question Answering**: Izacard and Grave (2021) investigate the synergy between passage retrieval and generative models for open-domain question answering.\n\nOverall, the section emphasizes the intersection of AI technologies and legal research, showcasing various methodologies and their contributions to enhancing legal decision-making processes."}, "excluded_embed_metadata_keys": ["file_name", "file_type", "file_size", "creation_date", "last_modified_date", "last_accessed_date"], "excluded_llm_metadata_keys": ["file_name", "file_type", "file_size", "creation_date", "last_modified_date", "last_accessed_date"], "relationships": {"1": {"node_id": "1d3f5bd7-239e-43d2-ae7f-1e7cd01c88fb", "node_type": "4", "metadata": {"file_path": "C:\\Users\\anteb\\PycharmProjects\\JupyterProject\\naive_rag\\data\\ai_articles\\Bridging Legal Knowledge and AI.md", "file_name": "Bridging Legal Knowledge and AI.md", "file_type": "text/markdown", "file_size": 52048, "creation_date": "2025-03-26", "last_modified_date": "2025-03-22"}, "hash": "8b9a69e79061b64e3a9958d47ca5223db6e2ffd6fcab4d00822647d6e5777090", "class_name": "RelatedNodeInfo"}, "2": {"node_id": "545fc94e-76b6-4074-ac0e-0bbd53265679", "node_type": "1", "metadata": {"file_path": "C:\\Users\\anteb\\PycharmProjects\\JupyterProject\\naive_rag\\data\\ai_articles\\Bridging Legal Knowledge and AI.md", "file_name": "Bridging Legal Knowledge and AI.md", "file_type": "text/markdown", "file_size": 52048, "creation_date": "2025-03-26", "last_modified_date": "2025-03-22"}, "hash": "324777c263e3f5afd3395476bff4fbdf77eb16473e3bf8496e954c06551e201e", "class_name": "RelatedNodeInfo"}, "3": {"node_id": "a8cd23a2-4330-4f1a-9685-8d5c8ea1b5ff", "node_type": "1", "metadata": {}, "hash": "bff7dad143efd56db007702c5e9cbb398c37b89fee9b14f55457762c3f761941", "class_name": "RelatedNodeInfo"}}, "metadata_template": "{key}: {value}", "metadata_separator": "\n", "text": "2019. BERT: Pre-training of Deep Bidirectional Transformers for Language Understanding. In North American Chapter of the Association for Computational Linguistics.\n\n[22] Jaspreet Singh Dhani, Ruchika Bhatt, Balaji Ganesan, Parikshet Sirohi, and Vasudha Bhatnagar. 2024. Similar Cases Recommendation using Legal Knowledge Graphs. arXiv:2107.04771 [cs.AI]\n\n[23] Biao Dong, Haoze Yu, and Haisheng Li. 2021. A Knowledge Graph Construction Approach for Legal Domain. Tehnicki vjesnik - Technical Gazette (2021).\n\n[24] Maksim Eren, Nick Solovyev, Ryan Barron, Manish Bhattarai, Duc Truong, Ismael Boureima, Erik Skau, Kim \u00d8. Rasmussen, and Boian Alexandrov. 2023. Tensor Extraction of Latent Features (T-ELF).\n\n[25] Shangbin Feng, Zhaoxuan Tan, Wenqian Zhang, Zhenyu Lei, and Yulia Tsvetkov. 2023. KALM: Knowledge-Aware Integration of Local, Document, and Global Contexts for Long Document Understanding.\n\n[26] Kelvin Guu, Kenton Lee, Zora Tung, Panupong Pasupat, and Ming-Wei Chang. 2020. REALM: retrieval-augmented language model pre-training. In Proceedings of the 37th International Conference on Machine Learning (ICML\u201920).\n\n[28] Zhiqi Huang, Shahrzad Naseri, Hamed Bonab, Sheikh Muhammad Sarwar, and James Allan. 2023. Hierarchical Transformer-based Query by Multiple Documents. In Proceedings of the 2023 ACM SIGIR International Conference on Theory of Information Retrieval (ICTIR \u201923).\n\n[29] Gautier Izacard and Edouard Grave. 2021. Leveraging Passage Retrieval with Generative Models for Open Domain Question Answering. In Proceedings of the 16th Conference of the European Chapter of the Association for Computational Linguistics: Main Volume.", "mimetype": "text/plain", "start_char_idx": 48966, "end_char_idx": 50614, "metadata_seperator": "\n", "text_template": "[Excerpt from document]\n{metadata_str}\nExcerpt:\n-----\n{content}\n-----\n", "class_name": "TextNode"}, "__type__": "1"}, {"__data__": {"id_": "a8cd23a2-4330-4f1a-9685-8d5c8ea1b5ff", "embedding": null, "metadata": {"file_path": "C:\\Users\\anteb\\PycharmProjects\\JupyterProject\\naive_rag\\data\\ai_articles\\Bridging Legal Knowledge and AI.md", "file_name": "Bridging Legal Knowledge and AI.md", "file_type": "text/markdown", "file_size": 52048, "creation_date": "2025-03-26", "last_modified_date": "2025-03-22", "document_title": "\"Transforming Legal Research and Decision-Making: A Comprehensive Framework Integrating Retrieval-Augmented Generation, Knowledge Graphs, Semantic Understanding, and Advanced Machine Learning Techniques\"", "section_summary": "The section discusses advancements in the integration of artificial intelligence (AI) and legal research, focusing on various methodologies and frameworks that enhance legal decision-making. Key topics include:\n\n1. **Generative Pre-Training**: The work by Alec Radford and Karthik Narasimhan on improving language understanding through generative pre-training.\n2. **Automated Legal Case Matching**: Research by Livio Robaldo and colleagues on using neural networks for matching legal cases.\n3. **Knowledge Graphs**: The extraction of legal knowledge for question-answering systems, as explored by Francesco Sovrano and others.\n4. **Legal Judgment Prediction**: The development of a multi-stage framework called LegalReasoner, which utilizes large language models and knowledge integration, as presented by Xuran Wang and team.\n5. **Knowledge Graph Construction**: The automatic construction of knowledge graphs for judicial cases, highlighted in a paper by Jie Zhou and colleagues.\n6. **Legal Case Retrieval Dataset**: The introduction of LeCaRD, a dataset for retrieving legal cases within the Chinese law system, by Yixiao Ma and others.\n7. **Connected Data Platforms**: Mention of Neo4j as a leading platform for managing connected data.\n\nEntities referenced include various authors and their contributions to the field, as well as the Neo4j platform, which supports data connectivity in legal contexts."}, "excluded_embed_metadata_keys": ["file_name", "file_type", "file_size", "creation_date", "last_modified_date", "last_accessed_date"], "excluded_llm_metadata_keys": ["file_name", "file_type", "file_size", "creation_date", "last_modified_date", "last_accessed_date"], "relationships": {"1": {"node_id": "1d3f5bd7-239e-43d2-ae7f-1e7cd01c88fb", "node_type": "4", "metadata": {"file_path": "C:\\Users\\anteb\\PycharmProjects\\JupyterProject\\naive_rag\\data\\ai_articles\\Bridging Legal Knowledge and AI.md", "file_name": "Bridging Legal Knowledge and AI.md", "file_type": "text/markdown", "file_size": 52048, "creation_date": "2025-03-26", "last_modified_date": "2025-03-22"}, "hash": "8b9a69e79061b64e3a9958d47ca5223db6e2ffd6fcab4d00822647d6e5777090", "class_name": "RelatedNodeInfo"}, "2": {"node_id": "3ed5f954-5841-4bc0-b0df-bd6b438b6b2a", "node_type": "1", "metadata": {"file_path": "C:\\Users\\anteb\\PycharmProjects\\JupyterProject\\naive_rag\\data\\ai_articles\\Bridging Legal Knowledge and AI.md", "file_name": "Bridging Legal Knowledge and AI.md", "file_type": "text/markdown", "file_size": 52048, "creation_date": "2025-03-26", "last_modified_date": "2025-03-22"}, "hash": "96072c72e384929bb5fe56e3d5194131a31fcb4f6177c38597b4dce5e6f03c58", "class_name": "RelatedNodeInfo"}}, "metadata_template": "{key}: {value}", "metadata_separator": "\n", "text": "In Proceedings of the 16th Conference of the European Chapter of the Association for Computational Linguistics: Main Volume.\n\n[44] Alec Radford and Karthik Narasimhan. 2018. Improving Language Understanding by Generative Pre-Training.\n\n[45] Livio Robaldo, Guido Boella, and Leendert van der Torre. 2020. A neural approach to automated legal case matching. Artificial Intelligence and Law 28, 1, 65\u201381.\n\n[46] Francesco Sovrano, Monica Palmirani, and Fabio Vitali. 2020. Legal Knowledge Extraction for Knowledge Graph Based Question-Answering. In International Conference on Legal Knowledge and Information Systems.\n\n[48] Xuran Wang, Xinguang Zhang, Vanessa Hoo, Zhouhang Shao, and Xuguang Zhang. 2024. LegalReasoner: A Multi-Stage Framework for Legal Judgment Prediction via Large Language Models and Knowledge Integration.\n\n[52] Jie Zhou, Xin Chen, Hang Zhang, and Zhe Li. 2024. Automatic Knowledge Graph Construction for Judicial Cases. arXiv:2404.09416 [cs.CL]\n\n[36] Yixiao Ma, Yunqiu Shao, Yueyue Wu, Yiqun Liu, Ruizhe Zhang, Min Zhang, and Shaoping Ma. 2021. LeCaRD: A Legal Case Retrieval Dataset for Chinese Law System. In Proceedings of the 44th International ACM SIGIR Conference on Research and Development in Information Retrieval.\n\n[37] Neo4j, Inc. 2023. Neo4j: The #1 Platform for Connected Data.", "mimetype": "text/plain", "start_char_idx": 50490, "end_char_idx": 51798, "metadata_seperator": "\n", "text_template": "[Excerpt from document]\n{metadata_str}\nExcerpt:\n-----\n{content}\n-----\n", "class_name": "TextNode"}, "__type__": "1"}, {"__data__": {"id_": "abe871e7-fd87-47e9-be48-c186d8619bd9", "embedding": null, "metadata": {"file_path": "C:\\Users\\anteb\\PycharmProjects\\JupyterProject\\naive_rag\\data\\ai_articles\\Chatbots and Their Applications in Medical Fields Current Status.md", "file_name": "Chatbots and Their Applications in Medical Fields Current Status.md", "file_type": "text/markdown", "file_size": 28958, "creation_date": "2025-03-26", "last_modified_date": "2025-03-23", "document_title": "\"Advancing Healthcare: A Comprehensive Review of Chatbots and AI in Medical Applications, Trends, Challenges, and Future Opportunities\"", "section_summary": "The section discusses a scoping review titled \"Chatbots and Their Applications in Medical Fields: Current Status and Future Trends,\" authored by a team of researchers from various medical institutions in India and the UK. The review focuses on the role of chatbots in healthcare, highlighting their benefits in areas such as patient education, clinical decision-making, communication, research, data analysis, and administrative tasks. \n\nKey topics include:\n- The definition and functionality of chatbots as computer programs that simulate conversations.\n- The objectives of the scoping review, which are to analyze the current role, challenges, and future potential of chatbots in medical science.\n- The methodology of the literature search conducted on April 9, 2024, across five databases, resulting in the identification of 1024 studies, with 14 articles ultimately selected for review after a screening process.\n\nEntities involved:\n- Authors: Vibhu Krishnan Viswanathan, Vijay Kumar Jain, Abhishek Vaish, Madhan Jeyaraman, Karthikeyan P Iyengar, Raju Vaishya.\n- Institutions: Dr Rela Institute and Medical Center, Atal Bihari Vajpayee Institute of Medical Sciences, Indraprastha Apollo Hospitals, ACS Medical College and Hospital, Southport and Ormskirk NHS Trust.\n- Databases used for literature search: PubMed, Scopus, Web of Science, Embase, Google Scholar. \n\nOverall, the review aims to provide insights into the current status and future trends of chatbot applications in the medical field."}, "excluded_embed_metadata_keys": ["file_name", "file_type", "file_size", "creation_date", "last_modified_date", "last_accessed_date"], "excluded_llm_metadata_keys": ["file_name", "file_type", "file_size", "creation_date", "last_modified_date", "last_accessed_date"], "relationships": {"1": {"node_id": "dad35ff1-58aa-45cb-b326-0446ff627bda", "node_type": "4", "metadata": {"file_path": "C:\\Users\\anteb\\PycharmProjects\\JupyterProject\\naive_rag\\data\\ai_articles\\Chatbots and Their Applications in Medical Fields Current Status.md", "file_name": "Chatbots and Their Applications in Medical Fields Current Status.md", "file_type": "text/markdown", "file_size": 28958, "creation_date": "2025-03-26", "last_modified_date": "2025-03-23"}, "hash": "4d0315e8f144da5f7f359eae5fa855cbb6e34f8f15c19f56fd0a30b52a22b7b2", "class_name": "RelatedNodeInfo"}, "3": {"node_id": "4742fd53-3b9e-4df0-85e2-2b9c0288a1af", "node_type": "1", "metadata": {}, "hash": "833bde7f2dc587974aa0143241483b16032806e5c764fb6afda9a0c7a4249f9b", "class_name": "RelatedNodeInfo"}}, "metadata_template": "{key}: {value}", "metadata_separator": "\n", "text": "# Chatbots and Their Applications in Medical Fields: Current Status and Future Trends: A Scoping Review*\n\nVibhu Krishnan Viswanathan\u00b9, Vijay Kumar Jain\u00b2, Abhishek Vaish\u00b3, Madhan Jeyaraman\u2074, Karthikeyan P Iyengar\u2075 and Raju Vaishya\u2076\n\n\u00b9Department of Orthopaedics, Dr Rela Institute and Medical Center, Chennai, Tamil Nadu, India,\n\u00b2Department of Orthopaedics, Atal Bihari Vajpayee Institute of Medical Sciences, Dr Ram Manohar Lohia Hospital, New Delhi, India,\n\u00b3Department of Orthopedics, Indraprastha Apollo Hospitals, New Delhi, India,\n\u2074Department of Orthopaedics, ACS Medical College and Hospital, Dr MGR Educational and Research Institute, Chennai, Tamil Nadu, India,\n\u2075Trauma and Orthopaedic Surgeon, Southport and Ormskirk NHS Trust, Southport, UK,\n\u2076Indraprastha Apollo Hospitals, New Delhi, India\n\n*The sixth author of this article is an editor of this journal. To avoid any potential conflict of interest, the peer-review process and decision-making for this article were handled by other editors and anonymous reviewers.\n\n# Abstract\n\nBackground and Aim: Chatbots are computer programs, which are devised to simulate conversations through voice or textual interactive forms. These applications offer multiple benefits in patient education, clinical decision-making, interpersonal communication, research activities, data analysis and administrative affairs. The present scoping review aims to analyse the current role, pitfalls, challenges and future scope of these modalities in diverse fields of medical science.\n\nMethods: Literature search was made on 9 April 2024, on five databases (PubMed, Scopus, Web of Science, Embase and Google Scholar). A narrative approach was used for the synthesis of results.\n\nResults: Our literature search yielded 1024 studies. After the de-duplication of manuscripts using Endnote, 342 articles were identified. After title and abstract screening, 74 articles were included for the next round of screening. Finally, 14 articles were selected for this scoping review.", "mimetype": "text/plain", "start_char_idx": 0, "end_char_idx": 2004, "metadata_seperator": "\n", "text_template": "[Excerpt from document]\n{metadata_str}\nExcerpt:\n-----\n{content}\n-----\n", "class_name": "TextNode"}, "__type__": "1"}, {"__data__": {"id_": "4742fd53-3b9e-4df0-85e2-2b9c0288a1af", "embedding": null, "metadata": {"file_path": "C:\\Users\\anteb\\PycharmProjects\\JupyterProject\\naive_rag\\data\\ai_articles\\Chatbots and Their Applications in Medical Fields Current Status.md", "file_name": "Chatbots and Their Applications in Medical Fields Current Status.md", "file_type": "text/markdown", "file_size": 28958, "creation_date": "2025-03-26", "last_modified_date": "2025-03-23", "document_title": "\"Advancing Healthcare: A Comprehensive Review of Chatbots and AI in Medical Applications, Trends, Challenges, and Future Opportunities\"", "section_summary": "The section discusses the growing role of chatbots and artificial intelligence (AI) in the medical field, highlighting the following key topics and entities:\n\n1. **Research Overview**: The document summarizes a scoping review that included 74 articles, ultimately selecting 14 for detailed analysis on chatbot applications in medicine.\n\n2. **Chatbot Applications**: It emphasizes the increasing development of diverse chatbot applications in medical science, particularly those utilizing machine learning strategies.\n\n3. **Potential Benefits**: Chatbots are noted for their potential to enhance various aspects of healthcare, including patient care, academic activities, and research.\n\n4. **Conclusion**: The review concludes that AI can streamline routine medical functions, improve accessibility, efficiency, and standards of care. However, it stresses the need for further validation through high-quality studies to address concerns regarding patient privacy, data security, and the accuracy of chatbot technologies.\n\n5. **Keywords**: The section includes keywords such as Artificial Intelligence, AI, Chatbots, ChatGPT, health care, and medical science.\n\n6. **Introduction to AI in Healthcare**: The introduction outlines the advancements in AI and its recognition in health systems, describing chatbots as virtual assistants that facilitate human-like interactions across various fields, including healthcare.\n\n7. **Leading Chatbots**: Notable chatbots mentioned include ChatGPT (Open AI), Bard AI (Google AI), Bing Chat (Microsoft AI), and Claude AI (Anthropic AI).\n\n8. **Correspondence Information**: The document provides contact details for Vijay Kumar Jain, affiliated with the Department of Orthopaedics at a medical institute in New Delhi, India. \n\nOverall, the section highlights the transformative potential of AI and chatbots in healthcare while acknowledging the need for careful implementation and validation."}, "excluded_embed_metadata_keys": ["file_name", "file_type", "file_size", "creation_date", "last_modified_date", "last_accessed_date"], "excluded_llm_metadata_keys": ["file_name", "file_type", "file_size", "creation_date", "last_modified_date", "last_accessed_date"], "relationships": {"1": {"node_id": "dad35ff1-58aa-45cb-b326-0446ff627bda", "node_type": "4", "metadata": {"file_path": "C:\\Users\\anteb\\PycharmProjects\\JupyterProject\\naive_rag\\data\\ai_articles\\Chatbots and Their Applications in Medical Fields Current Status.md", "file_name": "Chatbots and Their Applications in Medical Fields Current Status.md", "file_type": "text/markdown", "file_size": 28958, "creation_date": "2025-03-26", "last_modified_date": "2025-03-23"}, "hash": "4d0315e8f144da5f7f359eae5fa855cbb6e34f8f15c19f56fd0a30b52a22b7b2", "class_name": "RelatedNodeInfo"}, "2": {"node_id": "abe871e7-fd87-47e9-be48-c186d8619bd9", "node_type": "1", "metadata": {"file_path": "C:\\Users\\anteb\\PycharmProjects\\JupyterProject\\naive_rag\\data\\ai_articles\\Chatbots and Their Applications in Medical Fields Current Status.md", "file_name": "Chatbots and Their Applications in Medical Fields Current Status.md", "file_type": "text/markdown", "file_size": 28958, "creation_date": "2025-03-26", "last_modified_date": "2025-03-23"}, "hash": "0ab5439ef7f0d285eeb4a5d700ab39ac2c8d17b919d5eb06b4176ca1b0507a6e", "class_name": "RelatedNodeInfo"}, "3": {"node_id": "86cadabe-51ee-4b02-90ce-fff8112be8e4", "node_type": "1", "metadata": {}, "hash": "106060d1f4446f0c21a557df18edf8bfd2e32fed07b14c4ea409429c40aa0c2b", "class_name": "RelatedNodeInfo"}}, "metadata_template": "{key}: {value}", "metadata_separator": "\n", "text": "After title and abstract screening, 74 articles were included for the next round of screening. Finally, 14 articles were selected for this scoping review. Diverse chatbot applications have been developed at a growing rate for use in medical science. There is a gradual shift towards employing machine learning-based strategies to develop chatbot programs. Chatbots have a significant potential to revolutionise diverse aspects of medicine including patient care, academic activities, and research endeavours.\n\nConclusion: Artificial Intelligence can be highly effective in streamlining the routine functions and activities in medical care. Chatbots have the potential to meliorate the accessibility, efficiency and standard of medical care. However, the need for further validation through high-quality studies to ensure patient privacy, strategies to conform to patients\u2019 data security, accuracy and precision of the technology and mitigating potential pitfalls in its routine utilisation globally are warranted.\n\nKeywords: Artificial Intelligence, AI, Chatbots, ChatGPT, health care, medical science\n\n# INTRODUCTION\n\nArtificial intelligence (AI) has made significant developments in the past years and its utility in the domain of health systems has gained substantial recognition.[1,2] Chatbots are virtual assistants, which simulate human-like interactions and participate in conversations with users. They have emerged as promising applications in a wide range of fields such as education, health care and customer services.[3,4] The growing importance of AI has generally been attributed to multifaceted advancements such as substantial melioration of internet connectivity, consistent improvements in AI technology and extensive availability of smart devices.[5] Some leading chatbots include ChatGPT (Open AI), Bard AI (Google AI), Bing Chat (Microsoft AI) and Claude AI (anthropic AI).[5]\n\nAddress for correspondence: Vijay Kumar Jain, Department of Orthopaedics, Atal Bihari Vajpayee Institute of Medical Sciences, Dr Ram Manohar Lohia Hospital, New Delhi 110001, India.", "mimetype": "text/plain", "start_char_idx": 1850, "end_char_idx": 3930, "metadata_seperator": "\n", "text_template": "[Excerpt from document]\n{metadata_str}\nExcerpt:\n-----\n{content}\n-----\n", "class_name": "TextNode"}, "__type__": "1"}, {"__data__": {"id_": "86cadabe-51ee-4b02-90ce-fff8112be8e4", "embedding": null, "metadata": {"file_path": "C:\\Users\\anteb\\PycharmProjects\\JupyterProject\\naive_rag\\data\\ai_articles\\Chatbots and Their Applications in Medical Fields Current Status.md", "file_name": "Chatbots and Their Applications in Medical Fields Current Status.md", "file_type": "text/markdown", "file_size": 28958, "creation_date": "2025-03-26", "last_modified_date": "2025-03-23", "document_title": "\"Advancing Healthcare: A Comprehensive Review of Chatbots and AI in Medical Applications, Trends, Challenges, and Future Opportunities\"", "section_summary": "The section discusses a comprehensive review article titled \"Advancing Healthcare: A Comprehensive Review of Chatbots and AI in Medical Applications, Trends, Challenges, and Future Opportunities,\" authored by Viswanathan VK, Jain VK, Vaish A, Jayraman M, Iyengar KP, and Vaishya R. The article focuses on the current status and future trends of chatbots in various medical fields. \n\nKey topics include:\n\n1. **Eligibility Criteria**: The review includes studies on chatbots across diverse medical specialties while excluding opinions, editorials, letters, case reports, and non-English literature.\n\n2. **Data Extraction**: The methodology for data extraction involved using Endnote for database searches, followed by manual selection after de-duplication, screening titles and abstracts, and reviewing full articles for relevance.\n\n3. **Literature Search**: A comprehensive search was conducted on five databases (PubMed, Web of Science, Scopus, Embase, and Google Scholar) on April 9, 2024, to identify relevant studies.\n\nEntities mentioned include:\n- Authors: Viswanathan VK, Jain VK, Vaish A, Jayraman M, Iyengar KP, Vaishya R.\n- Publication: Apollo Medicine, Volume 21, Issue 4, Pages 386\u2013392, published on July 25, 2024.\n- License: Creative Commons Attribution-NonCommercial 4.0 License.\n- Databases used for literature search: PubMed, Web of Science, Scopus, Embase, Google Scholar. \n\nThe article emphasizes the significance of chatbots in healthcare and outlines the methodology for the review process."}, "excluded_embed_metadata_keys": ["file_name", "file_type", "file_size", "creation_date", "last_modified_date", "last_accessed_date"], "excluded_llm_metadata_keys": ["file_name", "file_type", "file_size", "creation_date", "last_modified_date", "last_accessed_date"], "relationships": {"1": {"node_id": "dad35ff1-58aa-45cb-b326-0446ff627bda", "node_type": "4", "metadata": {"file_path": "C:\\Users\\anteb\\PycharmProjects\\JupyterProject\\naive_rag\\data\\ai_articles\\Chatbots and Their Applications in Medical Fields Current Status.md", "file_name": "Chatbots and Their Applications in Medical Fields Current Status.md", "file_type": "text/markdown", "file_size": 28958, "creation_date": "2025-03-26", "last_modified_date": "2025-03-23"}, "hash": "4d0315e8f144da5f7f359eae5fa855cbb6e34f8f15c19f56fd0a30b52a22b7b2", "class_name": "RelatedNodeInfo"}, "2": {"node_id": "4742fd53-3b9e-4df0-85e2-2b9c0288a1af", "node_type": "1", "metadata": {"file_path": "C:\\Users\\anteb\\PycharmProjects\\JupyterProject\\naive_rag\\data\\ai_articles\\Chatbots and Their Applications in Medical Fields Current Status.md", "file_name": "Chatbots and Their Applications in Medical Fields Current Status.md", "file_type": "text/markdown", "file_size": 28958, "creation_date": "2025-03-26", "last_modified_date": "2025-03-23"}, "hash": "d660027551f5e23adc6d6cf002cf4db69231337f5a395671e972795edd24b7c1", "class_name": "RelatedNodeInfo"}, "3": {"node_id": "dc8337a3-ea31-4097-94a3-abb2b5204bc6", "node_type": "1", "metadata": {}, "hash": "1fab0d3edc87518b9b08774b46a3cacb20a434594e7a91f2c2e73b0c716e18f2", "class_name": "RelatedNodeInfo"}}, "metadata_template": "{key}: {value}", "metadata_separator": "\n", "text": "E-mail: drvijayortho@gmail.com\n\nCreative Commons Non Commercial CC BY-NC: This article is distributed under the terms of the Creative Commons Attribution-NonCommercial 4.0 License (http://www.creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc/4.0/) which permits non-commercial use, reproduction and distribution of the work without further permission provided the original work is attributed as specified on the Sage and Open Access pages (https://us.sagepub.com/en-us/nam/open-access-at-sage).\n\nApollo Medicine 21(4) 386\u2013392, 2024\n\n\u00a9 2024 The Author(s)\n\nSubmitted: 04-May-2024 Accepted: 17-May-2024 Published: 25-Jul-2024\n\nArticle reuse guidelines: in.sagepub.com/journals-permissions-india\n\nHow to cite this article: Viswanathan VK, Jain VK, Vaish A, Jayraman M, Iyengar KP and Vaishya R. Chatbots and their applications in medical fields: current status and future trends: A scoping review. Apollo Med 2024; 21:386\u2013392.\n---\n# Viswanathan, et al.: Chatbots and their applications in medical fields\n\n# Eligibility\n\nAll studies, which reported on chatbots and their applications across diverse medical specialities were considered. Opinions, editorials, letters, case reports and manuscripts published in non-English literature were excluded (Table 1).\n\n# Data Extraction\n\nThe outputs from our database search were extracted in Endnote and selected manually after de-duplication. Initially, the titles and abstracts of manuscripts were screened. During the next stage of screening, full articles were reviewed and finally the most relevant manuscripts were selected.\n\n# MeThODs\n\n# Literature Search\n\nA comprehensive literature search was conducted on five databases (PubMed, Web of Science, Scopus, Embase and Google Scholar) on 9 April 2024 to identify studies on the role of chatbots in medical science.", "mimetype": "text/plain", "start_char_idx": 3931, "end_char_idx": 5721, "metadata_seperator": "\n", "text_template": "[Excerpt from document]\n{metadata_str}\nExcerpt:\n-----\n{content}\n-----\n", "class_name": "TextNode"}, "__type__": "1"}, {"__data__": {"id_": "dc8337a3-ea31-4097-94a3-abb2b5204bc6", "embedding": null, "metadata": {"file_path": "C:\\Users\\anteb\\PycharmProjects\\JupyterProject\\naive_rag\\data\\ai_articles\\Chatbots and Their Applications in Medical Fields Current Status.md", "file_name": "Chatbots and Their Applications in Medical Fields Current Status.md", "file_type": "text/markdown", "file_size": 28958, "creation_date": "2025-03-26", "last_modified_date": "2025-03-23", "document_title": "\"Advancing Healthcare: A Comprehensive Review of Chatbots and AI in Medical Applications, Trends, Challenges, and Future Opportunities\"", "section_summary": "The section discusses the use of chatbots and artificial intelligence (AI) in the medical field, focusing on their applications, current status, and research findings. Key topics include:\n\n1. **Search Strategy**: The literature search utilized specific keywords related to AI and medical science, employing Boolean operators to refine results.\n\n2. **Results of Literature Search**: The search yielded 1,024 studies, which were narrowed down to 342 after de-duplication. Following title and abstract screening, 74 articles were reviewed, ultimately leading to the selection of 14 relevant articles for the final review.\n\n3. **Chatbot Models**: Various types of chatbots are discussed, highlighting their strengths and limitations. The choice of chatbot depends on the specific application requirements.\n\n4. **PRISMA Flow Diagram**: A flow diagram illustrates the process of article selection, detailing the number of records identified, screened, and excluded, culminating in the final selection of 14 articles.\n\n5. **Applications of Chatbots**: A table outlines specific applications of chatbots in medical science, including their roles in diabetes management and radiology.\n\nOverall, the section emphasizes the growing role of chatbots in healthcare, the methodology of the literature review, and the potential benefits of AI in medical applications."}, "excluded_embed_metadata_keys": ["file_name", "file_type", "file_size", "creation_date", "last_modified_date", "last_accessed_date"], "excluded_llm_metadata_keys": ["file_name", "file_type", "file_size", "creation_date", "last_modified_date", "last_accessed_date"], "relationships": {"1": {"node_id": "dad35ff1-58aa-45cb-b326-0446ff627bda", "node_type": "4", "metadata": {"file_path": "C:\\Users\\anteb\\PycharmProjects\\JupyterProject\\naive_rag\\data\\ai_articles\\Chatbots and Their Applications in Medical Fields Current Status.md", "file_name": "Chatbots and Their Applications in Medical Fields Current Status.md", "file_type": "text/markdown", "file_size": 28958, "creation_date": "2025-03-26", "last_modified_date": "2025-03-23"}, "hash": "4d0315e8f144da5f7f359eae5fa855cbb6e34f8f15c19f56fd0a30b52a22b7b2", "class_name": "RelatedNodeInfo"}, "2": {"node_id": "86cadabe-51ee-4b02-90ce-fff8112be8e4", "node_type": "1", "metadata": {"file_path": "C:\\Users\\anteb\\PycharmProjects\\JupyterProject\\naive_rag\\data\\ai_articles\\Chatbots and Their Applications in Medical Fields Current Status.md", "file_name": "Chatbots and Their Applications in Medical Fields Current Status.md", "file_type": "text/markdown", "file_size": 28958, "creation_date": "2025-03-26", "last_modified_date": "2025-03-23"}, "hash": "af6fe93c7a7e8b3497bf118acc721a2c3fa5638f15dfce5beacaa1a42e43e5ea", "class_name": "RelatedNodeInfo"}, "3": {"node_id": "0c584ee0-3280-4e31-b33a-7b98a03cd562", "node_type": "1", "metadata": {}, "hash": "84dedb0738e3df18792f59c35729823418ca27eab9f30396be27485df0717caf", "class_name": "RelatedNodeInfo"}}, "metadata_template": "{key}: {value}", "metadata_separator": "\n", "text": "The keywords employed in combination with Boolean operators for searching were (((artificial intelligence) OR (AI) OR (Chatbots) OR (ChatGPT) AND (Medical science) OR (Health care) OR (Surgery) OR (Hospital management))). Our search strategy is shown in the PRISMA flow chart (Figure 1).\n\n# ResUlTs\n\nOur literature search yielded 1,024 studies. After de-duplication of manuscripts, 342 articles were identified. After title and abstract screening, 74 articles were included for the next round of screening. Finally, 14 articles were selected (Figure 1).\n\n# Chatbot Models\n\nThere are different types of chatbots, with individual strengths and limitations. Some commonly used models are presented in Figure 2. The best choice for a chatbot depends upon the specific requirements of application. For example, while a...\n\n# Prisma Flow Diagram\n\n|Records Identified through search of 5 databases|n=1024| | | | |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n|Records after removal of duplicates| | |n=342| | |\n| | | |Records screened|n=74| |\n| | | | |Records excluded|n=268|\n|Full-text articles assessed for eligibility| | |n=34| | |\n|Records excluded; opinions or editorials| | | |14| |\n|Articles on non-relevant topics| | | |16| |\n|Duplicates| | | |4| |\n|Non-English literature| | |6| | |\n|Final articles selected for the review| | |n=14| | |\n\nFigure 1: PRISMA flow diagram\n\nApollo Medicine Volume 21 Issue 4 December 2024 387\n---\n# Viswanathan, et al.: Chatbots and their applications in medical fields\n\n# Table 1: Applications of Chatbots in medical science\n\n|1. Diabetes|To predict the future of diabetes technology|\n|---|---|\n|2. Radiology|Reporting, improved communication among patients, radiologist and referring physician, decision-making, analysis of research data|\n|3.", "mimetype": "text/plain", "start_char_idx": 5722, "end_char_idx": 7477, "metadata_seperator": "\n", "text_template": "[Excerpt from document]\n{metadata_str}\nExcerpt:\n-----\n{content}\n-----\n", "class_name": "TextNode"}, "__type__": "1"}, {"__data__": {"id_": "0c584ee0-3280-4e31-b33a-7b98a03cd562", "embedding": null, "metadata": {"file_path": "C:\\Users\\anteb\\PycharmProjects\\JupyterProject\\naive_rag\\data\\ai_articles\\Chatbots and Their Applications in Medical Fields Current Status.md", "file_name": "Chatbots and Their Applications in Medical Fields Current Status.md", "file_type": "text/markdown", "file_size": 28958, "creation_date": "2025-03-26", "last_modified_date": "2025-03-23", "document_title": "\"Advancing Healthcare: A Comprehensive Review of Chatbots and AI in Medical Applications, Trends, Challenges, and Future Opportunities\"", "section_summary": "The section discusses the applications of chatbots and AI in various medical fields, highlighting their roles in enhancing communication, decision-making, and patient education. Key topics include:\n\n1. **Applications in Specific Medical Fields**:\n   - **Radiology**: Improved reporting and communication among patients, radiologists, and referring physicians.\n   - **Cirrhosis and Hepatocellular Carcinoma**: Serving as an informational tool for both physicians and patients.\n   - **Mental Health**: Interpreting human behavior and emotions.\n   - **Cancer**: Extracting key content from medical records and offering clinical trial options.\n   - **Medical Education and Clinical Management**: Supporting documentation, communication, personalized learning, and research activities.\n   - **Obstetrics, Gynaecology, and Cardiology**: Handling clinical scenario-related queries and providing clinical decision support.\n   - **Urological Science**: Generating text to evaluate urological pathologies.\n   - **Orthopedics and Sports Medicine**: Assisting in activity tracking, diagnostic image evaluation, and injury risk prediction.\n\n2. **Types of Chatbots**:\n   - Differentiation between rule-based chatbots for simple tasks and conversational AI for complex medical diagnoses.\n   - Classification of generative models based on data type (visual, language, or a combination) and generation patterns (implicit or explicit).\n\n3. **Concerns and Considerations**:\n   - The potential negative impact of chatbots on the credibility and accuracy of published work.\n   - The importance of fact-checking information provided by chatbots to ensure authenticity.\n\n4. **Research Coordination**: Chatbots can facilitate research activities by organizing tasks such as data retrieval, organization, interpretation, and analysis.\n\nOverall, the section emphasizes the transformative potential of chatbots in healthcare while also acknowledging the need for careful validation of their outputs."}, "excluded_embed_metadata_keys": ["file_name", "file_type", "file_size", "creation_date", "last_modified_date", "last_accessed_date"], "excluded_llm_metadata_keys": ["file_name", "file_type", "file_size", "creation_date", "last_modified_date", "last_accessed_date"], "relationships": {"1": {"node_id": "dad35ff1-58aa-45cb-b326-0446ff627bda", "node_type": "4", "metadata": {"file_path": "C:\\Users\\anteb\\PycharmProjects\\JupyterProject\\naive_rag\\data\\ai_articles\\Chatbots and Their Applications in Medical Fields Current Status.md", "file_name": "Chatbots and Their Applications in Medical Fields Current Status.md", "file_type": "text/markdown", "file_size": 28958, "creation_date": "2025-03-26", "last_modified_date": "2025-03-23"}, "hash": "4d0315e8f144da5f7f359eae5fa855cbb6e34f8f15c19f56fd0a30b52a22b7b2", "class_name": "RelatedNodeInfo"}, "2": {"node_id": "dc8337a3-ea31-4097-94a3-abb2b5204bc6", "node_type": "1", "metadata": {"file_path": "C:\\Users\\anteb\\PycharmProjects\\JupyterProject\\naive_rag\\data\\ai_articles\\Chatbots and Their Applications in Medical Fields Current Status.md", "file_name": "Chatbots and Their Applications in Medical Fields Current Status.md", "file_type": "text/markdown", "file_size": 28958, "creation_date": "2025-03-26", "last_modified_date": "2025-03-23"}, "hash": "09c634dbab2d510fd53aae0178db5096b2cc543399440f079ee82721e44c4537", "class_name": "RelatedNodeInfo"}, "3": {"node_id": "e83eb62f-cf5c-404e-961a-672ff7c48ba9", "node_type": "1", "metadata": {}, "hash": "d0bf3edaa5755ab15c2eea1fe8f549ded3caaec1b3a9147ebdbca5450176c6ed", "class_name": "RelatedNodeInfo"}}, "metadata_template": "{key}: {value}", "metadata_separator": "\n", "text": "Radiology|Reporting, improved communication among patients, radiologist and referring physician, decision-making, analysis of research data|\n|3. Cirrhosis, and hepatocellular carcinoma|Informational tool for physicians and patients|\n|4. Mental health|Interpreting human behaviour and emotions|\n|5. Cancer|To extract key content from medical records|\n| |Offer the list of potential clinical trial options|\n| |Interpret next-generation sequencing results|\n|6. Medical education and clinical management|Documentation, communication with patients, support in decision-making, personalized learning, Research activities|\n|7. Obstetrics and gynaecology; cardiology|Model\u2019s ability to handle clinical scenario-related queries|\n| |Clinical decision support|\n| |Research support|\n| |Patient education|\n|8. Urological science|Text generation to evaluate urological pathologies and treatment in an understandable manner|\n|9. Orthopedics and sports medicine|ChatGPT-assisted activity tracking|\n| |Diagnostic image evaluation|\n| |Injury risk prediction|\n\n# Figure 2: Types of Chatbots with examples\n\nrule-based chatbot may be appropriate for a simple customer support application; conversational AI chatbots are necessary for complex medical diagnosis.\n\nSuch generative models are classified based on the type of data processed: visual [variational auto-encoder (VAE)], language [natural language processing (NLP) like ChatGPT] or combination of both (such as GPT4); or based upon the pattern in which they generate information (implicit or explicit models).\n\n388 Apollo Medicine Volume 21 Issue 4 December 2024\n---\n# Viswanathan, et al.: Chatbots and their applications in medical fields\n\nChatGPT academic writing and research; their potential negative impact on credibility, accuracy and authenticity of published work has also been acknowledged. In a review article by Vaishya et al., the need for researchers to fact-check all the statements provided by chatbots was emphasised to ascertain the authenticity of their work. Chatbots can organise research endeavours through coordination of activities such as retrieval, organisation, interpretation and analysis of data.", "mimetype": "text/plain", "start_char_idx": 7333, "end_char_idx": 9493, "metadata_seperator": "\n", "text_template": "[Excerpt from document]\n{metadata_str}\nExcerpt:\n-----\n{content}\n-----\n", "class_name": "TextNode"}, "__type__": "1"}, {"__data__": {"id_": "e83eb62f-cf5c-404e-961a-672ff7c48ba9", "embedding": null, "metadata": {"file_path": "C:\\Users\\anteb\\PycharmProjects\\JupyterProject\\naive_rag\\data\\ai_articles\\Chatbots and Their Applications in Medical Fields Current Status.md", "file_name": "Chatbots and Their Applications in Medical Fields Current Status.md", "file_type": "text/markdown", "file_size": 28958, "creation_date": "2025-03-26", "last_modified_date": "2025-03-23", "document_title": "\"Advancing Healthcare: A Comprehensive Review of Chatbots and AI in Medical Applications, Trends, Challenges, and Future Opportunities\"", "section_summary": "The section discusses the evolving role of chatbots and AI in the medical field, highlighting their applications, benefits, and challenges. Key topics include:\n\n1. **Evolution of Chatbots**: From early systems like Eliza to modern applications in various domains, including healthcare.\n2. **Clinical Benefits**: Chatbots assist in scheduling appointments, triaging patients, translating languages, evaluating symptoms, diagnosing conditions, and facilitating remote patient monitoring.\n3. **Administrative and Academic Roles**: They streamline hospital operations, enhance patient engagement, and improve relationships between healthcare providers and patients.\n4. **Mental Health and Education**: Chatbots support mental health initiatives and enhance educational curricula for medical trainees, while also providing health information to patients and the public.\n5. **Research and Academic Activities**: The potential of NLP and AI in academic writing and research is acknowledged, along with concerns regarding credibility and accuracy.\n\nEntities mentioned include specific chatbots like OneRemission, Babylon Health, and Wysa, which serve various healthcare needs."}, "excluded_embed_metadata_keys": ["file_name", "file_type", "file_size", "creation_date", "last_modified_date", "last_accessed_date"], "excluded_llm_metadata_keys": ["file_name", "file_type", "file_size", "creation_date", "last_modified_date", "last_accessed_date"], "relationships": {"1": {"node_id": "dad35ff1-58aa-45cb-b326-0446ff627bda", "node_type": "4", "metadata": {"file_path": "C:\\Users\\anteb\\PycharmProjects\\JupyterProject\\naive_rag\\data\\ai_articles\\Chatbots and Their Applications in Medical Fields Current Status.md", "file_name": "Chatbots and Their Applications in Medical Fields Current Status.md", "file_type": "text/markdown", "file_size": 28958, "creation_date": "2025-03-26", "last_modified_date": "2025-03-23"}, "hash": "4d0315e8f144da5f7f359eae5fa855cbb6e34f8f15c19f56fd0a30b52a22b7b2", "class_name": "RelatedNodeInfo"}, "2": {"node_id": "0c584ee0-3280-4e31-b33a-7b98a03cd562", "node_type": "1", "metadata": {"file_path": "C:\\Users\\anteb\\PycharmProjects\\JupyterProject\\naive_rag\\data\\ai_articles\\Chatbots and Their Applications in Medical Fields Current Status.md", "file_name": "Chatbots and Their Applications in Medical Fields Current Status.md", "file_type": "text/markdown", "file_size": 28958, "creation_date": "2025-03-26", "last_modified_date": "2025-03-23"}, "hash": "07ae9a164530639d3c5da9b8e5aef163e6bdaf9f6d419569920ce0431a5c003f", "class_name": "RelatedNodeInfo"}, "3": {"node_id": "4a1c9853-d932-4ea1-a571-69b8391ce903", "node_type": "1", "metadata": {}, "hash": "b5b38cba09fc32c5a24e012b6f6fdb5544a5376e9a4b92cff1dd85cce58055db", "class_name": "RelatedNodeInfo"}}, "metadata_template": "{key}: {value}", "metadata_separator": "\n", "text": "Chatbots can organise research endeavours through coordination of activities such as retrieval, organisation, interpretation and analysis of data.\n\n# Discussion\n\nSince the development of Eliza as one of the earliest conversational systems, chatbots have evolved as an integral part of diverse application domains. Apart from being used as personal assistants and service marketing tools, the development of state-of-the-art applications such as machine learning (ML) and deep learning (DL) have further expanded their utilities. In the realm of medical science, chatbots such as OneRemission (to help cancer survivors), Babylon Health (symptom evaluator) and Wysa (mental health chatbot) have been developed.\n\n# Clinical Benefits of Chatbots\n\nWith their language capabilities, chatbots and AI can be very helpful in scheduling patient appointments, triaging individuals in clinics and emergencies, as well as ensuring appropriate language translations and communications. In addition, they can be of substantial clinical importance in the evaluation of patients\u2019 symptoms and diagnosis of pathology. Such an ability of AI to process data and clinical presentations can be of utmost benefit in the decision-making process. AI can also be useful in organising regular patient surveillance, coordinating post-discharge care, arranging visits; and carrying out remote patient monitoring (telemedicine).\n\n# Administrative and Academic Roles of Chatbots\n\nAI can be useful in streamlining the routine tasks of hospital and clinic functioning. Chatbots can also be helpful in hospitality-related activities, patient engagement ventures and coordinated endeavours to improve interpersonal relationships between the provider and patients.\n\n# Role of Chatbots in Enhancement of Mental Health and Health Education\n\nChatbots can be beneficial in meliorating the curriculum for resident and trainee education. These applications can be effective in providing mental health support to patients, as well as enhancing their awareness through patient education strategies. They can be helpful in organising health-related information for the benefit of patients, physicians and general public.\n\n# Chatbots in Academic Activities and Research\n\nAlthough diverse researchers have highlighted the potential for NLP and AI technology to enhance the efficiency of academic writing and research; their potential negative impact on credibility, accuracy and authenticity of published work has also been acknowledged.", "mimetype": "text/plain", "start_char_idx": 9347, "end_char_idx": 11837, "metadata_seperator": "\n", "text_template": "[Excerpt from document]\n{metadata_str}\nExcerpt:\n-----\n{content}\n-----\n", "class_name": "TextNode"}, "__type__": "1"}, {"__data__": {"id_": "4a1c9853-d932-4ea1-a571-69b8391ce903", "embedding": null, "metadata": {"file_path": "C:\\Users\\anteb\\PycharmProjects\\JupyterProject\\naive_rag\\data\\ai_articles\\Chatbots and Their Applications in Medical Fields Current Status.md", "file_name": "Chatbots and Their Applications in Medical Fields Current Status.md", "file_type": "text/markdown", "file_size": 28958, "creation_date": "2025-03-26", "last_modified_date": "2025-03-23", "document_title": "\"Advancing Healthcare: A Comprehensive Review of Chatbots and AI in Medical Applications, Trends, Challenges, and Future Opportunities\"", "section_summary": "The section discusses the applications of chatbots and AI in the medical field, focusing on their roles in infectious diseases and oncology. \n\nKey Topics:\n1. **Inclusion and Exclusion Criteria**: The document outlines specific criteria for studies included in the review, emphasizing the importance of study design and language.\n2. **Infectious Disease Management**: \n   - The evaluation of ChatGPT's role in managing infectious diseases, particularly monkeypox and COVID-19.\n   - Benefits of chatbots include awareness creation, information dissemination, diagnosis, treatment, vaccine development, risk assessment, and protocol strategizing.\n   - Challenges highlighted include misinformation, inaccuracies, lack of human interaction, legal/ethical issues, patient accessibility, and language barriers.\n   - Emphasis on the need for proper training, validation, and surveillance to address these challenges.\n\n3. **Oncology**:\n   - Discussion of chatbots in monitoring various medical conditions, including oncological care.\n   - Noted absence of studies examining the safety of chatbot integration in healthcare.\n   - Recognition of AI's role in enhancing diagnostic and management processes, workflow efficiency, health promotion, and preventive strategies in cancer care.\n   - Mention of specific malignancies where AI technology has been evaluated, including hepatocellular carcinoma, breast cancer, prostate cancer, pancreatic cancer, brain malignancy, lung cancer, and cutaneous neoplasms.\n\nEntities:\n- **Cheng et al.**: Researchers evaluating ChatGPT's role in infectious diseases.\n- **Viswanathan et al.**: Authors discussing chatbot applications in medical fields.\n- **ChatGPT**: AI technology referenced in the context of infectious disease management.\n- **Various cancers**: Types of malignancies mentioned in relation to AI applications in oncology."}, "excluded_embed_metadata_keys": ["file_name", "file_type", "file_size", "creation_date", "last_modified_date", "last_accessed_date"], "excluded_llm_metadata_keys": ["file_name", "file_type", "file_size", "creation_date", "last_modified_date", "last_accessed_date"], "relationships": {"1": {"node_id": "dad35ff1-58aa-45cb-b326-0446ff627bda", "node_type": "4", "metadata": {"file_path": "C:\\Users\\anteb\\PycharmProjects\\JupyterProject\\naive_rag\\data\\ai_articles\\Chatbots and Their Applications in Medical Fields Current Status.md", "file_name": "Chatbots and Their Applications in Medical Fields Current Status.md", "file_type": "text/markdown", "file_size": 28958, "creation_date": "2025-03-26", "last_modified_date": "2025-03-23"}, "hash": "4d0315e8f144da5f7f359eae5fa855cbb6e34f8f15c19f56fd0a30b52a22b7b2", "class_name": "RelatedNodeInfo"}, "2": {"node_id": "e83eb62f-cf5c-404e-961a-672ff7c48ba9", "node_type": "1", "metadata": {"file_path": "C:\\Users\\anteb\\PycharmProjects\\JupyterProject\\naive_rag\\data\\ai_articles\\Chatbots and Their Applications in Medical Fields Current Status.md", "file_name": "Chatbots and Their Applications in Medical Fields Current Status.md", "file_type": "text/markdown", "file_size": 28958, "creation_date": "2025-03-26", "last_modified_date": "2025-03-23"}, "hash": "36a5b743667bd14ab4b1b6ccdeccc36c13a64cf2330d4cac8e8c73401f982372", "class_name": "RelatedNodeInfo"}, "3": {"node_id": "9a46d1a1-04c8-4ae2-9286-f3ffa1f78f18", "node_type": "1", "metadata": {}, "hash": "91009f639a39035a336f9ac3874d102a50c261cefa2106be53cee0a2cb368fcf", "class_name": "RelatedNodeInfo"}}, "metadata_template": "{key}: {value}", "metadata_separator": "\n", "text": "# Table 2: Inclusion and exclusion criteria\n\n|Inclusion criteria|Exclusion criteria| |\n|---|---|---|\n|Study design|Clinical trials, randomised controlled studies, and systematic review or meta-analysis|Case report, grey literature, and duplicate articles|\n|Language|English|Non-English|\n---\n# Viswanathan, et al.: Chatbots and their applications in medical fields\n\n# Infectious Disease\n\nRecently, Cheng et al.[39] evaluated the role of ChatGPT in the context of infectious diseases. The role of chatbots in the management of monkeypox and COVID-19 has been previously reported.[41,42] They recognised the huge potential of this technology in different aspects of managing infections, such as awareness creation, dissemination of information, diagnosis, treatment, vaccine development, risk assessment and strategising the protocols for risk mitigation.[41,43] However, they also emphasised the potential challenges including the potential for misinformation, inaccuracies in recommendations, deficiency of human interaction, legal or ethical issues (especially concerning patient confidentiality), patient accessibility and language barriers. Nevertheless, they emphasised that with proper training, validation and surveillance; the aforementioned challenges can be effectively addressed.[44]\n\n# Oncology\n\nA recent review[35] discussed the role of chatbots in monitoring diverse medical conditions such as oncological care, hypertension, asthma, varicose veins, orthopaedic pathologies and urological problems. They highlighted the fact that none of the reviewed studies examined the safety aspects of chatbot incorporation into healthcare. The role of AI technology in the management of oncological conditions has been widely acknowledged, especially since the management of these patients requires coordinated efforts of a multidisciplinary team. AI technology can meliorate the diagnostic and management processes, workflow efficiency related to patient monitoring, as well as health promotion and secondary preventive strategies in cancer patients.[36] AI technology has been evaluated in malignancies such as hepatocellular carcinoma, breast cancer, prostate cancer, pancreatic cancer, brain malignancy, lung cancer and cutaneous neoplasms.", "mimetype": "text/plain", "start_char_idx": 11839, "end_char_idx": 14084, "metadata_seperator": "\n", "text_template": "[Excerpt from document]\n{metadata_str}\nExcerpt:\n-----\n{content}\n-----\n", "class_name": "TextNode"}, "__type__": "1"}, {"__data__": {"id_": "9a46d1a1-04c8-4ae2-9286-f3ffa1f78f18", "embedding": null, "metadata": {"file_path": "C:\\Users\\anteb\\PycharmProjects\\JupyterProject\\naive_rag\\data\\ai_articles\\Chatbots and Their Applications in Medical Fields Current Status.md", "file_name": "Chatbots and Their Applications in Medical Fields Current Status.md", "file_type": "text/markdown", "file_size": 28958, "creation_date": "2025-03-26", "last_modified_date": "2025-03-23", "document_title": "\"Advancing Healthcare: A Comprehensive Review of Chatbots and AI in Medical Applications, Trends, Challenges, and Future Opportunities\"", "section_summary": "The section discusses the role of AI chatbots in various medical applications, highlighting several key topics:\n\n1. **Weight Reduction and Lifestyle Modifications**: AI chatbots are evaluated for their effectiveness in promoting health through physical activity, diet management, and lifestyle changes. The importance of standardizing chatbot design and monitoring for consistent results is emphasized.\n\n2. **Radiology**: The use of chatbots in radiology is explored, noting that generative AI models enhance imaging quality, data security, and MRI acquisition. These models also allow for editing and manipulation of data features, aiding in image processing.\n\n3. **Challenges of Chatbots**: Ethical and practical challenges associated with chatbots in medicine are discussed, including:\n   - Patient privacy breaches\n   - Infringement on patient autonomy\n   - Potential biases and unequal access\n   - Need for established protocols and diagnostic algorithms\n   - Medicolegal and ethical concerns regarding diagnostic errors\n   - Language biases and reduced physician empathy\n   - Disruption of interpersonal communication and transparency issues in decision-making\n\nThe need for a balance between automated decision-making and healthcare professional expertise is highlighted, along with the importance of having knowledgeable supervisors to optimize chatbot use. Additionally, a study is mentioned that compares AI applications using large language models (LLMs) to first-year orthopaedic residents, noting their performance and limitations.\n\nOverall, the section underscores the potential benefits of AI chatbots in healthcare while addressing significant challenges that need to be managed for effective implementation."}, "excluded_embed_metadata_keys": ["file_name", "file_type", "file_size", "creation_date", "last_modified_date", "last_accessed_date"], "excluded_llm_metadata_keys": ["file_name", "file_type", "file_size", "creation_date", "last_modified_date", "last_accessed_date"], "relationships": {"1": {"node_id": "dad35ff1-58aa-45cb-b326-0446ff627bda", "node_type": "4", "metadata": {"file_path": "C:\\Users\\anteb\\PycharmProjects\\JupyterProject\\naive_rag\\data\\ai_articles\\Chatbots and Their Applications in Medical Fields Current Status.md", "file_name": "Chatbots and Their Applications in Medical Fields Current Status.md", "file_type": "text/markdown", "file_size": 28958, "creation_date": "2025-03-26", "last_modified_date": "2025-03-23"}, "hash": "4d0315e8f144da5f7f359eae5fa855cbb6e34f8f15c19f56fd0a30b52a22b7b2", "class_name": "RelatedNodeInfo"}, "2": {"node_id": "4a1c9853-d932-4ea1-a571-69b8391ce903", "node_type": "1", "metadata": {"file_path": "C:\\Users\\anteb\\PycharmProjects\\JupyterProject\\naive_rag\\data\\ai_articles\\Chatbots and Their Applications in Medical Fields Current Status.md", "file_name": "Chatbots and Their Applications in Medical Fields Current Status.md", "file_type": "text/markdown", "file_size": 28958, "creation_date": "2025-03-26", "last_modified_date": "2025-03-23"}, "hash": "7241e6e6c718b7b4259fbdc7c8d8d279f93b059355a1867fd98e6296c16beeac", "class_name": "RelatedNodeInfo"}, "3": {"node_id": "7db3fb45-bbf6-4d9a-9b71-fcb495a38471", "node_type": "1", "metadata": {}, "hash": "3b33a9e520ac9a300eb9c1938371992f6ccb0177794ae1f81e4df466dfc31a8a", "class_name": "RelatedNodeInfo"}}, "metadata_template": "{key}: {value}", "metadata_separator": "\n", "text": "[37]\n\n# Weight Reduction and Lifestyle Modifications\n\nIn a recent review article,[45] the effectiveness of AI chatbots in improving general health of individuals through physical activity alterations, diet or weight management programmes and lifestyle modification strategies was evaluated. Based on their observations, although AI chatbots have tremendous potential to streamline such health-enhancing approaches; the standardisation of designing and monitoring the chatbot functioning is of foremost importance to achieve consistent results.\n\n# Radiology\n\nChatbots can be very useful in the field of radiology.[11,38] Generative AI models significantly improve the quality of imaging, enhance data security and expedite the acquisition of MRIs. In addition, inversion of generative models allows the editing and manipulation of features within latent space; and provides insights into the latent representations, thereby facilitating control over the generation of data as well as supporting image manipulation functions.[11]\n\n# Challenges of Chatbots\n\nIn the study by Valencia et al.,[5] ethical and practical challenges regarding the use of chatbots in medicine were discussed. The major considerations regarding the use of chatbots in the medical field include breaches in patient privacy, infringement on patient\u2019s autonomy and decision-making, potential bias, unequal access of patients to chatbots, need for establishing chatbot-based protocols and diagnostic algorithms, medicolegal and ethical concerns of errors in diagnosis, language biases, reduced physician empathy, disrupted interpersonal communications, and inadequate transparency in the decision-making process. They emphasised the need to establish a delicate balance between automated decision-making of digital assistants and the expertise of a healthcare professional. They also highlighted the need to involve a supervising individual, who is insightful and grasps the technical nuances regarding chatbots to ensure an optimal use of the technology.[46] They acknowledged that with continued research and improvements, the huge prospects of the digital communication platforms in meliorating healthcare delivery may be realised in the coming years. In another interesting study, it was concluded that AI applications using LLMs were comparable in terms of performance and knowledge to the first-year orthopaedic resident; and had a low likelihood to clear the Board Examination.[47,48] Ulusoy et al.", "mimetype": "text/plain", "start_char_idx": 14084, "end_char_idx": 16557, "metadata_seperator": "\n", "text_template": "[Excerpt from document]\n{metadata_str}\nExcerpt:\n-----\n{content}\n-----\n", "class_name": "TextNode"}, "__type__": "1"}, {"__data__": {"id_": "7db3fb45-bbf6-4d9a-9b71-fcb495a38471", "embedding": null, "metadata": {"file_path": "C:\\Users\\anteb\\PycharmProjects\\JupyterProject\\naive_rag\\data\\ai_articles\\Chatbots and Their Applications in Medical Fields Current Status.md", "file_name": "Chatbots and Their Applications in Medical Fields Current Status.md", "file_type": "text/markdown", "file_size": 28958, "creation_date": "2025-03-26", "last_modified_date": "2025-03-23", "document_title": "\"Advancing Healthcare: A Comprehensive Review of Chatbots and AI in Medical Applications, Trends, Challenges, and Future Opportunities\"", "section_summary": "The section discusses the role of chatbots and artificial intelligence (AI) in the medical field, highlighting their applications, limitations, and potential benefits. Key topics include:\n\n1. **Current Status of AI in Healthcare**: The excerpt references a study by Ulusoy et al. indicating that AI has not yet surpassed traditional methods in terms of reliability, precision, and readability for health-related information.\n\n2. **Limitations of the Study**: The authors acknowledge that their review has limitations due to its non-systematic nature, including a lack of specific inclusion strategies for assessing the methodological quality of the reviewed manuscripts. Various types of studies were included, but sample sizes were small, and there are gaps in understanding the evolving technology.\n\n3. **Conclusion on AI and Chatbots**: The authors conclude that AI can enhance the efficiency and accessibility of medical care while reducing costs and administrative burdens. However, they emphasize the need for high-quality studies to validate these technologies, ensure patient privacy, and improve accuracy.\n\n4. **Conflict of Interests and Funding**: The authors declare no conflicts of interest and state that they received no financial support for their work.\n\n5. **Author Contributions**: The credit author statement outlines the contributions of various authors in conceptualization, methodology, data curation, and writing.\n\n6. **Data Availability**: The study is categorized as a narrative review, and thus data availability is not applicable.\n\n7. **Use of AI Tools**: The authors utilized AI tools, specifically Grammarly and Bard, to enhance the manuscript's language quality.\n\nOverall, the section emphasizes the potential of chatbots in healthcare while recognizing the need for further research and validation."}, "excluded_embed_metadata_keys": ["file_name", "file_type", "file_size", "creation_date", "last_modified_date", "last_accessed_date"], "excluded_llm_metadata_keys": ["file_name", "file_type", "file_size", "creation_date", "last_modified_date", "last_accessed_date"], "relationships": {"1": {"node_id": "dad35ff1-58aa-45cb-b326-0446ff627bda", "node_type": "4", "metadata": {"file_path": "C:\\Users\\anteb\\PycharmProjects\\JupyterProject\\naive_rag\\data\\ai_articles\\Chatbots and Their Applications in Medical Fields Current Status.md", "file_name": "Chatbots and Their Applications in Medical Fields Current Status.md", "file_type": "text/markdown", "file_size": 28958, "creation_date": "2025-03-26", "last_modified_date": "2025-03-23"}, "hash": "4d0315e8f144da5f7f359eae5fa855cbb6e34f8f15c19f56fd0a30b52a22b7b2", "class_name": "RelatedNodeInfo"}, "2": {"node_id": "9a46d1a1-04c8-4ae2-9286-f3ffa1f78f18", "node_type": "1", "metadata": {"file_path": "C:\\Users\\anteb\\PycharmProjects\\JupyterProject\\naive_rag\\data\\ai_articles\\Chatbots and Their Applications in Medical Fields Current Status.md", "file_name": "Chatbots and Their Applications in Medical Fields Current Status.md", "file_type": "text/markdown", "file_size": 28958, "creation_date": "2025-03-26", "last_modified_date": "2025-03-23"}, "hash": "badfb5161bf5522ef7e92b7b7d2a513d606dbf338db7ec9b170ba3c1c87f3d4c", "class_name": "RelatedNodeInfo"}, "3": {"node_id": "0bcd1b6d-5975-4039-9d45-fccd978f8256", "node_type": "1", "metadata": {}, "hash": "b5bdf5263e650e0bd66859a73cbc9150e0baece93a38b97941e3b0300a54b702", "class_name": "RelatedNodeInfo"}}, "metadata_template": "{key}: {value}", "metadata_separator": "\n", "text": "[47,48] Ulusoy et al.[49] showed that AI has still not surpassed the traditional approaches for health-related information about reliability, precision and readability.\n\n390 Apollo Medicine Volume 21 Issue 4 December 2024\n---\n# Viswanathan, et al.: Chatbots and their applications in medical fields\n\n# LIMITATIONS\n\nOur study has limitations, which are inherent to non-systematic reviews. No specific inclusion strategy was employed to assess methodological quality of reviewed manuscripts. Retrospective, prospective, non-clinical, narrative reviews and biomechanical studies were included. The sample sizes were small. There are still substantial lacunae in our understanding of the technology and the technology continues to evolve at a rapid pace. Nevertheless, the current review comprehensively discusses the role of chatbots across diverse medical subspecialties.\n\n# CONCLUSION\n\nAI can be highly effective in streamlining routine functioning and activities in medical care across diverse specialities. Chatbots have the potential to meliorate the accessibility, efficiency and standard of medical care; as well as mitigate the expenditure and administrative difficulties in health care delivery. However, the need for validation through high-quality studies to ensure patient privacy, devise strategy to conform to patients\u2019 data security, enhance accuracy; and mitigate potential pitfalls in its routine utilisation is crucial.\n\n# DECLARATION OF CONFLICTING INTERESTS\n\nThe authors declared no potential conflicts of interest with respect to the research, authorship and/or publication of this article.\n\n# FUNDING\n\nThe authors received no financial support for the research, authorship and/or publication.\n\n# CREDIT AUTHOR STATEMENT\n\nVKJ, VKV and RV: Conceptualisation, methodology and software. VKV, VKJ, MJ and AV: Data curation and writing \u2013 original draft preparation. KPI and RV: Supervision. RV, VKJ, AV, MJ and KPI. RV: Writing \u2013 reviewing and editing.\n\n# DATA AVAILABILITY\n\nNot applicable (narrative review).\n\n# USE OF ARTIFICIAL INTELLIGENCE\n\nWe used Grammarly and Bard to improve the English and address the grammar and syntax errors of this manuscript.", "mimetype": "text/plain", "start_char_idx": 16536, "end_char_idx": 18705, "metadata_seperator": "\n", "text_template": "[Excerpt from document]\n{metadata_str}\nExcerpt:\n-----\n{content}\n-----\n", "class_name": "TextNode"}, "__type__": "1"}, {"__data__": {"id_": "0bcd1b6d-5975-4039-9d45-fccd978f8256", "embedding": null, "metadata": {"file_path": "C:\\Users\\anteb\\PycharmProjects\\JupyterProject\\naive_rag\\data\\ai_articles\\Chatbots and Their Applications in Medical Fields Current Status.md", "file_name": "Chatbots and Their Applications in Medical Fields Current Status.md", "file_type": "text/markdown", "file_size": 28958, "creation_date": "2025-03-26", "last_modified_date": "2025-03-23", "document_title": "\"Advancing Healthcare: A Comprehensive Review of Chatbots and AI in Medical Applications, Trends, Challenges, and Future Opportunities\"", "section_summary": "The section discusses the use of artificial intelligence (AI) in the medical field, specifically focusing on chatbots and their applications. It highlights the involvement of tools like Grammarly and Bard for improving the manuscript's language quality. The document includes an ORCID ID for Madhan Jeyaraman, indicating authorship and providing a unique identifier for academic contributions.\n\nAdditionally, the section lists several references that cover various aspects of AI in healthcare, including:\n\n1. The use of digital health technologies among South Asian communities.\n2. A systematic review protocol for AI applications in managing Parkinson's disease symptoms.\n3. An analysis of the benefi