 "creation_date": "2025-03-26", "last_modified_date": "2025-03-22", "document_title": "\"Navigating the Sustainable AI Trilemma: Balancing Energy Efficiency, Digital Equity, and Capability in Large Language Models and AI Agent Design\"", "section_summary": "The section discusses the challenges associated with the rapid development of large language models (LLMs), particularly focusing on their energy consumption and environmental impact. It introduces the concept of the \"Sustainable AI Trilemma,\" which emphasizes the trade-offs between capability, digital equity, and environmental sustainability in AI design. The text highlights that while LLM deployments prioritize performance, this often comes at the cost of equity and sustainability, especially with the increasing use of LLM-based agents in various domains such as enterprise automation and personalized assistance. The section raises concerns about the energy demands of LLM inference and the potential for exacerbating resource inequities and undermining global sustainability goals. It notes that current research primarily focuses on performance improvements, neglecting sustainability and equity metrics, and proposes using LLM agents as a case study to explore these challenges further. \n\nKey topics include:\n- Energy consumption of LLMs\n- Sustainable AI Trilemma (capability, digital equity, environmental sustainability)\n- LLM-based agents and their applications\n- Research focus on performance vs. sustainability and equity\n\nEntities mentioned:\n- Large Language Models (LLMs)\n- AI agents\n- Sustainability goals\n- Research metrics on performance, sustainability, and equity"}, "excluded_embed_metadata_keys": ["file_name", "file_type", "file_size", "creation_date", "last_modified_date", "last_accessed_date"], "excluded_llm_metadata_keys": ["file_name", "file_type", "file_size", "creation_date", "last_modified_date", "last_accessed_date"], "relationships": {"1": {"node_id": "5bbe2f3b-a5cf-473e-9e50-1d182dcad9fb", "node_type": "4", "metadata": {"file_path": "C:\\Users\\anteb\\PycharmProjects\\JupyterProject\\naive_rag\\data\\ai_articles\\ADDRESSING THE SUSTAINABLE AI TRILEMMA A CASE STUDY ON LLM AGENTS AND RAG.md", "file_name": "ADDRESSING THE SUSTAINABLE AI TRILEMMA A CASE STUDY ON LLM AGENTS AND RAG.md", "file_type": "text/markdown", "file_size": 72913, "creation_date": "2025-03-26", "last_modified_date": "2025-03-22"}, "hash": "47c48bbdebdc34c45547d1ddc9efb86758d4c6bea59cc0a6ae9786ef666e699e", "class_name": "RelatedNodeInfo"}, "2": {"node_id": "f9a4aab7-db90-49e2-9911-587e885a835e", "node_type": "1", "metadata": {"file_path": "C:\\Users\\anteb\\PycharmProjects\\JupyterProject\\naive_rag\\data\\ai_articles\\ADDRESSING THE SUSTAINABLE AI TRILEMMA A CASE STUDY ON LLM AGENTS AND RAG.md", "file_name": "ADDRESSING THE SUSTAINABLE AI TRILEMMA A CASE STUDY ON LLM AGENTS AND RAG.md", "file_type": "text/markdown", "file_size": 72913, "creation_date": "2025-03-26", "last_modified_date": "2025-03-22"}, "hash": "66f959adaa79ebdfd8a6f6e0c627a6f079e3a8cf67202508567c13f6ed667dbb", "class_name": "RelatedNodeInfo"}, "3": {"node_id": "30054381-b1c2-488b-ae41-72a0ab77e7ce", "node_type": "1", "metadata": {}, "hash": "1868feb6dd9a5366bbf460f7cdd0f946efafe6d7f4cfb18036c7661cbdd4914a", "class_name": "RelatedNodeInfo"}}, "metadata_template": "{key}: {value}", "metadata_separator": "\n", "text": "However, this rapid development comes with significant challenges, particularly in terms of energy use and its impact on the environment and society. While the energy consumption of LLM training has received much attention[1], recent evidence[2][3][4] suggests that the inference phase, particularly in large-scale deployments, has emerged as the dominant contributor to the overall energy footprint, highlighting a critical and growing concern in the sustainability of LLM-powered services.\n\nIn response to these challenges, we propose the concept of the Sustainable AI Trilemma, which draws parallels to the sustainability trilemma balancing Economy, Society, and Environment[5]. In the AI context, this trilemma highlights the trade-offs among capability, digital equity, and environmental sustainability. Current LLM deployments heavily prioritize capability\u2014pushing for greater model performance and broader applications\u2014often at the expense of equity and sustainability. This imbalance is made worse by the increasing use of LLM-based agents, and the scaling of multi-agents, which applies LLMs in a frequent manner for long-term operations.\n\nLLM-based agents, capable of executing complex, multi-step tasks, are increasingly integrated across domains such as enterprise automation, scientific research, and personalized assistance[6]. This paradigm amplifies the energy demands of LLM inference, raising urgent questions about the sustainable scalability of such AI services. Without deliberate attention to energy efficiency, the widespread use of these agents risks accelerating resource inequities[7] and undermining global sustainability goals[8].\n\nhttps://github.com/huiwxing/llmagent_trilemma\n---\n# A PREPRINT - JANUARY 15, 2025\n\nDespite these challenges, most research on LLM agents focuses on improving performance, with little consideration of sustainability or equity metrics. As a first step toward addressing the trilemma, we use LLM agents as a case study to better understand these challenges.", "mimetype": "text/plain", "start_char_idx": 1757, "end_char_idx": 3771, "metadata_seperator": "\n", "text_template": "[Excerpt from document]\n{metadata_str}\nExcerpt:\n-----\n{content}\n-----\n", "class_name": "TextNode"}, "__type__": "1"}, {"__data__": {"id_": "30054381-b1c2-488b-ae41-72a0ab77e7ce", "embedding": [0.022200392559170723, 0.039042070508003235, 0.04740822687745094, -0.004975949879735708, 0.04932205379009247, 0.042404938489198685, 0.022104701027274132, 0.03299984335899353, 0.0326991006731987, 0.031140698119997978, 0.019698746502399445, -0.06906181573867