103188, -0.03938554599881172, 0.018175821751356125, -0.016038300469517708, -0.0035786235239356756, 0.008612140081822872, -0.014962645247578621, 0.02912544459104538, 0.0313870795071125], "metadata": {"file_path": "C:\\Users\\anteb\\PycharmProjects\\JupyterProject\\naive_rag\\data\\ai_articles\\ADDRESSING THE SUSTAINABLE AI TRILEMMA A CASE STUDY ON LLM AGENTS AND RAG.md", "file_name": "ADDRESSING THE SUSTAINABLE AI TRILEMMA A CASE STUDY ON LLM AGENTS AND RAG.md", "file_type": "text/markdown", "file_size": 72913, "creation_date": "2025-03-26", "last_modified_date": "2025-03-22", "document_title": "\"Navigating the Sustainable AI Trilemma: Balancing Energy Efficiency, Digital Equity, and Capability in Large Language Models and AI Agent Design\"", "section_summary": "The section discusses the energy efficiency and environmental impact of large language models (LLMs) and AI agents, particularly focusing on the challenges posed by their deployment and operation. Key topics include:\n\n1. **Carbon Emissions**: The significant carbon emissions associated with AI services, highlighting that inference-related emissions for models like ChatGPT can be 25 times greater than those from training GPT-3.\n\n2. **Measurement Tools**: The development of specialized tools for estimating computational carbon footprints, such as Green Algorithms and LLMCarbon, which provide predictions for carbon emissions in various AI architectures.\n\n3. **Empirical Studies**: Research on LLM inference energy consumption, including large-scale testing of LLaMA models across different GPU platforms and the creation of predictive models for energy consumption and runtime.\n\n4. **Factors Affecting Energy Consumption**: Exploration of how model size, layer count, and optimization techniques (like batch processing and quantization) influence energy usage.\n\n5. **Sustainability Awareness**: Evaluation of the sustainability of commercial AI models, including GitHub Copilot and ChatGPT-3, and the need to consider trade-offs in optimizing AI energy consumption.\n\n6. **Memory Modules in LLM Agents**: The section introduces the memory module as a transformative feature in LLM agents, discussing the challenges it presents in terms of energy efficiency and design complexity.\n\nOverall, the excerpt emphasizes the importance of addressing the energy and environmental costs associated with the deployment of AI technologies while exploring potential inefficiencies in their design and operations."}, "excluded_embed_metadata_keys": ["file_name", "file_type", "file_size", "creation_date", "last_modified_date", "last_accessed_date"], "excluded_llm_metadata_keys": ["file_name", "file_type", "file_size", "creation_date", "last_modified_date", "last_accessed_date"], "relationships": {"1": {"node_id": "5bbe2f3b-a5cf-473e-9e50-1d182dcad9fb", "node_type": "4", "metadata": {"file_path": "C:\\Users\\anteb\\PycharmProjects\\JupyterProject\\naive_rag\\data\\ai_articles\\ADDRESSING THE SUSTAINABLE AI TRILEMMA A CASE STUDY ON LLM AGENTS AND RAG.md", "file_name": "ADDRESSING THE SUSTAINABLE AI TRILEMMA A CASE STUDY ON LLM AGENTS AND RAG.md", "file_type": "text/markdown", "file_size": 72913, "creation_date": "2025-03-26", "last_modified_date": "2025-03-22"}, "hash": "47c48bbdebdc34c45547d1ddc9efb86758d4c6bea59cc0a6ae9786ef666e699e", "class_name": "RelatedNodeInfo"}, "2": {"node_id": "83d2eb9c-9fb8-4b6d-9e75-2509be0b4bed", "node_type": "1", "metadata": {"file_path": "C:\\Users\\anteb\\PycharmProjects\\JupyterProject\\naive_rag\\data\\ai_articles\\ADDRESSING THE SUSTAINABLE AI TRILEMMA A CASE STUDY ON LLM AGENTS AND RAG.md", "file_name": "ADDRESSING THE SUSTAINABLE AI TRILEMMA A CASE STUDY ON LLM AGENTS AND RAG.md", "file_type": "text/markdown", "file_size": 72913, "creation_date": "2025-03-26", "last_modified_date": "2025-03-22"}, "hash": "9f19bd26b34068f958ce8c0d1245caafdada7d0fcdd44b88b4513e334d10a31d", "class_name": "RelatedNodeInfo"}, "3": {"node_id": "8dc10db9-10b4-4290-a5cf-18d625c4d261", "node_type": "1", "metadata": {}, "hash": "7dddb43fdf99eb7f5bf9ed9320d2269034578daf326aeb7f1df307eb14e7db06", "class_name": "RelatedNodeInfo"}}, "metadata_template": "{key}: {value}", "metadata_separator": "\n", "text": "[3] suggests that the multiplicative effect due to widespread AI deployment remains a crucial consideration. Studies in [2] have shown that for services like ChatGPT, inference-related carbon emissions in one year amount to 25 times that of GPT-3\u2019s training. This trend has driven the development of specialized measurement tools and analysis. Green Algorithms [13] provides an online platform for estimating computational carbon footprints, while LLMCarbon [8] specifically addresses LLM scenarios, offering end-to-end carbon emission predictions for both dense and mixture-of-experts (MoE) architectures.\n\nEmpirical studies have extensively investigated LLM inference energy consumption. [26] have conducted large-scale testing of LLaMA models across different GPU platforms (V100 and A100), including scenarios with up to 32-GPU model sharding. Through comprehensive characterization studies of mainstream LLMs, Wilkins et al. [27] have developed high-accuracy (R2 > 0.96) models for energy consumption and runtime prediction. Further research [28] has explored how factors such as model size, layer count, parallel attention mechanisms affect energy consumption, along with optimization techniques like batch processing and quantization. In code generation, Vartziotis et al. [29] have evaluated the sustainability awareness of commercial AI models like GitHub Copilot, ChatGPT-3, and Amazon CodeWhisperer. What\u2019s more, the optimization of AI energy consumption requires careful consideration of trade-offs - while some AI models aim to reduce carbon emissions, their training and deployment also generate environmental impacts, yielding positive net benefits only under appropriate scenarios [30].\n\n# 3 Computational and Energy Costs in LLM-based Agents: A Case Study on Memory Modules\n\nThe memory module is a transformative feature in LLM agents. However, the incorporation of memory introduces notable challenges in energy efficiency and design complexity. In this section, we take the memory module of LLM-based agents as an example to explore potential inefficiencies in resource utilization within their design and operations.", "mimetype": "text/plain", "start_char_idx": 12188, "end_char_idx": 14324, "metadata_seperator": "\n", "text_template": "[Excerpt from document]\n{metadata_str}\nExcerpt:\n-----\n{content}\n-----\n", "class_name": "TextNode"}, "__type__": "1"}, {"__data__": {"id_": "8dc10db9-10b4-4290-a5cf-18d625c4d261", "embedding": [0.0035605751909315586, 0.04117416962981224, 0.0514543280005455, 0.00726169953122735, 0.015607633627951145, 0.0373726524412632, 0.033571138978004456, 0.010380549356341362, -0.007141228765249252, 0.05547001212835312, 0.019543007016181946, -0.07913579046726227, -0.008024679496884346, -0.02829720266163349, -0.03745296597480774, -0.03825610503554344, 0.0038517124485224485, -0.01635722815990448, -0.005320784170180559, 0.022554771974682808, 0.06425098329782486, 0.045029230415821075, 0.022300444543361664, 0.029341280460357666, 0.02765469253063202, -0.008292391896247864, 0.004892444238066673, 0.038470275700092316, -0.019422536715865135, -0.02872554212808609, 0.06773123890161514, -0.02493741177022457, -0.0410938560962677, -0.01667848415672779, 0.0011478170054033399, 0.0920930728316307, 0.01596904546022415, 0.013894274830818176, 0.006023529451340437, 0.026061803102493286, -0.02374609187245369, -0.05777233839035034, -0.024843713268637657, 0.02346499264240265, -0.028939712792634964, 0.03954112529754639, -0.020453229546546936, 0.035980548709630966, -0.035150639712810516, 0.045618195086717606, -0.03900570049881935, -0.005561725702136755, 0.024428758770227432, -0.007930980063974857, -0.002685490297153592, -0.017401307821273804, 0.022380758076906204, -0.005217045545578003, 0.029689306393265724, -0.00