510933, -0.006400872953236103, -0.015440606512129307, -0.005997410975396633, 0.04222177341580391, 0.0022881031036376953, 0.020253075286746025, -0.00883255060762167, 0.009116064757108688, -0.023262685164809227, -0.010882575064897537, 0.0015447876648977399, -0.012772668153047562, 0.004605284426361322, -0.009886641055345535, 0.028947502374649048, -0.025167316198349, 0.007429520133882761, -0.0027933395467698574, -0.01230741385370493, 0.02079102396965027, 0.006742543540894985], "metadata": {"file_path": "C:\\Users\\anteb\\PycharmProjects\\JupyterProject\\naive_rag\\data\\ai_articles\\ADDRESSING THE SUSTAINABLE AI TRILEMMA A CASE STUDY ON LLM AGENTS AND RAG.md", "file_name": "ADDRESSING THE SUSTAINABLE AI TRILEMMA A CASE STUDY ON LLM AGENTS AND RAG.md", "file_type": "text/markdown", "file_size": 72913, "creation_date": "2025-03-26", "last_modified_date": "2025-03-22", "document_title": "\"Navigating the Sustainable AI Trilemma: Balancing Energy Efficiency, Digital Equity, and Capability in Large Language Models and AI Agent Design\"", "section_summary": "The section discusses the capabilities and challenges associated with large language models (LLMs) in the context of zero-shot ranking and retrieval-augmented generation (RAG). Key topics include:\n\n1. **LLM Performance**: Zhuang et al. highlight that LLMs like LLaMA and Falcon, when pre-trained on unstructured text, demonstrate strong zero-shot ranking abilities.\n\n2. **Prompt Engineering**: Various methods (LRL, UPR, RankGPT, PRP) utilize prompt engineering with models such as GPT-3/4, UL2, and T5 for zero-shot passage reranking. Different prompt types are employed, including query generation and relevance generation.\n\n3. **Computational Costs**: While prompt-based approaches enhance agent autonomy and reduce fine-tuning needs, they introduce additional computational costs during inference due to the simultaneous processing of multiple documents.\n\n4. **LLM Reranker Structure**: The basic structure of an LLM reranker is outlined, detailing inputs (new queries and retrieved memories), outputs (ranked indices), and energy consumption associated with the reranking process.\n\n5. **Compression Strategies**: After retrieval and reranking, documents may still exceed LLM input capacity, necessitating compression strategies. Current LLM-based summarization and specialized compression models (e.g., PRCA, RECOMP) are noted for their performance but also for generating more output tokens and consuming more energy compared to simpler methods.\n\nOverall, the section emphasizes the balance between enhancing LLM capabilities and managing computational efficiency, particularly in the context of energy consumption and output generation."}, "excluded_embed_metadata_keys": ["file_name", "file_type", "file_size", "creation_date", "last_modified_date", "last_accessed_date"], "excluded_llm_metadata_keys": ["file_name", "file_type", "file_size", "creation_date", "last_modified_date", "last_accessed_date"], "relationships": {"1": {"node_id": "5bbe2f3b-a5cf-473e-9e50-1d182dcad9fb", "node_type": "4", "metadata": {"file_path": "C:\\Users\\anteb\\PycharmProjects\\JupyterProject\\naive_rag\\data\\ai_articles\\ADDRESSING THE SUSTAINABLE AI TRILEMMA A CASE STUDY ON LLM AGENTS AND RAG.md", "file_name": "ADDRESSING THE SUSTAINABLE AI TRILEMMA A CASE STUDY ON LLM AGENTS AND RAG.md", "file_type": "text/markdown", "file_size": 72913, "creation_date": "2025-03-26", "last_modified_date": "2025-03-22"}, "hash": "47c48bbdebdc34c45547d1ddc9efb86758d4c6bea59cc0a6ae9786ef666e699e", "class_name": "RelatedNodeInfo"}, "2": {"node_id": "f06db60f-469c-43d1-acb7-7fd61f89df0b", "node_type": "1", "metadata": {"file_path": "C:\\Users\\anteb\\PycharmProjects\\JupyterProject\\naive_rag\\data\\ai_articles\\ADDRESSING THE SUSTAINABLE AI TRILEMMA A CASE STUDY ON LLM AGENTS AND RAG.md", "file_name": "ADDRESSING THE SUSTAINABLE AI TRILEMMA A CASE STUDY ON LLM AGENTS AND RAG.md", "file_type": "text/markdown", "file_size": 72913, "creation_date": "2025-03-26", "last_modified_date": "2025-03-22"}, "hash": "48fb9b40cd7ec7dcf6925376072cee6d2af48182adfd2532d350b5cda3006a9d", "class_name": "RelatedNodeInfo"}, "3": {"node_id": "0bb3548f-8125-42ec-bcdc-d6bff9226013", "node_type": "1", "metadata": {}, "hash": "269a5efb6844572de339c3a73345fa214f7d4112f1ec8c2a837dddd7c4d7f42f", "class_name": "RelatedNodeInfo"}}, "metadata_template": "{key}: {value}", "metadata_separator": "\n", "text": "Zhuang et al. [51] demonstrated that LLMs pre-trained on unstructured text, like LLaMA and Falcon, exhibit strong zero-shot ranking capabilities. These methods such as LRL [52], UPR [53], RankGPT [54], PRP [55] leverage prompt engineering with models like GPT-3/4, UL2, and T5 for zero-shot passage reranking, employing various prompt types, including query generation, relevance generation [56], pairwise ranking, and permutation for ranked lists. Although prompt-based approaches reduce fine-tuning overhead and enhance agent autonomy, they themselves introduce extra computational costs during inference by processing multiple retrieved documents simultaneously. However, similar to Retrieval Necessity Detection, considering that reranker outputs are typically concise (ranked indices or scores, a small constant of output token numbers), these approaches have the potential to be cost-effective with moderate number of documents retrieved.\n\nThe basic form of LLM reranker is as follows (if using permutation generation prompting):\n\n- Input: New query or queries, Retrieved related memories (token number = Tqn + Tm).\n- Output: Ranked index (token number = K, where K represents the number of top-K retrieved entries).\n- Energy: E3 = eK(Tqn + Tm, K) = \u03b1K,0 \u2217 (Tqn + Tm) + \u03b1K,1 \u2217 K + \u03b1K,2 \u2217 (Tqn + Tm) \u2217 K\n\n# Compression\n\nAfter retrieval and reranking, relevant documents may still exceed LLM input capacity and introduce noise. While compression strategies can help by extracting essential information, current approaches face significant trade-offs. LLM-based summarization and specialized compression models (e.g. PRCA [57], RECOMP [58]) achieve good performance, but generate significantly more output tokens and consume more energy (owing to the large output coefficient \u03b1K,1 and the number of output tokens \u03b2 \u2217 Tm, and the square interaction term \u03b2 \u2217 T2) than simpler steps like retrieval detection or reranking.", "mimetype": "text/plain", "start_char_idx": 23640, "end_char_idx": 25561, "metadata_seperator": "\n", "text_template": "[Excerpt from document]\n{metadata_str}\nExcerpt:\n-----\n{content}\n-----\n", "class_name": "TextNode"}, "__type__": "1"}, {"__data__": {"id_": "0bb3548f-8125-42ec-bcdc-d6bff9226013", "embedding": [0.019441885873675346, 0.027847683057188988, 0.05271715298295021, -0.0006358276004903018, 0.02634465880692005, 0.024117957800626755, 0.04662155732512474, 0.02548181265592575, -3.751034819288179e-05, 0.05460984632372856, 0.04314233735203743, -0.044227853417396545, -0.031758327037096024, -0.01887129247188568, -0.042780496180057526, -0.0519934743642807, -0.004759573843330145, -0.0287522803992033, 0.0013403698103502393, 0.02651166170835495, 0.024674633517861366, 0.005090099759399891, 0.03095114789903164, 0.06446300446987152, 0.012553027831017971, -0.007515116594731808, 0.01914963126182556, 0.03401286154985428, 0.015726078301668167, -0.04754007235169411, 0.050462618470191956, -0.030422305688261986, -0.034792207181453705, -0.03593339025974274, 0.008976389653980732, 0.07465016096830368, 0.0063321818597614765, -0.012434734962880611, 0.0036636192817240953, 0.01766052469611168, -0.013756838627159595, -0.045536041259765625, -0.042641330510377884, 0.041639313101768494, -0.020221231505274773, 0.03353968635201454, 0.005531961098313332, 0.009407812729477882, -0.014654477126896381, 0.051381129771471024, -0.02602457068860531, 0.018773874267935753, 0.020374316722154617, -0.010333285667002201, -0.007522074971348047, -0.0074733658693730