614387333393097, 0.00235216086730361, -0.02134299837052822, 0.03385339304804802, 0.007322346791625023], "metadata": {"file_path": "C:\\Users\\anteb\\PycharmProjects\\JupyterProject\\naive_rag\\data\\ai_articles\\ADDRESSING THE SUSTAINABLE AI TRILEMMA A CASE STUDY ON LLM AGENTS AND RAG.md", "file_name": "ADDRESSING THE SUSTAINABLE AI TRILEMMA A CASE STUDY ON LLM AGENTS AND RAG.md", "file_type": "text/markdown", "file_size": 72913, "creation_date": "2025-03-26", "last_modified_date": "2025-03-22", "document_title": "\"Navigating the Sustainable AI Trilemma: Balancing Energy Efficiency, Digital Equity, and Capability in Large Language Models and AI Agent Design\"", "section_summary": "The section discusses the evaluation of energy efficiency in large language models (LLMs) and AI agents through specific metrics. It introduces three key energy efficiency ratios: \n\n1. **Generation Energy-to-Relevance Ratio (GERR)** - Measures energy cost per token in relation to relevance.\n2. **Energy-to-Faithfulness Ratio (EFR)** - Assesses energy cost per token concerning faithfulness.\n3. **Energy-to-Correctness Ratio (ECR)** - Evaluates energy cost per token in terms of correctness.\n\nLower values of these ratios indicate better energy efficiency in achieving effectiveness goals. \n\nAdditionally, the section outlines the experimental settings for evaluating these models, specifying the datasets used:\n- **LoCoMo** for internal memory tasks.\n- **HotpotQA** and **MuSiQue** for external memory tasks focusing on multi-hop reasoning.\n- **AI ArXiv** dataset for retrieval-augmented generation tasks.\n\nThe experiments aim to analyze the performance of LLMs in various contexts, with a focus on energy efficiency and effectiveness in generating responses."}, "excluded_embed_metadata_keys": ["file_name", "file_type", "file_size", "creation_date", "last_modified_date", "last_accessed_date"], "excluded_llm_metadata_keys": ["file_name", "file_type", "file_size", "creation_date", "last_modified_date", "last_accessed_date"], "relationships": {"1": {"node_id": "5bbe2f3b-a5cf-473e-9e50-1d182dcad9fb", "node_type": "4", "metadata": {"file_path": "C:\\Users\\anteb\\PycharmProjects\\JupyterProject\\naive_rag\\data\\ai_articles\\ADDRESSING THE SUSTAINABLE AI TRILEMMA A CASE STUDY ON LLM AGENTS AND RAG.md", "file_name": "ADDRESSING THE SUSTAINABLE AI TRILEMMA A CASE STUDY ON LLM AGENTS AND RAG.md", "file_type": "text/markdown", "file_size": 72913, "creation_date": "2025-03-26", "last_modified_date": "2025-03-22"}, "hash": "47c48bbdebdc34c45547d1ddc9efb86758d4c6bea59cc0a6ae9786ef666e699e", "class_name": "RelatedNodeInfo"}, "2": {"node_id": "9e15e5de-c507-48a0-bc3f-e241cf83c94f", "node_type": "1", "metadata": {"file_path": "C:\\Users\\anteb\\PycharmProjects\\JupyterProject\\naive_rag\\data\\ai_articles\\ADDRESSING THE SUSTAINABLE AI TRILEMMA A CASE STUDY ON LLM AGENTS AND RAG.md", "file_name": "ADDRESSING THE SUSTAINABLE AI TRILEMMA A CASE STUDY ON LLM AGENTS AND RAG.md", "file_type": "text/markdown", "file_size": 72913, "creation_date": "2025-03-26", "last_modified_date": "2025-03-22"}, "hash": "423ebd6017d03a2cab30e7eae46d8aced86771ad34b4a37735d9ac6203527c0b", "class_name": "RelatedNodeInfo"}, "3": {"node_id": "6e70e8eb-c370-4e9c-b167-70de6fb3d551", "node_type": "1", "metadata": {}, "hash": "ea17e5d27038087659d94a9f7ebdbc281f4a8b61f45796e522b98851e84efcde", "class_name": "RelatedNodeInfo"}}, "metadata_template": "{key}: {value}", "metadata_separator": "\n", "text": "For generation efficiency, we measure the average latency per response token and energy consumption per token Etoken_g which is the division of Ereal by response token number. Similarly, there are three energy efficiency ratios:\n\n# Generation Energy-to-Relevance Ratio (GERR)\n\nGERR = Etoken_g\nRelevance\n\n# Energy-to-Faithfulness Ratio (EFR)\n\nEFR = Etoken_g\nFaithfulness\n\n# Energy-to-Correctness Ratio (ECR)\n\nECR = Etoken_g\nCorrectness\n\nThese ratios quantify the energy cost required to achieve unit improvement in each generation effectiveness dimension. Lower values indicate better energy efficiency in achieving the respective effectiveness goals.\n---\n# A PREPRINT - JANUARY 15, 2025\n\n# 4 Experiments\n\n# 4.1 Experiment settings\n\nDatasets. For Internal Memory, we treat the task as that of a daily assistant agent and utilize the LoCoMo [63] dataset, which comprises very long-term dialogues. For External Memory, we use HotpotQA [64] and MuSiQue [65], which are well-known datasets for evaluating multi-hop and complex reasoning abilities in retrieval-augmented tasks. Additionally, we incorporate the AI ArXiv dataset [66], with 100 pairs of queries and answers on topics of RAG extracted by Eibich et al. [67]. We follow the same node splitting configuration as in Eibich\u2019s experiments. For all datasets we sampled some data and the characteristics of these sub-datasets are shown in Table 1.", "mimetype": "text/plain", "start_char_idx": 32828, "end_char_idx": 34225, "metadata_seperator": "\n", "text_template": "[Excerpt from document]\n{metadata_str}\nExcerpt:\n-----\n{content}\n-----\n", "class_name": "TextNode"}, "__type__": "1"}, {"__data__": {"id_": "6e70e8eb-c370-4e9c-b167-70de6fb3d551", "embedding": [0.012732473202049732, 0.012295889668166637, 0.07998769730329514, -0.030804138630628586, 0.05196051299571991, 0.014672045595943928, 0.0004341225721873343, 0.028041500598192215, 0.012825515121221542, 0.028184641152620316, 0.015917379409074783, -0.05831601470708847, -0.05090126395225525, -0.024921005591750145, -0.022487593814730644, -0.021170688793063164, -0.023146046325564384, -0.04122487083077431, -0.012238632887601852, 0.034296803176403046, 0.035413309931755066, 0.017678026109933853, 0.04050916060805321, 0.014042221009731293, 0.020197322592139244, 0.004151115193963051, 0.035785477608442307, 0.04843922331929207, 0.012796886265277863, -0.017348799854516983, 0.03678746894001961, -0.018823161721229553, -0.025006892159581184, -0.020011238753795624, -