e": 72913, "creation_date": "2025-03-26", "last_modified_date": "2025-03-22", "document_title": "\"Navigating the Sustainable AI Trilemma: Balancing Energy Efficiency, Digital Equity, and Capability in Large Language Models and AI Agent Design\"", "section_summary": "The section discusses a study on the performance evaluation of large language models (LLMs) and AI agents, focusing on the sustainable AI trilemma, which involves balancing energy efficiency, digital equity, and capability. Key topics include:\n\n1. **Node Splitting Configuration**: The study follows a specific configuration based on previous experiments by Eibich, detailing the characteristics of various datasets used in the evaluation.\n\n2. **Dataset Characteristics**: A comparison table (Table 1) outlines the characteristics of four datasets: LoCoMo, HotpotQA, MuSiQue, and AI ArXiv, including metrics such as memory node number, memory node size, query types, difficulty, query length, answer length, and scenario.\n\n3. **Evaluation Metrics**: The evaluation of LLMs is conducted using various metrics, including Retrieval-Relevance, Generation-Relevance, and Faithfulness, utilizing UpTrain\u2019s LLM-based scorer with the GPT-4 API. Specific measures include Mean Reciprocal Rank (MRR), Recall, and ROUGE for assessing performance.\n\n4. **Hardware Configurations**: The study employs two hardware setups for performance evaluation: a resource-constrained environment with a single NVIDIA GeForce RTX 4080 GPU and an Intel i7-13700K processor, and a resource-abundant environment with a cluster of eight NVIDIA A100 GPUs and two AMD EPYC 7J13 processors, highlighting the differences in computing capabilities.\n\nOverall, the section emphasizes the methodology for evaluating LLM performance in relation to sustainability and resource management."}, "excluded_embed_metadata_keys": ["file_name", "file_type", "file_size", "creation_date", "last_modified_date", "last_accessed_date"], "excluded_llm_metadata_keys": ["