LEMMA A CASE STUDY ON LLM AGENTS AND RAG.md", "file_type": "text/markdown", "file_size": 72913, "creation_date": "2025-03-26", "last_modified_date": "2025-03-22", "document_title": "\"Navigating the Sustainable AI Trilemma: Balancing Energy Efficiency, Digital Equity, and Capability in Large Language Models and AI Agent Design\"", "section_summary": "The section discusses the methodology and findings related to energy measurement and model selection in the context of resource-constrained and resource-abundant environments for large language models (LLMs) and AI agents. Key topics and entities include:\n\n1. **Energy Measurement Tools**: \n   - **Powercap**: Used for measuring CPU energy consumption via RAPL.\n   - **Pynvml**: A Python binding for NVIDIA Management Library (NVML) used for GPU energy monitoring.\n\n2. **LLM Selection**:\n   - In resource-constrained setups, the **Llama-3.1-8B-Instruct** model with 8-bit quantization is used.\n   - In resource-abundant setups, the same model is deployed on one A100 GPU, while the **Llama-3.1-70B-Instruct** is used on four A100 GPUs.\n\n3. **Task 1: Memory Formation and Retrieval**:\n   - The experiment involves selecting tokens from two datasets: **LoCoMo** (Internal Memory) and **MuSiQue** (External Memory) with varying sizes.\n   - **LlamaIndex** is utilized for different indexing implementations, with a retrieval top-K set to 5.\n   - The section highlights the energy and time cost comparison of different indexing methods for datasets with similar token counts.\n\n4. **Energy Cost Analysis**:\n   - The energy cost per token is influenced by the indexing method, with vector-based (non-LLM) indexing showing significantly lower energy costs.\n\nOverall, the section emphasizes the importance of energy efficiency in AI model deployment and the impact of indexing methods on energy consumption."}, "excluded_embed_metadata_keys": ["file_name", "file_type", "file_size", "creation_date", "last_modified_date", "last_accessed_date"], "excluded_llm_metadata_keys": ["file_name", "file_type", "file_size", "creation_date", "last_modified_date", "last_accessed_date"], "relationships": {"1": {"node_id": "5bbe2f3b-a5cf-473e-9e50-1d182dcad9fb", "node_type": "4", "metadata": {"file_path": "C:\\Users\\anteb\\PycharmProjects\\JupyterProject\\naive_rag\\data\\ai_articles\\ADDRESSING THE SUSTAINABLE AI TRILEMMA A CASE STUDY ON LLM AGENTS AND RAG.md", "file_name": "ADDRESSING THE SUSTAINABLE AI TRILEMMA A CASE STUDY ON LLM AGENTS AND RAG.md", "file_type": "text/markdown", "file_size": 72913, "creation_date": "2025-03-26", "last_modified_date": "2025-03-22"}, "hash": "47c48bbdebdc34c45547d1ddc9efb86758d4c6bea59cc0a6ae9786ef666e699e", "class_name": "RelatedNodeInfo"}, "2": {"node_id": "6e70e8eb-c370-4e9c-b167-70de6fb3d551", "node_type": "1", "metadata": {"file_path": "C:\\Users\\anteb\\PycharmProjects\\JupyterProject\\naive_rag\\data\\ai_articles\\ADDRESSING THE SUSTAINABLE AI TRILEMMA A CASE STUDY ON LLM AGENTS AND RAG.md", "file_name": "ADDRESSING THE SUSTAINABLE AI TRILEMMA A CASE STUDY ON LLM AGENTS AND RAG.md", "file_type": "text/markdown", "file_size": 72913, "creation_date": "2025-03-26", "last_modified_date": "2025-03-22"}, "hash": "295ff6a04610f6c627c62d4cfeb3f9ef040b710c3d48141bb4a0745c88672a8a", "class_name": "RelatedNodeInfo"}, "3": {"node_id": "5a4fca86-61b2-4468-8850-0bf9aa7aac69", "node_type": "1", "metadata": {}, "hash": "772716dcb83877820252f4151e73cc73e0feaa1f3f526cfcb86292ccf7f780d7", "class_name": "RelatedNodeInfo"}}, "metadata_template": "{key}: {value}", "metadata_separator": "\n", "text": "Tasks 1 and 2 are conducted on the resource-constrained setup, while Task 3 leverages the resource-abundant configuration.\n\nEnergy measurement tools. For hardware energy measurement, we employed two widely-used tools: powercap and pynvml. Powercap [70] interfaces with RAPL (Running Average Power Limit), which is supported by both our Intel and AMD processors, to collect CPU energy consumption data through records in system files. For GPU energy monitoring, we utilized pynvml [71], the Python binding for NVIDIA Management Library (NVML), which provides direct access to GPU power sensors. Both tools offer millisecond-level sampling precision with minimal overhead to the system being measured.\n\nLLM selection. In the resource-constrained environment, we select the Llama-3.1-8B-Instruct model with 8-bit quantization. In the resource-abundant environment, we deploy the Llama-3.1-8B-Instruct on one A100 and Llama-3.1-70B-Instruct on four A100.\n\n# 4.2 Task 1: Memory Formation and Retrieval\n\nIn this experiment, we selected 22,865 tokens of ground-truth documents from LoCoMo (Internal Mem), three different sized subsets (22,226, 99,946 and 202,676 tokens) from MuSiQue (External Mem). We use LlamaIndex [72] for different indexing implementations and the retrieval top-K is set to 5. Ten experiments were repeated to take the average value.\n\na. Different dataset, same number of tokens Table 2 shows the energy and time cost comparison of different indexing methods for different datasets with similar number of total memory token counts.\n\nEnergy Cost Per Token. The pattern of energy cost per token reveals two critical influencing factors. The indexing method shows substantial impact, with Vector-based (non-LLM) indexing demonstrating significantly lower energy 2.", "mimetype": "text/plain", "start_char_idx": 35696, "end_char_idx": 37472, "metadata_seperator": "\n", "text_template": "[Excerpt from document]\n{metadata_str}\nExcerpt:\n-----\n{content}\n-----\n", "class_name": "TextNode"}, "__type__": "1"}, {"__data__": {"id_": "5a4fca86-61b2-4468-8850-0bf9aa7aac69", "embedding": [-0.005818651989102364, 0.03368985652923584, 0.056575629860162735, -0.01478965301066637, 0.05418706685304642, 0.047826819121837616, 0.05040980130434036, 0.03780040889978409, -0.0038015656173229218, 0.02339959144592285, 0.037578217685222626, -0.029523758217692375, -0.013789788819849491, -0.