rag\\data\\ai_articles\\ADDRESSING THE SUSTAINABLE AI TRILEMMA A CASE STUDY ON LLM AGENTS AND RAG.md", "file_name": "ADDRESSING THE SUSTAINABLE AI TRILEMMA A CASE STUDY ON LLM AGENTS AND RAG.md", "file_type": "text/markdown", "file_size": 72913, "creation_date": "2025-03-26", "last_modified_date": "2025-03-22", "document_title": "\"Navigating the Sustainable AI Trilemma: Balancing Energy Efficiency, Digital Equity, and Capability in Large Language Models and AI Agent Design\"", "section_summary": "The section discusses the energy efficiency and performance of different indexing methods used in large language models (LLMs) and AI agent design. Key topics include:\n\n1. **Energy Consumption**: \n   - Vector-based (non-LLM) indexing is highlighted for its significantly lower energy consumption (10\u22122 J/token) compared to LLM-dependent methods (10+1 J/token).\n   - The internal memory configuration (419 smaller nodes) consumes more energy than the external configuration (220 larger nodes).\n\n2. **Cost Metrics**: \n   - RERR (Relevance Energy Retrieval Rate) and EAR (Energy Accuracy Rate) metrics indicate that vector indexing achieves better retrieval effectiveness at lower energy costs (10\u22124 J) compared to LLM-dependent approaches (10\u22121 J).\n   - Internal data shows higher RERR and lower EAR values, while external memory exhibits the opposite trend.\n\n3. **Indexing Methods**: \n   - The Graph index, despite its sophistication, incurs higher energy costs in simpler queries compared to complex multi-hop queries, suggesting the importance of context-appropriate index selection.\n\n4. **Time Cost**: \n   - Node configuration affects processing times, with internal memory requiring longer times for indexing and querying than external data.\n   - The Vector-based method shows efficient processing times (10\u22125 s/token) for moderate token counts, while LLM-dependent methods require significantly longer indexing times.\n\nOverall, the section emphasizes the trade-offs between energy efficiency, processing time, and the choice of indexing methods in AI applications."}, "excluded_embed_metadata_keys": ["file_name", "file_type", "file_size", "creation_date", "last_modified_date", "last_accessed_date"], "excluded_llm_metadata_keys": ["file_name", "file_type", "file_size", "creation_date", "last_modified_date", "last_accessed_date"], "relationships": {"1": {"node_id": "5bbe2f3b-a5cf-473e-9e50-1d182dcad9fb", "node_type": "4", "metadata": {"file_path": "C:\\Users\\anteb\\PycharmProjects\\JupyterProject\\naive_rag\\data\\ai_articles\\ADDRESSING THE SUSTAINABLE AI TRILEMMA A CASE STUDY ON LLM AGENTS AND RAG.md", "file_name": "ADDRESSING THE SUSTAINABLE AI TRILEMMA A CASE STUDY ON LLM AGENTS AND RAG.md", "file_type": "text/markdown", "file_size": 72913, "creation_date": "2025-03-26", "last_modified_date": "2025-03-22"}, "hash": "47c48bbdebdc34c45547d1ddc9efb86758d4c6bea59cc0a6ae9786ef666e699e", "class_name": "RelatedNodeInfo"}, "2": {"node_id": "76bd1c22-0d58-45f9-8425-2a64a9603700", "node_type": "1", "metadata": {"file_path": "C:\\Users\\anteb\\PycharmProjects\\JupyterProject\\naive_rag\\data\\ai_articles\\ADDRESSING THE SUSTAINABLE AI TRILEMMA A CASE STUDY ON LLM AGENTS AND RAG.md", "file_name": "ADDRESSING THE SUSTAINABLE AI TRILEMMA A CASE STUDY ON LLM AGENTS AND RAG.md", "file_type": "text/markdown", "file_size": 72913, "creation_date": "2025-03-26", "last_modified_date": "2025-03-22"}, "hash": "820b4a50b2eecc7e9f37fd263faf9584f799fad6ad08883d996c61ba17a2b4c6", "class_name": "RelatedNodeInfo"}, "3": {"node_id": "aacdae21-3de9-4a12-a688-2b99ea2cbe03", "node_type": "1", "metadata": {}, "hash": "71fdf0732acf194e621e329594d0601a59296f3a406b8ff7efe361988229810c", "class_name": "RelatedNodeInfo"}}, "metadata_template": "{key}: {value}", "metadata_separator": "\n", "text": "The pattern of energy cost per token reveals two critical influencing factors. The indexing method shows substantial impact, with Vector-based (non-LLM) indexing demonstrating significantly lower energy 2.\n---\n# A PREPRINT - JANUARY 15, 2025\n\nconsumption (10\u22122 J/token) compared to LLM-dependent methods (Keyword, Summary, and Graph), which require approximately 10+1 J/token. Moreover, despite similar total token counts, the memory configuration significantly affects energy efficiency, with the internal memory setup (419 smaller nodes) consistently consuming more energy than the external configuration (220 larger nodes) across all indexing methods.\n\nRERR and EAR. The RERR and EAR cost metrics derive from the energy per token and essentially reflect the large differences between the different indexing approaches, with vector indexing achieving greater per-unit retrieval effectiveness at significantly lower energy cost (10\u22124J) compared to LLM-dependent approaches (10\u22121J). A distinctive pattern emerges in the cost distribution: internal data exhibits higher RERR and lower EAR values, while external memory shows the opposite trend, indicating varying energy investments required for achieving optimal Relevance and Accuracy across different scenarios. Notably, the Graph index, despite being the most sophisticated method, demands higher energy costs in simpler query scenarios (internal, 0.717 J and 1.035 J) compared to complex multi-hop queries (external, 0.565 J and 0.390 J), emphasizing the importance of context-appropriate index selection rather than defaulting to the most advanced option to avoid unnecessary energy expenditure.\n\nTime Cost. The time cost reveals that node configuration significantly impacts both writing and reading performance, with the internal memory\u2019s more nodes requiring longer processing times for both indexing and querying compared to external data. The Vector-based method, operating without LLM, demonstrates extremely efficient processing times (10\u22125 s/token) for the moderate token count (around 22k), while LLM-dependent methods demand substantially longer indexing times, 