01330465078354, 0.038323890417814255, 0.017397413030266762], "metadata": {"file_path": "C:\\Users\\anteb\\PycharmProjects\\JupyterProject\\naive_rag\\data\\ai_articles\\ADDRESSING THE SUSTAINABLE AI TRILEMMA A CASE STUDY ON LLM AGENTS AND RAG.md", "file_name": "ADDRESSING THE SUSTAINABLE AI TRILEMMA A CASE STUDY ON LLM AGENTS AND RAG.md", "file_type": "text/markdown", "file_size": 72913, "creation_date": "2025-03-26", "last_modified_date": "2025-03-22", "document_title": "\"Navigating the Sustainable AI Trilemma: Balancing Energy Efficiency, Digital Equity, and Capability in Large Language Models and AI Agent Design\"", "section_summary": "The section discusses the energy efficiency and performance of large language models (LLMs) in the context of a sustainable AI trilemma, focusing on a case study involving LLM agents and retrieval-augmented generation (RAG). Key topics include:\n\n1. **Generation Energy Optimality Ratio (GEOR)**: The results indicate that GEOR values for various datasets (LoCoMo, HotpotQA, MuSiQue) are extremely low, ranging from 1.012% to 1.456%. This highlights inefficiencies in energy utilization during the generation pipeline.\n\n2. **Factors Contributing to Energy Inefficiency**: Excessive energy consumption is attributed to the retrieval and processing of irrelevant memories, overhead from additional operations, and latency in retrieval and computation.\n\n3. **Resource-Constrained vs. Resource-Abundant Environments**: Experiments were conducted in both environments using Llama-3.1-8B-Instruct and Llama-3.1-70B-Instruct. The resource-abundant environment demonstrated better performance metrics, despite operating at full precision.\n\n4. **Cost-Effectiveness Paradox**: The section notes that the resource-constrained environment, despite using reduced precision (8-bit quantization), exhibited poor cost-efficiency ratios compared to the resource-abundant environment, which consumed less energy per memory token.\n\nOverall, the findings underscore the significant potential for improving energy efficiency in LLM design and the challenges posed by different operational environments."}, "excluded_embed_metadata_keys": ["file_name", "file_type", "file_size", "creation_date", "last_modified_date", "last_accessed_date"], "excluded_llm_metadata_keys": ["file_name", "file_type", "file_size", "creation_date", "last_modified_date", "last_accessed_date"], "relationships": {"1": {"node_id": "5bbe2f3b-a5cf-473e-9e50-1d182dcad9fb", "node_type": "4", "metadata": {"file_path": "C:\\Users\\anteb\\PycharmProjects\\JupyterProject\\naive_rag\\data\\ai_articles\\ADDRESSING THE SUSTAINABLE AI TRILEMMA A CASE STUDY ON LLM AGENTS AND RAG.md", "file_name": "ADDRESSING THE SUSTAINABLE AI TRILEMMA A CASE STUDY ON LLM AGENTS AND RAG.md", "file_type": "text/markdown", "file_size": 72913, "creation_date": "2025-03-26", "last_modified_date": "2025-03-22"}, "hash": "47c48bbdebdc34c45547d1ddc9efb86758d4c6bea59cc0a6ae9786ef666e699e", "class_name": "RelatedNodeInfo"}, "2": {"node_id": "f5fe0bc8-a30d-4865-82c9-56e6ff3f31ef", "node_type": "1", "metadata": {"file_path": "C:\\Users\\anteb\\PycharmProjects\\JupyterProject\\naive_rag\\data\\ai_articles\\ADDRESSING THE SUSTAINABLE AI TRILEMMA A CASE STUDY ON LLM AGENTS AND RAG.md", "file_name": "ADDRESSING THE SUSTAINABLE AI TRILEMMA A CASE STUDY ON LLM AGENTS AND RAG.md", "file_type": "text/markdown", "file_size": 72913, "creation_date": "2025-03-26", "last_modified_date": "2025-03-22"}, "hash": "27764f5494b6fc3eb119ac2872455ac7a30f2544a97b3e91ff428c57ab38e5ce", "class_name": "RelatedNodeInfo"}, "3": {"node_id": "5653fad3-fd1d-4a10-b1e3-786dec56366f", "node_type": "1", "metadata": {}, "hash": "1cab2ff185fa5aa4e4934ca4de5cd02783aa3d9492c96bc5f31e449c6bbe1828", "class_name": "RelatedNodeInfo"}}, "metadata_template": "{key}: {value}", "metadata_separator": "\n", "text": "The results show that the GEOR values are extremely low as shown in Table 6, all only about 1-2%. Several factors lead to excessive extra energy consumption, yielding these exceptionally low values: the retrieval and processing of many irrelevant memories, the accumulated overhead from additional operations, and the extended processing time from retrieval and computation latency. This indicates that while the LLM agent is capable of correctly answering memory-based tasks, there is a considerable gap between the system\u2019s current energy performance and the theoretical minimum, underscoring the inefficiency in energy utilization during the generation pipeline, as well as the significant room for improvement in optimizing the energy efficiency of the LLM agent\u2019s design pattern.\n\n**Generation Energy Optimality Ratio (when all operations are performed)**\n|Dataset|LoCoMo|HotpotQA|MuSiQue|\n|---|---|---|---|\n|GEOR|1.378%|1.012%|1.456%|\n\n# 4.4 Task 3: Resource-constrained vs. Resource-abundant Environment\n\nIn this part we repeated the same experiments for Task 1 and 2 in resource-abundant environment using Llama-3.1-8B-Instruct and Llama-3.1-70B-Instruct. The results shown in Figure 5 represent the average metrics of the three LLM-based index methods (Keyword, Summary, and Graph) for the Memory Formation and Retrieval phases, and Figure 4b, the energy of the Generation phases for performing all operations on the Arxiv dataset compared to the multiples of energy consumption of the baseline (Retrieval + Generation).\n\nCost-Effectiveness Paradox. Despite operating with reduced precision (8-bit quantization), the resource-constrained environment shows notably poor cost-efficiency ratios. The resource-abundant environment, running full-precision 8B model, achieves lower RERR (0.642 vs 0.769) and EAR (0.352 vs 0.488), and consumed less energy per memory token (18.7 vs 25.2 joules).", "mimetype": "text/plain", "start_char_idx": 48448, "end_char_idx": 50345, "metadata_seperator": "\n", "text_template": "[Excerpt from document]\n{metadata_str}\nExcerpt:\n-----\n{content}\n-----\n", "class_nam