": "AGENTIC RETRIEVAL-AUGMENTED GENERATION A SURVEY ON AGENTIC RAG.md", "file_type": "text/markdown", "file_size": 104275, "creation_date": "2025-03-25", "last_modified_date": "2025-03-20", "document_title": "\"Agentic Retrieval-Augmented Generation: Principles, Architectures, Applications, and Future Directions in Dynamic AI Systems Across Healthcare, Finance, and Education\"", "section_summary": "The section primarily discusses various research contributions and datasets related to natural language processing (NLP) and question answering. Key topics include:\n\n1. **Complex Sequential Question Answering**: The focus is on developing systems that can converse using linked question-answer pairs, leveraging knowledge graphs.\n\n2. **Fact Extraction and Verification**: The FEVER dataset is highlighted, which is designed for large-scale fact extraction and verification tasks.\n\n3. **Explainable Automated Fact-Checking**: Research by Kotonya and Toni emphasizes the importance of explainability in automated fact-checking, particularly for public health claims.\n\n4. **Implicit Reasoning in Question Answering**: The benchmark created by Geva et al. explores implicit reasoning strategies in question answering, exemplified by a playful question about Aristotle.\n\n5. **Aspect-Based Summarization**: The WikiaSP dataset is introduced for multi-domain aspect-based summarization, indicating a focus on summarizing information based on specific aspects.\n\n6. **Extreme Summarization**: Narayan et al. discuss topic-aware convolutional neural networks aimed at generating concise summaries, reflecting a trend towards extreme summarization techniques.\n\n7. **Semantic Compositionality**: The work by Socher et al. on recursive deep models addresses semantic compositionality, particularly in the context of sentiment analysis using a sentiment treebank.\n\nEntities mentioned include notable researchers (e.g., James Thorne, Neema Kotonya, Mor Geva, Shashi Narayan, Richard Socher) and various datasets (e.g., FEVER, WikiaSP) that contribute to advancements in NLP and AI systems."}, "excluded_embed_metadata_keys": ["file_name", "file_type", "file_size", "creation_date", "last_modified_date", "last_accessed_date"], "excluded_llm_metadata_keys": ["file_name", "file_type", "file_size", "creation_date", "last_modified_date", "last_accessed_date"], "relationships": {"1": {"node_id": "63cc26e7-3760-4de7-9cea-369ed82e58d6", "node_type": "4", "metadata": {"file_path": "C:\\Users\\anteb\\PycharmProjects\\JupyterProject\\naive_rag\\data\\ai_articles\\AGENTIC RETRIEVAL-AUGMENTED GENERATION A SURVEY ON AGENTIC RAG.md", "file_name": "AGENTIC RETRIEVAL-AUGMENTED GENERATION A SURVEY ON AGENTIC RAG.md", "file_type": "text/markdown", "file_size": 104275, "creation_date": "2025-03-25", "last_modified_date": "2025-03-20"}, "hash": "42831f3d26ca4cfa6d94fdd9504869cdd0ae1fd50debb205f921f3f248abd7cc", "class_name": "RelatedNodeInfo"}, "2": {"node_id": "32680891-7472-4c21-83ff-e6fc2f87cab6", "node_type": "1", "metadata": {"file_path": "C:\\Users\\anteb\\PycharmProjects\\JupyterProject\\naive_rag\\data\\ai_articles\\AGENTIC RETRIEVAL-AUGMENTED GENERATION A SURVEY ON AGENTIC RAG.md", "file_name": "AGENTIC RETRIEVAL-AUGMENTED GENERATION A SURVEY ON AGENTIC RAG.md", "file_type": "text/markdown", "file_size": 104275, "creation_date": "2025-03-25", "last_modified_date": "2025-03-20"}, "hash": "032f230c4adbf66b9327476bcf950f87e2d05a11020086319f3db842628f8124", "class_name": "RelatedNodeInfo"}, "3": {"node_id": "7d6d73e3-e442-4117-83b5-f726ae22cf3a", "node_type": "1", "metadata": {}, "hash": "ba619430effc90e42dba67906f90c3ed7021cc66ed5206e26ff8545728bb05ed", "class_name": "RelatedNodeInfo"}}, "metadata_template": "{key}: {value}", "metadata_separator": "\n", "text": "Complex sequential question answering: Towards learning to converse over linked question answer pairs with a knowledge graph. 2018.\n13. James Thorne, Andreas Vlachos, Christos Christodoulopoulos, and Arpit Mittal. FEV