82, 0.004655316937714815, 0.012248772196471691, 0.01372437085956335, -0.014129181392490864, -0.033821236342191696, 0.022799953818321228, 0.015082444064319134, 0.02123294770717621, -0.00422112550586462, -0.01765494793653488, -0.044032901525497437, -0.037973806262016296, 0.02078896202147007, -0.026038436219096184, -0.0424397774040699, 0.016832267865538597, -0.030347704887390137, 0.007312700152397156, -0.013776605017483234, 0.0030164888594299555, -0.0001078337591025047, -0.0020583292935043573, 0.009140875190496445, 0.034082405269145966], "metadata": {"file_path": "C:\\Users\\anteb\\PycharmProjects\\JupyterProject\\naive_rag\\data\\ai_articles\\Bridging Legal Knowledge and AI.md", "file_name": "Bridging Legal Knowledge and AI.md", "file_type": "text/markdown", "file_size": 52048, "creation_date": "2025-03-26", "last_modified_date": "2025-03-22", "document_title": "\"Transforming Legal Research and Decision-Making: A Comprehensive Framework Integrating Retrieval-Augmented Generation, Knowledge Graphs, Semantic Understanding, and Advanced Machine Learning Techniques\"", "section_summary": "The section discusses the integration of advanced technologies in the legal domain, specifically focusing on the use of Agentic Generative AI, Large Language Models (LLMs), Retrieval-Augmented Generation (RAG), Knowledge Graphs (KGs), and Vector Stores (VSs). The authors highlight the transformative potential of these technologies in enhancing legal research and decision-making by effectively navigating complex and interrelated legal data, including constitutions, statutes, regulations, and case law. \n\nKey topics include:\n- The application of AI in legal systems and research.\n- The role of RAG, KGs, and VSs in improving information retrieval and reasoning.\n- The use of Non-Negative Matrix Factorization (NMF) for constructing knowledge graphs.\n- The importance of identifying relationships among legal documents to uncover hidden connections and predict legal trends.\n\nKey entities mentioned:\n- Authors: Ryan C. Barron, Maksim E. Eren, Olga M. Serafimova, Cynthia Matuszek, Boian S. Alexandrov.\n- Institutions: Los Alamos National Laboratory, Holland & Hart LLP, UMBC.\n- Technologies: Agentic Generative AI, LLMs, RAG, KGs, VSs, NMF."}, "excluded_embed_metadata_keys": ["file_name", "file_type", "file_size", "creation_date", "last_modified_date", "last_accessed_date"], "excluded_llm_metadata_keys": ["file_name", "file_type", "file_size", "creation_date", "last_modified_date", "last_accessed_date"], "relationships": {"1": {"node_id": "1d3f5bd7-239e-43d2-ae7f-1e7cd01c88fb", "node_type": "4", "metadata": {"file_path": "C:\\Users\\anteb\\PycharmProjects\\JupyterProject\\naive_rag\\data\\ai_articles\\Bridging Legal Knowledge and AI.md", "file_name": "Bridging Legal Knowledge and AI.md", "file_type": "text/markdown", "file_size": 52048, "creation_date": "2025-03-26", "last_modified_date": "2025-03-22"}, "hash": "8b9a69e79061b64e3a9958d47ca5223db6e2ffd6fcab4d00822647d6e5777090", "class_name": "RelatedNodeInfo"}, "3": {"node_id": "3b0568f9-a756-4b98-9258-5836a8d4c9e7", "node_type": "1", "metadata": {}, "hash": "6561c3b32cbc4f1a9b65700b23088dd1656e2cd94e8f6984198f3cdb70f202e3", "class_name": "RelatedNodeInfo"}}, "metadata_template": "{key}: {value}", "metadata_separator": "\n", "text": "# Bridging Legal Knowledge and AI: Retrieval-Augmented Generation with Vector Stores, Knowledge Graphs, and Hierarchical Non-negative Matrix Factorization\n\n# Ryan C. Barron\n\nbarron@lanl.gov\n\nTheoretical Division, Los Alamos National Laboratory, Los Alamos, New Mexico, USA\n\n# Maksim E. Eren\n\nmaksim@lanl.gov\n\nAdvanced Research in Cyber Systems, Los Alamos National Laboratory, Los Alamos, New Mexico, USA\n\n# Olga M. Serafimova\n\nOMSerafimova@hollandhart.com\n\nHolland & Hart LLP, Santa Fe, New Mexico, USA\n\n# Cynthia Matuszek\n\ncmat@umbc.edu\n\nCSEE, UMBC, Baltimore, Maryland, USA\n\n# Boian S. Alexandrov\n\nboian@lanl.gov\n\nTheoretical Division, Los Alamos National Laboratory, Los Alamos, New Mexico, USA\n\n# ABSTRACT\n\nAgentic Generative AI, powered by Large Language Models (LLMs) and enhanced with Retrieval-Augmented Generation (RAG), Knowledge Graphs (KGs), and Vector Stores (VSs), represents a transformative technology applicable across specialized domains such as legal systems, research, recommender systems, cybersecurity, and global security, including proliferation research. This technology excels at inferring relationships within vast unstructured or semi-structured datasets. The legal domain we focus on here comprises inherently complex data characterized by extensive, interrelated, and semi-structured knowledge systems with complex relations. It comprises constitutions, statutes, regulations, and case law. Extracting insights and navigating the intricate networks of legal documents and their relations is crucial for effective legal research and decision-making. Here, we introduce a generative AI system that integrates RAG, VS, and KG, constructed via Non-Negative Matrix Factorization (NMF), to enhance legal information retrieval and AI reasoning and minimize hallucinations. In the legal system, these technologies empower AI agents to identify and analyze complex connections among cases, statutes, and legal precedents, uncovering hidden relationships and predicting legal trends\u2014challenging tasks that are essential for ensuring justice and improving operational efficiency.", "mimetype": "text/plain", "start_char_idx": 0, "end_char_idx": 2099, "metadata_seperator": "\n", "text_template": "[Excerpt from document]\n{metadata_str}\nExcerpt:\n-----\n{content}\n-----\n", "class_name": "TextNode"}, "__type__": "1"}, {"__data__": {"id_": "3b0568f9-a756-4b98-9258-5836a8d4c9e7", "embedding": [-0.003504826920107007, 0.06330286711454391, 0.03612818941473961, 0.0038582549896091223, 0.016886001452803612, -0.028195695951581, -0.02522428333759308, 0.024543605744838715, -0.016100605949759483, 0.04832799732685089, 0.021820