5735902786, -0.013700753450393677, -0.005661653820425272, -0.00459789065644145, 0.01555913407355547, -0.00010723739251261577, 0.008676717057824135, 0.024440914392471313], "metadata": {"file_path": "C:\\Users\\anteb\\PycharmProjects\\JupyterProject\\naive_rag\\data\\ai_articles\\Bridging Legal Knowledge and AI.md", "file_name": "Bridging Legal Knowledge and AI.md", "file_type": "text/markdown", "file_size": 52048, "creation_date": "2025-03-26", "last_modified_date": "2025-03-22", "document_title": "\"Transforming Legal Research and Decision-Making: A Comprehensive Framework Integrating Retrieval-Augmented Generation, Knowledge Graphs, Semantic Understanding, and Advanced Machine Learning Techniques\"", "section_summary": "The section discusses the integration of various techniques to enhance legal research and decision-making through a domain-specific Retrieval-Augmented Generation (RAG) system. Key topics include:\n\n1. **Non-Negative Matrix Factorization (NMF)**: A dimensionality reduction technique used to uncover latent patterns in unstructured legal text. NMF helps in clustering related cases and identifying new legal trends by factorizing word-embedding matrices into interpretable topics.\n\n2. **Integration of Components**: The framework combines high-recall semantic matching from vector stores, structured relationships from knowledge graphs, and topic discovery capabilities of NMF to improve retrieval quality and provide explainable reasoning over large datasets.\n\n3. **Relevant Work**: The section reviews contributions in RAG domains, semantic search, knowledge graph construction, and legal information systems, emphasizing the role of NMF in analyzing complex legal texts.\n\n4. **Applications of NMF in Legal Domain**: NMF has been applied to legal documents for topic discovery and clustering, with notable studies highlighting its interpretability and effectiveness in extracting meaningful components from large datasets.\n\n5. **Advancements in NMF**: Recent developments include the introduction of sparseness constraints for better interpretability and a guided semi-supervised NMF approach that incorporates domain knowledge to enhance topic relevance.\n\nOverall, the section emphasizes the importance of combining advanced machine learning techniques to improve legal research outcomes."}, "excluded_embed_metadata_keys": ["file_name", "file_type", "file_size", "creation_date", "last_modified_date", "last_accessed_date"], "excluded_llm_metadata_keys": ["file_name", "file_type", "file_size", "creation_date", "last_modified_date", "last_accessed_date"], "relationships": {"1": {"node_id": "1d3f5bd7-239e-43d2-ae7f-1e7cd01c88fb", "node_type": "4", "metadata": {"file_path": "C:\\Users\\anteb\\PycharmProjects\\JupyterProject\\naive_rag\\data\\ai_articles\\Bridging Legal Knowledge and AI.md", "file_name": "Bridging Legal Knowledge and AI.md", "file_type": "text/markdown", "file_size": 52048, "creation_date": "2025-03-26", "last_modified_date": "2025-03-22"}, "hash": "8b9a69e79061b64e3a9958d47ca5223db6e2ffd6fcab4d00822647d6e5777090", "class_name": "RelatedNodeInfo"}, "2": {"node_id": "3ad59e64-6fd5-4e1d-bbd0-7cd5e2b7f753", "node_type": "1", "metadata": {"file_path": "C:\\Users\\anteb\\PycharmProjects\\JupyterProject\\naive_rag\\data\\ai_articles\\Bridging Legal Knowledge and AI.md", "file_name": "Bridging Legal Knowledge and AI.md", "file_type": "text/markdown", "file_size": 52048, "creation_date": "2025-03-26", "last_modified_date": "2025-03-22"}, "hash": "2b069a81f58eb564617415bf722e07378c13f705c8435c3f25e3e1eda0a843e7", "class_name": "RelatedNodeInfo"}, "3": {"node_id": "2c68167b-4e1f-4c35-9d28-54c2842fd77f", "node_type": "1", "metadata": {}, "hash": "b2776cec43c6883adca50b7573c18ee55776f13b4a28a54ae095015f6d6220e6", "class_name": "RelatedNodeInfo"}}, "metadata_template": "{key}: {value}", "metadata_separator": "\n", "text": "- NMF: While KGs excel at mapping explicit legal connections, many latent topics and patterns remain hidden in unstructured text. NMF uncovers these by factorizing word-embedding matrices into interpretable topics, aiding tasks like clustering related cases or identifying new legal trends.\n\nBy combining the strengths of the three components into a domain-specific RAG system, we aim to deliver insights and more reliable outputs for a wide spectrum of legal tasks. The integrated framework leverages the high-recall semantic matching of vector stores, the structured relationships codified in knowledge graphs, and the topic discovery capabilities of NMF. This combination not only improves retrieval quality but also strengthens the system\u2019s ability to provide explainable reasoning over extremely large datasets.\n\n# 2 RELEVANT WORK\n\nThis section reviews contributions across RAG domains, semantic search using vector embeddings, knowledge graph construction, NMF, and legal information systems.\n\n# 2.1 Non-Negative Matrix Factorization for Pattern Discovery\n\nNMF is a dimensionality reduction technique used to uncover latent patterns in data. [33] analyzed NMF as an interpretable method for extracting features and topics from large datasets, specifically highlighting its ability to identify meaningful and non-overlapping components. Building on this work, [27] introduced sparseness constraints for better interpretability, improving applications of NMF in real-world scenarios through more focused feature selection. In the legal domain, NMF has been valuable for analyzing complex textual data, such as case law and statutes, and assisting with topic discovery and clustering. For instance, [14] applied NMF to legal documents to extract latent topics and visualize relationships, demonstrating how NMF\u2019s interpretable structure aids researchers in identifying underlying topics not readily apparent in raw text. They even applied NMF hierarchically to find fine-grained topics, however did not have a way to approximate the number of clusters automatically at each decomposition. More recently, [35] proposed a guided semi-supervised NMF approach for topic discovery in legal documents, using domain knowledge to steer factorization and ensure the extracted topics remain highly relevant. This semi-supervised extension bridges the gap between fully automated unsupervised techniques and expert-driven analysis.", "mimetype": "text/plain", "start_char_idx": 6343, "end_char_idx": 8766, "metadata_seperator": "\n", "text_template": "[Excerpt from document]\n{metadata_str}\nExcerpt:\n-----\n{content}\n-----\n", "class_name": "TextNode"}, "__type__": "1"}, {"__data__": {"id_": "2c68167b-4e1f-4c35-9d28-54c2842fd77f", "embedding": [-0.0026570851914584637, 0.03243206813931465, 0.025515832006931305, 0.018143724650144577, 0.005626768805086613, -0.013454750180244446, -0.012054570019245148, 0.03409925848245621, 0.003598136128857732, 0.04076802358031273, 0.0078670559450984, -0.05605928599834442, -0.0012422524159774184, -0.0061835842207074165, 0.020175613462924957, -0.017114754766225815, -0.03287491574883461, -0.018182799220085144, 0.014340445399284363, 0.017284078523516655, 0.04634269326925278, 0.004913653712719679, 0.007450258359313011, 0.03258837014436722, -0.01206759549677372, -0.04860902950167656, 0.04225286468863487, 0.07346059381961823, 0.017075680196285248, 0.0024942737072706223, 0.022520098835229874, -0.024578038603067398, -0.01979788951575756, -0.02677925117313862, -0.017010554671287537, 0.041471369564533234, -0.022975971922278404, -0.0222726259380579, -0.009150011464953423, 0.02110038325190544, 0.024669213220477104, -0.060435663908720016, 0.0017306872177869081, 0.04584774374961853, -0.025281384587287903, -0.006157534662634134, -0.010947451926767826, -0.022689424455165863, 0.010419942438602448, 0.05194341018795967, -0.03542780131101608, -0.045144397765398026, -0.005135077517479658, -0.013650123961269855, -0.01945924200117588, 0.04048147425055504, -0.04058567434549332, 0.010726028122007847, 0.01895126886665821, -0.019107569009065628, 0.03566225