Studies\"", "section_summary": "The section discusses the topic of Computer System Validation (CSV) within the context of Quality Management Systems (QMS) in the pharmaceutical industry. It highlights the importance of ensuring data integrity when using electronic records, emphasizing the need for compliance with regulatory requirements set by leading authorities such as the US Food and Drug Administration (FDA) and the European Medicines Agency (EMA). \n\nKey topics include:\n- The purpose of the paper, which is to present a practical example of QMS for computer systems validation.\n- The definition of QMS elements specific to computer system validation based on European and US legal requirements.\n- The validation process illustrated through a general V-model, detailing stages such as Planning, Specifying, Development/Building, Verification, and Reporting.\n- The use of process diagrams to demonstrate validation activities in a Supervisory Control and Data Acquisition (SCADA) manufacturing context.\n\nKey entities mentioned:\n- Borut Rusjan (author)\n- European Compliance Academy\n- US Food and Drug Administration (FDA)\n- European Medicines Agency (EMA)\n\nOverall, the excerpt emphasizes the critical role of structured validation processes in maintaining compliance and ensuring the integrity of electronic records in the pharmaceutical sector."}, "excluded_embed_metadata_keys": ["file_name", "file_type", "file_size", "creation_date", "last_modified_date", "last_accessed_date"], "excluded_llm_metadata_keys": ["file_name", "file_type", "file_size", "creation_date", "last_modified_date", "last_accessed_date"], "relationships": {"1": {"node_id": "2f2b69d9-5dae-4221-9dcb-f2fbedd34145", "node_type": "4", "metadata": {"file_path": "C:\\Users\\anteb\\PycharmProjects\\JupyterProject\\naive_rag\\data\\ai_articles\\COMPUTER SYSTEM VALIDATION EXAMPLE OF QUALITY MANAGEMENT SYSTEM.md", "file_name": "COMPUTER SYSTEM VALIDATION EXAMPLE OF QUALITY MANAGEMENT SYSTEM.md", "file_type": "text/markdown", "file_size": 53509, "creation_date": "2025-03-26", "last_modified_date": "2025-03-21"}, "hash": "1137d67616953033e3ad92507bb961ae6095138365fd269914446ae7383f7d2e", "class_name": "RelatedNodeInfo"}, "3": {"node_id": "9b1fe93f-84e8-4b53-8a9f-ae678f789f83", "node_type": "1", "metadata": {}, "hash": "8f9409bbcb73a7e79b9d8baa633ad6ca585cf94bfbf2ebd37fa50e7e95238b06", "class_name": "RelatedNodeInfo"}}, "metadata_template": "{key}: {value}", "metadata_separator": "\n", "text": "# COMPUTER SYSTEM VALIDATION: EXAMPLE OF QUALITY MANAGEMENT SYSTEM DESIGN AND OF PROCESS IMPLEMENTATION\n\nBorut Rusjan*\n\nReceived: 26. 12. 2019\n\nOriginal scientific paper\n\nAccepted: 14. 11. 2020\n\nUDC 005.6:004\n\nDOI: https://doi.org/10.30924/mjcmi.25.2.1\n\n# Abstract\n\nThe purpose of this paper is to present a computer-aided system validation example. Quality Management System (QMS) for computer systems validation and to identify and demonstrate the validation process on a practical case of a pharmaceutical company. Based on the European and the US legal requirements, we define QMS for computer system validation elements. Validation process example based on the use of a general V-model provides a thorough understanding of the actual validation implementation in practice. Computer system validation in a concrete organization can be implemented, based on general and specific standard operating procedures which form the QMS. Planning, Specifying, Development/Building, Verification and Report validation activities are presented through process diagrams based on a practical Supervisory Control and Data Acquisition (SCADA) manufacturing.\n\n# Keywords\n\nQuality Management System, computer systems, validation, V-model, pharmaceutical industry\n\n# 1. INTRODUCTION\n\nIn today\u2019s world, when we operate with electronic records, the possibility of changing or copying the contents of electronic records, without leaving any visible trace of the change, is extremely high. According to the European Compliance Academy (2011a, p. 8), even in the case of the use of computer systems, regulatory authorities want to ensure data integrity through computer systems validation in accordance with their requirements. The world\u2019s leading regulatory bodies that set minimum conditions and limitations, both in general and in the field of computer-aided systems in the pharmaceutical industry, are the US Food and Drug Administration (FDA) and the European Medicines Agency (EMA).", "mimetype": "text/plain", "start_char_idx": 0, "end_char_idx": 1968, "metadata_seperator": "\n", "text_template": "[Excerpt from document]\n{metadata_str}\nExcerpt:\n-----\n{content}\n-----\n", "class_name": "TextNode"}, "__type__": "1"}, {"__data__": {"id_": "9b1fe93f-84e8-4b53-8a9f-ae678f789f83", "embedding": [-0.029244856908917427, 0.05066995322704315, 0.051726676523685455, -0.015401762910187244, -0.0036655140575021505, -0.041767045855522156, 0.018149247393012047, 0.01310338731855154, 0.02361779846251011, 0.014767728745937347, 0.06028614565730095, -0.023287571966648102, -0.06060316413640976, -0.016339605674147606, 0.009160482324659824, -0.014913028106093407, -0.00953033659607172, 0.010342693887650967, -0.009682240895926952, 0.02229689247906208, 0.06039182096719742, 0.050088752061128616, 0.007264982908964157, 0.038491200655698776, -0.05127757042646408, -0.07592567056417465, -0.007753717713057995, 0.04012912139296532, -0.008797233924269676, -0.026233192533254623, 0.019826797768473625, -0.025546321645379066, -0.004263224080204964, 0.0038372317794710398, -0.02126658521592617, 0.0193776898086071, 0.03761940076947212, -0.011808899231255054, 0.013427008874714375, 0.03516251593828201, 0.012066476047039032, -0.0448051281273365, 0.0213326308876276, 0.03901956230401993, -0.022428983822464943, 0.0034475645516067743, 0.007832972332835197, -0.021108077839016914, -0.014490338042378426, -0.00526381004601717, -0.045148566365242004, -0.023842351511120796, 0.023670634254813194, -0.020038142