60276103019714, -0.00012446045002434403, -0.011764666065573692, -0.03714771568775177, 0.013385902158915997, 0.016934268176555634, -0.03279178962111473, 0.015722928568720818, -0.04018217697739601, 0.013948746956884861, 0.01578410714864731, -0.0017022977117449045, -0.0017038271762430668, 0.006172932218760252], "metadata": {"file_path": "C:\\Users\\anteb\\PycharmProjects\\JupyterProject\\naive_rag\\data\\ai_articles\\COMPUTER SYSTEM VALIDATION EXAMPLE OF QUALITY MANAGEMENT SYSTEM.md", "file_name": "COMPUTER SYSTEM VALIDATION EXAMPLE OF QUALITY MANAGEMENT SYSTEM.md", "file_type": "text/markdown", "file_size": 53509, "creation_date": "2025-03-26", "last_modified_date": "2025-03-21", "document_title": "\"Comprehensive Approaches to Quality Management and Computer System Validation in the Pharmaceutical Industry: Regulatory Frameworks, Best Practices, and Case Studies\"", "section_summary": "The section discusses regulatory requirements in the pharmaceutical industry, specifically focusing on the European Union's EudraLex guidelines, which govern medicinal products. Key topics include:\n\n1. **EudraLex Overview**: EudraLex consists of 10 volumes, with Volume 4 addressing good manufacturing practices (GMP) and computer systems.\n2. **Relevant Chapters and Annexes**: \n   - Chapter 4 emphasizes good documentation practices as part of quality assurance.\n   - Annex 11 pertains to computer systems classified as GxP (Good Practices), which includes GMP and GLP (Good Laboratory Practices).\n   - Annex 15 outlines qualification and validation processes.\n3. **Key Principles from Annex 11**:\n   - The principles apply to all GxP systems, not just GMP.\n   - A risk-based approach is essential in all areas of validation.\n   - Acceptance of electronic records is acknowledged.\n   - Validation focuses on system design verification.\n   - Consideration of external suppliers of GxP-relevant IT systems is necessary.\n   - Clear milestones between validation and operational phases of IT 