 21 CFR Part 11**:\n   - System controls, including validation, security of electronic records, unauthorized access controls, and change management.\n   - Controls for electronic signatures, including identification of the signee, timestamps, and responsibilities.\n   - The connection between electronic signatures and electronic data.\n   - User identification methods, such as biometrical identification and user administration.\n\n4. **Broader Considerations**: The section notes that effective computer system validation requires a comprehensive understanding of both technical and procedural controls, as well as adherence to additional regulatory requirements outlined in 21 CFR Part 211.\n\n5. **References**: The excerpt cites Lopez (2012) to support its claims regarding the technical and procedural controls necessary for electronic records and signatures.\n\nOverall, the section underscores the critical nature of adhering to regulatory requirements for ensuring the integrity and reliability of computer systems in the pharmaceutical industry."}, "excluded_embed_metadata_keys": ["file_name", "file_type", "file_size", "creation_date", "last_modified_date", "last_accessed_date"], "excluded_llm_metadata_keys": ["file_name", "file_type", "file_size", "creation_date", "last_modified_date", "last_accessed_date"], "relationships": {"1": {"node_id": "2f2b69d9-5dae-4221-9dcb-f2fbedd34145", "node_type": "4", "metadata": {"file_path": "C:\\Users\\anteb\\PycharmProjects\\JupyterProject\\naive_rag\\data\\ai_articles\\COMPUTER SYSTEM VALIDATION EXAMPLE OF QUALITY MANAGEMENT SYSTEM.md", "file_name": "COMPUTER SYSTEM VALIDATION EXAMPLE OF QUALITY MANAGEMENT SYSTEM.md", "file_type": "text/markdown", "file_size": 53509, "creation_date": "2025-03-26", "last_modified_date": "2025-03-21"}, "hash": "1137d67616953033e3ad92507bb961ae6095138365fd269914446ae7383f7d2e", "class_name": "RelatedNodeInfo"}, "2": {"node_id": "1098590a-3366-48b9-9f10-10e7f92a6408", "node_type": "1", "metadata": {"file_path": "C:\\Users\\anteb\\PycharmProjects\\JupyterProject\\naive_rag\\data\\ai_articles\\COMPUTER SYSTEM VALIDATION EXAMPLE OF QUALITY MANAGEMENT SYSTEM.md", "file_name": "COMPUTER SYSTEM VALIDATION EXAMPLE OF QUALITY MANAGEMENT SYSTEM.md", "file_type": "text/markdown", "file_size": 53509, "creation_date": "2025-03-26", "last_modified_date": "2025-03-21"}, "hash": "a0f1887188213e9c0b5f9edc3ed0cbec31fc0a27085214e579117ae890c09707", "class_name": "RelatedNodeInfo"}, "3": {"node_id": "c805d402-d81a-466c-9abd-3ad96b705d24", "node_type": "1", "metadata": {}, "hash": "fe25fd63959be3ed4e3ce18c042c2a3feb66ed08c13dae3574bee85aedb5244b", "class_name": "RelatedNodeInfo"}}, "metadata_template": "{key}: {value}", "metadata_separator": "\n", "text": "- External suppliers of GxP relevant IT systems are taken into consideration.\n- Milestone between the validation phase and the operational phase of IT systems should be clear.\n\nUS regulatory agency FDA and its regulatory requirements can be found in the section Code of Federal Regulations (CFR).\n\nFor validation of computer systems, 21 CFR Part 211 and 21 CFR Part 11 are important. Number 21 represents the section of foods and medicines, Part 211 represents current good manufacturing practices (cGMP), and Part 11 represents electronic records and electronic signatures (U.S. Food and Drug Administration - FDA, 2013a, 2013b).\n\nCrucial for the computer system validation from FDA point of view is 21 CFR Part 11, which covers electronic records and electronic signatures and includes (U.S. Food and Drug Administration - FDA, 2013b):\n\n- System controls (closed and open type of computer system) \u2013 system validation, electronic records security, unauthorized access controls, system change management etc.\n- Controls for electronic signatures \u2013 signee identification, timestamp, responsibility (e.g. author, reviewer, approver).\n- Connection between electronic signature and electronic data.\n- Users identification \u2013 biometrical identification, username and password, user administration etc.\n\nLopez (2012) states that 21 CFR Part 11 requirements are considering more technical and procedural controls in case of electronic records (creation, modification, storage, archiving, copy, restoration) and electronic signatures. Annex 11, on the other hand, covers all activities, needed for computer system validation. To be able to validate computer systems, we, therefore, also need to consider a broader picture, which includes the regulatory requirements 21 CFR Part 211 (for computer systems subsections D (\u00a7211.68) and E (\u00a7211.80).\n---\n# Management, Vol. 25, 2020, No. 2, pp. 1-23\n\n# B. Rusjan: COMPUTER SYSTEM VALIDATION: EXAMPLE OF QUALITY MANAGEMENT ...\n\n# 4.", "mimetype": "text/plain", "start_char_idx": 8281, "end_char_idx": 10247, "metadata_seperator": "\n", "text_template": "[Excerpt from document]\n{metadata_str}\nExcerpt:\n-----\n{content}\n-----\n", "class_name": "TextNode"}, "__type__": "1"}, {"__data__": {"id_": "c805d402-d81a-466c-9abd-3ad96b705d24", "embedding": [-0.030447885394096375, 0.05087314546108246, 0.041183020919561386, -0.0116792107000947, -0.023774050176143646, -0.05381818115711212, 0.039069242775440216, 0.0031231644097715616, 0.029236620292067528, 0.03465168923139572, 0.057475727051496506, -0.029854128137230873, -0.047453101724386215, -0.01657770946621895, 0.031089143827557564, -0.013965176418423653, -0.008532295003533363, 0.012635160237550735, -0.004248335026204586, 0.00033602965413592756, 0.06474331766366959, 0.05757072940468788, 0.007184465881437063, 0.04315429553389549, 0.005210222210735083, -0.08991863578557968, 0.007611971348524094, 0.04864061623811722, -0.00606820173561573, -0.04253678768873215, 0.020935889333486557, -0.031041642650961876, -0.028120355680584908, 0.013418919406831264, -0.0135732963681221, 0.009987001307308674, 0.03401042893528938, -0.0343429334461689, 0.018976490944623947, 0.01669646054506302, 0.0004015285230707377, -0.044151809066