0307], "metadata": {"file_path": "C:\\Users\\anteb\\PycharmProjects\\JupyterProject\\naive_rag\\data\\ai_articles\\COMPUTER SYSTEM VALIDATION EXAMPLE OF QUALITY MANAGEMENT SYSTEM.md", "file_name": "COMPUTER SYSTEM VALIDATION EXAMPLE OF QUALITY MANAGEMENT SYSTEM.md", "file_type": "text/markdown", "file_size": 53509, "creation_date": "2025-03-26", "last_modified_date": "2025-03-21", "document_title": "\"Comprehensive Approaches to Quality Management and Computer System Validation in the Pharmaceutical Industry: Regulatory Frameworks, Best Practices, and Case Studies\"", "section_summary": "The section discusses the life-cycle model of computer systems within the context of quality management and validation in the pharmaceutical industry. Key topics include:\n\n1. **Phases of Computer System Life-Cycle**:\n   - **Project Phase**: Involves the development and building of the computer system.\n   - **Operating Phase**: The system is in production use, where users can request upgrades or changes. Minor changes do not require full revalidation, only the affected parts.\n   - **Retirement Phase**: When a system is no longer usable, it must be retired following established procedures. Emphasis is placed on retaining Good Practice (GxP) relevant electronic records for a minimum of 10 years post-retirement. Data migration to new systems is also mentioned.\n\n2. **User Requirements**: The system can operate in both manual and automatic modes, with technologists monitoring the SCADA system and intervening as necessary.\n\n3. **Validation Importance**: The project phase is highlighted as crucial for successful validation of the computer system.\n\nEntities mentioned include:\n- **GxP**: Good Practice guidelines relevant to the retention of electronic records.\n- **SCADA**: Supervisory Control and Data Acquisition system used in production.\n\nOverall, the excerpt emphasizes the structured approach to managing computer systems in the pharmaceutical sector, focusing on validation, 