376625269651413, 0.00955129787325859, 0.006033507641404867, 0.012542474083602428, -0.017176689580082893, -0.009593427181243896], "metadata": {"file_path": "C:\\Users\\anteb\\PycharmProjects\\JupyterProject\\naive_rag\\data\\ai_articles\\COMPUTER SYSTEM VALIDATION EXAMPLE OF QUALITY MANAGEMENT SYSTEM.md", "file_name": "COMPUTER SYSTEM VALIDATION EXAMPLE OF QUALITY MANAGEMENT SYSTEM.md", "file_type": "text/markdown", "file_size": 53509, "creation_date": "2025-03-26", "last_modified_date": "2025-03-21", "document_title": "\"Comprehensive Approaches to Quality Management and Computer System Validation in the Pharmaceutical Industry: Regulatory Frameworks, Best Practices, and Case Studies\"", "section_summary": "The section discusses the process of specifying sub-processes in the context of computer system validation within the pharmaceutical industry. Key topics include:\n\n1. **User Requirements Specification (URS)**: The foundation for developing functional specifications and design documents.\n2. **Design Documents**: These include the Functional Specification (FS), Software Design Specification (SDS), and Hardware Design Specification (HDS), which are created based on the URS.\n3. **Design Qualification (DQ)**: A process that verifies the design meets the specified requirements.\n4. **Risk Assessment**: Conducting a functional risk assessment (FRA) based on user requirements.\n5. **Supplier Involvement**: The role of external suppliers in developing the SCADA monitoring/controlling system and creating design documents for review and approval.\n6. **Documentation and Compliance**: Emphasis on referencing URS IDs in design documents to ensure alignment with user requirements.\n\nEntities mentioned include:\n- **Computer System**: The subject of validation and specification.\n- **SCADA**: A specific type of monitoring/controlling system being developed.\n- **Design Qualification (DQ)** and **Functional Risk Assessment (FRA)**: Key processes in the validation framework.\n\nOverall, the section outlines the structured approach to ensuring that computer systems are developed in compliance with user requirements and regulatory standards."}, "excluded_embed_metadata_keys": ["file_name", "file_type", "file_size", "creation_date", "last_modified_date", "last_accessed_date"], "excluded_llm_metadata_keys": ["file_name", "file_type", "file_size", "creation_date", "last_modified_date", "last_accessed_date"], "relationships": {"1": {"node_id": "2f2b69d9-5dae-4221-9dcb-f2fbedd34145", "node_type": "4", "metadata": {"file_path": "C:\\Users\\anteb\\PycharmProjects\\JupyterProject\\naive_rag\\data\\ai_articles\\COMPUTER SYSTEM VALIDATION EXAMPLE OF QUALITY MANAGEMENT SYSTEM.md", "file_name": "COMPUTER SYSTEM VALIDATION EXAMPLE OF QUALITY MANAGEMENT SYSTEM.md", "file_type": "text/markdown", "file_size": 53509, "creation_date": "2025-03-26", "last_modified_date": "2025-03-21"}, "hash": "1137d67616953033e3ad92507bb961ae6095138365fd269914446ae7383f7d2e", "class_name": "RelatedNodeInfo"}, "2": {"node_id": "5a5bcc6b-d68d-403f-9c1f-9ffc3426b3c7", "node_type": "1", "metadata": {"file_path": "C:\\Users\\anteb\\PycharmProjects\\JupyterProject\\naive_rag\\data\\ai_articles\\COMPUTER SYSTEM VALIDATION EXAMPLE OF QUALITY MANAGEMENT SYSTEM.md", "file_name": "COMPUTER SYSTEM VALIDATION EXAMPLE OF QUALITY MANAGEMENT SYSTEM.md", "file_type": "text/markdown", "file_size": 53509, "creation_date": "2025-03-26", "last_modified_date": "2025-03-21"}, "hash": "0dfb87f732de8f69b340b112809d5471a3133443cf3e2b2e0dfb6a52aa2e384e", "class_name": "RelatedNodeInfo"}, "3": {"node_id": "b9308984-90f2-45cb-9bbc-5029ed1ef231", "node_type": "1", "metadata": {}, "hash": "99a24a734876c76918fef08059e7db7d47515f58004cbafa91fc0c5c1e734556", "class_name": "RelatedNodeInfo"}}, "metadata_template": "{key}: {value}", "metadata_separator": "\n", "text": "# Specifying sub-process\n\nWhen the new computer system basis is defined and HLRA and VP are developed, we begin to develop the computer system functionality and specifications, based on the approved URS. The sub-process diagram \u2013 Specifying (Figure 3) shows details of the key steps for conducting these activities.\n---\n# Management, Vol. 25, 2020, No. 2, pp. 1-23\n\n# B. Rusjan: COMPUTER SYSTEM VALIDATION: EXAMPLE OF QUALITY MANAGEMENT ...\n\n|INPUT|PROCESS / ACTIVITY|RESULT|\n|---|---|---|\n|User Requirements (URS)|Create Design Documents (HDS) Software Design Specification (SDS)| |\n|User Requirements|Conduct Design Qualification (DQ)|Design Qualification (DQ)|\n|Functional Specification (FS)|HW design (FDS) SW design Specification (SDS)| |\n|User Requirements (URS)|Conduct (functional) Risk Assessment (FRA)|(Functional) Risk Assessment (FRA)|\n\n# Figure 3. Sub-process diagram \u2013 Specifying\n\nA more detailed description of the specific steps within the sub-process Specifying is presented below.\n\nURS [R1] is the basis for creating the Functional Specification (FS), the Software Design Specification (SDS) and the Hardware Design Specification (HDS). The documentation is created by the computer system supplier, or, alternatively, produced by the company, when the computer system is internally developed. In our case, the monitoring/controlling system SCADA is developed by an external supplier, which creates the design documents and sends us the documents for review and approval.\n\nIn design documents, the suppliers describe how they see and understand the computer system, according to the given URS, while it is desirable to indicate the references to the URS ID, as shown in Table 2. Only in such a way, it can be ensured that the supplier will create a computer system in accordance with the URS. Table 2 presents an example of system functionalities described in FS.", "mimetype": "text/plain", "start_char_idx": 24136, "end_char_idx": 26016, "metadata_seperator": "\n", "text_template": "[Excerpt from document]\n{metadata_str}\nExcerpt:\n-----\n{content}\n-----\n", "class_name": "TextNode"}, 