Validation in the Pharmaceutical Industry: Regulatory Frameworks, Best Practices, and Case Studies\"", "section_summary": "The section discusses the installation and testing of computer systems within the context of quality management in the pharmaceutical industry. It outlines three critical testing phases: \n\n1. **Installation Qualification (IQ)** - A documented verification that the computer system is installed according to pre-approved specifications.\n2. **Operational Qualification (OQ)** - A documented verification that the system performs as intended across all anticipated operating ranges.\n3. **Performance Qualification (PQ)** - A documented verification that the system can operate and control production processes in its specified environment.\n\nThe results of these tests are compiled into test reports, categorized as IQ test reports, OQ test reports, and PQ test reports. Any errors or deviations during testing are documented in a Test and Deviation Report.\n\nAdditionally, the section emphasizes the importance of having the test specifications approved prior to testing, and it mentions that the expected results and other relevant details are recorded manually during the testing process. An example of an OQ test specification is referenced, highlighting the manual entry of data.\n\nKey entities mentioned include:\n- International Society for Pharmaceutical Engineering (ISPE)\n- Test reports (IQ, OQ, PQ)\n- Test and Deviation Report\n\nOverall, the section underscores the structured approach to validating computer systems in compliance with regulatory standards in the pharmaceutical sector."}, "excluded_embed_metadata_keys": ["file_name", "file_type", "file_size", "creation_date", "last_modified_date", "last_accessed_date"], "excluded_llm_metadata_keys": ["file_name", "file_type", "file_size", "creation_date", "last_modified_date", "last_accessed_date"], "relationships": {"1": {"node_id": "2f2b69d9-5dae-4221-9dcb-f2fbedd34145", "node_type": "4", "metadata"