-0.017208293080329895, -0.04448642581701279, -0.04825922101736069, -0.008441292680799961, 0.009275921620428562, -0.021727506071329117, -0.00021374654897954315, -0.03759225085377693, 0.01324550062417984, -0.009499846957623959, 0.006772033870220184, 0.0019389865919947624, -0.01605474203824997, 0.0031485205981880426, 0.010429474525153637], "metadata": {"file_path": "C:\\Users\\anteb\\PycharmProjects\\JupyterProject\\naive_rag\\data\\ai_articles\\AGENTIC RETRIEVAL-AUGMENTED GENERATION A SURVEY ON AGENTIC RAG.md", "file_name": "AGENTIC RETRIEVAL-AUGMENTED GENERATION A SURVEY ON AGENTIC RAG.md", "file_type": "text/markdown", "file_size": 104275, "creation_date": "2025-03-25", "last_modified_date": "2025-03-20", "document_title": "\"Agentic Retrieval-Augmented Generation: Principles, Architectures, Applications, and Future Directions in Dynamic AI Systems\"", "section_summary": "The section discusses various methodologies in the context of Agentic Retrieval-Augmented Generation (RAG) and their applications in dynamic AI systems. Key topics include:\n\n1. **Step-by-Step Reasoning**: Emphasizes the importance of structured reasoning to enhance accuracy in AI-generated outputs.\n\n2. **Routing**: This process involves classifying inputs and directing them to specialized prompts or processes to improve efficiency and response quality. Example applications include categorizing customer service queries.\n\n3. **Parallelization**: This method divides tasks into independent processes that run concurrently, thereby reducing latency and improving throughput. It includes:\n   - **Sectioning**: Breaking tasks into independent subtasks.\n   - **Voting**: Utilizing multiple outputs to enhance accuracy. Example applications include content moderation and code vulnerability analysis.\n\n4. **Orchestrator-Workers**: A workflow where a central orchestrator model dynamically assigns subtasks to specialized worker models based on input complexity. This approach allows for real-time adaptation