 to Use**: \n   - The evaluator-optimizer is particularly effective when clear evaluation criteria exist, improving outputs like literary translations and multi-round research queries.\n\n4. **Taxonomy of Agentic RAG Systems**: \n   - RAG systems are categorized into single-agent architectures, multi-agent systems, and hierarchical agentic architectures, each designed to address specific challenges and optimize performance.\n\n5. **Single-Agent Agentic RAG: Router**: \n   - This architecture centralizes decision-making, where a single agent manages retrieval, routing, and integration of information, making it effective for systems with limited tools or data sources.\n\nOverall, the section emphasizes the iterative nature of refining outputs in RAG systems and provides a framework for understanding different architectural designs."}, "excluded_embed_metadata_keys": ["file_name", "file_type", "file_size", "creation_date", "last_modified_date", "last_accessed_date"], "excluded_llm_metadata_keys": ["file_name", "file_type", "file_size", "creation_date", "last_modified_date", "last_accessed_date"], "relationships": {"1": {"node_id": "67f7d17c-65a8-47f0-8f2e-71490af5fab1", "node_type": "4", "metadata": {"file_path": "C:\\Users\\anteb\\PycharmProjects\\JupyterProject\\naive_rag\\data\\ai_articles\\AGENTIC RETRIEVAL-AUGMENTED GENERATION A SURVEY ON AGENTIC RAG.md", "file_name": "AGENTIC RETRIEVAL-AUGMENTED GENERATION A SURVEY ON AGENTIC RAG.md", "file_type": "text/markdown", "file_size": 104275, "creation_date": "2025-03-25", "last_modified_date": "2025-03-20"}, "hash": "42831f3d26ca4cfa6d94fdd9504869cdd0ae1fd50debb205f921f3f248abd7cc", "class_name": "RelatedNodeInfo"}, "2": {"node_id": "67ccf151-be45-4566-8a67-e8e8a7281b1b", "node_type": "1", "metadata": {"file_path": "C:\\Users\\anteb\\PycharmProjects\\JupyterProject\\naive_rag\\data\\ai_articles\\AGENTIC RETRIEVAL-AUGMENTED GENERATION A SURVEY ON AGENTIC RAG.md", "file_name": "AGENTIC RETRIEVAL-AUGMENTED GENERATION A SURVEY ON AGENTIC RAG.md", "file_type": "text/markdown", "file_size": 104275, "creation_date": "2025-03-25", "last_modified_date": "2025-03-20"}, "hash": "2512fa4bdf670edf07990056b8241918eb98c2fc0af6b14f4022e2b7178e2262", "class_name": "RelatedNodeInfo"}, "3": {"node_id": "da2c2324-922e-4e22-8ee5-b30ec62fa013", "node_type": "1", "metadata": {}, "hash": "6279a5d90a1599a96450120ed0b4b045b6f2c3f6ef7a51b7464e8bd8d8e9acb7", "class_name": "RelatedNodeInfo"}}, "metadata_template": "{key}: {value}", "metadata_separator": "\n", "text": "---\n# Example Applications:\n\n- Automatically modifying multiple files in a codebase based on the nature of requested changes.\n- Conducting real-time research by gathering and synthesizing relevant information from multiple sources.\n\n# 4.5 Evaluator-Optimizer: Refining Output Through Iteration\n\nThe evaluator-optimizer [12, 13] workflow iteratively improves content by generating an initial output and refining it based on feedback from an evaluation model.\n\n|Query|LM|LLM|Accepted|Response|\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n|Rejected + Feedback|Rejected + Feedback|Rejected + Feedback|Rejected + Feedback|Rejected + Feedback|\n\nFigure 15: Illustration of Evaluator-Optimizer Workflow\n\n# When to Use:\n\nEffective when iterative refinement significantly enhances response quality, especially when clear evaluation criteria exist.\n\n# Example Applications:\n\n- Improving literary translations through multiple evaluation and refinement cycles.\n- Conducting multi-round research queries where additional iterations refine search results.\n\n# 5 Taxonomy of Agentic RAG Systems\n\nAgentic Retrieval-Augmented Generation (RAG) systems can be categorized into distinct architectural frameworks based on their complexity and design principles. These include single-agent architectures, multi-agent systems, and hierarchical agentic architectures. Each framework is tailored to address specific challenges and optimize performance for diverse applications. This section provides a detailed taxonomy of these architectures, highlighting their characteristics, strengths, and limitations.\n\n# 5.1 Single-Agent Agentic RAG: Router\n\nA Single-Agent Agentic RAG: [30] serves as a centralized decision-making system where a single agent manages the retrieval, routing, and integration of information (as shown in Figure 16). This architecture simplifies the system by consolidating these tasks into one unified agent, making it particularly effective for setups with a limited number of tools or data sources.\n\n# Workflow\n\n1. Query Submission and Evaluation: The process begins when a user submits a query. A coordinating agent (or master retrieval agent) receives the query and analyzes it to determine the most suitable sources of information.\n2. Knowledge Source Selection: Based on the query\u2019s type, the coordinating agent chooses from a variety of retrieval options:\n---\n# 3.", "mimetype": "text/plain", "start_char_idx": 30860, "end_char_idx": 33205, "metadata_seperator": "\n", "text_template": "[Excerpt from document]\n{metadata_str}\nExcerpt:\n-----\n{content}\n-----\n", "class_name": "TextNode"}, "__type__": "1"}, {"__data__": {"id_": "da2c2324-922e-4e22-8ee5-b30ec62fa013", "embedding": [-0.019804663956165314, 0.03437790647149086, 0.02479586750268936, -0.00043372748768888414, -0.007380039896816015, -0.007753713056445122, -0.013919316232204437, 0.026891104876995087, -0.0019050645641982555, 0.0513533353805542, 0.03902212902903557, -0.07067756354808807, -0.010402786545455456, -0.016001207754015923, -0.005962084513157606, -0.013812552206218243, -0.04387987405061722, -0.032856523990631104, -0.024302083998918533, 0.0028325740713626146, 0.040650274604558945, -0.010602968744933605, 0.022340301424264908, 0.018350008875131607, -0.018803754821419716, -0.03867514431476593, -0.00825416762381792, 0.0662468671798706, -0.02573004923760891, -0.0021085827611386776, 0.026917794719338417, -0.026917794719338417, -0.006912949029356241, -0.022206846624612